1500:
** εἰκῇ**,
**without plan** or **purpose, at random, at a venture**, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Xenophanes Poeta Philosophus 2.13, 1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus 47, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 7.9, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 450, 885, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 431, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 28.5; εἰ. ζῆν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 979; πράττειν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 326d; λέγεσθαι [prev. author] “Ap.” 17c, etc.; νήφων παρ᾽ εἰ. λέγοντας 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 984b17, etc.; ἄρμενα εἰ. ἀποκλασθέντα 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 22.14.}}
**II** **in vain,** {{NT+1st c.BC+|PLips. 104.29 (1st c.BC), NT.1Cor.15.2, +others}}
**III** **slightly, moderately**, ἀγγεῖα εἰ. πεπυρωμένα {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Agatharchides Geographus 61. (Prob. for ἐϝεκῇ 'at will', compare ἑκών.)}}

1502:
** εἰκτός**, ή, όν,
(εἴκω) **yielding**, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Alexander Aphrodisiensis Philosophus “Quaestiones - pars” 62.4, “Etymologicum Magnum” 297.8.}}
**II** (ἔοικα) **like**, {{Refs 9th c.AD+|9th c.AD: Theognostus Grammaticus “Can.” 15.}}

1503:
** ἔοικα**,
**as,** ε, etc., _perfect_ with _present_ sense, **to be like**: rarely in other tenses, _3rd.pers._ _singular_ _imperfect_ εἶκε **it was opportune,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.520 (unless from εἴκω 111)}}: _future_ εἴξω **will be like,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1001}}; _perfect_ _3rd.pers._ _dual_ ἔϊκτον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.27}}; _1st pers._ _plural_ ἔοιγμεν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1239, “Ichn.” 95, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 99; ἐοίκαμεν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Laches” 193d}}; _3rd.pers._ _plural_ εἴξασι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 497, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 96, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 291a, “Sph.” 230a, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Comicus 22, 153, 4th c.BC: Eubulus Comicus 98.8; ἐοίκασι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 584d}}; _infinitive_ εἰκέναι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 167, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 185 (compare προσέοικα)}}; _participle_ εἰκώς (also ἐϊκώς {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.254, see entry εἰκός); εἰοικυῖαι 18.418: _Ionic dialect_ (not _Epic dialect_) οἶκα, ας, ε, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.82, 5.20, 106}}, _participle_ οἰκώς {{Refs|[prev. author] 6.125}}; but ἔοκια, ἐοικώς are found in other Ionic writers, as {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC(?): Semonides Iambographus 7.41, 6th c.BC: Anacreon Lyricus 84, 1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus 1, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Aër.” 6, 3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 266, and codices of 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus vary}}; _2nd pers._ _singular_ εἶκας (variant{οἶκας}) {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Alcman Lyricus 80}}: _pluperfect_ ἐῴκειν, εις, ει, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.411, etc.}}; _3rd.pers._ _plural_ ἐῴκεσαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.75, etc.}}, _Epic dialect_ ἐοίκεσαν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.102}}; _Epic dialect_ _3rd.pers._ _dual_ ἐΐκτην {{Refs 8th c.BC+|1.104, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.662, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Scutum Herculis” 390}} codices: _Attic dialect_ _pluperfect_ ᾔκειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1298 (Dawes from Scholia)}}:—_passive_, _3rd.pers._ _singular_ _perfect_ ἤϊκται {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Nicander Epicus “Theriaca” 658}}: _pluperfect_ ἤϊκτο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 20.31, +others, ἔϊκτο 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.107.}}
**I** **to be like, look like,** with _dative_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.474, etc.; Μαχάονι πάντα ἔοικε 11.613; κεφαλήν τε καὶ ὄμματα καλὰ ἔοικας κείνῳ 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.208}}; so εἶδός τε μέγεθός τε, δέμας, etc., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.58, 21.285, etc.; εἰς ὦπα ἔοικεν, ἄντα ἐῴκει, 3.158, 24.630, +others}}; μελαίνῃ κηρὶ ἔοικε **is considered like,** i.e. **hated like,** death, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.500: with}}_participle_, αἰεὶ γὰρ δίφρου ἐπιβησομένοισιν ἐΐκτην **seemed** always just about to set foot upon the chariot, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.379}}; ἔοικε σημαίνοντι **seems** to indicate, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 437a}}; τοὐναντίον ἔοικεν σπεύδοντι **seems** to urge the opposite, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Prt.” 361b, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.6.10, 4.3.8, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Sensu” 437b24}}; ἔοικεν τοῦτο ἀτόπῳ this **is like** an absurdity, **seems** absurd, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 62d; δαιμονίᾳ ἔοικεν εὐεργεσίᾳ 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 2.1: used by 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus in this sense only in}}_participle_ εἰκώς **like,** with _dative_, {{Refs|“Ag.” 760 (Lyric poetry), “Ch.” 560 (cf. IV. 1).}}
**I.2** ἐοικέναι κατά τι **to be analogous to,** {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 4.4.39.}}
**II** **seem,** with _infinitive_ (where we make the Verb impersonal): with _infinitive_ _present_, **methinks,** ἔοικα δέ τοι παραείδειν ὥς τε θεῷ I **seem** to sing (i. e. **methinks** I sing) to thee, as to a god, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.348}}; χλιδᾶν ἔοικας **methinks** thou art delicate, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 971; ἔοικα θρηνεῖν μάτην [prev. author] “Ch.” 926, compare 730; ἔοικα.. οὐκ εἰδέναι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 744; ἔοικα.. ἐποικτίρειν σε [prev. author] “Ph.” 317: with}}_future_ _infinitive_, θέλξειν μ᾽ ἔοικας **it seems likely** that thou wilt.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 900; ἐρεῖν ἔοικας [prev. author] “Pr.” 984; ἔοικα θεσπιῳδήσειν [prev. author] “Ag.” 1161; κτενεῖν ἔοικας [prev. author] “Ch.” 922; τὸν ἄνδρ᾽ ἔοικεν ὕπνος ἔξειν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 821; ἔοικα πράξειν οὐδέν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 813, compare “Cyc.” 99: with}}_aorist_ _infinitive_, πικροὺς ἔοιγμεν.. ἀγῶνας κηρῦξαι **methinks** we proclaimed, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1239}}: with _perfect_ _infinitive_, ἔοικεν ἐπωνομάσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 419c: with}}_participle_, ἔοικε κεκλημένη **seems** to be called, {{Refs|[prev. passage]}}; ἐοίκατε ἡδόμενοι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 6.3.8; κατακεκομμένη ἔοικεν ἡ σύνθεσις καὶ εὐκαταφρόνητος 4th c.BC: Demetrius Phalereus Rhetor “Demetrius on Style” 4.}}
**II.2** _impersonal_, ἔοικε **it seems**: ὡς ἔοικε as **it seems,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 576, 740, “El.” 772, 1341, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 551, etc., used by 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus}} merely to modify a statement, **probably, I believe, Phd.**61c, {{Refs|“R.” 332b, +others}}; ἔοικεν in answers, **so it seems,** {{Refs|[prev. work] 334a, 346c, +others}}
**II.3** personal in the same sense, ὡς ἔοικας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 516, “Tr.” 1241; ὡς εἴξασιν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 497.}}
**III** **beseem, befit,** with _dative_ _person_, τὸ μὲν ἀπιέναι.. οὐδενὶ καλῷ ἔοικε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 6.5.17 (unless οὐδενὶ κ. is _neuter_)}}; ἀνδράσι ἔοικεν τὰ τῆς γεωργίας {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“POxy.” 899.18 (200 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus)}}: with _dative_ et _infinitive_, τὰ μὲν οὔ τι καταθνητοῖσιν ἔοικεν ἄνδρεσσιν φορέειν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.440; compare 111.2 near the end}}
**III.2** most frequently _impersonal_, ἔοικε **it is fitting, reasonable,** mostly with _negative_ and followed by _infinitive_, οὐκ ἔστ᾽ οὐδὲ ἔοικε τεὸν ἔπος ἀρνήσασθαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.212; οὐ γὰρ ἔοικ᾽ ὀτρυνέμεν 4.286: frequently with _accusative_ et}}_infinitive_, {{Refs|12.212, +others}}; in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.196 an}}_infinitive_ must be supplied, εὐνῇ ἔνι μαλακῇ καταλέγμενος, ὥς σε ἔοικεν (i.e. καταλέξασθαι); ἐπεὶ οὐδὲ ἔοικε (i.e. εἶναι) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.119}}:—rare in _Attic dialect_, ἔοικεν νέῳ.. ὀργὴν ὑποφέρειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 879c.}}
**IV** _participle_ ἐοικώς, εἰκώς, _Ionic dialect_ οἰκώς, υῖα, ός,
**IV.1** **seeming like, like,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.449, etc.}}:—the longer form is found in _Attic dialect_ Prose, φόβος οὐδενὶ ἐοικώς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.71; εἰκώς 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 760 (Lyric poetry), “Ch.” 560, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 376, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1321.}}
**IV.2** **fitting, seemly,** μῦθοί γε ἐοικότες.., ὧδε ἐοικότα μυθήσασθαι, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.124, 125, compare 4.239; ἐοικότι κεῖται ὀλέθρῳ 1.46}}; ἐϊκυῖαν ἄκοιτιν **a suitable** wife, 'a help **meet for** **him',** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.399.}}
**IV.3** **likely, probable,** εἰκός ἐστι, ={ἔοικε}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 659, 1488, etc.}}; especially ὡς εἰκός, _Ionic dialect_ ὡς οἰκός, ={ὡς ἔοικε}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.45 (i.e. ἦν), 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 498, etc.; οἷον εἰκός 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 406c; καθάπερ εἰκός [prev. author] “Ti.” 24d; also ὡς τὸ εἰκός [prev. author] “Phd.” 67a, “R.” 407d, etc.}}; οἱ εἰκότες λόγοι, μῦθοι, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ti.” 48d, 59c}}; ἀδύνατα εἰκότα **plausible** miracles, opposed to δυνατὰ ἀπίθανα, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Poetica” 1460a27.}}
**IV.4** καὶ τὰ ἐοικότα and **the like,** αἶγες, αἴλουροι, καὶ τὰ ἐ. {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “Πυρρώνειοι ὑποτυπώσεις” 1.47, compare 3.180; ἄρτιον, περιττόν, τέλειον, τὰ ἐ. 2nd c.AD: Nicomachus Gerasenus Mathematicus “Arithmetica Introductio” 1.3.}}
**IV.5** _neuter_ _substantive_ εἰκός (which see).

1504:
** εἰκών**, ἡ,
_genitive_ όνος, _accusative_ όνα, etc.: _poetry_ and _Ionic dialect_ _nominative_ **εἰκώ** is implied (though not found) in _genitive_ εἰκοῦς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 77, _accusative_ εἰκώ 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 559, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 1162, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.69 (but εἰκόνα 2.143, both εἰκόνα and εἰκώ in 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 37d), 3rd c.BC: Maiistas Epicus 15: _accusative_ _plural_ εἰκούς 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 1178, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 559: (Εἴκω, ἔοικα, ϝεικ- “Inscription Cyprian dialect” 151 H.)}}:—**likeness, image**, whether picture or statue, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.130, 143, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 559, etc.; εἰ. γεγραμμένη 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.1117c; εἰ. γραπτά “IG” 4.940.23, compare 3.1330}}; of needlework, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 223 (_anapaest meter_)}}; **bust**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Alex.” 18}}; εἰ. βασιλικαί, = Latin _imagines imperatorum_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.AD: Libanius Sophista “Orationes” 56.13: generally, εἰ. τοῦ νοητοῦ θεὸς αἰσθητός 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 92c.}}
**2** **image in a mirror**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 1162, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 402b.}}
**3** **personal description,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|PTeb. 32.21 (2nd c.BC), etc.}}
**4** _metaphorically_, **living image, representation**, εἰ. ζῶσα τοῦ Διός {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“OGI” 90.3 (Rosetta, 2nd c.BC)}}; τοῦ θεοῦ {{NT|NT.2Cor.4.4.}}
**II** **semblance, phantom**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 1002; οὐ γὰρ ἐκεῖνος τέθνηκεν, ἀλλ᾽ ἐγὼ ἡ εἰ. αὐτοῦ 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “DMort.” 16.1}}; **imaginary form**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 588b}}; **image in the mind**, εἰκοὺς πατρός {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 1178; δοξῶν καὶ λόγων 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 39c, etc.}}; εἰκόνας σῆς ἀρετῆς thy virtue's **counterparts**, of children, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“epigram Gr.” 435.4; περίβολον ἔχειν δεσμωτηρίου εἰκόνα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 400c; ἐν εἰκόνι βασιλείας 2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 7.9.10.}}
**III** **similitude, comparison**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 559, “Ra.” 906, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 87b, “Men.” 80c, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 536.1; δι᾽ εἰκόνος λέγεσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 487e, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1407a11, 4th c.AD: Libanius Sophista “Epistulae” 8.1.}}
**IV** **pattern, archetype**, ποτὶ τὰν εἰκόνα [κόσμος] ἀπειργασμένος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-4th c.BC: Timaeus Locrus Philosophus 99d.}}

1505:
** εἰλικρίν-εια** [ρῐ], ἡ,
**unmixedness, purity**, opposed to μίξις, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Coloribus” 793a10; ἀέρος 2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 9.73, compare 3rd c.AD: Alexander Aphrodisiensis Philosophus “in librum de sensu commentarium - pars” 137.1: _metaphorically_, λογισμοῦ 4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus “de vita Pythagorica” 16.68 (_plural_)}}; in bad sense, **unrelievedness**, κακοῦ {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Ir.” p.25 W.}}
**II** **sincerity, uprightness**, {{NT+3rd c.AD+|NT.1Cor.5.8, +others, “POxy.” 1252 vii 38 (3rd c.AD).}}

1506:
** εἰλικριν-ής**, ές,
**unmixed, without alloy, pure**, ἐκ πυρὸς τοῦ -εστάτου καὶ ὕδατος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ διαίτης” 1.35}}; θέρμη, ψῦξις, {{Refs|[prev. author] “VM” 19}}; διὰ τὸ εἰλικρινῆ ἕκαστα εἶναι (i.e. τὰ φῦλα) **distinct and separate**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.5.14; εἴ τῳ γένοιτο αὐτὸ τὸ καλὸν ἰδεῖν εἰ., καθαρόν, ἄμεικτον 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 211e}}; τὸ ἧττον εἰ., opposed to τὸ καθαρώτερον, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 340b8; τῶν χρωμάτων οὐδὲν ὁρῶμεν εἰ. οἷόν ἐστιν, ἀλλὰ πάντα κεκραμένα [prev. author] “Col.” 793b13; τὸ λευκὸν [μέλι] οὐκ ἐκ θύμου εἰλικρινοῦς [prev. author] “HA” 627a3; εἰ. καὶ ἀμιγής [prev. author] “de An.” 426b4; ἐν μεγάλῳ εἰ. καὶ κενῷ 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 2p.37U. (perhaps καὶ εἰ.); τὸ ἓν εἰ. καὶ καθαρόν 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.393c.}}
**2** **pure, simple, absolute**, αὐτῇ καθ᾽ αὑτὴν εἰλικρινεῖ τῇ διανοίᾳ χρώμενος the **pure and absolute** intellect, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 66a; ψυχὴν αὐτὴν καθ᾽ αὑτὴν εἰ. ἀπαλλάξεσθαι [prev. work] 81c}}; γνωσόμεθα.. πᾶν τὸ εἰ. **the pure and absolute**, {{Refs|[prev. work] 67b}}; τὸ καθαρόν τε καὶ εἰ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Phlb.” 52d; τὰς τέρψεις εἰ. ἀποδιδόναι 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 1.46}}; ἡδονὴ εἰ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1176b20; εὐπορία -εστάτη 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Sententiae” 14}}; also of evil things, **sheer, absolute**, ἀδικία {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.2.3.}}
**3** **sincere**, ἀπόδεξις {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“OGI” 227.12 (Didyma, 3rd c.BC); εὔνοια [prev. work]763.41 (Milet., 2nd c.BC)}}; of persons, {{NT|NT.Phil.1.10}}. _adverb_ - νῶς {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“OGI” 441.5 (1st c.BC).}}
**4** **total**, ἐκλείψεις {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Cleomedes Astronomus 2.5.}}
**II** _adverb_ -νῶς **without mixture, of itself, simply, absolutely**, διὰ τὸ εἰ. εἶναι Ἕλληνας καὶ ἀμιγεῖς βαρβάρων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Menexenus” 245d}}; τὸ εἰ. ὄν **absolute** being, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “R.” 477a; εἰ. ὑπὸ τοῦ ἔρωτος ὡρμημένους [prev. author] “Smp.” 181c; εἰ. ὅλον λευκόν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Physica” 187b4}}; **without qualification**, - νῶς Ταραντῖνοι {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Tactica” 4.6}}: _Ionic dialect_ **εἰλικριν-έως**, κρίνεσθαι to have a **clear** crisis, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 4.7.}} —The word is confined to Prose.

1507:
Included with: ** ἑλίσσω**
or **ἐλίσσω** (the latter more frequently in codices of {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus)}}, _Attic dialect_ **ἑλίττω**, _Epic dialect_ _infinitive_ **-έμεν**{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.309}}; _Ionic dialect_ **εἰλίσσω** or **εἱλίσσω** (εἱ. is found in codices of {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus (see. below), but κατ-ελίσσειν 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Acut.(Sp.”) 37, κατειλίξαι [prev. author] “Morb.” 2.18, +others)}}: _future_ ἑλίξω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 711}}: _aorist_ εἴλιξα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 73a (εἵλ-}} codices, but κατ-ειλίξας {{Refs|“IG” 22.204.32)}}; _participle_ ἑλίξας {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.466}}, _Ionic dialect_ εἰλίξας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.34}}:—_middle_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.320}}: _future_ ἑλίξομαι {{Refs|17.728}}: _aorist_ ἑλιξάμην {{Refs|12.467, 17.283}}:—_passive_,_future_ ἑλιγήσομαι {{LXX|LXX.Isa.34.4}}: _aorist 1_ εἱλίχθην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 358}}; _participle_ ἑλιχθείς {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.74}}: _perfect_ εἵλιγμαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 791, ἐλήλιγμαι 2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 10.17.12}}: _pluperfect_ εἵλικτο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 927}}; _Ionic dialect_ _3rd.pers._ _plural_ εἱλίχατο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.90.}} —The _Ionic dialect_ form is found in Trag. (see. below, codices usually εἱλ-; but τ᾽ εἰ.{{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 138 (Lyric poetry, codex _middle_),compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1314, 1348 (codex Rav.)), in “IG” [same place], and codices of 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus (as “Ti.” [same place], ἀν-ειλίττων “Phlb.” 15e)}}; ἐπειλίξας is _falsa lectio_ in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 23.161. (ϝελ-, ἐϝελ-, compare εἴλω, ἐλελίζω ad near the end)}}:—**turn round** or **about**: _active_ in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} always of **turning** a chariot **round the doublingpost**, οἶσθα γὰρ εὖ περὶ τέρματ᾽ ἐλισσέμεν [ἵππους] {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.309,compare 466.}}
**2** generally, **roll**, ἑ. βίου πόρον **roll** life's stream **along**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 8(7).15}}; of the chariot of Day, αἰθὴρ κοινὸν φάος εἱλίσσων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 1092 (_anapaest meter_); ἥλιος.. εἱλίσσων φλόγα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 3}}; εἰ. κόνιν **roll the eddying** dust, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 1085 (_anapaest meter_)}}; ἑ. δίνας, of the Euripus, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 7, compare 1103 (Lyric poetry)}}; ἑ. κόρας, βλέφαρα, {{Refs|[prev. author] “HF” 868 (troch.), “Or.” 1266 (Lyric poetry).}}
**3** of any rapid motion, ἅλιον.. ἑ. πλάταν **ply** it **swiflly**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 358 (Lyric poetry)}}; of the dance, ἑ. πόδα **move the swift** foot, conjecture in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 171 (Lyric poetry), compare “IA” 215 (Lyric poetry)}}; εἱ. θιάσους **lead the dancing** bands, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “IT” 1145 (Lyric poetry); ἑ. χορούς 5th c.BC: Strattis Comicus 66.5}}: _absolutely_, **dance**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 234 (Lyric poetry), compare “Or.” 1292 (whence ἑ. τινά}} **dance in honour of**.., {{Refs|[prev. author] “HF” 690 (Lyric poetry), “IA” 1480 (Lyric poetry))}}; ἑ. βωμόν **dance round** it, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Hymnus in Delum” 321.}}
**4** **roll** or **wind round**, πλόκαμον περὶ ἄτρακτον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.34, compare 2.38}}; λίνον ἠλακάτᾳ δακτύλοις ἑ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 1432 (Lyric poetry)}}; χεῖρας ἀμφὶ γόνυ ἑ. **clasp** them round.., {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ph.” 1622.}}
**5** _metaphorically_, **turn in one's mind, revolve**, τοιαῦθ᾽ ἑ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 231, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Epinomis” 978d; μῆτιν 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 1.463}}; ἑ. κακοὺς λόγους **speak wily** words, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 892.}}
**6** κόλπους ἑ. **form winding** reaches, of rivers, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Dionysius Periegeta 630; ἀγκῶνας [prev. author] 979.}}
**II** _middle_ and _passive_, **turn oneself round** or **about** (but in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.49 εἱλίσσεθ᾽ ἑταίρους (as read by Nicanor)}} **rallied his** comrades), ἑλιχθέντων ὑπ᾽ Ἀχαιῶν **when** they **turned to face** the foe, {{Refs|[prev. work] 74, compare 408}}; so of a wild boar, ἑλιξάμενος **having turned to bay**, {{Refs|17.283}}; of a serpent, **coil himself**, ἑλισσόμενος περὶ χειῇ {{Refs|22.95}}; ἡ δέ τ᾽ ἐλισσομένη πέτεται (i.e. καλαῦροψ) the shepherd's staff flies **spinning through the air**, {{Refs|23.846}}; κνίση.. ἑλισσομένη περὶ καπνῷ **rolling** with the smoke, {{Refs|1.317}}; ἑλισσόμενοι περὶ δίνας **whirled round** in the eddies, {{Refs|21.11}}; of a river, δίνῃς ἀργυρέῃς εἱλιγμένος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 791, compare 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 1.32}}; of the waves, τὸ ἑλισσόμενον αἰεὶ κυμάτων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 6.55}}; of ocean, ἑλίσσεσθαι περὶ πᾶσαν χθόνα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 138}}; ὧραι ἑλισσόμεναι the **circling** hours, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 4.3.}}
**II.2** **turn hither and thither, go about**, ἀν᾽ ὅμιλον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.49; καθ᾽ ὅμιλον [prev. work] 467}}; ἑλίσσετο ἔνθα καὶ ἔνθα **turned himself** hither and thither, doubting what to do, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 20.24.}}
**II.3** _metaphorically_, **to be constantly in** or **about** a thing, περὶ φύσας {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.372}}; ἔν τινι, εἴς τι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 194b, 3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus cited in 4th c.AD: Eusebius Caesariensis Scriptor Ecclesiasticus “Praeparatio Evangelica” 3.4: with _genitive_, μέλιτός τε καὶ ἔργων εἱλίσσονται (i.e. μέλισσαι) 4th-3rd c.BC: Aratus Epicus 1030.}}
**II.4** **whirl in the dance**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 569 (Lyric poetry), “IA” 1055 (Lyric poetry).}}
**II.5** _middle_ in _active_ sense, ἧκε δέ μιν σφαιρηδὸν ἑλιξάμενος he threw it **with a whirl** like a ball, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.204.}}
**II.6** τὰς κεφαλὰς εἱλίχατο μίτρῃσι **have their** heads **rolled round** with turbans, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.90.}}

1508:
Related to: ** εἰ**,
_Attic dialect_-_Ionic dialect_ and {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD(?): Arcadius Grammaticus (for εἰκ, see below 11 ad [near the start]), = _Doric dialect_ and _Aeolic dialect_ αἰ, αἰκ (which see), 5th c.AD: Cypria}} ἤ {{Refs|“Inscription Cyprian dialect” 135.10 H., both εἰ and αἰ}} in _Epic dialect_:— Particle used interjectionally with _imperative_ and to express a wish, but usually either in conditions, **if**, or in indirect questions, **whether.** In the former use its regular negative is μ; in the latter, οὐ.
**A** INTERJECTIONALLY, in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}}, **come now**! with _imperative_, εἰ δὲ.. ἄκουσον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.262; εἰ δὲ καὶ αὐτοὶ φευγόντων [prev. work] 46; most frequently with ἄγε (which see), 1.302, +others}}
**A.2** in wishes, with _optative_, ἀλλ᾽ εἴ τις.. καλέσειεν {{Refs|10.111, compare 24.74}}; so later, εἴ μοι ξυνείη μοῖρα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 863 (Lyric poetry); εἴ μοι γένοιτο φθόγγος ἐν βραχίοσιν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 836: more frequently followed by γάρ, αἲ γὰρ δὴ οὕτως εἴη 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.189, +others; εἰ γὰρ γενοίμην ἀντὶ σοῦ νεκρός 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 1410; εἰ γὰρ γένοιτο 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 6.1.38; εἰ γὰρ ἐν τούτῳ εἴη 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 310d}}; of unattained wishes, in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus only with _optative_, εἰ γὰρ ἐγὼν.. Διὸς πάϊς αἰγιόχοιο εἴην 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.825; Ζεῦ πάτερ, αἰ γὰρ ἐμὸς πόσις εἴη 7th c.BC: Alcman Lyricus 29}}; later with past tenses of _indicative_, εἰ γάρ μ᾽ ὑπὸ γῆν.. ἧκεν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 152 (_anapaest meter_)}}; εἰ γὰρ τοσαύτην δύναμιν εἶχον ὥστε.. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 1072: twice in 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” with}}_infinitive_ (compare the use of _infinitive_ in commands), αἰ γὰρ τοῖος ἐὼν.. ἐμὸς γαμβρὸς καλέεσθαι {{Refs|7.311, compare 24.376.}}
**A.2.b** εἴθε, _Epic dialect_ αἴθε, is frequently used in wishes in the above constructions, εἴθε οἱ αὐτῷ Ζεὺς ἀγαθὸν τελέσειεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|2.33; εἴθ᾽ ὣς ἡβώοιμι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.157; ἰὼ γᾶ, εἴθ᾽ ἔμ᾽ ἐδέξω 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1537 (Lyric poetry); εἴθε σοι, ὦ Περίκλεις, τότε συνεγενόμην 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.2.46}}: later with _infinitive_, γαίης χθαμαλωτέρη εἴθε.. κεῖσθαι {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“Anthologia Graeca” 9.284 (1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Crinagoras Epigrammaticus).}}
**A.2.c** εἰ γάρ, εἴθε are also used with ὤφελον (_Epic dialect_ ὤφελλον), of past unattained wishes, αἴθ᾽ ὤφελλες στρατοῦ ἄλλου σημαίνειν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.84}}; εἰ γὰρ ὤφελον [κατιδεῖν] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 432c.}}
**A.2.d** followed by a clause expressing a consequence of the fulfilment of the wish, αἰ γὰρ τοῦτο.. ἔπος τετελεσμένον εἴη· τῷ κε τάχα γνοίης.. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.536, compare 17.496, +others}}; sometimes hard to distinguish from εἰ in conditions (which may be derived from this use), εἴ μοί τι πίθοιο, τό κεν πολὺ κέρδιον εἴη {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.28.}}
**B** IN CONDITIONS, **if**:
**B.I** with INDIC.,
**B.I.1** with all tenses (for _future_, see below {{Refs|2), to state a condition, with nothing implied as to its fulfilment, εἰ δ᾽ οὕτω τοῦτ᾽ ἐστίν, ἐμοὶ μέλλει φίλον εἶναι but}}**if** this is so, it will be.., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.564}}: any form of the Verb may stand in apodosi, εἰ θεοί τι δρῶσιν αἰσχρόν, οὐκ εἰσὶν θεοί {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 292.7; εἰ δοκεῖ, πλέωμεν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 526; εἰ Φαῖδρον ἀγνοῶ, καὶ ἐμαυτοῦ ἐπιλέλησμαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 228a; κάκιστ᾽ ἀπολοίμην, Ξανθίαν εἰ μὴ φιλῶ 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 579, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.475; εἰ θεοῦ ἦν, οὐκ ἦν αἰσχροκερδής· εἰ δ᾽ αἰσχροκερδής, οὐκ ἦν θεοῦ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 408c; εἰ ταῦτα λέγων διαφθείρω τοὺς νέους, ταῦτ᾽ ἂν εἴη βλαβερά [prev. author] “Ap.” 30b, compare 25b}}; εἰ οὗτοι ὀρθῶς ἀπέστησαν, ὑμεῖς ἂν οὐ χρεὼν ἄρχοιτε **if** these were right in their revolt, (it would follow that) you rule when you have no right, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.40.}}
**B.I.1.b** to express a general condition, **if ever, whenever**, sometimes with _present_, εἴ τις δύο ἢ καὶ πλείους τις ἡμέρας λογίζεται, μάταιός ἐστιν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 943}}: with _imperfect_, εἴ τίς τι ἠρώτα ἀπεκρίνοντο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.10}}: rarely with _aorist_, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 31.26.1, 2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “Πυρρώνειοι ὑποτυπώσεις” 1.84; compare 111.2.}}
**B.I.2** with _future_ (much less frequently than ἐάν with _subjunctive_), either to express a future supposition emphatically, εἰ φθάσομεν τοὺς πολεμίους κατακαίνοντες οὐδεὶς ἡμῶν ἀποθανεῖται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.1.19; εἰ μὴ βοηθήσετε οὐ περιέσται τἀκεῖ 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.91; εἰ αὕτη ἡ πόλις ληφθήσεται, ἔχεται ἡ πᾶσα Σικελία [prev. passage]}} in threats or warnings, εἰ μὴ καθέξεις γλῶσσαν ἔσται σοι κακά {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 5; εἰ τιμωρήσεις Πατρόκλῳ, αὐτὸς ἀποθανῇ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 28c, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 28.21: or}}
**B.I.2.b** to express a present intention or expectation, αἶρε πλῆκτρον εἰ μαχεῖ **if** you mean to fight, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 759; ἐγὼ μὲν οὐκ ἀνήρ.. εἰ ταῦτ᾽ ἀνατεὶ τῇδε κείσεται κράτη 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 485, compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.61, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 863.}}
**B.I.3** with historical tenses, implying that the condition is or was unfulfilled.
**B.I.3.a** with _imperfect_, referring to present time or to continued or repeated action in past time (in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus always the latter, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.715, +others): ταῦτα οὐκ ἂν ἐδύναντο ποιεῖν, εἰ μὴ διαίτῃ μετρίᾳ ἐχρῶντο they would not be able to do this (as they do)}}, **if** they did not live an abstemious life, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.2.16, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 489b; οὐκ ἂν νήσων ἐκράτει, εἰ μή τι καὶ ναυτικὸν εἶχεν he (from Agamemnon)}} would not have been master of islands, **if** he had not had also some naval force, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.9; αἰ δ᾽ ἦχες ἔσλων ἴμερον ἢ κάλων.. αἴδως κεν.. ἦχεν 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 28}}; εἰ ἦσαν ἄνδρες ἀγαθοὶ.. οὐκ ἄν ποτε ταῦτα ἔπασχον **if** they had been good men, they would never have suffered as they did, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 516e, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.1.5}}; εἰ γὰρ ἐγὼ τάδε ᾔδἐ.. οὐκ ἂν ὑπεξέφυγε **if** I had known this.., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.366.}}
**B.I.3.b** with _aorist_ referring to past time, εἰ μὴ ἔφυσε θεὸς μέλι.. ἔφασκον γλύσσονα σῦκα πέλεσθαι {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Xenophanes Poeta Philosophus 38}}; εἰ μὴ ὑμεῖς ἤλθετε, ἐπορευόμεθα ἂν ἐπὶ βασιλέα had you not come, we should be on our way.., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.1.4; καὶ ἴσως ἂν ἀπέθανον, εἰ μὴ ἡ ἀρχὴ διὰ ταχέων κατελύθη 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 32d, compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.680, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.364, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.5, 27.63}}: with _pluperfect_ in apodosi, εἰ τριάκοντα μόναι μετέπεσον τῶν ψήφων, ἀπεπεφεύγη ἄν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 36a.}}
**B.I.3.c** rarely with _pluperfect_ referring to action finished in past or present time, λοιπὸν δ᾽ ἂν ἦν ἡμῖν ἔτι περὶ τῆς πόλεως διαλεχθῆναι, εἰ μὴ προτέρα τῶν ἄλλων τὴν εἰρήνην ἐπεποίητο **if** she had not (as she has done) made peace before the rest, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 5.56, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 21c.}}
**B.II** with SUB{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus, εἰ is regularly joined with ἄν}} (_Epic dialect_ κε, κεν), compare ἐάν: [{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.AD(?): Arcadius Grammaticus εἰκαν in Tegean Inscrr. of 4th c.BC (“IG” 5(2).3.16, 31, 6.2, “SIG” 306.34) should be understood as εἰκ ἄν (εἰ: εἰκ ={οὐ}: οὐκ), since εἰ δ᾽ ἄν is also found in “IG” 5(2).3.2, 6.45, and εἰκ alone, [prev. work]3.21}}]; but ἄν (κε, κεν) are frequently absent in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus as 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.221, 14.373 (and compare below 2), and Lyric poetry, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus (who never uses εἰ with ἄν or κε (ν)) “P.” 4.266, +others; in dialects, αἰ δείλητ᾽ ἀγχωρεῖν “IG” 9(1).334.6 (_Locrian dialect_, 5th c.BC)}}, cf. Foed._Doric dialect_ cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.79; rarely in 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus, εἰ μὴ ἀναβῇ 2.13}}; occasionally in Trag., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 234, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 198 (Lyric poetry), etc.}}; very rarely in _Attic dialect_ Prose, εἰ ξυστῶσιν αἱ πόλεις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.21; εἴ τι που ἄλσος ἢ τέμενος ἀφειμένον ᾖ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 761c}}: in later Prose, εἴ τις θελήσῃ {{NT+3rd c.AD+|NT.Rev.11.5; εἰ φονεύῃ 3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 2.9.9, compare 5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus “Inst.” 26.}}
**B.II.1** when the apodosis is _future_, to express a future condition more distinctly and vividly than εἰ with _optative_, but less so than εἰ with _future_ _indicative_ (above {{Refs|1.2a)}}; εἰ δέ κεν ὣς ἕρξῃς καί τοι πείθωνται Ἀχαιοί, γνώσῃ ἔπειθ᾽.. **if** thou do thus.., thou shalt know, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.364, compare 1.128, 3.281, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.549; ἂν δέ τις ἀνθιστῆται, σὺν ὑμῖν πειρασόμεθα χειροῦσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.3.11}}; ἂν μὴ νῦν ἐθέλωμεν ἐκεῖ πολεμεῖν αὐτῷ, ἐνθάδ᾽ ἴσως ἀναγκασθησόμεθα τοῦτο ποιεῖν **if** we be not now willing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.50, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.3.27: followed by _imperative_, ἢν εἰρήνης δοκῆτε δεῖσθαι, ἄνευ ὅπλων ἥκετε [prev. work] 3.2.13, compare 5.4.30.}}
**B.II.2** when the apodosis is present, denoting customary or repeated action, to express a general condition, **if ever**, ἤν ποτε δασμὸς ἵκηται, σοὶ τὸ γέρας πολὺ μεῖζον (i.e. ἐστί) **whenever** a division comes, your prize is (always) greater, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.166}}; ἢν ἐγγὺς ἔλθῃ θάνατος, οὐδεὶς βούλεται θνῄσκειν **if** death come near, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 671}}; with ἄν omitted, εἴ περ γάρ τε χόλον.. καταπέψῃ ἀλλά.. ἔχει κότον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.81.}}
**B.II.2.b** with Rhet. present in apodosis, ἐὰν μὴ οἱ φιλόσοφοι βασιλεύσωσιν, οὐκ ἔστι κακῶν παῦλα there is (i.e. can be, will be) no rest.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 473d.}}
**B.III** with OPTATIVE (never with ἄν in early Gr., later ἐάν with _optative_, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Damascius Philosophus “de Principiis” 114, +others)}}
**B.III.1** to express a future condition less definitely than ἐάν with _subjunctive_, usually with _optative_ with ἄν in _apodosis_, ἦ κεν γηθήσαι Πρίαμος Πριάμοιό τε παῖδες.. εἰ σφῶιν τάδε πάντα πυθοίατο μαρναμένοιιν surely they would exult, **if** they should hear.., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.255, compare 7.28, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.223; εἴης φορητὸς οὐκ ἄν, εἰ πράσσοις καλῶς 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 979; οὐδὲ γὰρ ἄν με ἐπαινοίη, εἰ ἐξελαύνοιμι τοὺς εὐεργέτας 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.7.11; οἶκος δ᾽ αὐτός, εἰ φθογγὴν λάβοι, σαφέστατ᾽ ἂν λέξειεν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 37, etc.}}: _future_ _optative_ is _falsa lectio_ in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 164a}}: with _present_ _indicative_ in _apodosis_, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Xenophanes Poeta Philosophus 34.3, 3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 253}}: with _future_ _indicative_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Melissus Philosophus 5.}}
**B.III.1.b** in Hom.sometimes with _present_ _optative_, to express an unfulfilled present condition, εἰ μὲν νῦν ἐπὶ ἄλλῳ ἀεθλεύοιμεν, ἦ τ᾽ ἂν ἐγὼ τὰ πρῶτα φεροίμην **if** we were now contending, etc., [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.274: rarely in Trag., εἰ μὴ κνίζοι (={εἰ μὴ ἔκνιζε}) 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 568; also εἰ ἀναγκαῖον εἴη ἀδικεῖν ἢ ἀδικεῖσθαι, ἑλοίμην ἂν μᾶλλον ἀδικεῖσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 469c.}}]
**B.III.2** when the apodosis is past, denoting customary or repeated action, to express a general condition in past time (corresponding to use of _subjunctive_ in present time, above {{Refs|11.2)}}; once in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, εἴ τίς με.. ἐνίπτοι, ἀλλὰ σὺ τόν γ᾽.. κατέρυκες 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.768}}; εἰ δέ τινας θορυβουμένους αἴσθοιτο.., κατασβεννύναι τὴν ταραχὴν ἐπειρᾶτο **if** he should see (**whenever** he saw) any troops in confusion, he (always) tried, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.3.55, compare “An.” 4.5.13, “Mem.” 4.2.40}}; εἴ τις ἀντείποι, εὐθὺς ἐτεθνήκει **if** any one made objection, he was a dead man at once, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.66; ἀλλ᾽ εἴ τι μὴ φέροιμεν, ὤτρυνεν φέρειν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 755. For εἰ with _indicative_ in this sense see above 1.1}}: _indicative_ and _optative_ are found in same sentence, ἐμίσει, οὐκ εἴ τις κακῶς πάσχων ἠμύνετο, ἀλλ᾽ εἴ τις εὐεργετούμενος ἀχάριστος φαίνοιτο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Agesilaus” 11.3.}}
**B.III.3** in oratio obliqua after past tenses, representing ἐάν with _subjunctive_ or εἰ with a primary (never an historical) tense of the _indicative_ in oratio recta, ἐλογίζοντο ὡς, εἰ μὴ μάχοιντο, ἀποστήσοιντο αἱ πόλεις (representing ἐὰν μὴ μαχώμεθα, ἀποστήσονται) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 6.4.6, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.104, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 5.2.2}}; ἔλεγεν ὅτι, εἰ βλαβερὰ πεπραχὼς εἴη, δίκαιος εἴη ζημιοῦσθαι (representing εἰ βλαβερὰ πέπραχε, δίκαιός ἐστι){{Refs|[prev. work] 32, compare “An.” 6.6.25}}; εἰ δέ τινα φεύγοντα λήψοιτο, προηγόρευεν ὅτι ὡς πολεμίῳ χρήσοιτο (representing εἴ τινα λήψομαι, χρήσομαι) {{Refs|[prev. author] “Cyr.” 3.1.3}}; also, where oratio obliqua is implied in the leading clause, οὐκ ἦν τοῦ πολέμου πέρας Φιλίππῳ, εἰ μὴ Θηβαίους.. ἐχθροὺς ποιήσειε τῇ πόλει, i.e. Philip thought there would be no end to the war, **un**less he should make.. (his thought having been ἐὰν μὴ ποιήσω), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.145; ἐβούλοντο γὰρ σφίσιν, εἴ τινα λάβοιεν, ὑπάρχειν ἀντὶ τῶν ἔνδον, ἢν ἄρα τύχωσί τινες ἐζωγρημένοι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.5.}}
**B.III.4** with _optative_ with ἄν, only when the clause serves as apodosis as well as protasis,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 329b, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.18, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.5.3 (see. ἄν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus 111. d).}}
**B.IV** with _infinitive_, in oratio obliqua, only in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus, εἰ γὰρ δὴ δεῖν πάντως περιθεῖναι ἄλλῳ τέῳ τὴν βασιληΐην, [ἔφη] δικαιότερον εἶναι κτλ. 1.129; εἰ εἶναι τοῦτο μὴ φίλον 2.64, compare 172, 3.105, 108.}}
**B.V** after Verbs denoting **wonder, delight, indignation, disappointment, contentment**, and similar emotions, εἰ with _indicative_ is used instead of ὅτι, to express the object of the feeling in a hypothetical form, θαυμάζω εἰ μηδεὶς ὑμῶν μήτ᾽ ἐνθυμεῖται μήτ᾽ ὀργίζεται, ὁρῶν.. I wonder **that** no one of you is either concerned or angry when he sees.., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.43; οὐκ ἀγαπᾷ εἰ μὴ δίκην δέδωκεν, ἀλλ᾽ εἰ μὴ καὶ χρυσῷ στεφάνῳ στεφανωθήσεται ἀγανακτεῖ 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.147}}: after past tenses, ἐθαύμασε δ᾽ εἰ μὴ φανερόν ἐστιν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.1.13; δεινὸν εἰσῄει, εἰ μὴ.. δόξει 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.33; ἐθαύμαζον εἴ τι ἕξει τις χρήσασθαι τῷ λόγῳ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 95a; οὐδὲ ᾐσχύνθη εἰ.. ἐπάγει 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.105: in oratio obliqua (expressed or implied) with _optative_, ἐπεῖπεν ὡς δεινὸν (i.e. εἴη) εἰ.. μεγαλόψυχος γένοιτο 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.157; ᾤκτιρον εἰ ἁλώσοιντο 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.4.7}}; ἐθαύμαζε δ᾽ εἴ τις ἀρετὴν ἐπαγγελλόμενος ἀργύριον πράττοιτο he wondered **that** any one should demand money, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Mem.” 1.2.7}}; ἔχαιρον ἀγαπῶν εἴ τις ἐάσοι I rejoiced, being content **if** any one should let it pass, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 450a:}} —in this use the _negative_ οὐ is also found, ἀγανακτῶ εἰ ὁ Φίλιππος ἁρπάζων οὐ λυπεῖ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 8.55; δεινὸν ἂν εἴη εἰ οἱ ἐκείνων ξύμμαχοι οὐκ ἀπεροῦσιν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.121; τέρας λέγεις, εἰ οὐκ ἂν δύναιντο λαθεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 91d, etc.}}
**B.VI** in citing a fact as a ground of argument or appeal, **as surely as, since**, εἴ ποτ᾽ ἔην γε **if** there was [as there was], i.e. **as sure as** there was such an one, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.180, +others; εἰ τότε κοῦρος ἔα, νῦν αὖτέ με γῆρας ὀπάζει 4.321}}; πολλοὺς γὰρ οἶκε εἶναι εὐπετέστερον διαβάλλειν ἢ ἕνα, εἰ Κλεομένεα μὲν μοῦνον οὐκ οἷός τε ἐγένετο διαβαλεῖν, τρεῖς δὲ μυριάδας Ἀθηναίων ἐποίησε τοῦτο it seems easier to deceive many than one, **if** (as was the fact, i.e. **since**) he was not able.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.97, compare 1.60, +others}}
**B.VII** ELLIPTICAL CONSTRUCTIONS:
**B.VII.1** with apodosis implied in the context, εἰ having the force of **in case, supposing that**, πρὸς τὴν πόλιν, εἰ ἐπιβοηθοῖεν, ἐχώρουν they marched towards the city [so as to meet the citizens], **in case** they should rush out, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.100}}; ἱκέται πρὸς σὲ δεῦρ᾽ ἀφίγμεθα, εἴ τινα πόλιν φράσειας ἡμῖν εὔερον we have come hither to you, **in case** you should tell us of some fleecy city (i.e. that we might hear of it), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 120}}; παρέζεο καὶ λαβὲ γούνων, αἴ κέν πως ἐθέλῃσιν ἐπὶ Τρώεσσιν ἀρῆξαι sit by him and grasp his knees [so as to persuade him], **in case** he be willing to help the Trojans, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.408, compare 66, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.94, 3.92}}; ἄκουσον καὶ ἐμοῦ, ἐάν σοι ἔτι ταὐτὰ δοκῇ hear me also [that you may assent], **in case** the same opinion please you, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 358b}}; ἰδὲ δή, ἐάν σοι ὅπερ ἐμοὶ συνδοκῇ look now, **in case** you approve what I do, {{Refs|[prev. work] 434a.}}
**B.VII.2** with apodosis suppressed for rhetorical reasons, εἴ περ γάρ κ᾽ ἐθέλῃσιν Ὀλύμπιος.. στυφελίξαι **if** he wish to thrust him away, [he will do so], {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.580}}; εἰ μὲν δώσουσι γέρας—· εἰ δέ κε μὴ δώωσιν, ἐγὼ δέ κεν αὐτὸς ἕλωμαι **if** they shall give me a prize, [well and good]; but **if** they give not, then I will take one for myself, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1.135, compare 6.150, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 468}}; καὶ ἢν μὲν ξυμβῇ ἡ πεῖρα—· εἰ δὲ μή.. and **if** the attempt succeed, [well]; otherwise.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.3, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 325d.}}
**B.VII.3** with the Verb of the protasis omitted, chiefly in the following expressions:
**B.VII.3.a** εἰ μή **except**, οὐδὲν ἄλλο σιτέονται, εἰ μὴ ἰχθῦς μοῦνον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.200}}; μὰ τὼ θεώ, εἰ μὴ Κρίτυλλά γ᾽ [εἰμί]—nay, **if** I'm not Critylla! i.e. I am, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 898}}; εἰ μὴ ὅσον **except** only, ἐγὼ μέν μιν οὐκ εἶδον, εἰ μὴ ὅσον γραφῇ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.73, compare 1.45, 2.20; εἰ μὴ εἰ 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.17, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 480b, etc.}}; εἰ μή τι οὖν, ἀλλὰ σμικρόν γέ μοι τῆς ἀρχῆς χάλασον **if** nothing else, yet.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Men.” 86e; ironical, εἰ μὴ ἄρα ἡ τῆς ἀρετῆς ἐπιμέλεια διαφθορά ἐστιν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.2.8; εἰ μή πέρ γε τὸν ὑοσκύαμον χρήματα εἶναι φήσομεν [prev. author] “Oec.” 1.13.}}
**B.VII.3.b** εἰ δὲ μή but **if** not, i.e. **otherwise**, προηγόρευε τοῖς Λαμψακηνοῖσι μετιέναι Μιλτιάδεα, εἰ δὲ μή, σφέας πίτυος τρόπον ἀπείλεε ἐκτρίψειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.37, compare 56}}; after μάλιστα μέν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.32, 35, etc.:}} —after a preceding _negative_, μὴ τύπτ᾽· εἰ δὲ μή, σαυτόν ποτ᾽ αἰτιάσει don't beat me; **otherwise**, you will have yourself to blame, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1433; ὦ Κῦρε, μὴ οὕτω λέγε· εἰ δὲ μή, οὐ θαρροῦντά με ἕξεις 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.1.35; οὔτ᾽ ἐν τῷ ὕδατι τὰ ὅπλα ἦν ἔχειν· εἰ δὲ μή [prev. author] “An.” 4.3.6, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.28, 131, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 91c.}}
**B.VII.3.c** εἰ δέ sometimes stands for εἰ δὲ μή, εἰ μὲν βούλεται, ἑψέτω· εἰ δ᾽, ὅτι βούλεται, τοῦτο ποιείτω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 285c, compare “Smp.” 212c; εἰ δ᾽ οὖν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 722; εἰ δ᾽ οὕτως 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1094a24}}; εἰ δὲ τοῦτο and **if** so, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 2.1.29.}}
**B.VII.3.d** εἰ γάρ for **if** so, {{Refs|[prev. author] 7.3.6.}}
**B.VII.3.e** εἴ τις **if** any, i. e. **as much as** or **more than** any, τῶν γε νῦν αἴ τις ἐπιχθονίων, ὀρθῶς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 5.5; ὄτλον ἄλγιστον ἔσχον, εἴ τις Αἰτωλὶς γυνή 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 8, compare “OC” 734}}; εἴ τις ἄλλος, **siquis alius**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 6, etc.; εἴ τινες καὶ ἄλλοι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.2, etc.; εἴπερ τις ἄλλος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 501d}}; also κατ᾽ εἰ δέ τινα τρόπον in **any** way, {{Refs|“IG” 5(2).6.27 (from Tegea).}}
**B.VII.3.f** εἴ ποτε or εἴπερ ποτέ **now if** ever, ἡμῖν δὲ καλῶς, εἴπερ ποτέ, ἔχει.. ἡ ξυναλλαγή {{Refs 7th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.20, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 594; αἴ ποτα κἄλλοτα 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus “Supp.” 7.11, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 6.4.12, etc.}}; but in prayers, εἴ ποτέ τοι ἐπὶ νηὸν ἔρεψα.. τόδε μοι κρήηνον ἐέλδωρ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.39.}}
**B.VII.3.g** εἴ ποθεν (i.e. δυνατόν ἐστι) **if** from any quarter, i.e. from some quarter or other, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1204 (Lyric poetry)}}; so εἴ ποθι somewhere, anywhere, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Aj.” 885 (Lyric poetry); εἴ που 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.193.}}
**B.VII.3.h** εἴ πως{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 388, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.3.11}}: in an elliptical sentence (cf. VII. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1), πρέσβεις πέμψαντες, εἴ πως πείσειαν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.58.}}
**B.VIII** with other PARTICLES:
**B.VIII.1** for the distinction between καὶ εἰ (or καὶ ἐάν, or κἄν) even **if**, and εἰ καί (or ἐὰν καί) even **though**, see at {καί}:—the opposite of καὶ εἰ is οὐδ᾽ εἰ, not even **if**; that of εἰ καί is εἰ μηδέ, **if (although**) not even.
**B.VIII.2** for ὡς εἰ, ὡς εἴ τε, ὥσπερ εἰ, etc., see at {ὡς} and ὥσπερ.
**B.VIII.3** for εἰ ἄρα, see at {ἄρα}; for εἰ δή, εἴπερ, see at {εἰ δή, εἴπερ}; for εἴ γε, see at {γέ}.
**B.IX** in _negative_ oaths, = Hebrew **im**, {{LXX+NT|LXX.Psa.94(95).11, NT.Mark.8.12, +others}}
**C** IN INDIRECT QUESTIONS, **whether**, followed by the _indicative_, _subjunctive_, or _optative_, according to the principles of oratio obliqua:
**C.1** with IN{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator after primary tenses, representing the same tense in the direct question, σάφα δ᾽ οὐκ οἶδ᾽ εἰ θεός ἐστιν}} **whether** he is a god, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.183; εἰ ξυμπονήσεις.. σκόπει 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 41.}}
**C.2** with SUB{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus after primary tenses, representing a dubitative}}_subjunctive_ in the direct question, τὰ ἐκπώματα οὐκ οἶδ᾽ εἰ Χρυσάντᾳ τουτῳῒ δῶ **whether** I should give them, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.4.16: sometimes elliptical, ἐς τὰ χρηστήρια ἔπεμπε, εἰ στρατεύηται ἐπὶ τοὺς Πέρσας 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.75.}}
**C.3** OPT. after past tenses, representing either of the two previous constructions in the direct question, ἤρετο εἴ τις ἐμοῦ εἴη σοφώτερος he asked **whether** any one was wiser than I (direct ἔστι τις σοφώτερο;), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 21a; ἐπεκηρυκεύετο Πεισιστράτῳ, εἰ βούλοιτό οἱ τὴν θυγατέρα ἔχειν γυναῖκα 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.60: rarely}}_aorist_ _optative_ for the _aorist_ _indicative_, ἠρώτων αὐτὸν εἰ ἀναπλεύσειεν I asked him **whether** he had set sail (direct ἀνέπλευσα;), {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 50.55: but}}_aorist_ _optative_ usually represents _aorist_ _subjunctive_, τὸν θεὸν ἐπήροντο εἰ παραδοῖεν Κορινθίοις τὴν πόλιν.. καὶ τιμωρίαν τινὰ πειρῷντ᾽ ἀπ᾽ αὐτῶν ποιεῖσθαι they asked **whether** they should deliver their city to the Corinthians, and should try.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.25:}} —in both constructions the _indicative_ or _subjunctive_ may be retained, ψῆφον ἐβούλοντο ἐπαγαγεῖν εἰ χρὴ πολεμεῖν{{Refs|[prev. work] 119}}; ἐβουλεύοντο εἴτε κατακαύσωσιν.. εἴτε τι ἄλλο χρήσωνται **whether** they should burn them or should dispose of them in some other way, {{Refs|[prev. author] 2.4}}; ἀνακοινοῦσθαι αὐτὸν αὑτῷ εἰ δῷ ἐπιψηφίσαι τοῖς προέδροις [he said that] he consulted him **whether** he should give.., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.68.}}
**C.4** with OPT. and ἄν when this was the form of the direct question, ἠρώτων εἰ δοῖεν ἂν τούτων τὰ πιστά they asked **whether** they would give (direct δοιήτε ἄ;), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.8.7.}}
**C.5** the NEG. used with εἰ in indirect questions is οὐ, when οὐ would be used in the direct question, ἐνετέλλετο.. εἰρωτᾶν εἰ οὔ τι ἐπαισχύνεται **whether** he is not ashamed, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.90, etc.}}; but if μή would be required in the direct form, it is retained in the indirect, οὐ τοῦτο ἐρωτῶ, ἀλλ᾽ εἰ τοῦ μὲν δικαίου μὴ ἀξιοῖ πλέον ἔχειν μηδὲ βούλεται ὁ δίκαιος, τοῦ δὲ ἀδίκου (the direct question would be μὴ ἀξιοῖ μηδὲ βούλετα; he does not see fit nor wish, does he?) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 349b}}:—in double indirect questions, εἴτε.. εἴτε..; εἰ.. εἴτε..; εἴτε.. ἢ.., either οὐ or μή can be used in the second clause, ὅπως ἴδῃς εἴτ᾽ ἔνδον εἴτ᾽ οὐκ ἔνδον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 7; σκοπῶμεν εἰ ἡμῖν πρέπει ἢ οὔ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 451d}}; εἰ ἀληθὲς ἢ μή, πειράσομαι μαθεῖν{{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. work] 339a; πολλὰ ἂν περιεσκέψω, εἴτε ἐπιτρεπτέον εἴτε οὔ·.. οὐδένα λόγον οὐδὲ συμβουλὴν ποιῇ, εἴτε χρὴ ἐπιτρέπειν σαυτὸν αὐτῷ εἴτε μή [prev. author] “Prt.” 313a, 313b; ἀνάγκη τὴν ἐμὴν μητέρα, εἴτε θυγάτηρ ἦν Κίρωνος εἴτε μή, καὶ εἰ παρ᾽ ἐκείνῳ διῃτᾶτο ἢ οὔ, καὶ γάμους εἰ διττοὺς ὑπὲρ ταύτης εἱστίασεν ἢ μὴ.. πάντα ταῦτα εἰδέναι τοὺς οἰκέτας 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 8.9}}; τοὺς νόμους καταμανθάνειν εἰ καλῶς κεῖνται ἢ μή.. τοὺς λόγους εἰ ὀρθῶς ὑμᾶς διδάσκουσιν ἢ οὔ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.14.}}

1509:
Related to: ** εἰ**,
_Attic dialect_-_Ionic dialect_ and {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD(?): Arcadius Grammaticus (for εἰκ, see below 11 ad [near the start]), = _Doric dialect_ and _Aeolic dialect_ αἰ, αἰκ (which see), 5th c.AD: Cypria}} ἤ {{Refs|“Inscription Cyprian dialect” 135.10 H., both εἰ and αἰ}} in _Epic dialect_:— Particle used interjectionally with _imperative_ and to express a wish, but usually either in conditions, **if**, or in indirect questions, **whether.** In the former use its regular negative is μ; in the latter, οὐ.
**A** INTERJECTIONALLY, in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}}, **come now**! with _imperative_, εἰ δὲ.. ἄκουσον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.262; εἰ δὲ καὶ αὐτοὶ φευγόντων [prev. work] 46; most frequently with ἄγε (which see), 1.302, +others}}
**A.2** in wishes, with _optative_, ἀλλ᾽ εἴ τις.. καλέσειεν {{Refs|10.111, compare 24.74}}; so later, εἴ μοι ξυνείη μοῖρα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 863 (Lyric poetry); εἴ μοι γένοιτο φθόγγος ἐν βραχίοσιν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 836: more frequently followed by γάρ, αἲ γὰρ δὴ οὕτως εἴη 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.189, +others; εἰ γὰρ γενοίμην ἀντὶ σοῦ νεκρός 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 1410; εἰ γὰρ γένοιτο 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 6.1.38; εἰ γὰρ ἐν τούτῳ εἴη 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 310d}}; of unattained wishes, in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus only with _optative_, εἰ γὰρ ἐγὼν.. Διὸς πάϊς αἰγιόχοιο εἴην 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.825; Ζεῦ πάτερ, αἰ γὰρ ἐμὸς πόσις εἴη 7th c.BC: Alcman Lyricus 29}}; later with past tenses of _indicative_, εἰ γάρ μ᾽ ὑπὸ γῆν.. ἧκεν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 152 (_anapaest meter_)}}; εἰ γὰρ τοσαύτην δύναμιν εἶχον ὥστε.. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 1072: twice in 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” with}}_infinitive_ (compare the use of _infinitive_ in commands), αἰ γὰρ τοῖος ἐὼν.. ἐμὸς γαμβρὸς καλέεσθαι {{Refs|7.311, compare 24.376.}}
**A.2.b** εἴθε, _Epic dialect_ αἴθε, is frequently used in wishes in the above constructions, εἴθε οἱ αὐτῷ Ζεὺς ἀγαθὸν τελέσειεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|2.33; εἴθ᾽ ὣς ἡβώοιμι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.157; ἰὼ γᾶ, εἴθ᾽ ἔμ᾽ ἐδέξω 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1537 (Lyric poetry); εἴθε σοι, ὦ Περίκλεις, τότε συνεγενόμην 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.2.46}}: later with _infinitive_, γαίης χθαμαλωτέρη εἴθε.. κεῖσθαι {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“Anthologia Graeca” 9.284 (1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Crinagoras Epigrammaticus).}}
**A.2.c** εἰ γάρ, εἴθε are also used with ὤφελον (_Epic dialect_ ὤφελλον), of past unattained wishes, αἴθ᾽ ὤφελλες στρατοῦ ἄλλου σημαίνειν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.84}}; εἰ γὰρ ὤφελον [κατιδεῖν] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 432c.}}
**A.2.d** followed by a clause expressing a consequence of the fulfilment of the wish, αἰ γὰρ τοῦτο.. ἔπος τετελεσμένον εἴη· τῷ κε τάχα γνοίης.. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.536, compare 17.496, +others}}; sometimes hard to distinguish from εἰ in conditions (which may be derived from this use), εἴ μοί τι πίθοιο, τό κεν πολὺ κέρδιον εἴη {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.28.}}
**B** IN CONDITIONS, **if**:
**B.I** with INDIC.,
**B.I.1** with all tenses (for _future_, see below {{Refs|2), to state a condition, with nothing implied as to its fulfilment, εἰ δ᾽ οὕτω τοῦτ᾽ ἐστίν, ἐμοὶ μέλλει φίλον εἶναι but}}**if** this is so, it will be.., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.564}}: any form of the Verb may stand in apodosi, εἰ θεοί τι δρῶσιν αἰσχρόν, οὐκ εἰσὶν θεοί {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 292.7; εἰ δοκεῖ, πλέωμεν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 526; εἰ Φαῖδρον ἀγνοῶ, καὶ ἐμαυτοῦ ἐπιλέλησμαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 228a; κάκιστ᾽ ἀπολοίμην, Ξανθίαν εἰ μὴ φιλῶ 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 579, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.475; εἰ θεοῦ ἦν, οὐκ ἦν αἰσχροκερδής· εἰ δ᾽ αἰσχροκερδής, οὐκ ἦν θεοῦ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 408c; εἰ ταῦτα λέγων διαφθείρω τοὺς νέους, ταῦτ᾽ ἂν εἴη βλαβερά [prev. author] “Ap.” 30b, compare 25b}}; εἰ οὗτοι ὀρθῶς ἀπέστησαν, ὑμεῖς ἂν οὐ χρεὼν ἄρχοιτε **if** these were right in their revolt, (it would follow that) you rule when you have no right, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.40.}}
**B.I.1.b** to express a general condition, **if ever, whenever**, sometimes with _present_, εἴ τις δύο ἢ καὶ πλείους τις ἡμέρας λογίζεται, μάταιός ἐστιν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 943}}: with _imperfect_, εἴ τίς τι ἠρώτα ἀπεκρίνοντο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.10}}: rarely with _aorist_, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 31.26.1, 2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “Πυρρώνειοι ὑποτυπώσεις” 1.84; compare 111.2.}}
**B.I.2** with _future_ (much less frequently than ἐάν with _subjunctive_), either to express a future supposition emphatically, εἰ φθάσομεν τοὺς πολεμίους κατακαίνοντες οὐδεὶς ἡμῶν ἀποθανεῖται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.1.19; εἰ μὴ βοηθήσετε οὐ περιέσται τἀκεῖ 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.91; εἰ αὕτη ἡ πόλις ληφθήσεται, ἔχεται ἡ πᾶσα Σικελία [prev. passage]}} in threats or warnings, εἰ μὴ καθέξεις γλῶσσαν ἔσται σοι κακά {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 5; εἰ τιμωρήσεις Πατρόκλῳ, αὐτὸς ἀποθανῇ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 28c, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 28.21: or}}
**B.I.2.b** to express a present intention or expectation, αἶρε πλῆκτρον εἰ μαχεῖ **if** you mean to fight, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 759; ἐγὼ μὲν οὐκ ἀνήρ.. εἰ ταῦτ᾽ ἀνατεὶ τῇδε κείσεται κράτη 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 485, compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.61, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 863.}}
**B.I.3** with historical tenses, implying that the condition is or was unfulfilled.
**B.I.3.a** with _imperfect_, referring to present time or to continued or repeated action in past time (in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus always the latter, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.715, +others): ταῦτα οὐκ ἂν ἐδύναντο ποιεῖν, εἰ μὴ διαίτῃ μετρίᾳ ἐχρῶντο they would not be able to do this (as they do)}}, **if** they did not live an abstemious life, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.2.16, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 489b; οὐκ ἂν νήσων ἐκράτει, εἰ μή τι καὶ ναυτικὸν εἶχεν he (from Agamemnon)}} would not have been master of islands, **if** he had not had also some naval force, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.9; αἰ δ᾽ ἦχες ἔσλων ἴμερον ἢ κάλων.. αἴδως κεν.. ἦχεν 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 28}}; εἰ ἦσαν ἄνδρες ἀγαθοὶ.. οὐκ ἄν ποτε ταῦτα ἔπασχον **if** they had been good men, they would never have suffered as they did, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 516e, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.1.5}}; εἰ γὰρ ἐγὼ τάδε ᾔδἐ.. οὐκ ἂν ὑπεξέφυγε **if** I had known this.., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.366.}}
**B.I.3.b** with _aorist_ referring to past time, εἰ μὴ ἔφυσε θεὸς μέλι.. ἔφασκον γλύσσονα σῦκα πέλεσθαι {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Xenophanes Poeta Philosophus 38}}; εἰ μὴ ὑμεῖς ἤλθετε, ἐπορευόμεθα ἂν ἐπὶ βασιλέα had you not come, we should be on our way.., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.1.4; καὶ ἴσως ἂν ἀπέθανον, εἰ μὴ ἡ ἀρχὴ διὰ ταχέων κατελύθη 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 32d, compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.680, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.364, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.5, 27.63}}: with _pluperfect_ in apodosi, εἰ τριάκοντα μόναι μετέπεσον τῶν ψήφων, ἀπεπεφεύγη ἄν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 36a.}}
**B.I.3.c** rarely with _pluperfect_ referring to action finished in past or present time, λοιπὸν δ᾽ ἂν ἦν ἡμῖν ἔτι περὶ τῆς πόλεως διαλεχθῆναι, εἰ μὴ προτέρα τῶν ἄλλων τὴν εἰρήνην ἐπεποίητο **if** she had not (as she has done) made peace before the rest, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 5.56, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 21c.}}
**B.II** with SUB{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus, εἰ is regularly joined with ἄν}} (_Epic dialect_ κε, κεν), compare ἐάν: [{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.AD(?): Arcadius Grammaticus εἰκαν in Tegean Inscrr. of 4th c.BC (“IG” 5(2).3.16, 31, 6.2, “SIG” 306.34) should be understood as εἰκ ἄν (εἰ: εἰκ ={οὐ}: οὐκ), since εἰ δ᾽ ἄν is also found in “IG” 5(2).3.2, 6.45, and εἰκ alone, [prev. work]3.21}}]; but ἄν (κε, κεν) are frequently absent in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus as 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.221, 14.373 (and compare below 2), and Lyric poetry, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus (who never uses εἰ with ἄν or κε (ν)) “P.” 4.266, +others; in dialects, αἰ δείλητ᾽ ἀγχωρεῖν “IG” 9(1).334.6 (_Locrian dialect_, 5th c.BC)}}, cf. Foed._Doric dialect_ cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.79; rarely in 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus, εἰ μὴ ἀναβῇ 2.13}}; occasionally in Trag., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 234, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 198 (Lyric poetry), etc.}}; very rarely in _Attic dialect_ Prose, εἰ ξυστῶσιν αἱ πόλεις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.21; εἴ τι που ἄλσος ἢ τέμενος ἀφειμένον ᾖ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 761c}}: in later Prose, εἴ τις θελήσῃ {{NT+3rd c.AD+|NT.Rev.11.5; εἰ φονεύῃ 3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 2.9.9, compare 5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus “Inst.” 26.}}
**B.II.1** when the apodosis is _future_, to express a future condition more distinctly and vividly than εἰ with _optative_, but less so than εἰ with _future_ _indicative_ (above {{Refs|1.2a)}}; εἰ δέ κεν ὣς ἕρξῃς καί τοι πείθωνται Ἀχαιοί, γνώσῃ ἔπειθ᾽.. **if** thou do thus.., thou shalt know, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.364, compare 1.128, 3.281, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.549; ἂν δέ τις ἀνθιστῆται, σὺν ὑμῖν πειρασόμεθα χειροῦσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.3.11}}; ἂν μὴ νῦν ἐθέλωμεν ἐκεῖ πολεμεῖν αὐτῷ, ἐνθάδ᾽ ἴσως ἀναγκασθησόμεθα τοῦτο ποιεῖν **if** we be not now willing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.50, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.3.27: followed by _imperative_, ἢν εἰρήνης δοκῆτε δεῖσθαι, ἄνευ ὅπλων ἥκετε [prev. work] 3.2.13, compare 5.4.30.}}
**B.II.2** when the apodosis is present, denoting customary or repeated action, to express a general condition, **if ever**, ἤν ποτε δασμὸς ἵκηται, σοὶ τὸ γέρας πολὺ μεῖζον (i.e. ἐστί) **whenever** a division comes, your prize is (always) greater, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.166}}; ἢν ἐγγὺς ἔλθῃ θάνατος, οὐδεὶς βούλεται θνῄσκειν **if** death come near, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 671}}; with ἄν omitted, εἴ περ γάρ τε χόλον.. καταπέψῃ ἀλλά.. ἔχει κότον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.81.}}
**B.II.2.b** with Rhet. present in apodosis, ἐὰν μὴ οἱ φιλόσοφοι βασιλεύσωσιν, οὐκ ἔστι κακῶν παῦλα there is (i.e. can be, will be) no rest.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 473d.}}
**B.III** with OPTATIVE (never with ἄν in early Gr., later ἐάν with _optative_, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Damascius Philosophus “de Principiis” 114, +others)}}
**B.III.1** to express a future condition less definitely than ἐάν with _subjunctive_, usually with _optative_ with ἄν in _apodosis_, ἦ κεν γηθήσαι Πρίαμος Πριάμοιό τε παῖδες.. εἰ σφῶιν τάδε πάντα πυθοίατο μαρναμένοιιν surely they would exult, **if** they should hear.., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.255, compare 7.28, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.223; εἴης φορητὸς οὐκ ἄν, εἰ πράσσοις καλῶς 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 979; οὐδὲ γὰρ ἄν με ἐπαινοίη, εἰ ἐξελαύνοιμι τοὺς εὐεργέτας 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.7.11; οἶκος δ᾽ αὐτός, εἰ φθογγὴν λάβοι, σαφέστατ᾽ ἂν λέξειεν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 37, etc.}}: _future_ _optative_ is _falsa lectio_ in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 164a}}: with _present_ _indicative_ in _apodosis_, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Xenophanes Poeta Philosophus 34.3, 3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 253}}: with _future_ _indicative_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Melissus Philosophus 5.}}
**B.III.1.b** in Hom.sometimes with _present_ _optative_, to express an unfulfilled present condition, εἰ μὲν νῦν ἐπὶ ἄλλῳ ἀεθλεύοιμεν, ἦ τ᾽ ἂν ἐγὼ τὰ πρῶτα φεροίμην **if** we were now contending, etc., [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.274: rarely in Trag., εἰ μὴ κνίζοι (={εἰ μὴ ἔκνιζε}) 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 568; also εἰ ἀναγκαῖον εἴη ἀδικεῖν ἢ ἀδικεῖσθαι, ἑλοίμην ἂν μᾶλλον ἀδικεῖσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 469c.}}]
**B.III.2** when the apodosis is past, denoting customary or repeated action, to express a general condition in past time (corresponding to use of _subjunctive_ in present time, above {{Refs|11.2)}}; once in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, εἴ τίς με.. ἐνίπτοι, ἀλλὰ σὺ τόν γ᾽.. κατέρυκες 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.768}}; εἰ δέ τινας θορυβουμένους αἴσθοιτο.., κατασβεννύναι τὴν ταραχὴν ἐπειρᾶτο **if** he should see (**whenever** he saw) any troops in confusion, he (always) tried, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.3.55, compare “An.” 4.5.13, “Mem.” 4.2.40}}; εἴ τις ἀντείποι, εὐθὺς ἐτεθνήκει **if** any one made objection, he was a dead man at once, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.66; ἀλλ᾽ εἴ τι μὴ φέροιμεν, ὤτρυνεν φέρειν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 755. For εἰ with _indicative_ in this sense see above 1.1}}: _indicative_ and _optative_ are found in same sentence, ἐμίσει, οὐκ εἴ τις κακῶς πάσχων ἠμύνετο, ἀλλ᾽ εἴ τις εὐεργετούμενος ἀχάριστος φαίνοιτο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Agesilaus” 11.3.}}
**B.III.3** in oratio obliqua after past tenses, representing ἐάν with _subjunctive_ or εἰ with a primary (never an historical) tense of the _indicative_ in oratio recta, ἐλογίζοντο ὡς, εἰ μὴ μάχοιντο, ἀποστήσοιντο αἱ πόλεις (representing ἐὰν μὴ μαχώμεθα, ἀποστήσονται) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 6.4.6, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.104, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 5.2.2}}; ἔλεγεν ὅτι, εἰ βλαβερὰ πεπραχὼς εἴη, δίκαιος εἴη ζημιοῦσθαι (representing εἰ βλαβερὰ πέπραχε, δίκαιός ἐστι){{Refs|[prev. work] 32, compare “An.” 6.6.25}}; εἰ δέ τινα φεύγοντα λήψοιτο, προηγόρευεν ὅτι ὡς πολεμίῳ χρήσοιτο (representing εἴ τινα λήψομαι, χρήσομαι) {{Refs|[prev. author] “Cyr.” 3.1.3}}; also, where oratio obliqua is implied in the leading clause, οὐκ ἦν τοῦ πολέμου πέρας Φιλίππῳ, εἰ μὴ Θηβαίους.. ἐχθροὺς ποιήσειε τῇ πόλει, i.e. Philip thought there would be no end to the war, **un**less he should make.. (his thought having been ἐὰν μὴ ποιήσω), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.145; ἐβούλοντο γὰρ σφίσιν, εἴ τινα λάβοιεν, ὑπάρχειν ἀντὶ τῶν ἔνδον, ἢν ἄρα τύχωσί τινες ἐζωγρημένοι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.5.}}
**B.III.4** with _optative_ with ἄν, only when the clause serves as apodosis as well as protasis,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 329b, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.18, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.5.3 (see. ἄν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus 111. d).}}
**B.IV** with _infinitive_, in oratio obliqua, only in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus, εἰ γὰρ δὴ δεῖν πάντως περιθεῖναι ἄλλῳ τέῳ τὴν βασιληΐην, [ἔφη] δικαιότερον εἶναι κτλ. 1.129; εἰ εἶναι τοῦτο μὴ φίλον 2.64, compare 172, 3.105, 108.}}
**B.V** after Verbs denoting **wonder, delight, indignation, disappointment, contentment**, and similar emotions, εἰ with _indicative_ is used instead of ὅτι, to express the object of the feeling in a hypothetical form, θαυμάζω εἰ μηδεὶς ὑμῶν μήτ᾽ ἐνθυμεῖται μήτ᾽ ὀργίζεται, ὁρῶν.. I wonder **that** no one of you is either concerned or angry when he sees.., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.43; οὐκ ἀγαπᾷ εἰ μὴ δίκην δέδωκεν, ἀλλ᾽ εἰ μὴ καὶ χρυσῷ στεφάνῳ στεφανωθήσεται ἀγανακτεῖ 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.147}}: after past tenses, ἐθαύμασε δ᾽ εἰ μὴ φανερόν ἐστιν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.1.13; δεινὸν εἰσῄει, εἰ μὴ.. δόξει 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.33; ἐθαύμαζον εἴ τι ἕξει τις χρήσασθαι τῷ λόγῳ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 95a; οὐδὲ ᾐσχύνθη εἰ.. ἐπάγει 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.105: in oratio obliqua (expressed or implied) with _optative_, ἐπεῖπεν ὡς δεινὸν (i.e. εἴη) εἰ.. μεγαλόψυχος γένοιτο 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.157; ᾤκτιρον εἰ ἁλώσοιντο 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.4.7}}; ἐθαύμαζε δ᾽ εἴ τις ἀρετὴν ἐπαγγελλόμενος ἀργύριον πράττοιτο he wondered **that** any one should demand money, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Mem.” 1.2.7}}; ἔχαιρον ἀγαπῶν εἴ τις ἐάσοι I rejoiced, being content **if** any one should let it pass, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 450a:}} —in this use the _negative_ οὐ is also found, ἀγανακτῶ εἰ ὁ Φίλιππος ἁρπάζων οὐ λυπεῖ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 8.55; δεινὸν ἂν εἴη εἰ οἱ ἐκείνων ξύμμαχοι οὐκ ἀπεροῦσιν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.121; τέρας λέγεις, εἰ οὐκ ἂν δύναιντο λαθεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 91d, etc.}}
**B.VI** in citing a fact as a ground of argument or appeal, **as surely as, since**, εἴ ποτ᾽ ἔην γε **if** there was [as there was], i.e. **as sure as** there was such an one, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.180, +others; εἰ τότε κοῦρος ἔα, νῦν αὖτέ με γῆρας ὀπάζει 4.321}}; πολλοὺς γὰρ οἶκε εἶναι εὐπετέστερον διαβάλλειν ἢ ἕνα, εἰ Κλεομένεα μὲν μοῦνον οὐκ οἷός τε ἐγένετο διαβαλεῖν, τρεῖς δὲ μυριάδας Ἀθηναίων ἐποίησε τοῦτο it seems easier to deceive many than one, **if** (as was the fact, i.e. **since**) he was not able.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.97, compare 1.60, +others}}
**B.VII** ELLIPTICAL CONSTRUCTIONS:
**B.VII.1** with apodosis implied in the context, εἰ having the force of **in case, supposing that**, πρὸς τὴν πόλιν, εἰ ἐπιβοηθοῖεν, ἐχώρουν they marched towards the city [so as to meet the citizens], **in case** they should rush out, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.100}}; ἱκέται πρὸς σὲ δεῦρ᾽ ἀφίγμεθα, εἴ τινα πόλιν φράσειας ἡμῖν εὔερον we have come hither to you, **in case** you should tell us of some fleecy city (i.e. that we might hear of it), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 120}}; παρέζεο καὶ λαβὲ γούνων, αἴ κέν πως ἐθέλῃσιν ἐπὶ Τρώεσσιν ἀρῆξαι sit by him and grasp his knees [so as to persuade him], **in case** he be willing to help the Trojans, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.408, compare 66, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.94, 3.92}}; ἄκουσον καὶ ἐμοῦ, ἐάν σοι ἔτι ταὐτὰ δοκῇ hear me also [that you may assent], **in case** the same opinion please you, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 358b}}; ἰδὲ δή, ἐάν σοι ὅπερ ἐμοὶ συνδοκῇ look now, **in case** you approve what I do, {{Refs|[prev. work] 434a.}}
**B.VII.2** with apodosis suppressed for rhetorical reasons, εἴ περ γάρ κ᾽ ἐθέλῃσιν Ὀλύμπιος.. στυφελίξαι **if** he wish to thrust him away, [he will do so], {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.580}}; εἰ μὲν δώσουσι γέρας—· εἰ δέ κε μὴ δώωσιν, ἐγὼ δέ κεν αὐτὸς ἕλωμαι **if** they shall give me a prize, [well and good]; but **if** they give not, then I will take one for myself, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1.135, compare 6.150, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 468}}; καὶ ἢν μὲν ξυμβῇ ἡ πεῖρα—· εἰ δὲ μή.. and **if** the attempt succeed, [well]; otherwise.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.3, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 325d.}}
**B.VII.3** with the Verb of the protasis omitted, chiefly in the following expressions:
**B.VII.3.a** εἰ μή **except**, οὐδὲν ἄλλο σιτέονται, εἰ μὴ ἰχθῦς μοῦνον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.200}}; μὰ τὼ θεώ, εἰ μὴ Κρίτυλλά γ᾽ [εἰμί]—nay, **if** I'm not Critylla! i.e. I am, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 898}}; εἰ μὴ ὅσον **except** only, ἐγὼ μέν μιν οὐκ εἶδον, εἰ μὴ ὅσον γραφῇ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.73, compare 1.45, 2.20; εἰ μὴ εἰ 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.17, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 480b, etc.}}; εἰ μή τι οὖν, ἀλλὰ σμικρόν γέ μοι τῆς ἀρχῆς χάλασον **if** nothing else, yet.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Men.” 86e; ironical, εἰ μὴ ἄρα ἡ τῆς ἀρετῆς ἐπιμέλεια διαφθορά ἐστιν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.2.8; εἰ μή πέρ γε τὸν ὑοσκύαμον χρήματα εἶναι φήσομεν [prev. author] “Oec.” 1.13.}}
**B.VII.3.b** εἰ δὲ μή but **if** not, i.e. **otherwise**, προηγόρευε τοῖς Λαμψακηνοῖσι μετιέναι Μιλτιάδεα, εἰ δὲ μή, σφέας πίτυος τρόπον ἀπείλεε ἐκτρίψειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.37, compare 56}}; after μάλιστα μέν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.32, 35, etc.:}} —after a preceding _negative_, μὴ τύπτ᾽· εἰ δὲ μή, σαυτόν ποτ᾽ αἰτιάσει don't beat me; **otherwise**, you will have yourself to blame, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1433; ὦ Κῦρε, μὴ οὕτω λέγε· εἰ δὲ μή, οὐ θαρροῦντά με ἕξεις 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.1.35; οὔτ᾽ ἐν τῷ ὕδατι τὰ ὅπλα ἦν ἔχειν· εἰ δὲ μή [prev. author] “An.” 4.3.6, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.28, 131, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 91c.}}
**B.VII.3.c** εἰ δέ sometimes stands for εἰ δὲ μή, εἰ μὲν βούλεται, ἑψέτω· εἰ δ᾽, ὅτι βούλεται, τοῦτο ποιείτω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 285c, compare “Smp.” 212c; εἰ δ᾽ οὖν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 722; εἰ δ᾽ οὕτως 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1094a24}}; εἰ δὲ τοῦτο and **if** so, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 2.1.29.}}
**B.VII.3.d** εἰ γάρ for **if** so, {{Refs|[prev. author] 7.3.6.}}
**B.VII.3.e** εἴ τις **if** any, i. e. **as much as** or **more than** any, τῶν γε νῦν αἴ τις ἐπιχθονίων, ὀρθῶς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 5.5; ὄτλον ἄλγιστον ἔσχον, εἴ τις Αἰτωλὶς γυνή 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 8, compare “OC” 734}}; εἴ τις ἄλλος, **siquis alius**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 6, etc.; εἴ τινες καὶ ἄλλοι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.2, etc.; εἴπερ τις ἄλλος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 501d}}; also κατ᾽ εἰ δέ τινα τρόπον in **any** way, {{Refs|“IG” 5(2).6.27 (from Tegea).}}
**B.VII.3.f** εἴ ποτε or εἴπερ ποτέ **now if** ever, ἡμῖν δὲ καλῶς, εἴπερ ποτέ, ἔχει.. ἡ ξυναλλαγή {{Refs 7th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.20, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 594; αἴ ποτα κἄλλοτα 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus “Supp.” 7.11, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 6.4.12, etc.}}; but in prayers, εἴ ποτέ τοι ἐπὶ νηὸν ἔρεψα.. τόδε μοι κρήηνον ἐέλδωρ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.39.}}
**B.VII.3.g** εἴ ποθεν (i.e. δυνατόν ἐστι) **if** from any quarter, i.e. from some quarter or other, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1204 (Lyric poetry)}}; so εἴ ποθι somewhere, anywhere, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Aj.” 885 (Lyric poetry); εἴ που 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.193.}}
**B.VII.3.h** εἴ πως{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 388, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.3.11}}: in an elliptical sentence (cf. VII. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1), πρέσβεις πέμψαντες, εἴ πως πείσειαν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.58.}}
**B.VIII** with other PARTICLES:
**B.VIII.1** for the distinction between καὶ εἰ (or καὶ ἐάν, or κἄν) even **if**, and εἰ καί (or ἐὰν καί) even **though**, see at {καί}:—the opposite of καὶ εἰ is οὐδ᾽ εἰ, not even **if**; that of εἰ καί is εἰ μηδέ, **if (although**) not even.
**B.VIII.2** for ὡς εἰ, ὡς εἴ τε, ὥσπερ εἰ, etc., see at {ὡς} and ὥσπερ.
**B.VIII.3** for εἰ ἄρα, see at {ἄρα}; for εἰ δή, εἴπερ, see at {εἰ δή, εἴπερ}; for εἴ γε, see at {γέ}.
**B.IX** in _negative_ oaths, = Hebrew **im**, {{LXX+NT|LXX.Psa.94(95).11, NT.Mark.8.12, +others}}
**C** IN INDIRECT QUESTIONS, **whether**, followed by the _indicative_, _subjunctive_, or _optative_, according to the principles of oratio obliqua:
**C.1** with IN{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator after primary tenses, representing the same tense in the direct question, σάφα δ᾽ οὐκ οἶδ᾽ εἰ θεός ἐστιν}} **whether** he is a god, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.183; εἰ ξυμπονήσεις.. σκόπει 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 41.}}
**C.2** with SUB{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus after primary tenses, representing a dubitative}}_subjunctive_ in the direct question, τὰ ἐκπώματα οὐκ οἶδ᾽ εἰ Χρυσάντᾳ τουτῳῒ δῶ **whether** I should give them, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.4.16: sometimes elliptical, ἐς τὰ χρηστήρια ἔπεμπε, εἰ στρατεύηται ἐπὶ τοὺς Πέρσας 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.75.}}
**C.3** OPT. after past tenses, representing either of the two previous constructions in the direct question, ἤρετο εἴ τις ἐμοῦ εἴη σοφώτερος he asked **whether** any one was wiser than I (direct ἔστι τις σοφώτερο;), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 21a; ἐπεκηρυκεύετο Πεισιστράτῳ, εἰ βούλοιτό οἱ τὴν θυγατέρα ἔχειν γυναῖκα 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.60: rarely}}_aorist_ _optative_ for the _aorist_ _indicative_, ἠρώτων αὐτὸν εἰ ἀναπλεύσειεν I asked him **whether** he had set sail (direct ἀνέπλευσα;), {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 50.55: but}}_aorist_ _optative_ usually represents _aorist_ _subjunctive_, τὸν θεὸν ἐπήροντο εἰ παραδοῖεν Κορινθίοις τὴν πόλιν.. καὶ τιμωρίαν τινὰ πειρῷντ᾽ ἀπ᾽ αὐτῶν ποιεῖσθαι they asked **whether** they should deliver their city to the Corinthians, and should try.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.25:}} —in both constructions the _indicative_ or _subjunctive_ may be retained, ψῆφον ἐβούλοντο ἐπαγαγεῖν εἰ χρὴ πολεμεῖν{{Refs|[prev. work] 119}}; ἐβουλεύοντο εἴτε κατακαύσωσιν.. εἴτε τι ἄλλο χρήσωνται **whether** they should burn them or should dispose of them in some other way, {{Refs|[prev. author] 2.4}}; ἀνακοινοῦσθαι αὐτὸν αὑτῷ εἰ δῷ ἐπιψηφίσαι τοῖς προέδροις [he said that] he consulted him **whether** he should give.., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.68.}}
**C.4** with OPT. and ἄν when this was the form of the direct question, ἠρώτων εἰ δοῖεν ἂν τούτων τὰ πιστά they asked **whether** they would give (direct δοιήτε ἄ;), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.8.7.}}
**C.5** the NEG. used with εἰ in indirect questions is οὐ, when οὐ would be used in the direct question, ἐνετέλλετο.. εἰρωτᾶν εἰ οὔ τι ἐπαισχύνεται **whether** he is not ashamed, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.90, etc.}}; but if μή would be required in the direct form, it is retained in the indirect, οὐ τοῦτο ἐρωτῶ, ἀλλ᾽ εἰ τοῦ μὲν δικαίου μὴ ἀξιοῖ πλέον ἔχειν μηδὲ βούλεται ὁ δίκαιος, τοῦ δὲ ἀδίκου (the direct question would be μὴ ἀξιοῖ μηδὲ βούλετα; he does not see fit nor wish, does he?) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 349b}}:—in double indirect questions, εἴτε.. εἴτε..; εἰ.. εἴτε..; εἴτε.. ἢ.., either οὐ or μή can be used in the second clause, ὅπως ἴδῃς εἴτ᾽ ἔνδον εἴτ᾽ οὐκ ἔνδον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 7; σκοπῶμεν εἰ ἡμῖν πρέπει ἢ οὔ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 451d}}; εἰ ἀληθὲς ἢ μή, πειράσομαι μαθεῖν{{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. work] 339a; πολλὰ ἂν περιεσκέψω, εἴτε ἐπιτρεπτέον εἴτε οὔ·.. οὐδένα λόγον οὐδὲ συμβουλὴν ποιῇ, εἴτε χρὴ ἐπιτρέπειν σαυτὸν αὐτῷ εἴτε μή [prev. author] “Prt.” 313a, 313b; ἀνάγκη τὴν ἐμὴν μητέρα, εἴτε θυγάτηρ ἦν Κίρωνος εἴτε μή, καὶ εἰ παρ᾽ ἐκείνῳ διῃτᾶτο ἢ οὔ, καὶ γάμους εἰ διττοὺς ὑπὲρ ταύτης εἱστίασεν ἢ μὴ.. πάντα ταῦτα εἰδέναι τοὺς οἰκέτας 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 8.9}}; τοὺς νόμους καταμανθάνειν εἰ καλῶς κεῖνται ἢ μή.. τοὺς λόγους εἰ ὀρθῶς ὑμᾶς διδάσκουσιν ἢ οὔ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.14.}}

