ophus “ad Marcellam - Porphyrii Opuscula” 34}}; _3rd.pers._ _singular_ ἔστω (ἤτω {{LXX+5th c.BC+|LXX.Psa.103.31, and late Inscrr., “CIG” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” 2664, +others; but in 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 361c to be read ἴτω), _Doric dialect_ εἴτω, ἤτω, 4th c.AD: Heraclides Historicus cited in 12th c.AD: Eustathius Episcopus Thessalonicensis 1411.21, Elean ἤστω “Schwyzer” 424}}; _3rd.pers._ _plural_ ἔστωσαν, but ἔστων {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 502a, ὄντων [prev. author] “Lg.” 879b}}, and early _Attic dialect_ Inscrr., {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“IG” 12.22, etc. (ἔστωσαν first in 2nd c.BC, [prev. work]22.1328), _Doric dialect_ ἐόντων [prev. work]1126:}}_subjunctive_ ὦ, ᾖς, ᾖ, _Epic dialect_ ἔω {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.18}}; _3rd.pers._ _singular_ ἔῃ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.300, +others (also ἔῃσι 2.366, +others, ᾖσι (ν) 19.202, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 294)}}, also _Boeotian dialect_ ἔνθω {{Refs 8th c.BC+|“IG” 7.3172.165, μετείω 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.47 and perhaps εἴῃ 9.245, etc.}}; _Doric dialect_ _3rd.pers._ _plural_ ὦντι {{Refs|“SIG” 940.3 (from Crete), ἔωντι “GDI” 5040.14 (from Hierapytna)}}, _Boeotian dialect_ ἴωνθι {{Refs 6th c.BC+|“IG” 7.3171.46 (3rd c.BC): _optative_ εἴην, -ης (εἴησθα 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 715)}}, -η, also ἔοις, ἔοι, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.284, 142, +others, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.6}}; _3rd.pers._ _plural_ εἴοισαν Ἀρχ. Ἐφ. {{Refs|1911.133 (from Gonni)}}; _3rd.pers._ _dual_ εἴτην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Parmenides” 149e, “Sph.” 243e}}; _1st pers._ _plural_ εἶμεν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 921 (Lyric poetry), 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus}}; _2nd pers._ _plural_ εἶτε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 21.195}}; _3rd.pers._ _plural_ εἶεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.372, etc., εἴησαν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.2, etc.}}; Elean ἔα, ={εἴη}, [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|“SIG” 9 (6th c.BC), and σύν-εαν, ={συνεῖεν}, “GDI” 1149 (6th c.BC):}}]_infinitive_ εἶναι, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.AD(?): Arcadius Grammaticus ἦναι “SIG” 306.9 (Tegea, 4th c.BC)}}; _Epic dialect_ ἔμμεναι (also _Aeolic dialect_ ἔμμεν᾽ {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 34), ἔμμεν (also 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 6.42, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 623 (Lyric poetry)), ἔμεναι, ἔμεν, also ἔμειν “SIG” 1166 (from Dodona)}}; _Doric dialect_ εἶμεν Foed. cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.77, 79, “IG” 7.1.7 (from Megara), ἦμεν “Test.Epict.” 5.16, “Tab.Heracl.” 1.75, Cretan dialect ἦμεν or ἤμην “Leg.Gort.” 1.15, +others, “GDI” 4998i 2, +others, Megarian dialect εἴμεναι 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 775, εἴμειν “IG” 12(1).155.100 (from Rhodes), 14.952 (from Agrigentum); εἶν [prev. work] 12(9).211.10 (from Eretria), “SIG” 135.4 (from Olynthus), etc.:}}_participle_ ὤν, _Epic dialect_ ἐών, ἐοῦσα, ἐόν, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD: Cypria ἰών “Inscription Cyprian dialect” 135.23 H.}}; _Boeotian dialect_ _feminine_ ἰῶσα {{Refs|“IG” 7.3172.116 (Orchom.)}}, _Aeolic dialect_ and _Doric dialect_ _feminine_ ἔσσα {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 75.4, “IG” 4.952.2 (Epid.), 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 28.16, ἐοῖσα 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.265, ἔασσα “Lyrica Alexandrina Adespota” 9, Diotogenes Philosophus cited in 5th c.AD: Stobaeus, Joannes 4.7.62, εὖσα Erinna Lyrica 5.5}} (also _Ionic dialect_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 5.16, εὔντων 2.85), ἐᾶσα 5th-4th c.BC: Timaeus Locrus Philosophus 96d, “IG” 5(1).1470.8 (from Messene), ἴαττα “Leg.Gort.” 8.47; _accusative_ _sing._ εὖντα 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 2.3}}; _nominative_ _singular_ εἴς in {{Refs 7th c.BC+|4th c.AD: Heraclides Historicus cited in 12th c.AD: Eustathius Episcopus Thessalonicensis 1756.13, _plural_ ἔντες “Tab.Heracl.” 1.117; _dative_ _plural_ ἔντασσι [prev. work]104; _genitive_ _plural_ παρ-έντων 7th c.BC: Alcman Lyricus 64}}: _imperfect_ ἦν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.77, etc., _Epic dialect_ ἔον}} (also _Aeolic dialect_, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 127, 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica “Papyrus fragments” in 1787 “fragment” 3 ii 21), in _Attic dialect_ ἦ}} (uncertain in _Aeolic dialect_, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus “Supp.” 14.9), 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 77, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 61b, etc., but usually altered to ἦν in codices (and ἦν}} is required by metre in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 280), _contraction_ from _Epic dialect_ and _Ionic dialect_ ἦα (8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.808, +others, “IG” 12(8).449.2 (from Thasos)}}, whence Hom.and later _Ionic dialect_ ἔᾱ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.321, +others, ἔας 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.187, ἔατε [prev. author] 4.119)}}; _Epic dialect_ _3rd.pers._ _singular_ ἦεν, always with ν in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}}; ἔην as _1st pers._ _singular_, only {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.762 (assuming variant, +others ἔον), frequently as _3rd.pers._ _sing._ (generally before a consonant, so that ἔεν is possible), sometimes also ἤην}}; _2nd pers._ _singular_ ἦσθα, later ἦς (which is variant in {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 1.26), sometimes in LXX (LXX.Judg.11.35, LXX.Ruth.3.2, +others), compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Axiochus” 365e, Erinna Lyrica 4.4, NT.Matt.25.21, +others, ἦσθας 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Ἐπιτρέποντες” 156, _Epic dialect_ ἔησθα}}; _3rd.pers._ _singular_ ἦν, _Epic dialect_ ἔην, ἤην, ἦεν (see. above), _Doric dialect_ and _Aeolic dialect_ ἦς {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus “Supp.” 30.1, 5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 102, 5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 59, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 2.90, “SIG” 241.145 (Delph.)}}; _3rd.pers._ _dual_ ἤστην {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.10, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 387, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 982, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 272a, +others}}; _Doric dialect_ _1st pers._ _plural_ ἦμες {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Lycurgus” 21}}; _2nd pers._ _plural_ ἦτε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 276c, ἦστε 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 821, “Ec.” 1086}}; _3rd.pers._ _plural_ ἦσαν, _Ionic dialect_ and poetical ἔσαν (in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 321, 825, ἦν is not _plural_ for ἦσαν}}, but is rather a peculiarity of syntax, see below see, but is _3rd.pers._ _plural_ in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 46, +others, “SIG” 560.15 (Epidamnus, 3rd c.BC))}}; _Aeolic dialect_ ἔον {{NT+5th c.BC+|“Schwyzer” 644.12; later ἤμην “PSI” 4.362.21 (3rd c.BC), “SIG” 527.46 (Crete, 3rd c.BC), “IGRom.” 4.1740 (from Cyme), always in LXX as LXX.Bar.1.19, cf. NT.Matt.23.30, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.174a, etc., and sometimes in codices of earlier writers, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 7.34, “Tragica Adespota” 124 (cited from 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 931 by 4th-5th c.AD: Choeroboscus Grammaticus and from [prev. author] “Tr.” 474 by Aps.), 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 6.1.9, 4th c.BC: Hyperides Orator “adv. Athenogenem” 26}}, _2nd pers._ _singular_ ἦσο {{Refs|“epigram Gr.” 379 (from Aezani)}}, _3rd.pers._ _singular_ ἦστο {{Refs|“Supp.epigram” 1.455.7 (from Phrygia)}}, _1st pers._ _plural_ ἤμεθα {{NT+3rd c.BC+|“PPetr.” 2p.11 (3rd c.BC), LXX.Bar.1.19, LXX.1Ki.25.16, NT.Eph.2.3}}; _subjunctive_ ὦμαι {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PBaden” 48.12 (2nd c.BC), ἦται “GDI” , ἦνται probably in “IG” 5(1).1390.83 (from Andania)}}; _Ionic dialect_ and _Epic dialect_ also ἔσκον, used by {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 656 (Lyric poetry):}}_future_ ἔσομαι, ἔσται, _Epic dialect_ and _Aeolic dialect_ also ἔσσομαι, ἔσεται, ἔσσετα; _Aeolic dialect_ _2nd pers._ _singular_ ἔσσῃ probably in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 67, 87; _Doric dialect_ 2 and _3rd.pers._ _sing._ ἐσσῇ, ἐσσεῖται, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.393, 13.317, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 10.5, _3rd.pers._ _plural_ ἐσσοῦνται Foed. cited in 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.77 codices (but ἔσσονται “Tab.Heracl.” 1.113)}}, _infinitive_ ἐσσεῖσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 57.}} —All forms of the _present_ _indicative_ are enclitic (except _2nd pers._ _singular_ εἶ and _3rd.pers._ _plural_ ἔασι); but _3rd.pers._ _singular_ is written ἔστι when it begins a sentence or verse, or when it immediately follows οὐκ, καί, εἰ, ὡς, ἀλλά, or τοῦτ᾽, Hdn.Gr.{{Refs|1.553 (also μή _accusative_ to “Etymologicum Magnum” 301.3)}}; later Grammars wrote ἔστι as _substantive_ Verb, {{Refs 9th c.AD+|9th c.AD: Photius Legal icographus, etc., 12th c.AD: Eustathius Episcopus Thessalonicensis 880.22.}}
**A** as the _substantive_ Verb,
**A.I** of persons, **exist**, οὐκ ἔσθ᾽ οὗτος ἀνήρ, οὐδ᾽ ἔσσεται {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 16.437}}; ἔτ᾽ εἰσί they **are** still **in being**,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|15.433, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 445, etc.; τεθνηῶτος.. μηδ᾽ ἔτ᾽ ἐόντος 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.289}}; οὐκέτ᾽ ἐστί he **is** no more, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 1162}}; οὐδὲ δὴν ἦν he **was** not long-**lived**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.131}}; ὁ οὐκ ὤν, οἱ οὐκ ὄντες, of **those who are** no more, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.45, 44}}; οἱ ὄντες the **living**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 9.29.2}}; ὁ ὤν the {{LXX+8th c.BC+|“Eternal”, LXX.Exo.3.14, +others, 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.289; θεοὶ αἰὲν ἐόντες 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.290}}; ἐσσόμενοι **posterity**,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|2.119; κἀγὼ γὰρ ἦ ποτ᾽, ἀλλὰ νῦν οὐκ εἴμ᾽ ἔτι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 284}}; ὡς ἂν εἶεν ἅνθρωποι **might continue in being**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 190c; ζώντων καὶ ὄντων Ἀθηναίων 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.72, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Generatione et Corruptione” 318b25}}; of things, εἰ ἔστι ἀληθέως [ἡ τράπεζα] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.17, etc.}}; of cities, ὄλωλεν, οὐδ᾽ ἔτ᾽ ἐστὶ Τροία {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 1292, compare “Heracl.” 491}}; δοκεῖ μοι Καρχηδόνα μὴ εἶναι **censeo Carthaginem esse delendam**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Cat.Ma.” 27}}; ἂν ᾖ τὸ στράτευμα **be in existence**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 8.17}}; of money, **to be in hand**, τῶν ὄντων χρημάτων καὶ τῶν προσιόντων {{Refs|“IG” 12.91.25}}; τὰ ὄντα **property**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 511a, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Antonius” 24, etc.}}; τὸ ἐσόμενον ἐκ.. **future revenue** from.., {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“BCH” 46.420 (Olymos, 1st c.BC)}}; of place, τὴν οὖσαν ἐκκλησίαν the **local** church, {{NT|NT.Act.13.1}}; of time, τοῦ ὄντος μηνός in the **current** month, {{Refs|“BGU” 146.4, etc.}}; **in office**, ἱερέων τῶν ὄντων {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PPar.” 5.4 (2nd c.BC)}}; αἱ οὖσαι [ἐξουσίαι] the powers **that be, Ep. Rom.**{{Refs|13.1.}}
**A.II** of the **real** world, **be**, opposed to become, γίγνεται πάντα ἃ δή φαμεν εἶναι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 152d, etc.}}; τὸ ὄν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“Being”, 4th-5th c.BC: Parmenides Poeta Philosophus 8.35, 5th c.BC: Protagoras Philosophus 2, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 27d, etc.}}; opposed to τὸ μὴ ὄν, {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Gorgias Rhetor et Sophista “fragment” 3 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator, etc.; οὐδὲν γίνεται ἐκ τοῦ μὴ ὄντος 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 1p.5U.; ἐξ οὐκ ὄντων ἐποίησεν αὐτὰ ὁ θεός LXX.2Mac.7.28}}; τὰ ὄντα the **world of things**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus 7, 5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 129.5, etc.}}; ὄν_indeclinable_, τῶν ὂν εἰδῶν species **of Being**, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 6.2.10.}}
**A.II.2** of circumstances, events, etc., **to happen**, τά τ᾽ ἐόντα, τά τ᾽ ἐσσόμενα, πρό τ᾽ ἐόντα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.70; ἡ ἐσβολὴ ἔμελλεν ἔσεσθαι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.13, etc.}}; τῆς προδοσίας οὔσης since treachery **was there**, {{Refs|[prev. author] 4.103}}; ἕως ἂν ὁ πόλεμος ᾖ so long as it **last**, {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.58; αἱ σπονδαὶ ἐνιαυτὸν ἔσονται [prev. author] 4.118}}; τί ἐστι; what **is** it? what's the matter? {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 193}}; τί οὖν ἦν τοῦτ; how **came** it **to pass**? {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 58a: repeated with a _relative_ to avoid a positive assertion, ἔστι δ᾽ ὅπῃ νῦν ἔ.}} things **are** as they **are**, i.e. are ill, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 67.}}
**A.III** **be the fact** or **the case**, διπλασίαν ἂν τὴν δύναμιν εἰκάζεσθαι ἤ ἐστιν twice as large as it **really is**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.10}}; αὐτὸ ὅ ἐστι καλόν beauty **in its essence**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 211c, compare “Phd.” 74b}}; frequently in _participle_, τὸν ἐόντα λόγον λέγειν or φαίνειν the **true** story, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.95, 116}}; τῷ ἐόντι χρήσασθαι tell the **truth**,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 30; τὰ ὄντα ἀπαγγέλλειν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.8}}; σκῆψιν οὐκ οὖσαν, λόγον οὐκ ὄντα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 584, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1052}}; τῷ ὄντι **in reality, in fact**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 328d, etc.}}; to apply a quotation to a case in point, τῷ ὄντι κλαυσίγελως **real** 'smiles through tears' (with allusion to {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.484), 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 7.2.9, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Laches” 196d}}; κατὰ τὸ ἐόν according to the **fact, right**ly, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.97}}; πᾶν τὸ ἐόν the whole **truth**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 9.11; τοῦ ἐόντος ἀποτεύξεται 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀρχαίης ἰητρικῇς” 2.}}
**A.IV** followed by the _relative_, οὐκ ἔστιν ὅς or ὅστις **no one**, οὐκ ἔσθ᾽ ὃς.. ἀπαλάλκοι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.348; οὐκ ἔ. οὐδεὶς ὅς 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 903}}; οὐκ ἔ. ὅτῳ, ={οὐδενί}, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 293 (_anapaest meter_), compare 989: frequently in _plural_, εἰσὶν οἵ}}, = Latin _sunt qui_, used exactly like{ἔνιοι}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.88, 7.44, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 77d, “Grg.” 503a, etc. (εἰσί τινες οἵ.. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.24)}}; ἐστὶν ἃ χωρία, πολίσματα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 1.12, 65; ἐστὶν ἃ εἰπεῖν [prev. author] 2.67; ἦσαν οἵ 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.2.14}}; the _singular_ Verb is used even with _masculine_ and _feminine_ _plural_, ἐστὶν οἵ, αἵ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀγμῶν” 1, “VC” 4, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.3.16}}; more frequently in oblique cases, ποταμῶν ἐστὶ ὧν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.187; ἐστὶν ἀφ᾽ ὧν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.65}}; ἐστὶ παρ᾽ οἷς, ἐστὶν ἐν οἷς, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 1.23, 5.25: in questions ὅστις is used, ἔστιν ἥντινα δόξαν.. ἀπεκρίνατ; 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 85b: with _relative_ Particles, ἐστὶν ἔνθα}}, = Latin _est ubi_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.4.15, etc.}}; ἐ. ὅπῃ, ἔσθ᾽ ὅπου, **some**how, **some**where, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 331d, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 517, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 448, etc.}}; in questions expecting a _negative_ answer, ἐ. ὁπόθεν, ὅπω; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 35a, “R.” 493e, etc.; οὐ γάρ ἐσθ᾽ ὅπως 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 61, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.102, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 620}}; οὐκ ἔ. ὅπως οὐ **in any case, necessarily**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 188; οὐκ ἔ. ὡς 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 76e, etc.}}; ἐ. ὅτε, ἔσθ᾽ ὅτε, **some**times, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 180.2, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 56, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.21, etc.}}
**A.V** ἦν is sometimes used with _plural_ _masculine_ and _feminine_, usually at the beginning of a sentence, **there was**, τῆς δ᾽ ἦν τρεῖς κεφαλαί {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 321; (but in ἦν δ᾽ ἐρῳδιοί τε πολλοί 5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 46, compare 59, +others, it may be taken as _Doric dialect_ _3rd.pers._ _plural_)}}; ἦν δ᾽ ἀμφίπλεκτοι κλίμακες {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 520 (Lyric poetry)}}; ἦν ἄρα κἀκεῖνοι ταλακάρδιοι epigram cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.184; less frequently ἔστι, ἔστι δὲ μεταξὺ.. ἑπτὰ στάδιοι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.26, compare 7.34; ἔστι.. ἄρχοντές τε καὶ δῆμος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 463a}}; before dual Nouns, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 58, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 500d.}}
**A.VI** ἔστι _impersonal_, with _infinitive_, **it is possible**, ἔστι γὰρ ἀμφοτέροισιν ὀνείδεα μυθήσασθαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 20.246; ἔστι μὲν εὕδειν, ἔστι δὲ τερπομένοισιν ἀκούειν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.392}}; εἴ τί πού ἐστι (i.e. πιθέσθαι){{Refs 5th c.BC+|4.193; τοιάδε.. ἐστὶν ἀκοῦσαι 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 1055 (_anapaest meter_); ἔστι τεκμήρια ὁρᾶν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.2.13, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1163, 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.105, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.272, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 53.6, etc.}}; so in _imperative_, _optative_, and _subjunctive_, ἔστω ἀποφέρεσθαι τῷ βουλομένῳ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|“IG” 12.10.7; μυρία ἂν εἴη λέγειν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 271e; ὅπως ἂν ᾖ δρᾶν “IG” 2.1054.91: more frequently in _negative_ clauses, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.267, etc.}}; followed by ὥστε with _infinitive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 656}}: with _accusative_ et _infinitive_, ἁδόντα δ᾽ εἴη με τοῖς ἀγαθοῖς ὁμιλεῖν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 2.96; ἔστιν ἐκπεσεῖν ἀρχῆς Δία 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 757}}: sometimes not _impersonal_ in this sense, θάλασσα δ᾽ οὐκέτ᾽ ἦν ἰδεῖν {{Refs|[prev. author] “Pers.” 419.}}
**A.VI.b** ἔστω in argument, **let it be granted**, ἔστω τοῦτο ἀληθὲς εἶναι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Compositione Verborum” 25; ἔστω σοι τοῦθ᾽ οὕτως 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.987b; ἔστω εἶναί τινα τοιοῦτον 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator Chr.74.24.}}
**B** most frequently, **to be**, the Copula connecting the predicate with the Subject, both being in the same case: hence, **signify, import**, τὸ γὰρ εἴρειν λέγειν ἐστίν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 398d}}; especially in the phrase τοῦτ᾽ ἔστι, **hoc est**; Σκαιόλαν, ὅπερ ἐστὶ Λαϊόν {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Publicola” 17: with numerals, τὰ δὶς πέντε δέκα ἐστίν}} twice five **are** ten, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.4.7}}; εἶναί τις or τι, **to be** somebody, something, **be** of some consequence, see at {τι; οὐδὲν εἶναι} {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 562d, etc.}}
**B.2** _periphrastic_ with the Participle to represent the finite Verb: with _perfect_ _participle_ once in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, τετληότες εἰμέν, for τετλήκαμεν, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.873}}; so in Trag. and _Attic dialect_, ἦν τεθνηκώς, for ἐτεθνήκει, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 869}}; ἔσται δεδορκώς{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 1179; εἰμὶ γεγώς 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1299; πεφυκός ἐστι 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1473; δεδρακότες εἰσίν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.68; κατακεκονότες ἔσεσθε 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.6.36}}: with _aorist_ _participle_, once in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, βλήμενος ἦν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.211}}; so προδείσας εἰμί, οὐ σιωπήσας ἔσε; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 90, 1146, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 460}}: with _present_ _participle_, ἦν προκείμενον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Pers.” 371; φεύγων Ὀρέστης ἐστίν [prev. author] “Ch.” 136; εἴην οὐκ ἂν εὖ φρονῶν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1330}}; τί δ᾽ ἐστί.. φέρο; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “OT” 991, compare 274, 708; λέγων ἐστίν τις 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 1179; ἦν τίς σ᾽ ὑβρίζων [prev. author] “HF” 313; πόρρω ἤδη εἶ πορευόμενος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Lysis” 204b; βαδίζων εἰμί 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 36; frequently in 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus, ἦσαν ἱέντες 1.57, +others; even εἰσὶ διάφοροι ἐόντες 3.49 (assuming variant):}} — if the _Article_ is joined with the Part., the noun is made emphatic, Κᾶρές εἰσι οἱ καταδέξαντες the persons who showed her **were** Carians, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 1.171; αὐτὸς ἦν ὁ μαρτυρῶν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 798; δόλος ἦν ὁ φράσας 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 197 (_anapaest meter_).}}
**C** εἶναι is frequently modified in sense by the addition of _adverbs_, or the cases of Nouns without or with Preps.:
**C.I** εἶναι with _adverbs_, where the _adverb_ often merely represents a Noun and stands as the predicate, ἅλις δέ οἱ ἦσαν ἄρουραι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.122, etc.}}; ἀκέων, ἀκήν εἶναι, **to be** silent,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|4.22, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.82; σῖγα πᾶς ἔστω λεώς 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 532; διαγνῶναι χαλεπῶς ἦν ἄνδρα ἕκαστον 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.424}}; ἀσφαλέως ἡ κομιδὴ ἔσται **will go on** safely, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.134}}; ἐγγύς, πόρρω εἶναι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.88, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 356e: frequently _impersonal_ with words implying good or ill fortune, Κουρήτεσσι κακῶς ἦν}} **it fared** ill with them, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.551; εὖ γὰρ ἔσται 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 89, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 1188, etc.; ἡδέως ἂν αὐτοῖς εἴη 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 59.30.}}
**C.II** with _genitive_, to express descent or extraction, πατρὸς δ᾽ εἴμ᾽ ἀγαθοῖο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.109; αἵματός εἰς ἀγαθοῖο 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.611, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.71, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.71, etc.; πόλεως μεγίστης εἶ 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.3.19.}}
**C.II.b** to express the material of which a thing is made, ἡ κρηπίς ἐστι λίθων μεγάλων **consists of**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.93; τῆς πόλιος ἐούσης δύο φαρσέων [prev. work] 186}}; τοιούτων ἔργων ἐστὶ ἡ τυραννίς **is made up of**.., {{Refs|[prev. author] 5.92}}.ή, etc.
**C.II.c** to express the class to which a person or thing belongs, εἶ γὰρ τῶν φίλων **you are one of** them, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 345; ἐτύγχανε βουλῆς ὤν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.70; ὅσοι ἦσαν τῶν προτέρων στρατιωτῶν [prev. author] 7.44; Κριτίας τῶν τριάκοντα ὤν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.2.31}}; ἔστι τῶν αἰσχρῶν **it is in the class of** disgraceful things, i. e. **it is** disgraceful, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 2.2.}}
**C.II.d** to express that a thing belongs to another, Τροίαν Ἀχαιῶν οὖσαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 269; τὸ πεδίον ἦν μέν κοτε Χορασμίων 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.117, etc.}}: hence, **to be of the party of**, ἦσαν.. τινὲς μὲν φιλίππου, τινὲς δὲ τοῦ βελτίστου {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 9.56, compare 37.53}}; **to be de pendent upon**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 737, etc.}}; **to be at the mercy of**, ἔστι τοῦ λέγοντος, ἢν φόβους λέγῃ {{Refs|[prev. author] “OT” 917.}}
**C.II.e** to express one's duty, business, custom, nature, and the like, οὔτοι γυναικός ἐστι **'tis** not a woman's **part**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 940; τὸ ἐπιτιμᾶν παντὸς εἶναι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 1.16}}; τὸ δὲ ναυτικὸν τέχνης ἐστίν **is matter of** art, **requires** art, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.142, compare 83.}}
**C.II.f** in LXX, **to be occupied about**, ἦσαν τοῦ θύειν {{LXX|LXX.2Ch.30.17}}; ἔσεσθαι, with _genitive_, **to be about to**, ἐσόμεθα τοῦ σῶσαί σε {{LXX|LXX.2Ki.10.11.}}
**C.III** with the _dative_, ἐστί μοι I **have**, frequently in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, etc.}}
**C.III.2** with two datives., σφίσι τε καὶ Ἀθηναίοισι εἶναι οὐδὲν πρῆγμα that they and the Athenians **have** nothing **to do** one with another, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.84; μηδὲν εἶναι σοὶ καὶ φιλίππῳ πρᾶγμα 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.283}}; more shortly, σοί τε καὶ τούτοισι πρήγμασι τί ἐστ; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.33}}; τί τῷ νόμῳ καὶ τῇ βασάν; {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 29.36}}; τί ἐμοὶ καὶ σο; Latin _quid tecum est mihi?_ {{NT|NT.Mark.5.7, etc.}}; also ἐμοὶ οὐδὲν πρὸς τοὺς τοιούτους (i.e. ἐστίν) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.12; ἐν οἷς πρὸς τοὺς ἐναντίους ἐστὶ τῷ δήμῳ 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.278}}; ἔσται αὐτῳ πρὸς τὸν θεόν, in tomb inscriptions, {{Refs|“JHS” 18.113, etc.}}
**C.III.3** with ἄσμενος, βουλόμενος, etc., added, ἐμοὶ δέ κεν ἀσμένῳ εἴη **'twould be** to my delight, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.108; οὐκ ἂν σφίσι βουλομένοις εἶναι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.35; προσδεχομένῳ [prev. author] 6.46; θέλοντι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1356 (Lyric poetry); ἡδομένοις 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Laches” 187c.}}
**C.IV** with Preps., εἶναι ἀπό τινος, ={εἶναί τινος}{{Refs 5th c.BC+|(above 11.a), 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.6.9; εἰσὶν ἀπ᾽ ἐναντίων αὗται πραγμάτων 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 12d}}; but εἶναι ἀπ᾽ οἴκου **to be** away from.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.99.}}
**C.IV.2** εἶναι ἔκ τινος **to be sprung** from, εἴμ᾽ ἐκ Παιονίης, Μυρμιδόνων ἔξ εἰμι, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.154, 24.397, etc.}}; ἔστιν ἐξ ἀνάγκης **it is** of necessity, i. e. necessary, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 256d.}}
**C.IV.3** εἶναι ἐν.. **to be** in a certain state, ἐν εὐπαθείῃσι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.22}}; ἐν ἀθυμία, etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.46, etc.; ἐν ταραχαῖς 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.218}}; εἶναι ἐν ἀξιώματι **to be** in esteem, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.130}}; οἱ ἐν τέλεϊ ἐόντες those in office, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.18, etc.}}; but εἶναι ἐν τέχνῃ, ἐν φιλοσοφία **to be engaged** in.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 562, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 59a.}}
**C.IV.3.b** ἐν σοί ἐστι **it depends** on thee, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.109, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 963; ἐν σοὶ γάρ ἐσμεν [prev. author] “OT” 314; so also ἐπί τινι [prev. author] “Ph.” 1003, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.6.2, etc.}}
**C.IV.4** εἶναι διά.., much like{εἶναι ἐν}.., εἶναι διὰ φόβου, ={φοβεῖσθαι}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.34}}; εἶναι δι᾽ ὄχλου, ={ὀχληρὸν εἶναι}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 1.73; εἶναι διὰ μόχθων 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.6.25}}; εἶναι δι᾽ αἰτίας, ={αἰτιᾶσθαι}, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 1.70}}; Geometry texts, **pass** through, διὰ τᾶς ἑτέρας διαμέτρου ἐόντος τοῦ ἐπιπέδου {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Archimedes Geometra “περὶ κωνοειδέων καὶ σφαιροειδέων” 20.}}
**C.IV.5** εἶναι ἐφ᾽ ἑαυτῆς **to be** by oneself, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 25.23}}; εἶναι ἐπὶ ὀνόματος **to bear** a name, {{Refs|[prev. author] 39.21}}; εἶναι ἐπὶ τοῖς πράγμασιν **to be engaged** in.., {{Refs|[prev. author] 2.12}}; εἶναι ἐπί τινα **to be** against him, {{Refs|[prev. author] 6.33}}; εἶναι ἐφ᾽ ἑξήκοντα στάδια **to reach** sixty stadia, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.6.11}}; εἶναι ἐπὶ τὰς ἁφάς **pass** through the points of contact, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Biographus Perg. “Con.” 4.1}}; εἶναι ἐπί τινι, see above 3 b.
**C.IV.6** εἶναι πρός τινος **to be** in one's favour, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.10, 29, etc.}}; **to suit**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.2.11, etc.}}; εἶναι πρός τινι **engaged** in, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 84c, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vita Apollonii” 5.31}}; πρὸς τοῖς ἰδίοις **mind** one's own affairs, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1309a6, “Ath.” 16.3; εἶναι πρὸς τὸ κωλύειν 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.26.3}}; πρὸς τὸ πονεῖν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|Teles p.46 H.; εἶναι περί τι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.5.7, etc.}}
**C.IV.7** εἶναι παρά τινι or τινα, = παρειναι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Cyr.” 6.2.15, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.140.ά}} (assuming variant).
**C.IV.8** εἶναι ὑπό τινα or τινι **to be** subject to.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 5.2.17 (assuming variant), 6.2.4.}}
**C.IV.9** περὶ τούτων ἐστίν that **is** the question, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Ἐπιτρέποντες” 30.}}
**C.IV.10** εἶναι ἀπό.., in Geometry texts, **to be constructed** upon, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Archimedes Geometra “περὶ σφαίρας καὶ κυλίνδρου” 2.9, “Con.Sph.” 7.}}
**D** ἐστί is very frequently omitted, mostly in the _present_ _indicative_ before certain predicates, as ἀνάγκη, ἄξιον, δυνατόν, εἰκός, ἕτοιμον, οἷόν τε, ῥᾴδιον, χρεών, etc., and after the _neuter_ of Verbals in -τέος, and such forms as θαυμαστὸν ὅσον: less frequently with other persons and moods, εἰμί omitted, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 92, “Aj.” 813}}; εἶ, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.206}}; ἐσμέν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 634}}; ἐστέ, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.463}}; εἰσί, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 499 (Lyric poetry), “IG” 2.778 B}}; _subjunctive_ ᾖ, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.376, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 659, 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.32}}; _optative_ εἴη, {{Refs|“IG” 22.1183.12}}; _imperfect_ ἦν, {{Refs|[prev. work]2.778 B}}; _future_ ἔσονται, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.394.}}
**E** the Inf. frequently seems redundant,
**E.1** in phrases implying power or will to do a thing, ἑκὼν εἶναι (see. ἑκών) κατὰ δύναμιν εἶναι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 2.32; εἰς δύναμιν εἶναι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 300c}}; τὸ ἐπ᾽ ἐκείνοις εἶναι, **quantum in illis esset**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.48, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 3.5.9, compare 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 13.58; τὸ ἐπὶ σφᾶς εἶναι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.28; τὸ κατὰ τοῦτον εἶναι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.6.9; κατὰ τοῦτο εἶναι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 317a}}; τὸ τήμερον, τὸ νῦν εἶναι, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Cra.” 396e, “La.” 201c, Theopomp. Comedy texts 98, Decrees cited in 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 31.2, etc.}}
**E.2** after Verbs of naming or choosing, σοφιστὴν ὀνομάζουσι τὸν ἄνδρα εἶναι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 311e; σύμμαχόν μιν εἵλοντο εἶναι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.134}}; of giving, δῶκε ξεινήϊον εἶναι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.20.}}
**F** _imperfect_ ἦνissts. used where other languages take the _present_,
**F.1** after ἄρα, to express a fact which **is and has always been** the same, δέρμα δὲ ἀνθρώπου.. ἦν ἄρα σχεδὸν δερμάτων πάντων λαμπρότατον human skin then **it appears is**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.64; Κύπρις οὐκ ἄρ᾽ ἦν θεός 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 359}}; ὡς ἄρ᾽ ἦσθ᾽ ἐμὸς πατὴρ ὀρθῶς{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|[prev. work] 1169; ἦ πολύμοχθον ἄρ᾽ ἦν γένος.. ἁμερίων [prev. author] “IA” 1330; ἦ στωμύλος ἦσθα 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 5.79}}; so also when there is reference to a past thought, τουτὶ τί ἦ; what **is** this? {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 157, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 387c: so in the Aristotelian formula τὸ τί ἦν εἶναι (“APo.” 82b38, +others)}}, used to express **the essential nature** of a thing, where τί ἦν (for ἐστί) takes the place of the _dative_ in such phrases as τὸ ἀγαθῷ εἶναι, τὸ μεγέθει εἶναι, {{Refs|“APr.” 67b12, “de An.” 429b10.}}
**G** ἐγώ εἰμι, in {{LXX|LXX, pleonastic for ἐγώ, ἐγώ εἰμι οὐχ ἥμαρτον LXX.Judg.11.27, compare 6.18; also ἔσται πᾶς ἀποκτενεῖ με LXX.Gen.4.14.}}

1511:
** εἰμί**
(**sum**), _Aeolic dialect_ **ἔμμι** {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 2.15, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 20.32}}; Cretan dialect **ἠμί** {{Refs|“GDI” 4959a}}; _2nd pers._ _singular_ εἶ, _Epic dialect_ and _Ionic dialect_ εἰς {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.388, +others, _Aeolic dialect_ ἔσσι}}, _Epic dialect_ and _Doric dialect_ ἐσσί {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.176, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 6.90, 5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 134; ἐσί “GDI” 4959a}}; _3rd.pers._ _singular_ ἐστί, _Doric dialect_ ἐντί {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“IG” 12(1).677 (from Rhodes), 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 1.17, etc.}}; _3rd.pers._ _dual_ ἐστόν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.112}}; _1st pers._ _plural_ ἐσμέν, _Epic dialect_ and _Ionic dialect_ εἰμέν (also in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 3.60), ἐμέν 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Fragmenta” 294}}, _Doric dialect_ εἰμές {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 15.73, but ἠμέν “GDI” 5178.34}}; _3rd.pers._ _plural_ εἰσί (-ίν), _Epic dialect_ and _Ionic dialect_ ἔασι (-ιν) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.73, 6th c.BC: Xenophanes Poeta Philosophus 8.1, 5th-6th c.BC: Antimachus Colophonius Elegiacus et Epicus 29, 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 4.84}}, _Doric dialect_ ἐντί {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 1.24, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 11.45, “IG” 9(1).32.22 (from Phocis), etc.: _imperative_ ἴσθι (ἔσθι 4th-5th c.BC: Hecataeus Milesius Historicus 361 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus)}}, _Epic dialect_ and Lyric poetry also in _middle_ form ἔσσο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.200, 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 1.28, Macedonius Lyricus “Paean” 31, late Prose ἔσο 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.241d, 2nd c.AD: Marcus Antoninus Imperator 3.5, 3rd c.AD: Heliodorus Scriptor Eroticus 5.12, 3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “ad Marcellam - Porphyrii Opuscula” 34}}; _3rd.pers._ _singular_ ἔστω (ἤτω {{LXX+5th c.BC+|LXX.Psa.103.31, and late Inscrr., “CIG” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” 2664, +others; but in 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 361c to be read ἴτω), _Doric dialect_ εἴτω, ἤτω, 4th c.AD: Heraclides Historicus cited in 12th c.AD: Eustathius Episcopus Thessalonicensis 1411.21, Elean ἤστω “Schwyzer” 424}}; _3rd.pers._ _plural_ ἔστωσαν, but ἔστων {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 502a, ὄντων [prev. author] “Lg.” 879b}}, and early _Attic dialect_ Inscrr., {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“IG” 12.22, etc. (ἔστωσαν first in 2nd c.BC, [prev. work]22.1328), _Doric dialect_ ἐόντων [prev. work]1126:}}_subjunctive_ ὦ, ᾖς, ᾖ, _Epic dialect_ ἔω {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.18}}; _3rd.pers._ _singular_ ἔῃ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.300, +others (also ἔῃσι 2.366, +others, ᾖσι (ν) 19.202, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 294)}}, also _Boeotian dialect_ ἔνθω {{Refs 8th c.BC+|“IG” 7.3172.165, μετείω 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.47 and perhaps εἴῃ 9.245, etc.}}; _Doric dialect_ _3rd.pers._ _plural_ ὦντι {{Refs|“SIG” 940.3 (from Crete), ἔωντι “GDI” 5040.14 (from Hierapytna)}}, _Boeotian dialect_ ἴωνθι {{Refs 6th c.BC+|“IG” 7.3171.46 (3rd c.BC): _optative_ εἴην, -ης (εἴησθα 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 715)}}, -η, also ἔοις, ἔοι, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.284, 142, +others, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.6}}; _3rd.pers._ _plural_ εἴοισαν Ἀρχ. Ἐφ. {{Refs|1911.133 (from Gonni)}}; _3rd.pers._ _dual_ εἴτην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Parmenides” 149e, “Sph.” 243e}}; _1st pers._ _plural_ εἶμεν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 921 (Lyric poetry), 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus}}; _2nd pers._ _plural_ εἶτε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 21.195}}; _3rd.pers._ _plural_ εἶεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.372, etc., εἴησαν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.2, etc.}}; Elean ἔα, ={εἴη}, [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|“SIG” 9 (6th c.BC), and σύν-εαν, ={συνεῖεν}, “GDI” 1149 (6th c.BC):}}]_infinitive_ εἶναι, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.AD(?): Arcadius Grammaticus ἦναι “SIG” 306.9 (Tegea, 4th c.BC)}}; _Epic dialect_ ἔμμεναι (also _Aeolic dialect_ ἔμμεν᾽ {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 34), ἔμμεν (also 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 6.42, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 623 (Lyric poetry)), ἔμεναι, ἔμεν, also ἔμειν “SIG” 1166 (from Dodona)}}; _Doric dialect_ εἶμεν Foed. cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.77, 79, “IG” 7.1.7 (from Megara), ἦμεν “Test.Epict.” 5.16, “Tab.Heracl.” 1.75, Cretan dialect ἦμεν or ἤμην “Leg.Gort.” 1.15, +others, “GDI” 4998i 2, +others, Megarian dialect εἴμεναι 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 775, εἴμειν “IG” 12(1).155.100 (from Rhodes), 14.952 (from Agrigentum); εἶν [prev. work] 12(9).211.10 (from Eretria), “SIG” 135.4 (from Olynthus), etc.:}}_participle_ ὤν, _Epic dialect_ ἐών, ἐοῦσα, ἐόν, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD: Cypria ἰών “Inscription Cyprian dialect” 135.23 H.}}; _Boeotian dialect_ _feminine_ ἰῶσα {{Refs|“IG” 7.3172.116 (Orchom.)}}, _Aeolic dialect_ and _Doric dialect_ _feminine_ ἔσσα {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 75.4, “IG” 4.952.2 (Epid.), 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 28.16, ἐοῖσα 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.265, ἔασσα “Lyrica Alexandrina Adespota” 9, Diotogenes Philosophus cited in 5th c.AD: Stobaeus, Joannes 4.7.62, εὖσα Erinna Lyrica 5.5}} (also _Ionic dialect_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 5.16, εὔντων 2.85), ἐᾶσα 5th-4th c.BC: Timaeus Locrus Philosophus 96d, “IG” 5(1).1470.8 (from Messene), ἴαττα “Leg.Gort.” 8.47; _accusative_ _sing._ εὖντα 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 2.3}}; _nominative_ _singular_ εἴς in {{Refs 7th c.BC+|4th c.AD: Heraclides Historicus cited in 12th c.AD: Eustathius Episcopus Thessalonicensis 1756.13, _plural_ ἔντες “Tab.Heracl.” 1.117; _dative_ _plural_ ἔντασσι [prev. work]104; _genitive_ _plural_ παρ-έντων 7th c.BC: Alcman Lyricus 64}}: _imperfect_ ἦν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.77, etc., _Epic dialect_ ἔον}} (also _Aeolic dialect_, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 127, 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica “Papyrus fragments” in 1787 “fragment” 3 ii 21), in _Attic dialect_ ἦ}} (uncertain in _Aeolic dialect_, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus “Supp.” 14.9), 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 77, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 61b, etc., but usually altered to ἦν in codices (and ἦν}} is required by metre in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 280), _contraction_ from _Epic dialect_ and _Ionic dialect_ ἦα (8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.808, +others, “IG” 12(8).449.2 (from Thasos)}}, whence Hom.and later _Ionic dialect_ ἔᾱ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.321, +others, ἔας 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.187, ἔατε [prev. author] 4.119)}}; _Epic dialect_ _3rd.pers._ _singular_ ἦεν, always with ν in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}}; ἔην as _1st pers._ _singular_, only {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.762 (assuming variant, +others ἔον), frequently as _3rd.pers._ _sing._ (generally before a consonant, so that ἔεν is possible), sometimes also ἤην}}; _2nd pers._ _singular_ ἦσθα, later ἦς (which is variant in {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 1.26), sometimes in LXX (LXX.Judg.11.35, LXX.Ruth.3.2, +others), compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Axiochus” 365e, Erinna Lyrica 4.4, NT.Matt.25.21, +others, ἦσθας 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Ἐπιτρέποντες” 156, _Epic dialect_ ἔησθα}}; _3rd.pers._ _singular_ ἦν, _Epic dialect_ ἔην, ἤην, ἦεν (see. above), _Doric dialect_ and _Aeolic dialect_ ἦς {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus “Supp.” 30.1, 5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 102, 5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 59, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 2.90, “SIG” 241.145 (Delph.)}}; _3rd.pers._ _dual_ ἤστην {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.10, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 387, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 982, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 272a, +others}}; _Doric dialect_ _1st pers._ _plural_ ἦμες {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Lycurgus” 21}}; _2nd pers._ _plural_ ἦτε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 276c, ἦστε 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 821, “Ec.” 1086}}; _3rd.pers._ _plural_ ἦσαν, _Ionic dialect_ and poetical ἔσαν (in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 321, 825, ἦν is not _plural_ for ἦσαν}}, but is rather a peculiarity of syntax, see below see, but is _3rd.pers._ _plural_ in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 46, +others, “SIG” 560.15 (Epidamnus, 3rd c.BC))}}; _Aeolic dialect_ ἔον {{NT+5th c.BC+|“Schwyzer” 644.12; later ἤμην “PSI” 4.362.21 (3rd c.BC), “SIG” 527.46 (Crete, 3rd c.BC), “IGRom.” 4.1740 (from Cyme), always in LXX as LXX.Bar.1.19, cf. NT.Matt.23.30, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.174a, etc., and sometimes in codices of earlier writers, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 7.34, “Tragica Adespota” 124 (cited from 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 931 by 4th-5th c.AD: Choeroboscus Grammaticus and from [prev. author] “Tr.” 474 by Aps.), 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 6.1.9, 4th c.BC: Hyperides Orator “adv. Athenogenem” 26}}, _2nd pers._ _singular_ ἦσο {{Refs|“epigram Gr.” 379 (from Aezani)}}, _3rd.pers._ _singular_ ἦστο {{Refs|“Supp.epigram” 1.455.7 (from Phrygia)}}, _1st pers._ _plural_ ἤμεθα {{NT+3rd c.BC+|“PPetr.” 2p.11 (3rd c.BC), LXX.Bar.1.19, LXX.1Ki.25.16, NT.Eph.2.3}}; _subjunctive_ ὦμαι {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PBaden” 48.12 (2nd c.BC), ἦται “GDI” , ἦνται probably in “IG” 5(1).1390.83 (from Andania)}}; _Ionic dialect_ and _Epic dialect_ also ἔσκον, used by {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 656 (Lyric poetry):}}_future_ ἔσομαι, ἔσται, _Epic dialect_ and _Aeolic dialect_ also ἔσσομαι, ἔσεται, ἔσσετα; _Aeolic dialect_ _2nd pers._ _singular_ ἔσσῃ probably in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 67, 87; _Doric dialect_ 2 and _3rd.pers._ _sing._ ἐσσῇ, ἐσσεῖται, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.393, 13.317, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 10.5, _3rd.pers._ _plural_ ἐσσοῦνται Foed. cited in 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.77 codices (but ἔσσονται “Tab.Heracl.” 1.113)}}, _infinitive_ ἐσσεῖσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 57.}} —All forms of the _present_ _indicative_ are enclitic (except _2nd pers._ _singular_ εἶ and _3rd.pers._ _plural_ ἔασι); but _3rd.pers._ _singular_ is written ἔστι when it begins a sentence or verse, or when it immediately follows οὐκ, καί, εἰ, ὡς, ἀλλά, or τοῦτ᾽, Hdn.Gr.{{Refs|1.553 (also μή _accusative_ to “Etymologicum Magnum” 301.3)}}; later Grammars wrote ἔστι as _substantive_ Verb, {{Refs 9th c.AD+|9th c.AD: Photius Legal icographus, etc., 12th c.AD: Eustathius Episcopus Thessalonicensis 880.22.}}
**A** as the _substantive_ Verb,
**A.I** of persons, **exist**, οὐκ ἔσθ᾽ οὗτος ἀνήρ, οὐδ᾽ ἔσσεται {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 16.437}}; ἔτ᾽ εἰσί they **are** still **in being**,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|15.433, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 445, etc.; τεθνηῶτος.. μηδ᾽ ἔτ᾽ ἐόντος 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.289}}; οὐκέτ᾽ ἐστί he **is** no more, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 1162}}; οὐδὲ δὴν ἦν he **was** not long-**lived**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.131}}; ὁ οὐκ ὤν, οἱ οὐκ ὄντες, of **those who are** no more, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.45, 44}}; οἱ ὄντες the **living**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 9.29.2}}; ὁ ὤν the {{LXX+8th c.BC+|“Eternal”, LXX.Exo.3.14, +others, 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.289; θεοὶ αἰὲν ἐόντες 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.290}}; ἐσσόμενοι **posterity**,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|2.119; κἀγὼ γὰρ ἦ ποτ᾽, ἀλλὰ νῦν οὐκ εἴμ᾽ ἔτι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 284}}; ὡς ἂν εἶεν ἅνθρωποι **might continue in being**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 190c; ζώντων καὶ ὄντων Ἀθηναίων 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.72, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Generatione et Corruptione” 318b25}}; of things, εἰ ἔστι ἀληθέως [ἡ τράπεζα] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.17, etc.}}; of cities, ὄλωλεν, οὐδ᾽ ἔτ᾽ ἐστὶ Τροία {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 1292, compare “Heracl.” 491}}; δοκεῖ μοι Καρχηδόνα μὴ εἶναι **censeo Carthaginem esse delendam**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Cat.Ma.” 27}}; ἂν ᾖ τὸ στράτευμα **be in existence**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 8.17}}; of money, **to be in hand**, τῶν ὄντων χρημάτων καὶ τῶν προσιόντων {{Refs|“IG” 12.91.25}}; τὰ ὄντα **property**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 511a, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Antonius” 24, etc.}}; τὸ ἐσόμενον ἐκ.. **future revenue** from.., {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“BCH” 46.420 (Olymos, 1st c.BC)}}; of place, τὴν οὖσαν ἐκκλησίαν the **local** church, {{NT|NT.Act.13.1}}; of time, τοῦ ὄντος μηνός in the **current** month, {{Refs|“BGU” 146.4, etc.}}; **in office**, ἱερέων τῶν ὄντων {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PPar.” 5.4 (2nd c.BC)}}; αἱ οὖσαι [ἐξουσίαι] the powers **that be, Ep. Rom.**{{Refs|13.1.}}
**A.II** of the **real** world, **be**, opposed to become, γίγνεται πάντα ἃ δή φαμεν εἶναι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 152d, etc.}}; τὸ ὄν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“Being”, 4th-5th c.BC: Parmenides Poeta Philosophus 8.35, 5th c.BC: Protagoras Philosophus 2, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 27d, etc.}}; opposed to τὸ μὴ ὄν, {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Gorgias Rhetor et Sophista “fragment” 3 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator, etc.; οὐδὲν γίνεται ἐκ τοῦ μὴ ὄντος 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 1p.5U.; ἐξ οὐκ ὄντων ἐποίησεν αὐτὰ ὁ θεός LXX.2Mac.7.28}}; τὰ ὄντα the **world of things**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus 7, 5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 129.5, etc.}}; ὄν_indeclinable_, τῶν ὂν εἰδῶν species **of Being**, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 6.2.10.}}
**A.II.2** of circumstances, events, etc., **to happen**, τά τ᾽ ἐόντα, τά τ᾽ ἐσσόμενα, πρό τ᾽ ἐόντα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.70; ἡ ἐσβολὴ ἔμελλεν ἔσεσθαι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.13, etc.}}; τῆς προδοσίας οὔσης since treachery **was there**, {{Refs|[prev. author] 4.103}}; ἕως ἂν ὁ πόλεμος ᾖ so long as it **last**, {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.58; αἱ σπονδαὶ ἐνιαυτὸν ἔσονται [prev. author] 4.118}}; τί ἐστι; what **is** it? what's the matter? {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 193}}; τί οὖν ἦν τοῦτ; how **came** it **to pass**? {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 58a: repeated with a _relative_ to avoid a positive assertion, ἔστι δ᾽ ὅπῃ νῦν ἔ.}} things **are** as they **are**, i.e. are ill, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 67.}}
**A.III** **be the fact** or **the case**, διπλασίαν ἂν τὴν δύναμιν εἰκάζεσθαι ἤ ἐστιν twice as large as it **really is**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.10}}; αὐτὸ ὅ ἐστι καλόν beauty **in its essence**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 211c, compare “Phd.” 74b}}; frequently in _participle_, τὸν ἐόντα λόγον λέγειν or φαίνειν the **true** story, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.95, 116}}; τῷ ἐόντι χρήσασθαι tell the **truth**,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 30; τὰ ὄντα ἀπαγγέλλειν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.8}}; σκῆψιν οὐκ οὖσαν, λόγον οὐκ ὄντα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 584, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1052}}; τῷ ὄντι **in reality, in fact**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 328d, etc.}}; to apply a quotation to a case in point, τῷ ὄντι κλαυσίγελως **real** 'smiles through tears' (with allusion to {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.484), 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 7.2.9, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Laches” 196d}}; κατὰ τὸ ἐόν according to the **fact, right**ly, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.97}}; πᾶν τὸ ἐόν the whole **truth**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 9.11; τοῦ ἐόντος ἀποτεύξεται 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀρχαίης ἰητρικῇς” 2.}}
**A.IV** followed by the _relative_, οὐκ ἔστιν ὅς or ὅστις **no one**, οὐκ ἔσθ᾽ ὃς.. ἀπαλάλκοι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.348; οὐκ ἔ. οὐδεὶς ὅς 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 903}}; οὐκ ἔ. ὅτῳ, ={οὐδενί}, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 293 (_anapaest meter_), compare 989: frequently in _plural_, εἰσὶν οἵ}}, = Latin _sunt qui_, used exactly like{ἔνιοι}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.88, 7.44, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 77d, “Grg.” 503a, etc. (εἰσί τινες οἵ.. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.24)}}; ἐστὶν ἃ χωρία, πολίσματα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 1.12, 65; ἐστὶν ἃ εἰπεῖν [prev. author] 2.67; ἦσαν οἵ 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.2.14}}; the _singular_ Verb is used even with _masculine_ and _feminine_ _plural_, ἐστὶν οἵ, αἵ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀγμῶν” 1, “VC” 4, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.3.16}}; more frequently in oblique cases, ποταμῶν ἐστὶ ὧν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.187; ἐστὶν ἀφ᾽ ὧν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.65}}; ἐστὶ παρ᾽ οἷς, ἐστὶν ἐν οἷς, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 1.23, 5.25: in questions ὅστις is used, ἔστιν ἥντινα δόξαν.. ἀπεκρίνατ; 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 85b: with _relative_ Particles, ἐστὶν ἔνθα}}, = Latin _est ubi_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.4.15, etc.}}; ἐ. ὅπῃ, ἔσθ᾽ ὅπου, **some**how, **some**where, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 331d, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 517, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 448, etc.}}; in questions expecting a _negative_ answer, ἐ. ὁπόθεν, ὅπω; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 35a, “R.” 493e, etc.; οὐ γάρ ἐσθ᾽ ὅπως 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 61, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.102, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 620}}; οὐκ ἔ. ὅπως οὐ **in any case, necessarily**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 188; οὐκ ἔ. ὡς 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 76e, etc.}}; ἐ. ὅτε, ἔσθ᾽ ὅτε, **some**times, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 180.2, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 56, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.21, etc.}}
**A.V** ἦν is sometimes used with _plural_ _masculine_ and _feminine_, usually at the beginning of a sentence, **there was**, τῆς δ᾽ ἦν τρεῖς κεφαλαί {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 321; (but in ἦν δ᾽ ἐρῳδιοί τε πολλοί 5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 46, compare 59, +others, it may be taken as _Doric dialect_ _3rd.pers._ _plural_)}}; ἦν δ᾽ ἀμφίπλεκτοι κλίμακες {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 520 (Lyric poetry)}}; ἦν ἄρα κἀκεῖνοι ταλακάρδιοι epigram cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.184; less frequently ἔστι, ἔστι δὲ μεταξὺ.. ἑπτὰ στάδιοι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.26, compare 7.34; ἔστι.. ἄρχοντές τε καὶ δῆμος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 463a}}; before dual Nouns, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 58, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 500d.}}
**A.VI** ἔστι _impersonal_, with _infinitive_, **it is possible**, ἔστι γὰρ ἀμφοτέροισιν ὀνείδεα μυθήσασθαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 20.246; ἔστι μὲν εὕδειν, ἔστι δὲ τερπομένοισιν ἀκούειν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.392}}; εἴ τί πού ἐστι (i.e. πιθέσθαι){{Refs 5th c.BC+|4.193; τοιάδε.. ἐστὶν ἀκοῦσαι 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 1055 (_anapaest meter_); ἔστι τεκμήρια ὁρᾶν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.2.13, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1163, 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.105, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.272, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 53.6, etc.}}; so in _imperative_, _optative_, and _subjunctive_, ἔστω ἀποφέρεσθαι τῷ βουλομένῳ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|“IG” 12.10.7; μυρία ἂν εἴη λέγειν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 271e; ὅπως ἂν ᾖ δρᾶν “IG” 2.1054.91: more frequently in _negative_ clauses, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.267, etc.}}; followed by ὥστε with _infinitive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 656}}: with _accusative_ et _infinitive_, ἁδόντα δ᾽ εἴη με τοῖς ἀγαθοῖς ὁμιλεῖν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 2.96; ἔστιν ἐκπεσεῖν ἀρχῆς Δία 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 757}}: sometimes not _impersonal_ in this sense, θάλασσα δ᾽ οὐκέτ᾽ ἦν ἰδεῖν {{Refs|[prev. author] “Pers.” 419.}}
**A.VI.b** ἔστω in argument, **let it be granted**, ἔστω τοῦτο ἀληθὲς εἶναι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Compositione Verborum” 25; ἔστω σοι τοῦθ᾽ οὕτως 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.987b; ἔστω εἶναί τινα τοιοῦτον 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator Chr.74.24.}}
**B** most frequently, **to be**, the Copula connecting the predicate with the Subject, both being in the same case: hence, **signify, import**, τὸ γὰρ εἴρειν λέγειν ἐστίν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 398d}}; especially in the phrase τοῦτ᾽ ἔστι, **hoc est**; Σκαιόλαν, ὅπερ ἐστὶ Λαϊόν {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Publicola” 17: with numerals, τὰ δὶς πέντε δέκα ἐστίν}} twice five **are** ten, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.4.7}}; εἶναί τις or τι, **to be** somebody, something, **be** of some consequence, see at {τι; οὐδὲν εἶναι} {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 562d, etc.}}
**B.2** _periphrastic_ with the Participle to represent the finite Verb: with _perfect_ _participle_ once in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, τετληότες εἰμέν, for τετλήκαμεν, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.873}}; so in Trag. and _Attic dialect_, ἦν τεθνηκώς, for ἐτεθνήκει, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 869}}; ἔσται δεδορκώς{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 1179; εἰμὶ γεγώς 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1299; πεφυκός ἐστι 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1473; δεδρακότες εἰσίν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.68; κατακεκονότες ἔσεσθε 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.6.36}}: with _aorist_ _participle_, once in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, βλήμενος ἦν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.211}}; so προδείσας εἰμί, οὐ σιωπήσας ἔσε; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 90, 1146, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 460}}: with _present_ _participle_, ἦν προκείμενον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Pers.” 371; φεύγων Ὀρέστης ἐστίν [prev. author] “Ch.” 136; εἴην οὐκ ἂν εὖ φρονῶν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1330}}; τί δ᾽ ἐστί.. φέρο; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “OT” 991, compare 274, 708; λέγων ἐστίν τις 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 1179; ἦν τίς σ᾽ ὑβρίζων [prev. author] “HF” 313; πόρρω ἤδη εἶ πορευόμενος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Lysis” 204b; βαδίζων εἰμί 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 36; frequently in 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus, ἦσαν ἱέντες 1.57, +others; even εἰσὶ διάφοροι ἐόντες 3.49 (assuming variant):}} — if the _Article_ is joined with the Part., the noun is made emphatic, Κᾶρές εἰσι οἱ καταδέξαντες the persons who showed her **were** Carians, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 1.171; αὐτὸς ἦν ὁ μαρτυρῶν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 798; δόλος ἦν ὁ φράσας 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 197 (_anapaest meter_).}}
**C** εἶναι is frequently modified in sense by the addition of _adverbs_, or the cases of Nouns without or with Preps.:
**C.I** εἶναι with _adverbs_, where the _adverb_ often merely represents a Noun and stands as the predicate, ἅλις δέ οἱ ἦσαν ἄρουραι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.122, etc.}}; ἀκέων, ἀκήν εἶναι, **to be** silent,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|4.22, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.82; σῖγα πᾶς ἔστω λεώς 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 532; διαγνῶναι χαλεπῶς ἦν ἄνδρα ἕκαστον 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.424}}; ἀσφαλέως ἡ κομιδὴ ἔσται **will go on** safely, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.134}}; ἐγγύς, πόρρω εἶναι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.88, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 356e: frequently _impersonal_ with words implying good or ill fortune, Κουρήτεσσι κακῶς ἦν}} **it fared** ill with them, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.551; εὖ γὰρ ἔσται 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 89, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 1188, etc.; ἡδέως ἂν αὐτοῖς εἴη 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 59.30.}}
**C.II** with _genitive_, to express descent or extraction, πατρὸς δ᾽ εἴμ᾽ ἀγαθοῖο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.109; αἵματός εἰς ἀγαθοῖο 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.611, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.71, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.71, etc.; πόλεως μεγίστης εἶ 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.3.19.}}
**C.II.b** to express the material of which a thing is made, ἡ κρηπίς ἐστι λίθων μεγάλων **consists of**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.93; τῆς πόλιος ἐούσης δύο φαρσέων [prev. work] 186}}; τοιούτων ἔργων ἐστὶ ἡ τυραννίς **is made up of**.., {{Refs|[prev. author] 5.92}}.ή, etc.
**C.II.c** to express the class to which a person or thing belongs, εἶ γὰρ τῶν φίλων **you are one of** them, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 345; ἐτύγχανε βουλῆς ὤν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.70; ὅσοι ἦσαν τῶν προτέρων στρατιωτῶν [prev. author] 7.44; Κριτίας τῶν τριάκοντα ὤν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.2.31}}; ἔστι τῶν αἰσχρῶν **it is in the class of** disgraceful things, i. e. **it is** disgraceful, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 2.2.}}
**C.II.d** to express that a thing belongs to another, Τροίαν Ἀχαιῶν οὖσαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 269; τὸ πεδίον ἦν μέν κοτε Χορασμίων 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.117, etc.}}: hence, **to be of the party of**, ἦσαν.. τινὲς μὲν φιλίππου, τινὲς δὲ τοῦ βελτίστου {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 9.56, compare 37.53}}; **to be de pendent upon**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 737, etc.}}; **to be at the mercy of**, ἔστι τοῦ λέγοντος, ἢν φόβους λέγῃ {{Refs|[prev. author] “OT” 917.}}
**C.II.e** to express one's duty, business, custom, nature, and the like, οὔτοι γυναικός ἐστι **'tis** not a woman's **part**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 940; τὸ ἐπιτιμᾶν παντὸς εἶναι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 1.16}}; τὸ δὲ ναυτικὸν τέχνης ἐστίν **is matter of** art, **requires** art, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.142, compare 83.}}
**C.II.f** in LXX, **to be occupied about**, ἦσαν τοῦ θύειν {{LXX|LXX.2Ch.30.17}}; ἔσεσθαι, with _genitive_, **to be about to**, ἐσόμεθα τοῦ σῶσαί σε {{LXX|LXX.2Ki.10.11.}}
**C.III** with the _dative_, ἐστί μοι I **have**, frequently in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, etc.}}
**C.III.2** with two datives., σφίσι τε καὶ Ἀθηναίοισι εἶναι οὐδὲν πρῆγμα that they and the Athenians **have** nothing **to do** one with another, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.84; μηδὲν εἶναι σοὶ καὶ φιλίππῳ πρᾶγμα 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.283}}; more shortly, σοί τε καὶ τούτοισι πρήγμασι τί ἐστ; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.33}}; τί τῷ νόμῳ καὶ τῇ βασάν; {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 29.36}}; τί ἐμοὶ καὶ σο; Latin _quid tecum est mihi?_ {{NT|NT.Mark.5.7, etc.}}; also ἐμοὶ οὐδὲν πρὸς τοὺς τοιούτους (i.e. ἐστίν) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.12; ἐν οἷς πρὸς τοὺς ἐναντίους ἐστὶ τῷ δήμῳ 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.278}}; ἔσται αὐτῳ πρὸς τὸν θεόν, in tomb inscriptions, {{Refs|“JHS” 18.113, etc.}}
**C.III.3** with ἄσμενος, βουλόμενος, etc., added, ἐμοὶ δέ κεν ἀσμένῳ εἴη **'twould be** to my delight, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.108; οὐκ ἂν σφίσι βουλομένοις εἶναι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.35; προσδεχομένῳ [prev. author] 6.46; θέλοντι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1356 (Lyric poetry); ἡδομένοις 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Laches” 187c.}}
**C.IV** with Preps., εἶναι ἀπό τινος, ={εἶναί τινος}{{Refs 5th c.BC+|(above 11.a), 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.6.9; εἰσὶν ἀπ᾽ ἐναντίων αὗται πραγμάτων 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 12d}}; but εἶναι ἀπ᾽ οἴκου **to be** away from.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.99.}}
**C.IV.2** εἶναι ἔκ τινος **to be sprung** from, εἴμ᾽ ἐκ Παιονίης, Μυρμιδόνων ἔξ εἰμι, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.154, 24.397, etc.}}; ἔστιν ἐξ ἀνάγκης **it is** of necessity, i. e. necessary, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 256d.}}
**C.IV.3** εἶναι ἐν.. **to be** in a certain state, ἐν εὐπαθείῃσι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.22}}; ἐν ἀθυμία, etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.46, etc.; ἐν ταραχαῖς 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.218}}; εἶναι ἐν ἀξιώματι **to be** in esteem, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.130}}; οἱ ἐν τέλεϊ ἐόντες those in office, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.18, etc.}}; but εἶναι ἐν τέχνῃ, ἐν φιλοσοφία **to be engaged** in.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 562, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 59a.}}
**C.IV.3.b** ἐν σοί ἐστι **it depends** on thee, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.109, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 963; ἐν σοὶ γάρ ἐσμεν [prev. author] “OT” 314; so also ἐπί τινι [prev. author] “Ph.” 1003, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.6.2, etc.}}
**C.IV.4** εἶναι διά.., much like{εἶναι ἐν}.., εἶναι διὰ φόβου, ={φοβεῖσθαι}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.34}}; εἶναι δι᾽ ὄχλου, ={ὀχληρὸν εἶναι}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 1.73; εἶναι διὰ μόχθων 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.6.25}}; εἶναι δι᾽ αἰτίας, ={αἰτιᾶσθαι}, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 1.70}}; Geometry texts, **pass** through, διὰ τᾶς ἑτέρας διαμέτρου ἐόντος τοῦ ἐπιπέδου {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Archimedes Geometra “περὶ κωνοειδέων καὶ σφαιροειδέων” 20.}}
**C.IV.5** εἶναι ἐφ᾽ ἑαυτῆς **to be** by oneself, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 25.23}}; εἶναι ἐπὶ ὀνόματος **to bear** a name, {{Refs|[prev. author] 39.21}}; εἶναι ἐπὶ τοῖς πράγμασιν **to be engaged** in.., {{Refs|[prev. author] 2.12}}; εἶναι ἐπί τινα **to be** against him, {{Refs|[prev. author] 6.33}}; εἶναι ἐφ᾽ ἑξήκοντα στάδια **to reach** sixty stadia, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.6.11}}; εἶναι ἐπὶ τὰς ἁφάς **pass** through the points of contact, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Biographus Perg. “Con.” 4.1}}; εἶναι ἐπί τινι, see above 3 b.
**C.IV.6** εἶναι πρός τινος **to be** in one's favour, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.10, 29, etc.}}; **to suit**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.2.11, etc.}}; εἶναι πρός τινι **engaged** in, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 84c, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vita Apollonii” 5.31}}; πρὸς τοῖς ἰδίοις **mind** one's own affairs, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1309a6, “Ath.” 16.3; εἶναι πρὸς τὸ κωλύειν 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.26.3}}; πρὸς τὸ πονεῖν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|Teles p.46 H.; εἶναι περί τι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.5.7, etc.}}
**C.IV.7** εἶναι παρά τινι or τινα, = παρειναι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Cyr.” 6.2.15, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.140.ά}} (assuming variant).
**C.IV.8** εἶναι ὑπό τινα or τινι **to be** subject to.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 5.2.17 (assuming variant), 6.2.4.}}
**C.IV.9** περὶ τούτων ἐστίν that **is** the question, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Ἐπιτρέποντες” 30.}}
**C.IV.10** εἶναι ἀπό.., in Geometry texts, **to be constructed** upon, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Archimedes Geometra “περὶ σφαίρας καὶ κυλίνδρου” 2.9, “Con.Sph.” 7.}}
**D** ἐστί is very frequently omitted, mostly in the _present_ _indicative_ before certain predicates, as ἀνάγκη, ἄξιον, δυνατόν, εἰκός, ἕτοιμον, οἷόν τε, ῥᾴδιον, χρεών, etc., and after the _neuter_ of Verbals in -τέος, and such forms as θαυμαστὸν ὅσον: less frequently with other persons and moods, εἰμί omitted, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 92, “Aj.” 813}}; εἶ, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.206}}; ἐσμέν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 634}}; ἐστέ, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.463}}; εἰσί, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 499 (Lyric poetry), “IG” 2.778 B}}; _subjunctive_ ᾖ, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.376, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 659, 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.32}}; _optative_ εἴη, {{Refs|“IG” 22.1183.12}}; _imperfect_ ἦν, {{Refs|[prev. work]2.778 B}}; _future_ ἔσονται, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.394.}}
**E** the Inf. frequently seems redundant,
**E.1** in phrases implying power or will to do a thing, ἑκὼν εἶναι (see. ἑκών) κατὰ δύναμιν εἶναι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 2.32; εἰς δύναμιν εἶναι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 300c}}; τὸ ἐπ᾽ ἐκείνοις εἶναι, **quantum in illis esset**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.48, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 3.5.9, compare 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 13.58; τὸ ἐπὶ σφᾶς εἶναι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.28; τὸ κατὰ τοῦτον εἶναι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.6.9; κατὰ τοῦτο εἶναι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 317a}}; τὸ τήμερον, τὸ νῦν εἶναι, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Cra.” 396e, “La.” 201c, Theopomp. Comedy texts 98, Decrees cited in 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 31.2, etc.}}
**E.2** after Verbs of naming or choosing, σοφιστὴν ὀνομάζουσι τὸν ἄνδρα εἶναι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 311e; σύμμαχόν μιν εἵλοντο εἶναι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.134}}; of giving, δῶκε ξεινήϊον εἶναι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.20.}}
**F** _imperfect_ ἦνissts. used where other languages take the _present_,
**F.1** after ἄρα, to express a fact which **is and has always been** the same, δέρμα δὲ ἀνθρώπου.. ἦν ἄρα σχεδὸν δερμάτων πάντων λαμπρότατον human skin then **it appears is**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.64; Κύπρις οὐκ ἄρ᾽ ἦν θεός 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 359}}; ὡς ἄρ᾽ ἦσθ᾽ ἐμὸς πατὴρ ὀρθῶς{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|[prev. work] 1169; ἦ πολύμοχθον ἄρ᾽ ἦν γένος.. ἁμερίων [prev. author] “IA” 1330; ἦ στωμύλος ἦσθα 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 5.79}}; so also when there is reference to a past thought, τουτὶ τί ἦ; what **is** this? {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 157, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 387c: so in the Aristotelian formula τὸ τί ἦν εἶναι (“APo.” 82b38, +others)}}, used to express **the essential nature** of a thing, where τί ἦν (for ἐστί) takes the place of the _dative_ in such phrases as τὸ ἀγαθῷ εἶναι, τὸ μεγέθει εἶναι, {{Refs|“APr.” 67b12, “de An.” 429b10.}}
**G** ἐγώ εἰμι, in {{LXX|LXX, pleonastic for ἐγώ, ἐγώ εἰμι οὐχ ἥμαρτον LXX.Judg.11.27, compare 6.18; also ἔσται πᾶς ἀποκτενεῖ με LXX.Gen.4.14.}}

1512:
** εἴπερ**
or **εἴ περ**, strengthened for εἰ, **if really, if indeed**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.25, etc.}}; especially **even if, even though**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.117, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.167, etc.; εἴ. καί 9.35; εἴ. τε 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.225; εἴ. γε 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 198, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 312a, etc.; εἴ. γε δή [prev. author] “Tht.” 182c}}; with words between, εἴ. γάρ τε χόλον γε.. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.81; εἴπερ ἔσται γε 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1249, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 275e; καλῶς, εἴπερ ποτέ, ἔχει 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.20; εἴπερ ἄρα 4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 7.216b.}}
**II** in _Attic dialect_ and Trag. to imply that the supposition agrees with the fact, **if as is the fact, since**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.14, etc.}}; but with _imperfect_ it implies that it is contrary to the fact, εἴπερ ἦν πέλας **if** I had been (but I was not), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 312, compare 604}}; also εἴ. ἐκτελεῖς ἅπερ λέγεις **if only** you will keep your word, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ichn.” 48.}}
**III** with an ellipse, **if you must**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 227}}; ἀλλ᾽ εἴ. but **if so**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Parmenides” 150b, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1101a12; compare εἰ 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus VII.}}

1513:
Included with: ** εἰ**,
_Attic dialect_-_Ionic dialect_ and {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD(?): Arcadius Grammaticus (for εἰκ, see below 11 ad [near the start]), = _Doric dialect_ and _Aeolic dialect_ αἰ, αἰκ (which see), 5th c.AD: Cypria}} ἤ {{Refs|“Inscription Cyprian dialect” 135.10 H., both εἰ and αἰ}} in _Epic dialect_:— Particle used interjectionally with _imperative_ and to express a wish, but usually either in conditions, **if**, or in indirect questions, **whether.** In the former use its regular negative is μ; in the latter, οὐ.
**A** INTERJECTIONALLY, in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}}, **come now**! with _imperative_, εἰ δὲ.. ἄκουσον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.262; εἰ δὲ καὶ αὐτοὶ φευγόντων [prev. work] 46; most frequently with ἄγε (which see), 1.302, +others}}
**A.2** in wishes, with _optative_, ἀλλ᾽ εἴ τις.. καλέσειεν {{Refs|10.111, compare 24.74}}; so later, εἴ μοι ξυνείη μοῖρα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 863 (Lyric poetry); εἴ μοι γένοιτο φθόγγος ἐν βραχίοσιν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 836: more frequently followed by γάρ, αἲ γὰρ δὴ οὕτως εἴη 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.189, +others; εἰ γὰρ γενοίμην ἀντὶ σοῦ νεκρός 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 1410; εἰ γὰρ γένοιτο 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 6.1.38; εἰ γὰρ ἐν τούτῳ εἴη 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 310d}}; of unattained wishes, in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus only with _optative_, εἰ γὰρ ἐγὼν.. Διὸς πάϊς αἰγιόχοιο εἴην 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.825; Ζεῦ πάτερ, αἰ γὰρ ἐμὸς πόσις εἴη 7th c.BC: Alcman Lyricus 29}}; later with past tenses of _indicative_, εἰ γάρ μ᾽ ὑπὸ γῆν.. ἧκεν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 152 (_anapaest meter_)}}; εἰ γὰρ τοσαύτην δύναμιν εἶχον ὥστε.. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 1072: twice in 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” with}}_infinitive_ (compare the use of _infinitive_ in commands), αἰ γὰρ τοῖος ἐὼν.. ἐμὸς γαμβρὸς καλέεσθαι {{Refs|7.311, compare 24.376.}}
**A.2.b** εἴθε, _Epic dialect_ αἴθε, is frequently used in wishes in the above constructions, εἴθε οἱ αὐτῷ Ζεὺς ἀγαθὸν τελέσειεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|2.33; εἴθ᾽ ὣς ἡβώοιμι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.157; ἰὼ γᾶ, εἴθ᾽ ἔμ᾽ ἐδέξω 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1537 (Lyric poetry); εἴθε σοι, ὦ Περίκλεις, τότε συνεγενόμην 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.2.46}}: later with _infinitive_, γαίης χθαμαλωτέρη εἴθε.. κεῖσθαι {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“Anthologia Graeca” 9.284 (1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Crinagoras Epigrammaticus).}}
**A.2.c** εἰ γάρ, εἴθε are also used with ὤφελον (_Epic dialect_ ὤφελλον), of past unattained wishes, αἴθ᾽ ὤφελλες στρατοῦ ἄλλου σημαίνειν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.84}}; εἰ γὰρ ὤφελον [κατιδεῖν] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 432c.}}
**A.2.d** followed by a clause expressing a consequence of the fulfilment of the wish, αἰ γὰρ τοῦτο.. ἔπος τετελεσμένον εἴη· τῷ κε τάχα γνοίης.. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.536, compare 17.496, +others}}; sometimes hard to distinguish from εἰ in conditions (which may be derived from this use), εἴ μοί τι πίθοιο, τό κεν πολὺ κέρδιον εἴη {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.28.}}
**B** IN CONDITIONS, **if**:
**B.I** with INDIC.,
**B.I.1** with all tenses (for _future_, see below {{Refs|2), to state a condition, with nothing implied as to its fulfilment, εἰ δ᾽ οὕτω τοῦτ᾽ ἐστίν, ἐμοὶ μέλλει φίλον εἶναι but}}**if** this is so, it will be.., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.564}}: any form of the Verb may stand in apodosi, εἰ θεοί τι δρῶσιν αἰσχρόν, οὐκ εἰσὶν θεοί {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 292.7; εἰ δοκεῖ, πλέωμεν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 526; εἰ Φαῖδρον ἀγνοῶ, καὶ ἐμαυτοῦ ἐπιλέλησμαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 228a; κάκιστ᾽ ἀπολοίμην, Ξανθίαν εἰ μὴ φιλῶ 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 579, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.475; εἰ θεοῦ ἦν, οὐκ ἦν αἰσχροκερδής· εἰ δ᾽ αἰσχροκερδής, οὐκ ἦν θεοῦ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 408c; εἰ ταῦτα λέγων διαφθείρω τοὺς νέους, ταῦτ᾽ ἂν εἴη βλαβερά [prev. author] “Ap.” 30b, compare 25b}}; εἰ οὗτοι ὀρθῶς ἀπέστησαν, ὑμεῖς ἂν οὐ χρεὼν ἄρχοιτε **if** these were right in their revolt, (it would follow that) you rule when you have no right, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.40.}}
**B.I.1.b** to express a general condition, **if ever, whenever**, sometimes with _present_, εἴ τις δύο ἢ καὶ πλείους τις ἡμέρας λογίζεται, μάταιός ἐστιν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 943}}: with _imperfect_, εἴ τίς τι ἠρώτα ἀπεκρίνοντο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.10}}: rarely with _aorist_, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 31.26.1, 2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “Πυρρώνειοι ὑποτυπώσεις” 1.84; compare 111.2.}}
**B.I.2** with _future_ (much less frequently than ἐάν with _subjunctive_), either to express a future supposition emphatically, εἰ φθάσομεν τοὺς πολεμίους κατακαίνοντες οὐδεὶς ἡμῶν ἀποθανεῖται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.1.19; εἰ μὴ βοηθήσετε οὐ περιέσται τἀκεῖ 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.91; εἰ αὕτη ἡ πόλις ληφθήσεται, ἔχεται ἡ πᾶσα Σικελία [prev. passage]}} in threats or warnings, εἰ μὴ καθέξεις γλῶσσαν ἔσται σοι κακά {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 5; εἰ τιμωρήσεις Πατρόκλῳ, αὐτὸς ἀποθανῇ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 28c, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 28.21: or}}
**B.I.2.b** to express a present intention or expectation, αἶρε πλῆκτρον εἰ μαχεῖ **if** you mean to fight, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 759; ἐγὼ μὲν οὐκ ἀνήρ.. εἰ ταῦτ᾽ ἀνατεὶ τῇδε κείσεται κράτη 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 485, compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.61, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 863.}}
**B.I.3** with historical tenses, implying that the condition is or was unfulfilled.
**B.I.3.a** with _imperfect_, referring to present time or to continued or repeated action in past time (in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus always the latter, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.715, +others): ταῦτα οὐκ ἂν ἐδύναντο ποιεῖν, εἰ μὴ διαίτῃ μετρίᾳ ἐχρῶντο they would not be able to do this (as they do)}}, **if** they did not live an abstemious life, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.2.16, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 489b; οὐκ ἂν νήσων ἐκράτει, εἰ μή τι καὶ ναυτικὸν εἶχεν he (from Agamemnon)}} would not have been master of islands, **if** he had not had also some naval force, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.9; αἰ δ᾽ ἦχες ἔσλων ἴμερον ἢ κάλων.. αἴδως κεν.. ἦχεν 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 28}}; εἰ ἦσαν ἄνδρες ἀγαθοὶ.. οὐκ ἄν ποτε ταῦτα ἔπασχον **if** they had been good men, they would never have suffered as they did, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 516e, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.1.5}}; εἰ γὰρ ἐγὼ τάδε ᾔδἐ.. οὐκ ἂν ὑπεξέφυγε **if** I had known this.., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.366.}}
**B.I.3.b** with _aorist_ referring to past time, εἰ μὴ ἔφυσε θεὸς μέλι.. ἔφασκον γλύσσονα σῦκα πέλεσθαι {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Xenophanes Poeta Philosophus 38}}; εἰ μὴ ὑμεῖς ἤλθετε, ἐπορευόμεθα ἂν ἐπὶ βασιλέα had you not come, we should be on our way.., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.1.4; καὶ ἴσως ἂν ἀπέθανον, εἰ μὴ ἡ ἀρχὴ διὰ ταχέων κατελύθη 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 32d, compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.680, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.364, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.5, 27.63}}: with _pluperfect_ in apodosi, εἰ τριάκοντα μόναι μετέπεσον τῶν ψήφων, ἀπεπεφεύγη ἄν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 36a.}}
**B.I.3.c** rarely with _pluperfect_ referring to action finished in past or present time, λοιπὸν δ᾽ ἂν ἦν ἡμῖν ἔτι περὶ τῆς πόλεως διαλεχθῆναι, εἰ μὴ προτέρα τῶν ἄλλων τὴν εἰρήνην ἐπεποίητο **if** she had not (as she has done) made peace before the rest, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 5.56, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 21c.}}
**B.II** with SUB{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus, εἰ is regularly joined with ἄν}} (_Epic dialect_ κε, κεν), compare ἐάν: [{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.AD(?): Arcadius Grammaticus εἰκαν in Tegean Inscrr. of 4th c.BC (“IG” 5(2).3.16, 31, 6.2, “SIG” 306.34) should be understood as εἰκ ἄν (εἰ: εἰκ ={οὐ}: οὐκ), since εἰ δ᾽ ἄν is also found in “IG” 5(2).3.2, 6.45, and εἰκ alone, [prev. work]3.21}}]; but ἄν (κε, κεν) are frequently absent in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus as 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.221, 14.373 (and compare below 2), and Lyric poetry, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus (who never uses εἰ with ἄν or κε (ν)) “P.” 4.266, +others; in dialects, αἰ δείλητ᾽ ἀγχωρεῖν “IG” 9(1).334.6 (_Locrian dialect_, 5th c.BC)}}, cf. Foed._Doric dialect_ cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.79; rarely in 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus, εἰ μὴ ἀναβῇ 2.13}}; occasionally in Trag., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 234, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 198 (Lyric poetry), etc.}}; very rarely in _Attic dialect_ Prose, εἰ ξυστῶσιν αἱ πόλεις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.21; εἴ τι που ἄλσος ἢ τέμενος ἀφειμένον ᾖ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 761c}}: in later Prose, εἴ τις θελήσῃ {{NT+3rd c.AD+|NT.Rev.11.5; εἰ φονεύῃ 3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 2.9.9, compare 5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus “Inst.” 26.}}
**B.II.1** when the apodosis is _future_, to express a future condition more distinctly and vividly than εἰ with _optative_, but less so than εἰ with _future_ _indicative_ (above {{Refs|1.2a)}}; εἰ δέ κεν ὣς ἕρξῃς καί τοι πείθωνται Ἀχαιοί, γνώσῃ ἔπειθ᾽.. **if** thou do thus.., thou shalt know, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.364, compare 1.128, 3.281, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.549; ἂν δέ τις ἀνθιστῆται, σὺν ὑμῖν πειρασόμεθα χειροῦσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.3.11}}; ἂν μὴ νῦν ἐθέλωμεν ἐκεῖ πολεμεῖν αὐτῷ, ἐνθάδ᾽ ἴσως ἀναγκασθησόμεθα τοῦτο ποιεῖν **if** we be not now willing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.50, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.3.27: followed by _imperative_, ἢν εἰρήνης δοκῆτε δεῖσθαι, ἄνευ ὅπλων ἥκετε [prev. work] 3.2.13, compare 5.4.30.}}
**B.II.2** when the apodosis is present, denoting customary or repeated action, to express a general condition, **if ever**, ἤν ποτε δασμὸς ἵκηται, σοὶ τὸ γέρας πολὺ μεῖζον (i.e. ἐστί) **whenever** a division comes, your prize is (always) greater, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.166}}; ἢν ἐγγὺς ἔλθῃ θάνατος, οὐδεὶς βούλεται θνῄσκειν **if** death come near, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 671}}; with ἄν omitted, εἴ περ γάρ τε χόλον.. καταπέψῃ ἀλλά.. ἔχει κότον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.81.}}
**B.II.2.b** with Rhet. present in apodosis, ἐὰν μὴ οἱ φιλόσοφοι βασιλεύσωσιν, οὐκ ἔστι κακῶν παῦλα there is (i.e. can be, will be) no rest.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 473d.}}
**B.III** with OPTATIVE (never with ἄν in early Gr., later ἐάν with _optative_, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Damascius Philosophus “de Principiis” 114, +others)}}
**B.III.1** to express a future condition less definitely than ἐάν with _subjunctive_, usually with _optative_ with ἄν in _apodosis_, ἦ κεν γηθήσαι Πρίαμος Πριάμοιό τε παῖδες.. εἰ σφῶιν τάδε πάντα πυθοίατο μαρναμένοιιν surely they would exult, **if** they should hear.., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.255, compare 7.28, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.223; εἴης φορητὸς οὐκ ἄν, εἰ πράσσοις καλῶς 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 979; οὐδὲ γὰρ ἄν με ἐπαινοίη, εἰ ἐξελαύνοιμι τοὺς εὐεργέτας 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.7.11; οἶκος δ᾽ αὐτός, εἰ φθογγὴν λάβοι, σαφέστατ᾽ ἂν λέξειεν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 37, etc.}}: _future_ _optative_ is _falsa lectio_ in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 164a}}: with _present_ _indicative_ in _apodosis_, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Xenophanes Poeta Philosophus 34.3, 3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 253}}: with _future_ _indicative_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Melissus Philosophus 5.}}
**B.III.1.b** in Hom.sometimes with _present_ _optative_, to express an unfulfilled present condition, εἰ μὲν νῦν ἐπὶ ἄλλῳ ἀεθλεύοιμεν, ἦ τ᾽ ἂν ἐγὼ τὰ πρῶτα φεροίμην **if** we were now contending, etc., [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.274: rarely in Trag., εἰ μὴ κνίζοι (={εἰ μὴ ἔκνιζε}) 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 568; also εἰ ἀναγκαῖον εἴη ἀδικεῖν ἢ ἀδικεῖσθαι, ἑλοίμην ἂν μᾶλλον ἀδικεῖσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 469c.}}]
**B.III.2** when the apodosis is past, denoting customary or repeated action, to express a general condition in past time (corresponding to use of _subjunctive_ in present time, above {{Refs|11.2)}}; once in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, εἴ τίς με.. ἐνίπτοι, ἀλλὰ σὺ τόν γ᾽.. κατέρυκες 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.768}}; εἰ δέ τινας θορυβουμένους αἴσθοιτο.., κατασβεννύναι τὴν ταραχὴν ἐπειρᾶτο **if** he should see (**whenever** he saw) any troops in confusion, he (always) tried, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.3.55, compare “An.” 4.5.13, “Mem.” 4.2.40}}; εἴ τις ἀντείποι, εὐθὺς ἐτεθνήκει **if** any one made objection, he was a dead man at once, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.66; ἀλλ᾽ εἴ τι μὴ φέροιμεν, ὤτρυνεν φέρειν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 755. For εἰ with _indicative_ in this sense see above 1.1}}: _indicative_ and _optative_ are found in same sentence, ἐμίσει, οὐκ εἴ τις κακῶς πάσχων ἠμύνετο, ἀλλ᾽ εἴ τις εὐεργετούμενος ἀχάριστος φαίνοιτο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Agesilaus” 11.3.}}
**B.III.3** in oratio obliqua after past tenses, representing ἐάν with _subjunctive_ or εἰ with a primary (never an historical) tense of the _indicative_ in oratio recta, ἐλογίζοντο ὡς, εἰ μὴ μάχοιντο, ἀποστήσοιντο αἱ πόλεις (representing ἐὰν μὴ μαχώμεθα, ἀποστήσονται) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 6.4.6, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.104, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 5.2.2}}; ἔλεγεν ὅτι, εἰ βλαβερὰ πεπραχὼς εἴη, δίκαιος εἴη ζημιοῦσθαι (representing εἰ βλαβερὰ πέπραχε, δίκαιός ἐστι){{Refs|[prev. work] 32, compare “An.” 6.6.25}}; εἰ δέ τινα φεύγοντα λήψοιτο, προηγόρευεν ὅτι ὡς πολεμίῳ χρήσοιτο (representing εἴ τινα λήψομαι, χρήσομαι) {{Refs|[prev. author] “Cyr.” 3.1.3}}; also, where oratio obliqua is implied in the leading clause, οὐκ ἦν τοῦ πολέμου πέρας Φιλίππῳ, εἰ μὴ Θηβαίους.. ἐχθροὺς ποιήσειε τῇ πόλει, i.e. Philip thought there would be no end to the war, **un**less he should make.. (his thought having been ἐὰν μὴ ποιήσω), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.145; ἐβούλοντο γὰρ σφίσιν, εἴ τινα λάβοιεν, ὑπάρχειν ἀντὶ τῶν ἔνδον, ἢν ἄρα τύχωσί τινες ἐζωγρημένοι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.5.}}
**B.III.4** with _optative_ with ἄν, only when the clause serves as apodosis as well as protasis,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 329b, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.18, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.5.3 (see. ἄν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus 111. d).}}
**B.IV** with _infinitive_, in oratio obliqua, only in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus, εἰ γὰρ δὴ δεῖν πάντως περιθεῖναι ἄλλῳ τέῳ τὴν βασιληΐην, [ἔφη] δικαιότερον εἶναι κτλ. 1.129; εἰ εἶναι τοῦτο μὴ φίλον 2.64, compare 172, 3.105, 108.}}
**B.V** after Verbs denoting **wonder, delight, indignation, disappointment, contentment**, and similar emotions, εἰ with _indicative_ is used instead of ὅτι, to express the object of the feeling in a hypothetical form, θαυμάζω εἰ μηδεὶς ὑμῶν μήτ᾽ ἐνθυμεῖται μήτ᾽ ὀργίζεται, ὁρῶν.. I wonder **that** no one of you is either concerned or angry when he sees.., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.43; οὐκ ἀγαπᾷ εἰ μὴ δίκην δέδωκεν, ἀλλ᾽ εἰ μὴ καὶ χρυσῷ στεφάνῳ στεφανωθήσεται ἀγανακτεῖ 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.147}}: after past tenses, ἐθαύμασε δ᾽ εἰ μὴ φανερόν ἐστιν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.1.13; δεινὸν εἰσῄει, εἰ μὴ.. δόξει 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.33; ἐθαύμαζον εἴ τι ἕξει τις χρήσασθαι τῷ λόγῳ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 95a; οὐδὲ ᾐσχύνθη εἰ.. ἐπάγει 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.105: in oratio obliqua (expressed or implied) with _optative_, ἐπεῖπεν ὡς δεινὸν (i.e. εἴη) εἰ.. μεγαλόψυχος γένοιτο 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.157; ᾤκτιρον εἰ ἁλώσοιντο 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.4.7}}; ἐθαύμαζε δ᾽ εἴ τις ἀρετὴν ἐπαγγελλόμενος ἀργύριον πράττοιτο he wondered **that** any one should demand money, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Mem.” 1.2.7}}; ἔχαιρον ἀγαπῶν εἴ τις ἐάσοι I rejoiced, being content **if** any one should let it pass, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 450a:}} —in this use the _negative_ οὐ is also found, ἀγανακτῶ εἰ ὁ Φίλιππος ἁρπάζων οὐ λυπεῖ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 8.55; δεινὸν ἂν εἴη εἰ οἱ ἐκείνων ξύμμαχοι οὐκ ἀπεροῦσιν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.121; τέρας λέγεις, εἰ οὐκ ἂν δύναιντο λαθεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 91d, etc.}}
**B.VI** in citing a fact as a ground of argument or appeal, **as surely as, since**, εἴ ποτ᾽ ἔην γε **if** there was [as there was], i.e. **as sure as** there was such an one, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.180, +others; εἰ τότε κοῦρος ἔα, νῦν αὖτέ με γῆρας ὀπάζει 4.321}}; πολλοὺς γὰρ οἶκε εἶναι εὐπετέστερον διαβάλλειν ἢ ἕνα, εἰ Κλεομένεα μὲν μοῦνον οὐκ οἷός τε ἐγένετο διαβαλεῖν, τρεῖς δὲ μυριάδας Ἀθηναίων ἐποίησε τοῦτο it seems easier to deceive many than one, **if** (as was the fact, i.e. **since**) he was not able.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.97, compare 1.60, +others}}
**B.VII** ELLIPTICAL CONSTRUCTIONS:
**B.VII.1** with apodosis implied in the context, εἰ having the force of **in case, supposing that**, πρὸς τὴν πόλιν, εἰ ἐπιβοηθοῖεν, ἐχώρουν they marched towards the city [so as to meet the citizens], **in case** they should rush out, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.100}}; ἱκέται πρὸς σὲ δεῦρ᾽ ἀφίγμεθα, εἴ τινα πόλιν φράσειας ἡμῖν εὔερον we have come hither to you, **in case** you should tell us of some fleecy city (i.e. that we might hear of it), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 120}}; παρέζεο καὶ λαβὲ γούνων, αἴ κέν πως ἐθέλῃσιν ἐπὶ Τρώεσσιν ἀρῆξαι sit by him and grasp his knees [so as to persuade him], **in case** he be willing to help the Trojans, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.408, compare 66, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.94, 3.92}}; ἄκουσον καὶ ἐμοῦ, ἐάν σοι ἔτι ταὐτὰ δοκῇ hear me also [that you may assent], **in case** the same opinion please you, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 358b}}; ἰδὲ δή, ἐάν σοι ὅπερ ἐμοὶ συνδοκῇ look now, **in case** you approve what I do, {{Refs|[prev. work] 434a.}}
**B.VII.2** with apodosis suppressed for rhetorical reasons, εἴ περ γάρ κ᾽ ἐθέλῃσιν Ὀλύμπιος.. στυφελίξαι **if** he wish to thrust him away, [he will do so], {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.580}}; εἰ μὲν δώσουσι γέρας—· εἰ δέ κε μὴ δώωσιν, ἐγὼ δέ κεν αὐτὸς ἕλωμαι **if** they shall give me a prize, [well and good]; but **if** they give not, then I will take one for myself, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1.135, compare 6.150, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 468}}; καὶ ἢν μὲν ξυμβῇ ἡ πεῖρα—· εἰ δὲ μή.. and **if** the attempt succeed, [well]; otherwise.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.3, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 325d.}}
**B.VII.3** with the Verb of the protasis omitted, chiefly in the following expressions:
**B.VII.3.a** εἰ μή **except**, οὐδὲν ἄλλο σιτέονται, εἰ μὴ ἰχθῦς μοῦνον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.200}}; μὰ τὼ θεώ, εἰ μὴ Κρίτυλλά γ᾽ [εἰμί]—nay, **if** I'm not Critylla! i.e. I am, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 898}}; εἰ μὴ ὅσον **except** only, ἐγὼ μέν μιν οὐκ εἶδον, εἰ μὴ ὅσον γραφῇ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.73, compare 1.45, 2.20; εἰ μὴ εἰ 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.17, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 480b, etc.}}; εἰ μή τι οὖν, ἀλλὰ σμικρόν γέ μοι τῆς ἀρχῆς χάλασον **if** nothing else, yet.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Men.” 86e; ironical, εἰ μὴ ἄρα ἡ τῆς ἀρετῆς ἐπιμέλεια διαφθορά ἐστιν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.2.8; εἰ μή πέρ γε τὸν ὑοσκύαμον χρήματα εἶναι φήσομεν [prev. author] “Oec.” 1.13.}}
**B.VII.3.b** εἰ δὲ μή but **if** not, i.e. **otherwise**, προηγόρευε τοῖς Λαμψακηνοῖσι μετιέναι Μιλτιάδεα, εἰ δὲ μή, σφέας πίτυος τρόπον ἀπείλεε ἐκτρίψειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.37, compare 56}}; after μάλιστα μέν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.32, 35, etc.:}} —after a preceding _negative_, μὴ τύπτ᾽· εἰ δὲ μή, σαυτόν ποτ᾽ αἰτιάσει don't beat me; **otherwise**, you will have yourself to blame, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1433; ὦ Κῦρε, μὴ οὕτω λέγε· εἰ δὲ μή, οὐ θαρροῦντά με ἕξεις 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.1.35; οὔτ᾽ ἐν τῷ ὕδατι τὰ ὅπλα ἦν ἔχειν· εἰ δὲ μή [prev. author] “An.” 4.3.6, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.28, 131, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 91c.}}
**B.VII.3.c** εἰ δέ sometimes stands for εἰ δὲ μή, εἰ μὲν βούλεται, ἑψέτω· εἰ δ᾽, ὅτι βούλεται, τοῦτο ποιείτω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 285c, compare “Smp.” 212c; εἰ δ᾽ οὖν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 722; εἰ δ᾽ οὕτως 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1094a24}}; εἰ δὲ τοῦτο and **if** so, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 2.1.29.}}
**B.VII.3.d** εἰ γάρ for **if** so, {{Refs|[prev. author] 7.3.6.}}
**B.VII.3.e** εἴ τις **if** any, i. e. **as much as** or **more than** any, τῶν γε νῦν αἴ τις ἐπιχθονίων, ὀρθῶς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 5.5; ὄτλον ἄλγιστον ἔσχον, εἴ τις Αἰτωλὶς γυνή 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 8, compare “OC” 734}}; εἴ τις ἄλλος, **siquis alius**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 6, etc.; εἴ τινες καὶ ἄλλοι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.2, etc.; εἴπερ τις ἄλλος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 501d}}; also κατ᾽ εἰ δέ τινα τρόπον in **any** way, {{Refs|“IG” 5(2).6.27 (from Tegea).}}
**B.VII.3.f** εἴ ποτε or εἴπερ ποτέ **now if** ever, ἡμῖν δὲ καλῶς, εἴπερ ποτέ, ἔχει.. ἡ ξυναλλαγή {{Refs 7th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.20, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 594; αἴ ποτα κἄλλοτα 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus “Supp.” 7.11, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 6.4.12, etc.}}; but in prayers, εἴ ποτέ τοι ἐπὶ νηὸν ἔρεψα.. τόδε μοι κρήηνον ἐέλδωρ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.39.}}
**B.VII.3.g** εἴ ποθεν (i.e. δυνατόν ἐστι) **if** from any quarter, i.e. from some quarter or other, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1204 (Lyric poetry)}}; so εἴ ποθι somewhere, anywhere, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Aj.” 885 (Lyric poetry); εἴ που 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.193.}}
**B.VII.3.h** εἴ πως{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 388, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.3.11}}: in an elliptical sentence (cf. VII. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1), πρέσβεις πέμψαντες, εἴ πως πείσειαν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.58.}}
**B.VIII** with other PARTICLES:
**B.VIII.1** for the distinction between καὶ εἰ (or καὶ ἐάν, or κἄν) even **if**, and εἰ καί (or ἐὰν καί) even **though**, see at {καί}:—the opposite of καὶ εἰ is οὐδ᾽ εἰ, not even **if**; that of εἰ καί is εἰ μηδέ, **if (although**) not even.
**B.VIII.2** for ὡς εἰ, ὡς εἴ τε, ὥσπερ εἰ, etc., see at {ὡς} and ὥσπερ.
**B.VIII.3** for εἰ ἄρα, see at {ἄρα}; for εἰ δή, εἴπερ, see at {εἰ δή, εἴπερ}; for εἴ γε, see at {γέ}.
**B.IX** in _negative_ oaths, = Hebrew **im**, {{LXX+NT|LXX.Psa.94(95).11, NT.Mark.8.12, +others}}
**C** IN INDIRECT QUESTIONS, **whether**, followed by the _indicative_, _subjunctive_, or _optative_, according to the principles of oratio obliqua:
**C.1** with IN{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator after primary tenses, representing the same tense in the direct question, σάφα δ᾽ οὐκ οἶδ᾽ εἰ θεός ἐστιν}} **whether** he is a god, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.183; εἰ ξυμπονήσεις.. σκόπει 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 41.}}
**C.2** with SUB{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus after primary tenses, representing a dubitative}}_subjunctive_ in the direct question, τὰ ἐκπώματα οὐκ οἶδ᾽ εἰ Χρυσάντᾳ τουτῳῒ δῶ **whether** I should give them, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.4.16: sometimes elliptical, ἐς τὰ χρηστήρια ἔπεμπε, εἰ στρατεύηται ἐπὶ τοὺς Πέρσας 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.75.}}
**C.3** OPT. after past tenses, representing either of the two previous constructions in the direct question, ἤρετο εἴ τις ἐμοῦ εἴη σοφώτερος he asked **whether** any one was wiser than I (direct ἔστι τις σοφώτερο;), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 21a; ἐπεκηρυκεύετο Πεισιστράτῳ, εἰ βούλοιτό οἱ τὴν θυγατέρα ἔχειν γυναῖκα 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.60: rarely}}_aorist_ _optative_ for the _aorist_ _indicative_, ἠρώτων αὐτὸν εἰ ἀναπλεύσειεν I asked him **whether** he had set sail (direct ἀνέπλευσα;), {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 50.55: but}}_aorist_ _optative_ usually represents _aorist_ _subjunctive_, τὸν θεὸν ἐπήροντο εἰ παραδοῖεν Κορινθίοις τὴν πόλιν.. καὶ τιμωρίαν τινὰ πειρῷντ᾽ ἀπ᾽ αὐτῶν ποιεῖσθαι they asked **whether** they should deliver their city to the Corinthians, and should try.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.25:}} —in both constructions the _indicative_ or _subjunctive_ may be retained, ψῆφον ἐβούλοντο ἐπαγαγεῖν εἰ χρὴ πολεμεῖν{{Refs|[prev. work] 119}}; ἐβουλεύοντο εἴτε κατακαύσωσιν.. εἴτε τι ἄλλο χρήσωνται **whether** they should burn them or should dispose of them in some other way, {{Refs|[prev. author] 2.4}}; ἀνακοινοῦσθαι αὐτὸν αὑτῷ εἰ δῷ ἐπιψηφίσαι τοῖς προέδροις [he said that] he consulted him **whether** he should give.., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.68.}}
**C.4** with OPT. and ἄν when this was the form of the direct question, ἠρώτων εἰ δοῖεν ἂν τούτων τὰ πιστά they asked **whether** they would give (direct δοιήτε ἄ;), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.8.7.}}
**C.5** the NEG. used with εἰ in indirect questions is οὐ, when οὐ would be used in the direct question, ἐνετέλλετο.. εἰρωτᾶν εἰ οὔ τι ἐπαισχύνεται **whether** he is not ashamed, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.90, etc.}}; but if μή would be required in the direct form, it is retained in the indirect, οὐ τοῦτο ἐρωτῶ, ἀλλ᾽ εἰ τοῦ μὲν δικαίου μὴ ἀξιοῖ πλέον ἔχειν μηδὲ βούλεται ὁ δίκαιος, τοῦ δὲ ἀδίκου (the direct question would be μὴ ἀξιοῖ μηδὲ βούλετα; he does not see fit nor wish, does he?) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 349b}}:—in double indirect questions, εἴτε.. εἴτε..; εἰ.. εἴτε..; εἴτε.. ἢ.., either οὐ or μή can be used in the second clause, ὅπως ἴδῃς εἴτ᾽ ἔνδον εἴτ᾽ οὐκ ἔνδον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 7; σκοπῶμεν εἰ ἡμῖν πρέπει ἢ οὔ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 451d}}; εἰ ἀληθὲς ἢ μή, πειράσομαι μαθεῖν{{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. work] 339a; πολλὰ ἂν περιεσκέψω, εἴτε ἐπιτρεπτέον εἴτε οὔ·.. οὐδένα λόγον οὐδὲ συμβουλὴν ποιῇ, εἴτε χρὴ ἐπιτρέπειν σαυτὸν αὐτῷ εἴτε μή [prev. author] “Prt.” 313a, 313b; ἀνάγκη τὴν ἐμὴν μητέρα, εἴτε θυγάτηρ ἦν Κίρωνος εἴτε μή, καὶ εἰ παρ᾽ ἐκείνῳ διῃτᾶτο ἢ οὔ, καὶ γάμους εἰ διττοὺς ὑπὲρ ταύτης εἱστίασεν ἢ μὴ.. πάντα ταῦτα εἰδέναι τοὺς οἰκέτας 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 8.9}}; τοὺς νόμους καταμανθάνειν εἰ καλῶς κεῖνται ἢ μή.. τοὺς λόγους εἰ ὀρθῶς ὑμᾶς διδάσκουσιν ἢ οὔ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.14.}}

1514:
** εἰρην-εύω**,
**bring to peace, reconcile**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 77.12, gloss on 2nd c.AD: Babrius Fabularum Scriptor 39.4.}}
**II** _intransitive_, **keep peace, live peaceably**, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 180b; πρός τινα 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 21.16; μετὰ πάντων NT.Rom.12.18}}:—_middle_, πρὸς τοὺς κρείττους εἰρηνεύεσθαι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1359b39, compare “OGI” 199.1 (from Adule)}}; χώρα -ομένη ἐκ παλαιοῦ {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 5.8.7.}}

1515:
** εἰρήν-η**
(see. below), ἡ, **peace**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.486, etc.}}; ἐπ᾽ εἰρήνης in **time of peace**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.797; ἔθηκε πᾶσιν εἰ. φίλοις 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 769}}; εἰ. τἀκεῖθεν τέκνοις on that side they have **peace**, have naught to fear, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 1004}}; εἰ. γίγνεται **peace** is made, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.74}}: hence later, **a peace, treaty of peace**, ἡ βασιλέως εἰ. {{Refs|“IG” 22.103.24, etc.}}; εἰ. ποιεῖν 'Αρμενίοις καὶ Χαλδαίοις make **peace** between.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.2.12; εἰ. ποιεῖσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 3.8, 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.77}}; εἰ. κατεργάζεσθαι, πράττειν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 3.8, 17; διαπράξασθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 6.3.4; εἰρήνης δεῖσθαι [prev. work] 2.2.13}}; εἰρήνην δέχεσθαι to accept **it**,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 22; λαβεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 3.7}}; εἰ. ἄγειν keep **peace**, be at **peace**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 386, etc.; πρὸς ἀλλήλους 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 465b}}; εἰ. ἄγειν (variant{ἔχειν}) enjoy **peace**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.6.6}}; λύειν break **it**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.71}}; πολλὴ εἰ. τινὸς γίγνεται profound **peace**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 329c}}; ἐν εἰρήνῃ λέγειν, τὸν βίον διάγειν, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Smp.” 189b, “R.” 372d}}; πόλεμον εἰρήνης χάριν [αἱρεῖσθαι] {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1333a35}}; εἰρήνης ἄρξας, ={εἰρηναρχήσας}, {{Refs|“IGRom.” 3.784, compare 452.}}
**II** **the goddess of peace**, daughter of Zeus and Themis, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 902, compare 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 13.7, 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus “fragment” 3.1, “IG” 3.170, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Cimon” 13, etc.}}
**III** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Pythagoras Philosophus}} name for **three,** {{Refs|Theol.Ar. 16}}; for **six**, {{Refs|[prev. work]37.}}
**IV** Hebraism in {{Refs|LXX, ἐρωτῆσαί τινα εἰς εἰρήνην}} **greet** a person, inquire after their health, {{LXX|LXX.Judg.18.15, LXX.1Ki.17.22}}; ἐρ. τινὰ τὰ εἰς εἰ{{LXX|. [prev. work] 10.4; so ἐπερωτᾶν εἰς εἰ. τοῦ πολέμου LXX.2Ki.11.7}}; in salutations, εἰ. σο; {{NT+4th c.BC+|“4 Ki.” 4.26, cf. NT.Luke.24.36, +others; εἰ. ἡ εἴσοδός σου “3 Ki.” 2.13. (ϝειράνα “IG” 5(1).1509 (Sparta, 4th c.BC, uncertain); ἰράνα [prev. work]4.917 (Epid.), 12(3).29.12 (from Telos)}}; compare _Boeotian dialect_ πολέμω καἰράνας {{Refs|[prev. work]7.2407, but Cretan dialect πολέμω χ[ἰ]ρήνας “GDI” 5018.5}}; εἰρήνα Pi.[same place], B.[same place], {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“SIG” 241.80 (Delph., 4th c.BC), later εἰράνα “IG” 5(1).935.14 (2nd c.BC).)}}

1516:
** εἰρην-ικός**, ή, όν,
**of** or **for peace**, λόγων -ώτατος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 5.3; χρεία 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1254b32}}; θυσίαι **peace** offerings, {{LXX+2nd c.AD+|LXX.1Ki.11.15, +others; ἄγγελοι καὶ δαίμονες 2nd-3rd c.AD: Hermes Trismegistus cited in 5th c.AD: Stobaeus, Joannes 1.49.45.}}
**2** **of** or **in peace, peaceful**, βίος, πρᾶξις, etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 829a, “R.” 399b, etc.; ἐπιστῆμαι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 1.17}}: _superlative_, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.634.}} _adverb_ -κῶς **peaceably**, opposed to πολεμικῶς, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 5.46, 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ τοῦ καθ᾽ Ὅμηρον ἀγαθοῦ βασιλέως” p.45}} O., etc.: _comparative_ -ώτερον {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Fug.” 5.}}
**3** **peaceable**, of persons, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 2.24.}}

1518:
** εἰρηνο-ποιός**, ὁ,
**peace-maker**, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 6.3.4, NT.Matt.5.9, 1st c.AD: Cornutus Philosophus “de Natura Deorum” 23, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Nicias” 11.}}
**II** _plural_, = Latin {{Refs|“Fetiales”, [prev. author] 2.279b.}}

1519:
ἐν** εἰς**
or **ἐς**, PREP. WITH ACC. ONLY:—both forms are found in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}}, _Ionic dialect_ poets, and early metrical Inscrr.; ἐς is best attested in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus and 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus}}, and is found in nearly all early _Ionic dialect_ Inscrr. (except {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“IG” 12(8).262.16 (Thasos, 5th c.BC), [prev. work]7.235.1 (Oropus, 4th c.BC)); εἰς in _Attic dialect_ Inscrr. from 4th c.BC, “IG” 2.115, etc.}}; and usually in _Attic dialect_ Prose (except {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus) and Comedy texts (except in parody): Trag. apparently prefer εἰς, but ἐς}} is used before vowels for the sake of meter; ἐς was retained in the phrases ἐς κόρακας (whence the Verb σκορακίζω), ἐς μακαρίαν. _Aeolic dialect_ poets have εἰς before vowels, ἐς before consonants, and this is given as the rule in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus by “Anecdota Graeca - MSS. Bibl. Oxon.” 1.172, compare 2nd c.AD(?): Helladius Epigrammaticus cited in 9th c.AD: Photius Legal icographus, etc. “Bibliotheca - Patrologia Graeca” p.533B. (Orig. ἐνς, as in “IG” 4.554.7 (from Argos), “GDI” 4986.11 (from Crete)}}; compare ἐν, ἰν. The diphthong is genuine in _Aeolic dialect_ εἰς, but spurious in _Attic dialect_-_Ionic dialect_) Radical sense **into**, and then more loosely, **to**:
**I** OF PLACE, the oldest and commonest usage, εἰς ἅλα **into** or **to** the sea, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.141, +others; εἰς ἅλαδε 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.351; ἔς ῥ᾽ ἀσαμίνθους 4.48; ἐς οἶνον βάλε φάρμακον [prev. work] 220}}; frequently of places, **to**, εἰς Εὔβοιαν {{Refs|3.174}}; ἐς Αἴγυπτον, etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.5, etc.}}; ἐς Μίλητον **into the territory of** Miletus,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 14; εἰς Ἑλλήσποντον εἰσέπλει 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 1.1.2; ἀφίκετο εἰς Μήδους πρὸς Κυαξάρην [prev. author] “Cyr.” 2.1.2}}; εἰς ἅρματα βαίνειν to step **into**.., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.115; εἰς ἐλάτην ἀναβῆναι 14.287}}; opposed to ἐκ, in such phrases as ἐς σφυρὸν ἐκ πτέρνης, ἐς πόδας ἐκ κεφαλῆς, from heel **to** ankle-joint, from head **to** foot, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|22.397, 23.169; ἐκ πάτου ἐς σκοπιήν 20.137; ἐς μυχὸν ἐξ οὐδοῦ 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 7.87}}; κἠς ἔτος ἐξ ἔτεος from year **to** year, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 18.15}}: with Verbs implying motion or direction, as of looking, ἰδεῖν εἰς οὐρανόν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.364}}; εἰς ὦπα ἰδέσθαι to look **in** the face, {{Refs|9.373}}, etc.; εἰς ὦπα ἔοικεν he is like **in** face (i.e. ἰδόντι), {{Refs|3.158}}, etc.; ἐς ὀφθαλμούς τινος ἐλθεῖν to come **before** another's eyes,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|24.204; ἐς ὄψιν ἀπικνέεσθαί τινος 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.136; καλέσαι τινὰ ἐς ὄψιν [prev. author] 5.106, etc.}}; ἐς ταὐτὸν ἥκειν come **to** the same point, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 273}}: less frequently after a _substantive_, ὁδὸς ἐς λαύρην {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.128}}; τὸ ἐς Παλλήνην τεῖχος **facing** Pallene, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.56; ξύνοδος ἐς τὴν Δῆλον [prev. author] 3.104, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 173d.}}
**I.b** _Epic dialect_ and _Ionic dialect_, also with _accusative_ _person_ (_Attic dialect_ ὡς, πρός, παρά), {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.312, 15.402, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.127, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.147}}; also in _Attic dialect_ with collective Nouns, ἐς τὸν δῆμον παρελθόντες {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.45, or plurals, εἰς ὑμᾶς εἰσῆλθον 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.103}}; especially of consulting an oracle, ἐς θεὸν ἐλθεῖν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 7.31; εἰς Ἄμμων᾽ ἐλθόντες 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 619.}}
**I.2** with Verbs expressing **restin** a place, when a previous motion **into** or **to** it is implied, ἐς μέγαρον κατέθηκεν ἐπὶ θρόνου he put it **in** the house (i.e. he brought it **into** the house, and put it **there**), {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 20.96}}; ἐς θρόνους ἕζοντο they sat them down **upon** the seats, {{Refs|4.51, compare 1.130}}; ἐφάνη λὶς εἰς ὁδόν the lion appeared **in** the path, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.276; ἀπόστολος ἐς τὴν Μίλητον ἦν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.21 (assuming variant); αὐτὸς ἐς Αακεδαίμονα ἀπόστολος ἐγίνετο [prev. author] 5.38; ἐς κώμην παραγίνονται [prev. author] 1.185; παρῆν ἐς Σάρδις [prev. author] 6.1; ἐς δόμους μένειν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 80 (codex Laur.); ἐς τὴν νῆσον κατέκλῃσε 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.109, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.13}}; ἀπόβασιν ποιήσασθαι ἐς.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.33, etc.}}; later used like{ἐν}, τὴν γῆν εἰς ἣν ὑμεῖς κατοικεῖτε [{{LXX+1st c.BC+|LXX.Num.35.34; τὸ χρυσίον ὃ εἰλήφεσαν εἰς Ῥώμην 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 14.117; οἰκεῖν εἰς τὰ Ὕπατα 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Asin.” 1; εἰς Ἐκβάτανα ἀποθανεῖν 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Varia Historia” 7.8; εἰς ἅπασαν τὴν γῆν 10th c.AD: Suidas Legal icographus see {Καλλίμαχος}}}]: generally, τοὔνομα εἰς τὴν Ἑλλάδα, φασίν, Ἱππομιγὴς δύναται {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Varia Historia” 9.16.}}
**I.3** with Verbs of saying or speaking, εἰς relates to the persons **to** or **before** whom one speaks, εἰπεῖν ἐς πάντας, ἐς πάντας αὔδα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.26, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 93; λέγειν εἰς τὸ μέσον τῶν ταξιάρχων 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.3.7; αἱ ἐς τὸ φανερὸν λεγόμεναι αἰτίαι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.23}}: with other Verbs, εἰς τοὺς Ἕλληνας σαυτὸν σοφιστὴν παρέχων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 312a; καλὸν ἐς τοὺς Ἕλληνας τὸ ἀγώνισμα φανεῖσθαι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.56}}; ἐπαχθὴς ἦν ἐς τοὺς πολλούς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 6.54; στρατιὰν ἐπαγγέλλων ἐς τοὺς ξυμμάχους [prev. author] 7.17; διαβεβλῆσθαι εἴς τινα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 539c.}}
**I.4** elliptical usages,
**I.4.a** after Verbs which have no sense of motion to or into a place, τὴν πόλιν ἐξέλιπον εἰς χωρίον ὀχυρόν they quitted the city **for** a strong position, i.e. to seek a strong position, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.2.24}}; γράμματα ἑάλωσαν εἰς Ἀθήνας letters were captured [and sent] **to** Athens, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “HG” 1.1.23, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 468a; ἀνίστασθαι ἐς Ἄργος 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 59, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 116a.}}
**I.4.b** participles signifying motion are frequently omitted with εἰς, τοῖς στρατηγοῖς τοῖς εἰς Σικελίαν (i.e. ἀποδειχθεῖσιν) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.11, etc.}}
**I.4.c** with _genitive_, mostly of proper names, as εἰς Ἀΐδαο, _Attic dialect_ εἰς Ἅιδου [δόμους], {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.48}}; ἐς Ἀθηναίης [ἱερόν] **to** the temple of Athena, {{Refs|6.379; ἐς Πριάμοιο [οἶκον] 24.160, compare 309}}; εἰς Αἰγύπτοιο [ῥόον] {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.581; ἐς τοῦ Κλεομένεος 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.51; εἰς Ἀσκληπιοῦ 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 411; ἐπὶ δεῖπνον [ἰέναι] εἰς Ἀγάθωνος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 174a: with Appellatives, ἀνδρὸς ἐς ἀφνειοῦ}} **to** a rich man's house, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.482; ἐς πατρός 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.195}}; πέμπειν εἰς διδασκάλων send **to** school, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Respublica Lacedaemoniorum” 2.1; εἰς δ. φοιτᾶν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 326c}}; ἐς σεωυτοῦ, ἑωυτοῦ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.108, 9.108, etc.}}
**II** OF TIME,
**II.1** to denote a certain point or limit of time, **up to, until**, ἐς ἠῶ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.375}}; ἐς ἠέλιον καταδύντα **till** sunset, {{Refs|9.161 (but also}}**towards** or **near** sunset,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|3.138); ἐκ νεότητος ἐς γῆρας 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.86; ἐκ παιδὸς ἐς γῆρας 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.180}}; ἐς ἐμέ **up to** my time, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.92, +others: with _adverbs_, εἰς ὅτε (compare ἔς τε)}} **against** the time when.., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.99}}; εἰς πότ; **until** when ? how long ? {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1185 (Lyric poetry, compare εἰσόκ; εἰς ὁπότε 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.99}}; ἐς τ; ={εἰς πότε}; {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.465}}; ἐς ὅ **until**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.93, etc.; ἐς οὗ [prev. author] 1.67, 3.31, etc.; ἐς τόδε [prev. author] 7.29, etc.}}
**II.2** to determine a period, εἰς ἐνιαυτόν **for** a year, i.e. a whole year, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.32, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.526}}; **within** the year,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|[prev. work] 86 (compare ἐς ἐνίαυτον 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus “Supp.” 8.12); εἰς ὥρας 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.135}}; ἐς θέρος ἢ ἐς ὀπώρην **for** the summer, i.e. **throughout** it, {{Refs|14.384}}; ἡ εἰς ἐνιαυτὸν κειμένη δαπάνη εἰς τὸν μῆνα δαπανᾶται the expenditure **for** a year is expended **in** the month, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 7.36; μισθοδοτεῖν τινὰς εἰς ἓξ μῆνας 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 19.15; χοίνικα κριθῶν εἰς τέσσαρας ἡμέρας διεμέτρει 2nd-1st c.BC: Posidonius Historicus 36J.}}; εἰς ἑσπέραν ἥκειν to come **at** even, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 998}}; εἰς τρίτην ἡμέραν or εἰς τρίτην alone, **on** the third day, **in** two days, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Hp.Ma.” 286b, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.3.27; ἥκειν ἐς τὴν ὑστεραίαν [prev. author] “An.” 2.3.25; ἥκειν εἰς τὸ ἔαρ “Hellenica Oxyrhynchia” 17.4}}; ἐς τέλος **at** last, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.40}}; ἐς καιρόν **in** season, {{Refs|[prev. author] 4.139}}; οὐκ ἐς ἀναβολάς, ἀμβολάς, **with** no delay, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 8.21, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 270, etc.}}; ἐς τότε **at** this time, variant in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 7.317 (but εἰς τότε}} **at** that time (in the _future_), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 14.24, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 830b)}}; ἐς ὕστερον or τὸ ὕστερον, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.126, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.20: with _adverbs_, ἐς αὔριον 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.538, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 858b; ἔς περ ὀπίσσω 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 20.199; ἐς αὖθις 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.63 (see. εἰσαῦθις}} (; ἐς αὐτίκα μάλ᾽ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 367}}; εἰς ἔπειτα (see. εἰσέπειτα (; ἐς τὸ ἔ., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.64; ἐς ὀψέ [prev. author] 8.23}}; εἰς ἅπαξ, see at {εἰσάπα; εἰς ἔτι}, see at {εἰσέτι}.
**III** to express MEASURE OR LIMIT, without reference to Time, ἐς δίσκουρα λέλειπτο was left behind **as far as** a quoit's throw, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.523}}; ἐς δραχμὴν διέδωκε paid them **as much as** a drachma, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.29; ἱματισμὸν ζητῆσαι εἰς δύο τάλαντα 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Characteres” 23.8}}; so ἐς τὰ μάλιστα **to** the greatest degree, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.20, etc.; ἐς τοσοῦτο τύχης ἀπίκευ [prev. author] 1.124; εἰς τοσοῦτο ἥκειν 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 27.10; εἰς τοῦτο θράσους καὶ ἀναιδείας ἀφίκετο 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.194}}; ἐς ὅ ἐμέμνηντο **so far as** they remembered, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.66; ἐς τὸ ἔσχατον 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.229, etc.; εἰς ἅλις 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 25.17.}}
**III.2** frequently with Numerals, ἐς τριακάδας δέκα ναῶν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 339}}; ναῦς ἐς τὰς τετρακοσίας, διακοσίας, **to the number of** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|400, etc., 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.74, 100, etc.}}; εἰς ἕνα, εἰς δύο, εἰς τέσσαρας, one, two, four **deep**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.3.21}}; but εἰς τέσσαρας four **abreast**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeneas Tacticus 40.6: with _adverbs_, ἐς τρίς or ἐστρίς}} **thrice**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 2.68, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.86}}; of round numbers, **about**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.1.10.}}
**III.3** distributive, εἰς φυλάς **by** tribes, {{LXX|LXX.1Ki.10.21, cf. LXX.2Ki.18.}}
**III.4**
**IV** to express RELATION, **towards, in regard to**, ἐξαμαρτεῖν εἰς θεούς {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 945, etc.}}; ἁμάρτημα εἴς τινα, αἰτίαι ἐς ἀλλήλους, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 8.96, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.66; ὄνειδος ὀνειδίζειν εἴς τινα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 522; ἔχθρη ἔστινα 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.65; φιλία ἐς ἀμφοτέρους 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.9}}; λέγειν ἐς.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.86; γνώμη ἀποδεχθεῖσα ἐς τὴν γέφυραν [prev. author] 4.98; ἡ ἐς γῆν καὶ θάλασσαν ἀρχή 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.46.}}
**IV.b** of the subject of a work, especially in titles, e.g. τὰ ἐς Ἀπολλώνιον {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “VA”}}; of the object of a dedication, as in titles of hymns, ἐπινίκια, etc.
**IV.2** **in regard to**, πρῶτος εἰς εὐψυχίαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 326; σκώπτειν ἐς τὰ ῥάκια 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 740, compare “Eq.” 90; διαβάλλειν τινὰ ἔς τι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.88; αἰτία ἐπιφερομένη ἐς μαλακίαν [prev. author] 5.75; μέμφεσθαι εἰς φιλίαν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.6.30; εἰς τὰ πολεμικὰ καταφρονεῖσθαι [prev. author] “HG” 7.4.30; πόλεως εὐδοκιμωτάτης εἰς σοφίαν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 29d}}; **in respect of**, εὐτυχεῖν ἐς τέκνα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 542, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 35b, etc.; εἰς χρήματα ζημιοῦσθαι [prev. author] “Lg.” 774b, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 22.55}}; ἐς τὰ ἄλλα Th.I.I; εἰς ἄπαντα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 489; ἐς τὰ πάνθ᾽ ὁμῶς 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 736; εἰς μὲν ταῦτα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Lysis” 210a}}; τό γ᾽ εἰς ἑαυτόν, τὸ εἰς ἐμέ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 706, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 691, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ichn.” 346; ἐς ὀλίγους μᾶλλον τὰς ἀρχὰς ποιεῖν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.53; ἐς πλείονας οἰκεῖν [prev. author] 2.37}}; for τελεῖν ἐς Ἕλληνας, Βοιωτούς, ἄνδρας, etc., see at {τελέω}.
**IV.3** of Manner, ἐς τὸν νῦν τρόπον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 1.6; τίθεμεν τἆλλα εἰς τὸν αὐτὸν λόγο; 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 353d; ἐς ἓν μέλος 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 18.7: frequently periphrastic for _adverbs_, ἐς κοινὸν φράζειν, λέγειν, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 844, “Eu.” 408}}; ἐς τὸ πᾶν, ={πάντως}, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ag.” 682 (Lyric poetry)}}; ἐς τάχος, ={ταχέως}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 686}}; ἐς εὐτέλειαν, ={εὐτελῶς}, [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Av.” 805; ἐς τἀρχαῖον [prev. author] “Nu.” 593; εἰς καλόν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 78, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 76e; ἐς δέον γεγονέναι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.119, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1416, and see at {δέον}.}}]
**V** ofan end or limit, ἔρχεσθαι, τελευτᾶν, λήγειν ἐς.., to end in.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.120, 3.125, 4.39, etc.; ἐς ἑβδομήκοντα ἔτεα ου,ρον ἀνθρώπῳ προτίθημι [prev. author] 1.32}}; καταξαίνειν ἐς φοινικίδα to cut **into** red rags, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 320 (troch.); στρέφειν τι εἰς αἷμα NT.Rev.11.6}}; εἰς ἄνδρας ἐκ μειρακίων τελευτᾶν, εἰς ἄνδρα γενειᾶν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 173b, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 14.28; ἐκτρέφειν τὸ σπέρμα εἰς καρπόν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 17.10: so with εἶναι or γίγνομαι}} to form a predicate, ἔσται εἰς ἔθνη {{LXX|LXX.Gen.17.16; ἐγενήθη εἰς γυναῖκα [prev. work] 20.12}}; πιστὸς (i.e. ἦν) εἰς προφήτην {{LXX+NT|LXX.1Ki.3.20; ἐγένετο εἰς δένδρον NT.Luke.13.19, +others}}
**V.2** of Purpose or Object, εἰπεῖν εἰς ἀγαθόν, πείσεται εἰς ἀγαθόν, **for** good, **for** his good, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.102, 11.789; εἰς ἀγαθὰ μυθεῖσθαι 23.305; ἐς πόλεμον θωρήξομαι 8.376, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.29, etc.}}; ἐς φόβον **to cause** fear, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.310; ἐς ὑποδήματα δεδόσθαι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.98; κόσμος ὁ εἰς ἑορτάς 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 9.6; ἐπιτηδεότατος, εὐπρεπής, ἔς τι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.115, 2.116}}; εἰς κάλλος ζῆν to live **for** show, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.1.33, compare “Ages.” 9.1; ἐς δαίτην ἐκάλεσσε 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Aetia” 1.1.5; εἰς κέρδος τι δρᾶν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ph.” III; πάσας φωνὰς ἱέντων εἰς ἀπόφυξιν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 562; εἰς γράμματα παιδὶ δεκετεῖ ἐνιαυτοὶ τρεῖς 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 809e}}; εἰς τὸ πρᾶγμα εἶναι to be **pertinent, to the purpose,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 36.54}}; frequently of expenditure **on** an object, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“IG” 22.102.11, 116.41, +others; ἐς τὸ δέον 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 859, etc.; ἐς δᾷδα [prev. work] 612.}}
**B** POSITION: εἰς is sometimes parted from its _accusative_ by several words, εἰς ἀμφοτέρω Διομήδεος ἅρματα βήτην {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.115; εἰς δὲ μονάρχου δῆμος ἀϊδρίῃ δουλοσύνην ἔπεσεν 6th c.BC: Solon Lyricus 9: seldom (only in Poets)}} put after its case, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.59, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.137, 15.541, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 126 (Lyric poetry)}}: after an _adverb_, αὔριον ἔς· τῆμος δὲ.. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 7.318.}}

1520:
** εἷς**, μίᾰ, ἕν
(μίη only in late _Ionic dialect_ Prose): _genitive_ ἑνός, μιᾶς, ἑνός:—_Epic dialect_ **ἕεις** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 145, “Anthologia Graeca” 7.341 (5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus), conjecture in 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.603}}:—_Doric dialect_ **ἧς** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Rhinthon Comicus 12, “Tab.Heracl.” 1.136}}:—_Epic dialect_, _Aeolic dialect_, and _Ionic dialect_ _feminine_ ἴᾰ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.354, probably in 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ νούσων” 4.37; _accusative_ ἴαν 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 33.6 (probably), 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 69.1 (compare μηδεΐα), 6th c.BC: Corinna Lyrica “Supp.” 2.56, “IG” 9(2).517.22(Thess.); _genitive_ ἰῆς 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.173, 24.496; _dative_ ἰῇ 9.319, 11.174, etc.}}: _neuter_ _dative_ (ἰῷ κίον ἤματι) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|6.422. (In Comedy texts οὐδὲ (μηδὲ) εἷς, οὐδὲ (μηδὲ) ἕν, occur, mostly at the end of an iambic trimeter, without elision, 5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 302, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 927, “Pl.” 37, 138, +others)}} (Orig. **ἕνς**, assim. ἔν (δ) probably in {{Refs|“Leg.Gort.” 9.50, from *εμς}} Indo-European **sem**-(compare ὁμός); μία from **sm**-ί; ἴα is not related to μία, but probably to _pronominal_ stem **i**-(Latin _is_), compareἰός.)
**1** as a Numeral, εἷς κοίρανος ἔστω {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.204, etc.}}; strengthened, εἷς οἶος, μία οἴη, a single
**A** **one, one** alone,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|4.397, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 7.65; μία μούνη 23.227; εἷς μοῦνος 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.119, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 1053, etc.; εἷς καὶ μόνος 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 1.74; εἷς ὤν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 247, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 894, etc.; opposed to πολύς, μία τὰς πολλὰς ψυχὰς ὀλέσασα 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1456, compare 1465, “Ch.” 299, etc.}}
**b** emphatically with a _superlative_, εἷς οἰωνὸς ἄριστος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.243, etc.: frequently in Trag., εἷς ἀνὴρ πλεῖστον..πόνον παρασχών 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 327; πλείστας ἀνὴρ εἷς.. ἔγημε 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 460; κάλλιστ᾽ ἀνὴρ εἷς [prev. author] “OT” 1380; ἕνακριθέντ᾽ ἄριστον [prev. author] “Ph.” 1344}}; also in Prose, ἐπὶ πλεῖστον δὴ χλιδῆς εἷς ἀνὴρ ἀπίκετο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.127, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.68; Μυτιληναίους μάλιστα δὴ μίαν πόλιν [prev. author] 3.39; πάντων εἷς ἀνὴρ μεγίστων αἴτιος κακῶν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.143}}: without a _superlative_, Ἐτεοκλέης ἂν εἷς πολὺς κατὰ πτόλιν ὑμνοῖθ᾽ {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 6.}}
**c** in oppos., made emphatic by the _Article_, ὁ εἷς, ἡ μία, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 20.272, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 20.110, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Crito” 48a; τοῦ ἑνὸς οἱ δύο ἀγαθοὶ βελτίους 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1287b13, compare 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 6.22.}}
**d** with a _negative_, εἷς οὐδείς no **single man,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.32; ἓν οὐδὲ ἓν ἴαμα 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.51}}; οὐκ ἐν ἄλλῳ ἑνί γε χωρίῳ in no other **single** country, {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.80}}; οὐχ εἷς, i.e. more than **one,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 103, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 96}}; εἷς οὐ.., εἷς μή.., emphatic for οὐδείς, μηδείς, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 549, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.6.12}}; more emphatic, οὐδὲ εἷς, μηδὲ εἷς, see at {οὐδείς},{μηδείς}.
**e** εἷς ἕκαστος each **one,** each **by himself,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.123, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 332c, etc.}}; αἴσθησις μία ἑνός (i.e. γένους) **one** of **each,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 1003b19: _plural_, ἑκάτεροι ἕνες “POxy.” 276.8 (1st c.AD).}}
**f** with κατά, καθ᾽ ἓν ἕκαστον each **singly,** piece by piece, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.9, etc.}}; καθ᾽ ἕν **one** by **one,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 217a, etc.}}; καθ᾽ ἕν, τό, **list,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Elephantine Papyri 20.7 (3rd c.BC), etc.}}; καθ᾽ ἕν᾽ ἡμῶν ἕκαστον ἀποστερεῖν to deprive each of us **singly,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.142, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Ἐπιτρέποντες” 164, 186}}; εἷς κατὰ εἷς **one** by **one,** {{NT|NT.Mark.14.19}}; but καθ᾽ ἓν γίγνεσθαι, εἶναι, to be **united,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.46, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 5.2.16.}}
**g** with other Preps., ἓν ἀνθ᾽ ἑνός **above all,**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 331b, “Phlb.” 63c}}; but μίαν ἀντὶ μιᾶς **alternately,** {{Refs|PStrassb. 25.13}}, etc.; ἐπὶ μίαν ἑκάστην ῥάβδον τιθέντες θεσπίζουσι **one by one, separately,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.67; ἐπὶ ἑνός 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 157a; ἓν ἐφ᾽ ἑνί [prev. author] “Sph.” 229b, “Lg.” 758b: ἓν πρὸς ἕν, with or without συμβάλλειν, in comparisons, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.50, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 647b; πρὸς ἕν᾽ εἷς 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.131: παρ᾽ ἕνα}}**alternately,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Salt.” 12; εἰς ἓν συναγαγεῖν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 1640; ἰσχὺς τοσαύτη εἰς ἓν ξυστᾶσα 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.85; εἰς ἓν μοίρας συνέκυρσας 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 1172; ἐς μίαν βουλεύειν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.379; in full, ἐς μίαν βουλήν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.111; εἰς μίαν νοεῖν 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 5.9; ἓν ἐξ ἑνὸς ἐπισεσώρευκεν 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 1.10.5, compare 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Asin.” 54}}; ἀπὸ μιᾶς with **one accord,** {{NT|NT.Luke.14.18; ὑφ᾽ ἕν}}**at once,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 10.124; also ὑφ᾽ ἓν θέσθαι τὸ ὂν τῷ μὴ ὄντι 3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 6.2.1}}; compare ὑφέν.
**h** in _compound_ numerals, as an ordinal, τῷ ἑνὶ καὶ τριηκοστῷ [ἔτει] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.89, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.109, etc.}}; so in _Attic dialect_ Inscrr., {{Refs|“IG” 2.660.30, +others,: later εἷς}} alone,=**first,** {{LXX|LXX.Gen.1.5; μιᾷ τοῦ μηνός [prev. work] 8.13.}}
**i** μίαν μίαν, ={κατὰ μίαν}, {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 201; εἷς εἷς LXX.1Ch.24.6.}}
**i.2** **one,** i.e. **the same,** τώ μοι μία γείνατο μήτηρ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.238, etc.}}; εἷς καὶ ὁ αὐτός **one** and the same, ἓν καὶ ταὐτὸν ἀριθμῷ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 1039a28, etc.; ὑπὸ μίαν καὶ τὰν αὐτὰν ἀρχάν Perictione Philosophus cited in 5th c.AD: Stobaeus, Joannes 3.1.121; ταὐτὸν καὶ ἕν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Physica” 201b3; so ἓν καὶ ὅμοιον 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 271a; εἷς καὶ κοινός 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.699f: with _dative_, ἐμοὶ μιᾶς ἐγένετ᾽ ἐκ ματρός 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 156; ἐκ μιᾶς οἰνοχόης Ἐπικούρῳ πεπωκότες 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.1089a.}}
**i.2.b** **possessing unity,** ἧττον μία ἡ μίμησις ἡ τῶν ἐποποιῶν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Poetica” 1462b3; λίαν ἓν ποιεῖν τὴν πόλιν [prev. author] “Pol.” 1263b7; τὰ κυρίως ἕνα 5th-6th c.AD: Damascius Philosophus “de Principiis” 437.}}
**i.3** **one,** opposed to **another,** ἓν μὲν..ἓν δὲ.. [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1139a6, “Pol.” 1285b38, etc.; ὁ μὲν..εἷς δὲ..εἷς δ᾽ αὖ.. 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.421 {εἷσα}, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 369d; εἷς μὲν..ἕτερος δὲ.. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 1.7.23.}}]
**i.4** indefinitely, εἷς τις some **one,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 118, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 471e, etc.; ἐξ ἑνός γέ του τρόπου 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.34; rarely τις εἷς 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 269, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Parmenides” 145d}}; εἷς γάρ τις ἦν ἕκαστος οὑξειργασμένος each **single** one was suspected, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 262; εἷς ὁστισοῦν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1325b28}}; εἷς ὁ πρῶτος, [{{Refs 1st c.BC+|\{1st c.BC-1st c.AD\}: Germanicus Caesar Epigrammaticus}}] **der erste beste,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 8.33, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 1.9, compare 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Herm.” 61}}: alone, like our _indefinite_ _Article_, **a, an,** Κάδμου θυγατέρων μιᾷ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 917}}; εἷς κάπηλος, στρατηγός, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1292, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.50; εἷς Ἀθηναίων 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.87, cf. LXX.Gen.21.15, NT.Matt.21.19, etc.}}; εἷς ἀπό.. {{LXX|LXX.Lev.6.3(22).}}
**i.5** οὐχ εἷς **many,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 103, 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Hymnus in Dianam” 33}}; οὐχ εἷς οὐδὲ δύο not **one** or two only, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 29.12}}; οὐ μίαν οὐδὲ δύο not **once** nor twice, {{LXX+2nd c.BC+|LXX.4Ki.6.10; ἓν ἢ καὶ δύο ληφθὲν μαρτύριον 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.38.10; εἷς ἢ δεύτερος 4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 6.190d: proverbial, εἷς ἀνὴρ οὐδεὶς ἀνήρ}} **one** man's no man, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Dio Chrysostomus Sophista 48.10.}}
**i.6** in Mathematics texts, τὸ ἕν **unity,** opposed to πλῆθος, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Pythagoras Philosophus “fragment” 5, etc.: _plural_, ἕνα}}**units,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 1056b21; ὁ ἀριθμός ἐστιν ἕνα πλείω [prev. author] “Ph.” 207b7; τῶν προτέρων ἑνῶν 5th-6th c.AD: Damascius Philosophus “de Principiis” 460.}}
**i.7** Philos., ἕν, τό, **unity,** the **One,** ἐκ πάντων ἓν καὶ ἐξ ἑνὸς πάντα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus 10, compare 5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 17.1, etc.}}: later _indeclinable_, ἓν εἶναι τοῦ ἓν παρουσίᾳ {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 6.6.14, compare 5.5.5.}}

1521:
** εἰσάγω** [ᾰ],
_Ionic dialect_ _imperfect_ ἐσάγεσκον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.196}}: _perfect_ -αγήοχα Epist. Philipp. cited in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.39}}: _perfect_ _passive_ ἐσῆγμαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.49}}:—**lead in** or **into,** especially **into one's dwelling, introduce,** with _doubleaccusative_, αὐτοὺς εἰσῆγον θεῖον δόμον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.43}}; Κρήτην εἰσήγαγ᾽ ἑταίρους **he led** his comrades **to** Crete,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|3.191; ἐς. τινὰ ἐς.. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.196, etc.: with _dative_, τινὰ δόμοις 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 1112 codices; εἰ. ψυχᾷ χάριν [prev. author] “Hipp.” 526 (Lyric poetry)}}; ὅταν σε καιρὸς εἰσάγῃ, ={ὅταν καιρὸς ᾖ σὲ εἰσιέναι}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 39; νὺξ εἰ. πόνον [prev. author] “Tr.” 29}}:—_middle_, **admit** forces into a city, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.16, 108}}; **take in with one, introduce into** a league or conspiracy, Ὀτάνης ἐσάγεται Ἰνταφρένεα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.70}}:— _passive_, τὴν θερμότητα εἰσάγεσθαι εἰς τοὺς πόρους {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Igne” 38.}}
**2** ἐσαγαγεῖν or ἐσαγαγέσθαι γυναῖκα **to lead** a wife **into one's house,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.40, 6.63.}}
**3** **bring in,** σῖτον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.26}}; **import,** οἶνον Ἀθήναζε κατ᾽ ἐμπορίαν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 35.35}}:—_middle_, εἰσάγεσθαι καὶ ἐξάγεσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Respublica Atheniensium” 2.3, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.145; εἰ. ὧν ἐνδεεῖς 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1257a32:}} —_passive_, εἰσαγόμενα καὶ ἐξαγ. **imports** and exports, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Rh.” 1359b22, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.6, “SIG” 37 (Teos, 5th c.BC).}}
**4** εἰ. εἰς τοὺς φράτερας **introduce** a child to the members of one's φρατρία, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 30.2 (_passive_), 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 3.75, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 57.54; εἰς Κήρυκας 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.127; εἰ. τινὰς εἰς τὴν πολιτείαν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1308a8}}; τινὰς ἐς σπονδάς **secure** their **adhesion,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.35}}; ἰατρὸν εἰσάγειν τινί **call in** a physician **for** another, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.4.3, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 47.67}}:—_middle_, of the physician himself when ill, εἰσάγεσθαι ἄλλους ἰατρούς {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1287a41.}}
**5** **introduce new** customs, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.49 (_passive_)}}; τελετὰς πονηράς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 260; σόφισμα [prev. author] “Ph.” 1408; δεινότατον ἔθος εἰς τὴν πολιτείαν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.2}}; εἰ. τὰ εἴδη the doctrineofideas, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1096a13; αὐλὸν καὶ ῥυθμὸν εἰς τὸν πόλεμον ἀντὶ σάλπιγγος 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 4.20.6.}}
**6** δούλιον εἰσᾶγον αἶσαν, for δ. ἆγον εἰς αἶσαν, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 77 (Lyric poetry).}}
**II** **bring in, bring forward,** especially on the stage, χορόν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ach.” II; Ἥραν ἠλλοιωμένην 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 381d; δράματα [prev. author] “Ap.” 35b, compare 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Hist.Conscr.” 58}}; of an orator, εἰ. σεαυτὸν ποιόν τινα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1417b7}}; **represent** in art, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Cornutus Philosophus “de Natura Deorum” 28, +others (_passive_).}}
**II.2** εἰ. τινὰ εἰς τὴν βουλήν **bring** a culprit **before** the Council, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 7.3.5, etc.}}
**II.3** aslaw-term, εἰ. δίκην or γραφήν **to bring** a cause **into court,** of **the prosecutor,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 580, 582, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 24.10, “PHal.” 1.125, etc.; ὑπόθεσιν “OG” 1669.41 (Egypt, 1st c.AD)}}; also of the εἰσαγωγεύς II, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 6.42, “IG” 12(7).3.40 (from Arcesine), etc.}}; οἱ δὲ θεσμοθέται εἰσαγόντων εἰς τὴν Ἡλιαίαν Legal cited in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.47.}}
**II.3.b** εἰ. τινά **bring forward** the case of an officer at the εὔθυναι (which see), {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.117}}: generally, **bring** a person **into court, prosecute,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 25c, +others}}; in full, εἰ. εἰς δικαστήριον{{Refs|[prev. work] 29a, “Grg.” 521c (_passive_),compare “Lg.” 910e, +others}}
**II.4** **pay in,** τὴν τιμὴν ἐπὶ τὴν δημοσίαν τράπεζαν {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“IG” 22.1013.28; ἀργύριον “PHib.” 46.18 (3rd c.BC), etc.}}
**II.5** **enter, register,** {{Refs|POxy. 1535.8 (_passive_), etc.}}
**III** **introduce to** a subject, **instruct**:—_passive_, εἰσαγόμενοι, οἱ, **beginners,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.175, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus “Libr.Propr.Prooem.”, etc.}}
**IV** _intransitive_, **enter,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|Scholia T. 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.252.}}

1522:
** εἰσᾰκούω**,
**hearken** or **give ear to** one, ὡς ἔφατ᾽· οὐδ᾽ ἐσάκουσε.. Ὀδυσσεύς {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.97, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.133, +others: with _accusative_, φωνὴν ἐσάκουσαν “hymnus ad Cererem” 284, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 559, etc.: with _genitive_ _person_, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 789; τῶν ἐμῶν λόγων 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 1368: with _dative_ _person_, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.214, etc.; τινί τι [prev. author] 9.60}}; **give way, yield** to a request, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.126, 3.4}}; of God, τινός or τῆς προσευχῆς {{LXX|LXX.Psa.4.2, etc.}}:—so in _passive_, of the prayer, {{NT|NT.Luke.1.13}}; of the person, {{NT|NT.Matt.6.7.}}
**2** in Poets, simply, **hear,** τούτου λέγοντος εἰσήκουσ᾽ ἐγώ, ὡς.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 351}}; τίνος βροτῶν λόγον τόνδ᾽ εἰ.; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “El.” 884, compare “Aj.” 318, 4th-3rd c.BC: Axiopistus Poeta Ethicus 1.12; ζῶντ᾽ εἰσακούσας παῖδα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 416.}}
**3** **perceive, feel effect of,** τοῦ ἐγκεφάλου ἐσακούσαντος τοῦ τρώματος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “προρρητικόν” 2.14.}}
**II** _passive_ in strict sense, ἔξωθεν εἰς τὰς οἰκίας εἰσακούεται μᾶλλον ἢ ἔσωθεν ἔξω [{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 903b13 (variant{εἴσω ἀκ}.).}}]

1523:
** εἰσδέχομαι**,
_Ionic dialect_ **ἐσδέκ-**, **take into, admit,** ἐς τὸ ἱρόν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.144, compare 206: with _accusative_ _person_, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 238; εἰ. φρουράν “IG” 22.43.22: with _accusative_ _locative_, οὐκ εἰσεδέξατ᾽ οἶκον 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 876: with _dative_, ἄντροις εἰ. τινά}} **receive** him **in** the cave, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Cyc.” 35: rarely with _genitive_, τόνδ᾽ εἰσεδέξω τειχέων,=τειχέων εἴσω ἐδέξω (cf. Scholia (at [prev. work])), 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 451: with _accusative_ double, ἐσδέξασθαί τινα συνοικιστῆρα}} **admit** him as a fellow-colonist, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 186; εἰ. τινά ὑπόστεγον 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 376:}}_aorist_ I εἰσδεχθῆναι in _passive_ sense, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Tox.” 30, “Merc.Cond.” 10.}}
**2** with _accusative_ _of things_, σκῆψιν ἁγὼν οὗτος οὐκ ἐσδέξεται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 392; εἰ. εὐνομίαν διὰ τῆς μουσικῆς 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 425a.}}
**3** of certain animals, **take in** their young after birth, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 566b17, “GA” 754a29.}}

1524:
** εἴσειμι**,
_infinitive_ -ιέναι, serving as _future_ to εἰσέρχομαι: _imperfect_ εἰσῄειν:— **enter, go into,** οὐδ᾽ Ἀχιλῆος ὀφθαλμοὺς εἴσειμι I **will** not **come before** Achilles' eyes, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.463: more frequently with Preps., οὐκ εἴσειμι μετ᾽ ἀνέρας 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.184; παρὰ βασιλέα 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.99; mostly with εἰς, ἐς τὸ μέγαρον [prev. work] 65, etc.}}; πρός τινα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 953, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.4.5}}; ἐσιέναι ἐς σπονδάς **enter** into a treaty, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.30: _absolutely_, τὸν εἰσιόντα μῆνα}} the **ensuing** month, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.42; τὸν εἰσιόντα ἐνιαυτόν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 31.2, compare “POxy.” 1278.17 (3rd c.AD), etc.}}
**II** of the Chorus or actors, **come upon** the stage, **enter,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 664c}}; τὸ τοὺς τυράννους..εἰσιέναι **take the part of** king, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.247, compare 4th c.AD: Libanius Sophista “Orationes” 30.28.}}
**III** of public speakers, **come into the assembly,** εἰς ἀγοράν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 24.60; καθ᾽ ὅτι ἂν ἐσίῃ ἡ πρεσβεία 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.118}}; of judges, **come into court,** εἰ. κρινοῦντες {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.210.}}
**III.2** of the parties to a lawsuit, **come before the court,** εἰς ὑμᾶς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.80, etc.; εἰ. περί τινος 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.211; πρός τινα [prev. author] 54.32.}}
**III.3** of the charges or actions, ἡ δίκη εἰσῄει {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 5.17}}; δίκας εἰσιέναι κατά τινος **enter upon** actions, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 28.17, compare 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 8.44.}}
**III.4** **enter on** an office, εἰς ἀρχήν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 59.72}}; ὁ ἐσιών the **new** king, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.59.}}
**IV** _metaphorically_, **come into one's mind,** Ἀστυάγεα ἀνάγνωσις ἐσήιε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 1.116; καίτοι μ᾽ ἐσῄει δεῖμα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 1668; ἔιεος εἰσῄει με 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 58e: with _dative_, ἄιγος εἰσῄει φρενί 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 1580, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 59a; δέος τινὶ εἰσῄει περί τινος [prev. author] “R.” 330d.}}
**IV.2** _impersonal_, εἰσῄει αὐτοὺς ὅπως ἂν.. they **began to think** how they might.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.9.17}}: with _infinitive_, οὐδενὸς εἰσῄει μοι φθονεῖν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 23.188.}}
**V** rarely of things, τὰ εἰσιόντα **what enters into one, food,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.6.17.}}

1525:
** εἰσέρχομαι**,
_future_ -ειεύσομαι: _aorist_ -ήλῠθον, -ῆλθον: in _Attic dialect_, _future_ is supplied by εἴσειμι, and _imperfect_ by εἰσῄειν:— **go in** or **into, enter,** in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus and Poets mostly with _accusative_, Φρυγίην εἰσήλυθον 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.184; ἀλλ᾽ εἰσέρχεο τεῖχος 22.56; αὐιάν 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 10.16}}; ἄλσος, δόμους, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 1167, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 563; οἴκαδε 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 5.4.28; οἴκαδε εἰς ἐμαυτοῦ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Hp.Ma.” 304d}}; εἰσῆλθ᾽ ἑκατόμβας **invaded** the hecatombs, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.321}}: but in Prose mostly with Preps., ἐς οἴκημα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.134, etc.}}; ἐς. ἐς τὰς σπονδάς **come into** the treaty, {{Refs|[prev. author] 5.36}}; εἰς τὸν πόλεμον variant in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.1.27}}; εἰ. εἰς τοὺς ἐφήβους **enter the ranks of** the Ephebi, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Cyr.” 1.5.1}}; also εἰ. πρός τινα **enter** his **house, visit** him, {{Refs|[prev. work] 3.3.13}}; of a doctor, **pay a visit,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 18(2).36; εἰ. ἐπὶ τὸ δεῖπνον 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.3.21}}: _absolutely_, of money, etc., **come in,** προσόδους εἰσελθούσας {{Refs|[prev. author] “Vect.” 5.12.}}
**II** of the Chorus, actors, etc., **come upon** the stage, **enter,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 580b, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 6.1.9, etc.}}; **enter the lists,** in a contest, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 700}}; πρός τινα in competition with.., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.319.}}
**III** as law-term, of the accuser, **come into court,** εἰς ὑμᾶς (i.e. τοὺς δικαστάς) {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 59.1; but also τοὺς ὑπὲρ τῶν κοινῶν -εληλυθότας δικαστάς [prev. author] 18.278.}}
**III.2** of the parties, with _accusative_, εἰ. τὴν γραφήν **enter** the charge, {{Refs|[prev. author] 18.105; εἰ. δίκας [prev. author] 28.17 (so also εἰ. [τὴν καταχειροτονίαν] [prev. author] 21.6}}; εἰ. λόγον κατά τινος argument Isoc.II).
**III.3** of the accused, **come before the court,** δεῦρο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 29c; εἰς δικαστήριον [prev. author] “Grg.” 522b; εἰς ὑμᾶς 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.103, compare 21.176}}; εἰσελθόντες δ᾽ ὡς ὑμᾶς is probably in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1410a18.}}
**III.4** of the cause, **to be brought in,** ποῖ οὖν δεῖ ταύτην εἰσελθεῖν τὴν δίκη; {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 35.49.}}
**IV** **enter on an office,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 6.44; ἐς. ἐς τὴν ὑπατείαν 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 41.39; ἐπὶ τὴν ἀρχήν [prev. author] 64.7.}}
**V** **consult** a table, εἰ. εἰς ὄργανον {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 20.12.}}
**VI** _metaphorically_, [μένος] ἄνδρας ἐσέρχεται courage **enters into** the men, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.157}}; πείνη δ᾽ οὔ ποτε δῆμον ἐσέρχεται famine never **enters** the land, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.407; Κροῖσον γέλως ἐσῆλθε 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.125; ὥς με πόλλ᾽ εἰσέρχεται.. ἄλγη 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 845; πόθος μ᾽ εἰσέρχεται 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 1410; νιν εἰσῆλθεν τάδε [prev. work] 57}}: with _dative_, εἰσῆλθε τοῖν τρὶς ἀθλίοιν ἔρις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 372; [Κύπρις] εἰσέρχεται μὲν ἰχθύων.. γένει [prev. author] “fragment” 941.9; δέος εἰ. τινὶ περί τινος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 330d; ὑποψία εἰ. μοι [prev. author] “Ly.” 218c.}}
**VI.2** **come into one's mind,** Κροίσῳ ἐσελθεῖν τὸ τοῦ Σόλωνος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.86, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 147c}}; ἐσελθεῖν τισὶ ἡδονήν, οἶκτον, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.24, 3.14.}}
**VI.2.b** _impersonal_, with _infinitive_, τὸν δὲ ἐσῆλθε θεῖον εἶναι τὸ πρῆγμα **it came into** his **head** that.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 3.42; ἐσῆλθέ με κατοικτῖραι [prev. author] 7.46; εἰσῆλθε δή με..φοβηθῆναι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 835d; τὸν δὲ ἐσῆλθε ὡς εἴη τέρας 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.137; εἰσελθέτω σε μήποθ᾽ ὡς.. 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 1002.}}

1527:
** εἷς**, μίᾰ, ἕν
(μίη only in late _Ionic dialect_ Prose): _genitive_ ἑνός, μιᾶς, ἑνός:—_Epic dialect_ **ἕεις** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 145, “Anthologia Graeca” 7.341 (5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus), conjecture in 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.603}}:—_Doric dialect_ **ἧς** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Rhinthon Comicus 12, “Tab.Heracl.” 1.136}}:—_Epic dialect_, _Aeolic dialect_, and _Ionic dialect_ _feminine_ ἴᾰ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.354, probably in 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ νούσων” 4.37; _accusative_ ἴαν 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 33.6 (probably), 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 69.1 (compare μηδεΐα), 6th c.BC: Corinna Lyrica “Supp.” 2.56, “IG” 9(2).517.22(Thess.); _genitive_ ἰῆς 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.173, 24.496; _dative_ ἰῇ 9.319, 11.174, etc.}}: _neuter_ _dative_ (ἰῷ κίον ἤματι) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|6.422. (In Comedy texts οὐδὲ (μηδὲ) εἷς, οὐδὲ (μηδὲ) ἕν, occur, mostly at the end of an iambic trimeter, without elision, 5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 302, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 927, “Pl.” 37, 138, +others)}} (Orig. **ἕνς**, assim. ἔν (δ) probably in {{Refs|“Leg.Gort.” 9.50, from *εμς}} Indo-European **sem**-(compare ὁμός); μία from **sm**-ί; ἴα is not related to μία, but probably to _pronominal_ stem **i**-(Latin _is_), compareἰός.)
**1** as a Numeral, εἷς κοίρανος ἔστω {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.204, etc.}}; strengthened, εἷς οἶος, μία οἴη, a single
**A** **one, one** alone,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|4.397, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 7.65; μία μούνη 23.227; εἷς μοῦνος 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.119, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 1053, etc.; εἷς καὶ μόνος 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 1.74; εἷς ὤν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 247, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 894, etc.; opposed to πολύς, μία τὰς πολλὰς ψυχὰς ὀλέσασα 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1456, compare 1465, “Ch.” 299, etc.}}
**b** emphatically with a _superlative_, εἷς οἰωνὸς ἄριστος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.243, etc.: frequently in Trag., εἷς ἀνὴρ πλεῖστον..πόνον παρασχών 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 327; πλείστας ἀνὴρ εἷς.. ἔγημε 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 460; κάλλιστ᾽ ἀνὴρ εἷς [prev. author] “OT” 1380; ἕνακριθέντ᾽ ἄριστον [prev. author] “Ph.” 1344}}; also in Prose, ἐπὶ πλεῖστον δὴ χλιδῆς εἷς ἀνὴρ ἀπίκετο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.127, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.68; Μυτιληναίους μάλιστα δὴ μίαν πόλιν [prev. author] 3.39; πάντων εἷς ἀνὴρ μεγίστων αἴτιος κακῶν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.143}}: without a _superlative_, Ἐτεοκλέης ἂν εἷς πολὺς κατὰ πτόλιν ὑμνοῖθ᾽ {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 6.}}
**c** in oppos., made emphatic by the _Article_, ὁ εἷς, ἡ μία, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 20.272, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 20.110, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Crito” 48a; τοῦ ἑνὸς οἱ δύο ἀγαθοὶ βελτίους 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1287b13, compare 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 6.22.}}
**d** with a _negative_, εἷς οὐδείς no **single man,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.32; ἓν οὐδὲ ἓν ἴαμα 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.51}}; οὐκ ἐν ἄλλῳ ἑνί γε χωρίῳ in no other **single** country, {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.80}}; οὐχ εἷς, i.e. more than **one,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 103, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 96}}; εἷς οὐ.., εἷς μή.., emphatic for οὐδείς, μηδείς, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 549, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.6.12}}; more emphatic, οὐδὲ εἷς, μηδὲ εἷς, see at {οὐδείς},{μηδείς}.
**e** εἷς ἕκαστος each **one,** each **by himself,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.123, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 332c, etc.}}; αἴσθησις μία ἑνός (i.e. γένους) **one** of **each,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 1003b19: _plural_, ἑκάτεροι ἕνες “POxy.” 276.8 (1st c.AD).}}
**f** with κατά, καθ᾽ ἓν ἕκαστον each **singly,** piece by piece, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.9, etc.}}; καθ᾽ ἕν **one** by **one,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 217a, etc.}}; καθ᾽ ἕν, τό, **list,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Elephantine Papyri 20.7 (3rd c.BC), etc.}}; καθ᾽ ἕν᾽ ἡμῶν ἕκαστον ἀποστερεῖν to deprive each of us **singly,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.142, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Ἐπιτρέποντες” 164, 186}}; εἷς κατὰ εἷς **one** by **one,** {{NT|NT.Mark.14.19}}; but καθ᾽ ἓν γίγνεσθαι, εἶναι, to be **united,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.46, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 5.2.16.}}
**g** with other Preps., ἓν ἀνθ᾽ ἑνός **above all,**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 331b, “Phlb.” 63c}}; but μίαν ἀντὶ μιᾶς **alternately,** {{Refs|PStrassb. 25.13}}, etc.; ἐπὶ μίαν ἑκάστην ῥάβδον τιθέντες θεσπίζουσι **one by one, separately,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.67; ἐπὶ ἑνός 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 157a; ἓν ἐφ᾽ ἑνί [prev. author] “Sph.” 229b, “Lg.” 758b: ἓν πρὸς ἕν, with or without συμβάλλειν, in comparisons, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.50, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 647b; πρὸς ἕν᾽ εἷς 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.131: παρ᾽ ἕνα}}**alternately,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Salt.” 12; εἰς ἓν συναγαγεῖν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 1640; ἰσχὺς τοσαύτη εἰς ἓν ξυστᾶσα 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.85; εἰς ἓν μοίρας συνέκυρσας 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 1172; ἐς μίαν βουλεύειν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.379; in full, ἐς μίαν βουλήν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.111; εἰς μίαν νοεῖν 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 5.9; ἓν ἐξ ἑνὸς ἐπισεσώρευκεν 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 1.10.5, compare 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Asin.” 54}}; ἀπὸ μιᾶς with **one accord,** {{NT|NT.Luke.14.18; ὑφ᾽ ἕν}}**at once,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 10.124; also ὑφ᾽ ἓν θέσθαι τὸ ὂν τῷ μὴ ὄντι 3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 6.2.1}}; compare ὑφέν.
**h** in _compound_ numerals, as an ordinal, τῷ ἑνὶ καὶ τριηκοστῷ [ἔτει] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.89, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.109, etc.}}; so in _Attic dialect_ Inscrr., {{Refs|“IG” 2.660.30, +others,: later εἷς}} alone,=**first,** {{LXX|LXX.Gen.1.5; μιᾷ τοῦ μηνός [prev. work] 8.13.}}
**i** μίαν μίαν, ={κατὰ μίαν}, {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 201; εἷς εἷς LXX.1Ch.24.6.}}
**i.2** **one,** i.e. **the same,** τώ μοι μία γείνατο μήτηρ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.238, etc.}}; εἷς καὶ ὁ αὐτός **one** and the same, ἓν καὶ ταὐτὸν ἀριθμῷ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 1039a28, etc.; ὑπὸ μίαν καὶ τὰν αὐτὰν ἀρχάν Perictione Philosophus cited in 5th c.AD: Stobaeus, Joannes 3.1.121; ταὐτὸν καὶ ἕν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Physica” 201b3; so ἓν καὶ ὅμοιον 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 271a; εἷς καὶ κοινός 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.699f: with _dative_, ἐμοὶ μιᾶς ἐγένετ᾽ ἐκ ματρός 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 156; ἐκ μιᾶς οἰνοχόης Ἐπικούρῳ πεπωκότες 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.1089a.}}
**i.2.b** **possessing unity,** ἧττον μία ἡ μίμησις ἡ τῶν ἐποποιῶν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Poetica” 1462b3; λίαν ἓν ποιεῖν τὴν πόλιν [prev. author] “Pol.” 1263b7; τὰ κυρίως ἕνα 5th-6th c.AD: Damascius Philosophus “de Principiis” 437.}}
**i.3** **one,** opposed to **another,** ἓν μὲν..ἓν δὲ.. [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1139a6, “Pol.” 1285b38, etc.; ὁ μὲν..εἷς δὲ..εἷς δ᾽ αὖ.. 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.421 {εἷσα}, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 369d; εἷς μὲν..ἕτερος δὲ.. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 1.7.23.}}]
**i.4** indefinitely, εἷς τις some **one,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 118, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 471e, etc.; ἐξ ἑνός γέ του τρόπου 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.34; rarely τις εἷς 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 269, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Parmenides” 145d}}; εἷς γάρ τις ἦν ἕκαστος οὑξειργασμένος each **single** one was suspected, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 262; εἷς ὁστισοῦν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1325b28}}; εἷς ὁ πρῶτος, [{{Refs 1st c.BC+|\{1st c.BC-1st c.AD\}: Germanicus Caesar Epigrammaticus}}] **der erste beste,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 8.33, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 1.9, compare 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Herm.” 61}}: alone, like our _indefinite_ _Article_, **a, an,** Κάδμου θυγατέρων μιᾷ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 917}}; εἷς κάπηλος, στρατηγός, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1292, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.50; εἷς Ἀθηναίων 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.87, cf. LXX.Gen.21.15, NT.Matt.21.19, etc.}}; εἷς ἀπό.. {{LXX|LXX.Lev.6.3(22).}}
**i.5** οὐχ εἷς **many,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 103, 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Hymnus in Dianam” 33}}; οὐχ εἷς οὐδὲ δύο not **one** or two only, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 29.12}}; οὐ μίαν οὐδὲ δύο not **once** nor twice, {{LXX+2nd c.BC+|LXX.4Ki.6.10; ἓν ἢ καὶ δύο ληφθὲν μαρτύριον 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.38.10; εἷς ἢ δεύτερος 4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 6.190d: proverbial, εἷς ἀνὴρ οὐδεὶς ἀνήρ}} **one** man's no man, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Dio Chrysostomus Sophista 48.10.}}
**i.6** in Mathematics texts, τὸ ἕν **unity,** opposed to πλῆθος, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Pythagoras Philosophus “fragment” 5, etc.: _plural_, ἕνα}}**units,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 1056b21; ὁ ἀριθμός ἐστιν ἕνα πλείω [prev. author] “Ph.” 207b7; τῶν προτέρων ἑνῶν 5th-6th c.AD: Damascius Philosophus “de Principiis” 460.}}
**i.7** Philos., ἕν, τό, **unity,** the **One,** ἐκ πάντων ἓν καὶ ἐξ ἑνὸς πάντα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus 10, compare 5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 17.1, etc.}}: later _indeclinable_, ἓν εἶναι τοῦ ἓν παρουσίᾳ {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 6.6.14, compare 5.5.5.}}

1529:
** εἴσοδος**
or **ἔσοδος**, ἡ, **entrance**:
**I** **place of entrance, entry,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.90, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.9, etc.}}; ἐσόδους Φοίβου **the entrance** to his temple, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 104 (_anapaest meter_)}}; of a mountain-pass, ἡ διὰ Τρηχῖνος ἔ. ἐς τὴν Ἑλλάδα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.176}}; in a theatre, **entrance** for the Chorus, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 326, “Av.” 296}}, see Scholia; **entrance-door** of a court of justice, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 63.2, etc.: _metaphorically_, καλῶν ἔσοδοι}} **paths** to glory, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 5.116.}}
**II** **entering, entrance,** εἴ. παρασχεῖν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 4.4.7, etc.: _plural_, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 30.}}
**II.2** **entrance into the lists** to contend in the games, ἱππείαν ἔ.(compare εἰσέρχομαι II) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 6.50}}; also ἡ εἴ. τῆς δίκης εἰς τὸ δικαστήριον **the introduction** of it, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Crito” 45e.}}
**II.3** **right** or **privilege of entrance,** ἔσοδον εἶναι παρὰ βασιλέα ἄνευ ἀγγέλου {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.118.}}
**II.4** **visit,** κακῶν γυναικῶν εἴσοδοι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 930, compare 952, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 1.20}}; of a doctor, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 16.523.}}
**II.5** **study, investigation,** Vett. Val.259.7; ἀκροθιγεῖς τὰς εἰσόδους ποιήσασθαι {{Refs|[prev. work] 222.11}}; also, **method,** {{Refs|[prev. work]108.19.}}
**III** **that which comes in, revenue,** opposed to ἔξοδος, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 6.13.1, compare “IG” 14.423 (from Tauromenium), 5(I).1390.64 (from Andania), “PPetr.” 3p.151.}}

1530:
** εἰσπηδάω**,
**leap in,** ἐς τὰς λίμνας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.132; εἰς τὸν πηλόν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.5.8.}}
**2** **burst in,** εἰσπηδήσας πρός με νύκτωρ Μειδίας Test. cited in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.22}}; εἰς τὴν οἰκίαν {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|[prev. work]78,compare “PHal.” 1.169 (3rd c.BC)}}; εἰς τὰ συνέδρια {{Refs|“Hellenica Oxyrhynchia” 10.2}}: _absolutely_, **rush in,** {{NT+4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Σαμία” 219, NT.Act.16.29.}}

1533:
** εἰσφέρω**,
_future_ εἰσοίσω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 367}}: _aorist_ I εἰσήνεγκα {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus 78.2 (assuming variant)}}: _perfect_ εἰσενήνοχα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 27.36}}: _pluperfect_ **-όχειν**{{Refs|[prev. author] 24.19}}:—**carry in,** εἴσω {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 7.6; ἐς. ἀγγελίας 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.114; ἐς τὠυτὸ ἐς. [prev. author] 9.70; τινὰ εἰς τὸ λογιστήριον “PAmh.” 2.77.22 (2nd c.AD).}}
**2** **bring in, contribute,** τῖμον Archil.[same place]; χρήματα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Hiero” 9.7, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Publicola” 12; εἰ. τινὶ ἔρανον 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 177c, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.1.12}}; at Athens, etc., **pay the propertytax** (see. εἰσφορά II), ἐς. ἐσφοράν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.19, etc.; εἰσφοράς 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.2.12, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 18.7: and _absolutely_, εἰ. εἰς τὴν πόλιν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 27.36; εἰ. ἀφ᾽ ὑπαρχούσης οὐσίας [prev. author] 21.157.}}
**3** **bring in** or **upon,** πένθος δόμοις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 367}}; νόσον καινὴν γυναιξί{{Refs|[prev. work] 353; πόλεμον Ἑλλήνων χθονί [prev. author] “Hel.” 38}}; δειλίαν ἐσφέρει τοῖς ἀλκίμοισι **brings** cowardice **into** the brave, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Supp.” 540.}}
**4** **introduce,** καινὰ δαιμόνια {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.1.2; ψεῦδος 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.58.12}}; especially of political measures, **bring forward, propose,** γνώμην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.80; γνώμην ἐς τὸν δῆμον 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.67}}; εἰ. νόμον,=Latin _legem rogare,_ {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 23.218, 24.19; ψηφίσματα “IG” 22.1329.10}}; τιμάς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work]1343.29: _absolutely_, ἐς. ἐς τὰς βουλὰς περί τινος 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.38; εἰς τοὺς νομοφύλακας 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 772c; τὴν δὲ βουλὴν εἰσενεγκεῖν, ὅτῳ τρόπῳ.. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 1.7.7}}:—_passive_, τὰ εἰσφερόμενα [ψηφίσματα] {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1298b33.}}
**4.b** of persons, **propose, nominate,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 961b:}} —_passive_ {{Refs|[prev. passage]}}; τοὺς -ομένους ὑπὸ τῶν ὑπάτων πρεσβευτάς {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 35.4.5.}}
**II** _middle_, _future_ ἐσοίσομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 664 (Lyric poetry)}}: Ionic _aorist_ I ἐσενείκασθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus (see. below):}}_perfect_ _passive_ εἰσενήνεγμαι (see. below):—**carry with one, sweep along,** of a river, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.495.}}
**II.2** **bring in for oneself,** τὰ ἐκ τῶν ἀγρῶν ἐς τὸ τεῖχος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.34, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.115:}} —so in _passive_, σῖτον ἐσενηνεῖχθαι or **-έχθαι**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.41.}}
**II.3** **bring in with one, introduce,** τοὔνομα ἐς τὴν ποίησιν {{Refs|[prev. author] 2.23}}; πῶμ᾽ ηὗρε κεἰσηνέγκατο θνητοῖς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 279}}; [λόγον] ἐσφέρεσθαι **to utter** it, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Hel.” 664 (Lyric poetry); ν᾽ μνᾶς εἰς τὸν οἶκον εἰσενηνεγμένη}}**having brought** {{Refs|50}} minae **as a dowry** into the family, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 27.4, compare 41.4; προῖκα εἰσενεγκαμένῃ 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Characteres” 22.10.}}
**II.4** **contribute,** εἰσενήνεκται.. οὐκ ἔλαττον μ᾽ μνῶν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 19.43, compare “Michel” 473.9 (Mylasa, 2nd c.BC)}}; **apply, employ,** πᾶσαν εἰ. σπουδὴν καὶ φιλοτιμίαν {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 21.29.12, compare 3rd c.BC: Chrysippus Stoicus 2.293, “IG” 22.1343.23, “Inscription Prien.” 111.126 (1st c.BC), 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 1.84; ἀνδρείαν 1st c.AD: Onosander (Onasander) Tacticus 4.2; θάρσος 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 18.8.5}}; ἰσχύν{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. work] 17.5.6; φιλονεικίαν 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Varia Historia” 12.64.}}
**II.5** like{προσφέρεσθαι}, **eat,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀρχαίης ἰητρικῇς” 3, Ant. 4th c.AD: Libanius Sophista 11.1}}; **drink** water, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Generatione Animalium” 767a32.}}
**II.6** **draw breath,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “Somn.Vig.” 456a17.}}
**III**_passive_, **to be brought in, introduced,** ἐσενειχθέντος σιδηρίου {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.37.}}
**III.2** **rush in,** ἐς τὴν ὕλην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.98.}}

1534:
** εἶτα**,
_Ionic dialect_ **εἶτεν** (which see, compare ἔπειτα, -εν), _adverb_, used to denote the Sequence of one act or state upon another:
**I** of Sequence in time, without any notion of Cause, **then, next,** πρῶτα μέν.., εἶτα.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 262, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 251a, etc.}}; **soon, presently,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 452}}; εἶτα τί τοῦτ; **well,** what **then?** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 347, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 309a}}; εἶτα.. τότε **then..** after that, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 1036 codices (perhaps τόδε): frequently repeated, sometimes alternating with ἔπειτα}} **then.., next.., then.., after** that.., etc., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 154, etc.}}; with πάλιν, {{Refs|“SIG” 1171}}; εἶτ᾽ οὖν **also,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Scholia 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 7.68.}}
**I.2** frequently with finite Verb after a _participle_, expressing surprise or incongruity, **and then, and yet,** μή μοι προτείνων κέρδος εἶτ᾽ ἀποστέρει {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 777; ἆρα κλύουσα, μῆτερ, εἶτ᾽ ἔρξεις κακῶ; 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 1058, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 53, “Aj.” 468, 1092, 1094, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.2.25, etc.; compare ἔπειτα 1.3.}}
**II** to denote Consequence, **and so, therefore, accordingly;** especially in questions or exclamations to express surprise, indignation, contempt, sarcasm, and the like, **and then..? and so..?** κᾆτ᾽ οὐ δέχονται λιτά; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1019, compare “OC” 418; εἶτ᾽ ἐγὼ μὲν οὐ φρον; 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 666}}; κᾆτα ποῦ᾽ στιν ἡ δίκ; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ph.” 548; εἶτ᾽ ἐσίγας, Πλοῦτος ὤ; 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 79; εἶτ᾽ ἄνδρα τῶν αὑτοῦ τι χρὴ προϊένα; [prev. author] “Nu.” 1214; εἶτ᾽ οὐκ αἰσχύνεσθ; 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 1.24, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 28b; οὐκ οἴεσθε δεῖν χρήματα εἰσφέρειν, εἶτα θαυμάζετε..; 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.203; εἶτ᾽ οὐκ ἐπῳδούς φασιν ἰσχύειν τινέ; 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 217.15; εἶτ᾽ οὐ περίεργόν ἐστιν ἄνθρωπος φυτό; 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 141.1, etc.}}

1535:
** εἴτε**,
_Doric dialect_ **αἴτε**, generally doubled, εἴτε..εἴτε.., Latin _sive..sive.., either..or.., whether..or..,_ so that two cases are put as equally possible or equivalent; thrice repeated, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 606; εἴτ᾽ οὖν.., εἴτε.. [prev. author] “OT” 1049; εἴτ᾽ οὖν.., εἴτε καί.. 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 843; εἴτ᾽ οὖν.., εἴτ᾽ οὖν.. [prev. author] “Ch.” 683; εἴτε.., εἴτ᾽ ἄρ᾽ οὖν.. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 345; εἴτε.., εἴτ᾽ αὖ.. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 34b; εἴτε καί.., εἴτε καί.. [prev. author] “R.” 471d: with Substantives, τὴν εἴθ᾽ ἡδονὴν εἴτε ἀπονίαν ἢ εὐστάθειαν 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.1089d: the first εἴτε}} is sometimes omitted in Poets, ξεῖνος, αἴτ᾽ ὦν ἀστός {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.78; αἰνεῖν, εἴτε με ψέγειν θέλεις 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1403; μύραινά γ᾽, εἴτ᾽ ἔχιδν᾽ ἔφυ [prev. author] “Ch.” 1002; λόγοισιν, εἴτ᾽ ἔργοισιν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 517, compare “Tr.” 236}}; and even in Prose, πόλις, εἴτε ἰδιῶται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 864a, compare 907d, “Sph.” 224e: the first εἴτε is sometimes replaced by εἰ, as εἰ..εἴτε..}} Latin _utrum..an..,_ variant in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.35; εἰ.. εἴτε καί.. 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 768; εἰ..εἴτε μή [prev. author] “Eu.” 468; εἰ μὲν.., εἴτε καὶ μή.. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.1.7}}; sometimes ἤ (ἠὲ καί.. variant in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.349) stands for the second εἴτε, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 896, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 277d, “IG” 1.40.5; or for the first, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 178 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 115 (Lyric poetry)}}; εἴτε.. εἴτε.., with _subjunctive_ (compare εἰ), variant in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Archytas Tarentinus Philosophus ap 5th c.AD: Stobaeus, Joannes 3.1.105.}}
**II** in indirect questions, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.90, etc.; σκοπεῖτε εἴτ᾽ ὀρθῶς λογίζομαι ταῦτ᾽ εἴτε μή 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 15.11.}}

1536:
Related to: ** εἰ**,
_Attic dialect_-_Ionic dialect_ and {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD(?): Arcadius Grammaticus (for εἰκ, see below 11 ad [near the start]), = _Doric dialect_ and _Aeolic dialect_ αἰ, αἰκ (which see), 5th c.AD: Cypria}} ἤ {{Refs|“Inscription Cyprian dialect” 135.10 H., both εἰ and αἰ}} in _Epic dialect_:— Particle used interjectionally with _imperative_ and to express a wish, but usually either in conditions, **if**, or in indirect questions, **whether.** In the former use its regular negative is μ; in the latter, οὐ.
**A** INTERJECTIONALLY, in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}}, **come now**! with _imperative_, εἰ δὲ.. ἄκουσον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.262; εἰ δὲ καὶ αὐτοὶ φευγόντων [prev. work] 46; most frequently with ἄγε (which see), 1.302, +others}}
**A.2** in wishes, with _optative_, ἀλλ᾽ εἴ τις.. καλέσειεν {{Refs|10.111, compare 24.74}}; so later, εἴ μοι ξυνείη μοῖρα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 863 (Lyric poetry); εἴ μοι γένοιτο φθόγγος ἐν βραχίοσιν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 836: more frequently followed by γάρ, αἲ γὰρ δὴ οὕτως εἴη 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.189, +others; εἰ γὰρ γενοίμην ἀντὶ σοῦ νεκρός 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 1410; εἰ γὰρ γένοιτο 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 6.1.38; εἰ γὰρ ἐν τούτῳ εἴη 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 310d}}; of unattained wishes, in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus only with _optative_, εἰ γὰρ ἐγὼν.. Διὸς πάϊς αἰγιόχοιο εἴην 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.825; Ζεῦ πάτερ, αἰ γὰρ ἐμὸς πόσις εἴη 7th c.BC: Alcman Lyricus 29}}; later with past tenses of _indicative_, εἰ γάρ μ᾽ ὑπὸ γῆν.. ἧκεν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 152 (_anapaest meter_)}}; εἰ γὰρ τοσαύτην δύναμιν εἶχον ὥστε.. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 1072: twice in 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” with}}_infinitive_ (compare the use of _infinitive_ in commands), αἰ γὰρ τοῖος ἐὼν.. ἐμὸς γαμβρὸς καλέεσθαι {{Refs|7.311, compare 24.376.}}
**A.2.b** εἴθε, _Epic dialect_ αἴθε, is frequently used in wishes in the above constructions, εἴθε οἱ αὐτῷ Ζεὺς ἀγαθὸν τελέσειεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|2.33; εἴθ᾽ ὣς ἡβώοιμι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.157; ἰὼ γᾶ, εἴθ᾽ ἔμ᾽ ἐδέξω 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1537 (Lyric poetry); εἴθε σοι, ὦ Περίκλεις, τότε συνεγενόμην 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.2.46}}: later with _infinitive_, γαίης χθαμαλωτέρη εἴθε.. κεῖσθαι {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“Anthologia Graeca” 9.284 (1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Crinagoras Epigrammaticus).}}
**A.2.c** εἰ γάρ, εἴθε are also used with ὤφελον (_Epic dialect_ ὤφελλον), of past unattained wishes, αἴθ᾽ ὤφελλες στρατοῦ ἄλλου σημαίνειν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.84}}; εἰ γὰρ ὤφελον [κατιδεῖν] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 432c.}}
**A.2.d** followed by a clause expressing a consequence of the fulfilment of the wish, αἰ γὰρ τοῦτο.. ἔπος τετελεσμένον εἴη· τῷ κε τάχα γνοίης.. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.536, compare 17.496, +others}}; sometimes hard to distinguish from εἰ in conditions (which may be derived from this use), εἴ μοί τι πίθοιο, τό κεν πολὺ κέρδιον εἴη {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.28.}}
**B** IN CONDITIONS, **if**:
**B.I** with INDIC.,
**B.I.1** with all tenses (for _future_, see below {{Refs|2), to state a condition, with nothing implied as to its fulfilment, εἰ δ᾽ οὕτω τοῦτ᾽ ἐστίν, ἐμοὶ μέλλει φίλον εἶναι but}}**if** this is so, it will be.., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.564}}: any form of the Verb may stand in apodosi, εἰ θεοί τι δρῶσιν αἰσχρόν, οὐκ εἰσὶν θεοί {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 292.7; εἰ δοκεῖ, πλέωμεν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 526; εἰ Φαῖδρον ἀγνοῶ, καὶ ἐμαυτοῦ ἐπιλέλησμαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 228a; κάκιστ᾽ ἀπολοίμην, Ξανθίαν εἰ μὴ φιλῶ 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 579, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.475; εἰ θεοῦ ἦν, οὐκ ἦν αἰσχροκερδής· εἰ δ᾽ αἰσχροκερδής, οὐκ ἦν θεοῦ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 408c; εἰ ταῦτα λέγων διαφθείρω τοὺς νέους, ταῦτ᾽ ἂν εἴη βλαβερά [prev. author] “Ap.” 30b, compare 25b}}; εἰ οὗτοι ὀρθῶς ἀπέστησαν, ὑμεῖς ἂν οὐ χρεὼν ἄρχοιτε **if** these were right in their revolt, (it would follow that) you rule when you have no right, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.40.}}
**B.I.1.b** to express a general condition, **if ever, whenever**, sometimes with _present_, εἴ τις δύο ἢ καὶ πλείους τις ἡμέρας λογίζεται, μάταιός ἐστιν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 943}}: with _imperfect_, εἴ τίς τι ἠρώτα ἀπεκρίνοντο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.10}}: rarely with _aorist_, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 31.26.1, 2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “Πυρρώνειοι ὑποτυπώσεις” 1.84; compare 111.2.}}
**B.I.2** with _future_ (much less frequently than ἐάν with _subjunctive_), either to express a future supposition emphatically, εἰ φθάσομεν τοὺς πολεμίους κατακαίνοντες οὐδεὶς ἡμῶν ἀποθανεῖται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.1.19; εἰ μὴ βοηθήσετε οὐ περιέσται τἀκεῖ 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.91; εἰ αὕτη ἡ πόλις ληφθήσεται, ἔχεται ἡ πᾶσα Σικελία [prev. passage]}} in threats or warnings, εἰ μὴ καθέξεις γλῶσσαν ἔσται σοι κακά {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 5; εἰ τιμωρήσεις Πατρόκλῳ, αὐτὸς ἀποθανῇ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 28c, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 28.21: or}}
**B.I.2.b** to express a present intention or expectation, αἶρε πλῆκτρον εἰ μαχεῖ **if** you mean to fight, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 759; ἐγὼ μὲν οὐκ ἀνήρ.. εἰ ταῦτ᾽ ἀνατεὶ τῇδε κείσεται κράτη 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 485, compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.61, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 863.}}
**B.I.3** with historical tenses, implying that the condition is or was unfulfilled.
**B.I.3.a** with _imperfect_, referring to present time or to continued or repeated action in past time (in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus always the latter, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.715, +others): ταῦτα οὐκ ἂν ἐδύναντο ποιεῖν, εἰ μὴ διαίτῃ μετρίᾳ ἐχρῶντο they would not be able to do this (as they do)}}, **if** they did not live an abstemious life, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.2.16, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 489b; οὐκ ἂν νήσων ἐκράτει, εἰ μή τι καὶ ναυτικὸν εἶχεν he (from Agamemnon)}} would not have been master of islands, **if** he had not had also some naval force, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.9; αἰ δ᾽ ἦχες ἔσλων ἴμερον ἢ κάλων.. αἴδως κεν.. ἦχεν 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 28}}; εἰ ἦσαν ἄνδρες ἀγαθοὶ.. οὐκ ἄν ποτε ταῦτα ἔπασχον **if** they had been good men, they would never have suffered as they did, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 516e, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.1.5}}; εἰ γὰρ ἐγὼ τάδε ᾔδἐ.. οὐκ ἂν ὑπεξέφυγε **if** I had known this.., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.366.}}
**B.I.3.b** with _aorist_ referring to past time, εἰ μὴ ἔφυσε θεὸς μέλι.. ἔφασκον γλύσσονα σῦκα πέλεσθαι {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Xenophanes Poeta Philosophus 38}}; εἰ μὴ ὑμεῖς ἤλθετε, ἐπορευόμεθα ἂν ἐπὶ βασιλέα had you not come, we should be on our way.., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.1.4; καὶ ἴσως ἂν ἀπέθανον, εἰ μὴ ἡ ἀρχὴ διὰ ταχέων κατελύθη 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 32d, compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.680, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.364, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.5, 27.63}}: with _pluperfect_ in apodosi, εἰ τριάκοντα μόναι μετέπεσον τῶν ψήφων, ἀπεπεφεύγη ἄν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 36a.}}
**B.I.3.c** rarely with _pluperfect_ referring to action finished in past or present time, λοιπὸν δ᾽ ἂν ἦν ἡμῖν ἔτι περὶ τῆς πόλεως διαλεχθῆναι, εἰ μὴ προτέρα τῶν ἄλλων τὴν εἰρήνην ἐπεποίητο **if** she had not (as she has done) made peace before the rest, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 5.56, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 21c.}}
**B.II** with SUB{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus, εἰ is regularly joined with ἄν}} (_Epic dialect_ κε, κεν), compare ἐάν: [{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.AD(?): Arcadius Grammaticus εἰκαν in Tegean Inscrr. of 4th c.BC (“IG” 5(2).3.16, 31, 6.2, “SIG” 306.34) should be understood as εἰκ ἄν (εἰ: εἰκ ={οὐ}: οὐκ), since εἰ δ᾽ ἄν is also found in “IG” 5(2).3.2, 6.45, and εἰκ alone, [prev. work]3.21}}]; but ἄν (κε, κεν) are frequently absent in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus as 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.221, 14.373 (and compare below 2), and Lyric poetry, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus (who never uses εἰ with ἄν or κε (ν)) “P.” 4.266, +others; in dialects, αἰ δείλητ᾽ ἀγχωρεῖν “IG” 9(1).334.6 (_Locrian dialect_, 5th c.BC)}}, cf. Foed._Doric dialect_ cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.79; rarely in 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus, εἰ μὴ ἀναβῇ 2.13}}; occasionally in Trag., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 234, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 198 (Lyric poetry), etc.}}; very rarely in _Attic dialect_ Prose, εἰ ξυστῶσιν αἱ πόλεις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.21; εἴ τι που ἄλσος ἢ τέμενος ἀφειμένον ᾖ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 761c}}: in later Prose, εἴ τις θελήσῃ {{NT+3rd c.AD+|NT.Rev.11.5; εἰ φονεύῃ 3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 2.9.9, compare 5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus “Inst.” 26.}}
**B.II.1** when the apodosis is _future_, to express a future condition more distinctly and vividly than εἰ with _optative_, but less so than εἰ with _future_ _indicative_ (above {{Refs|1.2a)}}; εἰ δέ κεν ὣς ἕρξῃς καί τοι πείθωνται Ἀχαιοί, γνώσῃ ἔπειθ᾽.. **if** thou do thus.., thou shalt know, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.364, compare 1.128, 3.281, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.549; ἂν δέ τις ἀνθιστῆται, σὺν ὑμῖν πειρασόμεθα χειροῦσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.3.11}}; ἂν μὴ νῦν ἐθέλωμεν ἐκεῖ πολεμεῖν αὐτῷ, ἐνθάδ᾽ ἴσως ἀναγκασθησόμεθα τοῦτο ποιεῖν **if** we be not now willing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.50, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.3.27: followed by _imperative_, ἢν εἰρήνης δοκῆτε δεῖσθαι, ἄνευ ὅπλων ἥκετε [prev. work] 3.2.13, compare 5.4.30.}}
**B.II.2** when the apodosis is present, denoting customary or repeated action, to express a general condition, **if ever**, ἤν ποτε δασμὸς ἵκηται, σοὶ τὸ γέρας πολὺ μεῖζον (i.e. ἐστί) **whenever** a division comes, your prize is (always) greater, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.166}}; ἢν ἐγγὺς ἔλθῃ θάνατος, οὐδεὶς βούλεται θνῄσκειν **if** death come near, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 671}}; with ἄν omitted, εἴ περ γάρ τε χόλον.. καταπέψῃ ἀλλά.. ἔχει κότον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.81.}}
**B.II.2.b** with Rhet. present in apodosis, ἐὰν μὴ οἱ φιλόσοφοι βασιλεύσωσιν, οὐκ ἔστι κακῶν παῦλα there is (i.e. can be, will be) no rest.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 473d.}}
**B.III** with OPTATIVE (never with ἄν in early Gr., later ἐάν with _optative_, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Damascius Philosophus “de Principiis” 114, +others)}}
**B.III.1** to express a future condition less definitely than ἐάν with _subjunctive_, usually with _optative_ with ἄν in _apodosis_, ἦ κεν γηθήσαι Πρίαμος Πριάμοιό τε παῖδες.. εἰ σφῶιν τάδε πάντα πυθοίατο μαρναμένοιιν surely they would exult, **if** they should hear.., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.255, compare 7.28, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.223; εἴης φορητὸς οὐκ ἄν, εἰ πράσσοις καλῶς 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 979; οὐδὲ γὰρ ἄν με ἐπαινοίη, εἰ ἐξελαύνοιμι τοὺς εὐεργέτας 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.7.11; οἶκος δ᾽ αὐτός, εἰ φθογγὴν λάβοι, σαφέστατ᾽ ἂν λέξειεν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 37, etc.}}: _future_ _optative_ is _falsa lectio_ in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 164a}}: with _present_ _indicative_ in _apodosis_, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Xenophanes Poeta Philosophus 34.3, 3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 253}}: with _future_ _indicative_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Melissus Philosophus 5.}}
**B.III.1.b** in Hom.sometimes with _present_ _optative_, to express an unfulfilled present condition, εἰ μὲν νῦν ἐπὶ ἄλλῳ ἀεθλεύοιμεν, ἦ τ᾽ ἂν ἐγὼ τὰ πρῶτα φεροίμην **if** we were now contending, etc., [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.274: rarely in Trag., εἰ μὴ κνίζοι (={εἰ μὴ ἔκνιζε}) 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 568; also εἰ ἀναγκαῖον εἴη ἀδικεῖν ἢ ἀδικεῖσθαι, ἑλοίμην ἂν μᾶλλον ἀδικεῖσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 469c.}}]
**B.III.2** when the apodosis is past, denoting customary or repeated action, to express a general condition in past time (corresponding to use of _subjunctive_ in present time, above {{Refs|11.2)}}; once in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, εἴ τίς με.. ἐνίπτοι, ἀλλὰ σὺ τόν γ᾽.. κατέρυκες 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.768}}; εἰ δέ τινας θορυβουμένους αἴσθοιτο.., κατασβεννύναι τὴν ταραχὴν ἐπειρᾶτο **if** he should see (**whenever** he saw) any troops in confusion, he (always) tried, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.3.55, compare “An.” 4.5.13, “Mem.” 4.2.40}}; εἴ τις ἀντείποι, εὐθὺς ἐτεθνήκει **if** any one made objection, he was a dead man at once, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.66; ἀλλ᾽ εἴ τι μὴ φέροιμεν, ὤτρυνεν φέρειν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 755. For εἰ with _indicative_ in this sense see above 1.1}}: _indicative_ and _optative_ are found in same sentence, ἐμίσει, οὐκ εἴ τις κακῶς πάσχων ἠμύνετο, ἀλλ᾽ εἴ τις εὐεργετούμενος ἀχάριστος φαίνοιτο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Agesilaus” 11.3.}}
**B.III.3** in oratio obliqua after past tenses, representing ἐάν with _subjunctive_ or εἰ with a primary (never an historical) tense of the _indicative_ in oratio recta, ἐλογίζοντο ὡς, εἰ μὴ μάχοιντο, ἀποστήσοιντο αἱ πόλεις (representing ἐὰν μὴ μαχώμεθα, ἀποστήσονται) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 6.4.6, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.104, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 5.2.2}}; ἔλεγεν ὅτι, εἰ βλαβερὰ πεπραχὼς εἴη, δίκαιος εἴη ζημιοῦσθαι (representing εἰ βλαβερὰ πέπραχε, δίκαιός ἐστι){{Refs|[prev. work] 32, compare “An.” 6.6.25}}; εἰ δέ τινα φεύγοντα λήψοιτο, προηγόρευεν ὅτι ὡς πολεμίῳ χρήσοιτο (representing εἴ τινα λήψομαι, χρήσομαι) {{Refs|[prev. author] “Cyr.” 3.1.3}}; also, where oratio obliqua is implied in the leading clause, οὐκ ἦν τοῦ πολέμου πέρας Φιλίππῳ, εἰ μὴ Θηβαίους.. ἐχθροὺς ποιήσειε τῇ πόλει, i.e. Philip thought there would be no end to the war, **un**less he should make.. (his thought having been ἐὰν μὴ ποιήσω), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.145; ἐβούλοντο γὰρ σφίσιν, εἴ τινα λάβοιεν, ὑπάρχειν ἀντὶ τῶν ἔνδον, ἢν ἄρα τύχωσί τινες ἐζωγρημένοι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.5.}}
**B.III.4** with _optative_ with ἄν, only when the clause serves as apodosis as well as protasis,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 329b, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.18, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.5.3 (see. ἄν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus 111. d).}}
**B.IV** with _infinitive_, in oratio obliqua, only in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus, εἰ γὰρ δὴ δεῖν πάντως περιθεῖναι ἄλλῳ τέῳ τὴν βασιληΐην, [ἔφη] δικαιότερον εἶναι κτλ. 1.129; εἰ εἶναι τοῦτο μὴ φίλον 2.64, compare 172, 3.105, 108.}}
**B.V** after Verbs denoting **wonder, delight, indignation, disappointment, contentment**, and similar emotions, εἰ with _indicative_ is used instead of ὅτι, to express the object of the feeling in a hypothetical form, θαυμάζω εἰ μηδεὶς ὑμῶν μήτ᾽ ἐνθυμεῖται μήτ᾽ ὀργίζεται, ὁρῶν.. I wonder **that** no one of you is either concerned or angry when he sees.., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.43; οὐκ ἀγαπᾷ εἰ μὴ δίκην δέδωκεν, ἀλλ᾽ εἰ μὴ καὶ χρυσῷ στεφάνῳ στεφανωθήσεται ἀγανακτεῖ 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.147}}: after past tenses, ἐθαύμασε δ᾽ εἰ μὴ φανερόν ἐστιν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.1.13; δεινὸν εἰσῄει, εἰ μὴ.. δόξει 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.33; ἐθαύμαζον εἴ τι ἕξει τις χρήσασθαι τῷ λόγῳ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 95a; οὐδὲ ᾐσχύνθη εἰ.. ἐπάγει 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.105: in oratio obliqua (expressed or implied) with _optative_, ἐπεῖπεν ὡς δεινὸν (i.e. εἴη) εἰ.. μεγαλόψυχος γένοιτο 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.157; ᾤκτιρον εἰ ἁλώσοιντο 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.4.7}}; ἐθαύμαζε δ᾽ εἴ τις ἀρετὴν ἐπαγγελλόμενος ἀργύριον πράττοιτο he wondered **that** any one should demand money, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Mem.” 1.2.7}}; ἔχαιρον ἀγαπῶν εἴ τις ἐάσοι I rejoiced, being content **if** any one should let it pass, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 450a:}} —in this use the _negative_ οὐ is also found, ἀγανακτῶ εἰ ὁ Φίλιππος ἁρπάζων οὐ λυπεῖ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 8.55; δεινὸν ἂν εἴη εἰ οἱ ἐκείνων ξύμμαχοι οὐκ ἀπεροῦσιν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.121; τέρας λέγεις, εἰ οὐκ ἂν δύναιντο λαθεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 91d, etc.}}
**B.VI** in citing a fact as a ground of argument or appeal, **as surely as, since**, εἴ ποτ᾽ ἔην γε **if** there was [as there was], i.e. **as sure as** there was such an one, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.180, +others; εἰ τότε κοῦρος ἔα, νῦν αὖτέ με γῆρας ὀπάζει 4.321}}; πολλοὺς γὰρ οἶκε εἶναι εὐπετέστερον διαβάλλειν ἢ ἕνα, εἰ Κλεομένεα μὲν μοῦνον οὐκ οἷός τε ἐγένετο διαβαλεῖν, τρεῖς δὲ μυριάδας Ἀθηναίων ἐποίησε τοῦτο it seems easier to deceive many than one, **if** (as was the fact, i.e. **since**) he was not able.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.97, compare 1.60, +others}}
**B.VII** ELLIPTICAL CONSTRUCTIONS:
**B.VII.1** with apodosis implied in the context, εἰ having the force of **in case, supposing that**, πρὸς τὴν πόλιν, εἰ ἐπιβοηθοῖεν, ἐχώρουν they marched towards the city [so as to meet the citizens], **in case** they should rush out, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.100}}; ἱκέται πρὸς σὲ δεῦρ᾽ ἀφίγμεθα, εἴ τινα πόλιν φράσειας ἡμῖν εὔερον we have come hither to you, **in case** you should tell us of some fleecy city (i.e. that we might hear of it), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 120}}; παρέζεο καὶ λαβὲ γούνων, αἴ κέν πως ἐθέλῃσιν ἐπὶ Τρώεσσιν ἀρῆξαι sit by him and grasp his knees [so as to persuade him], **in case** he be willing to help the Trojans, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.408, compare 66, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.94, 3.92}}; ἄκουσον καὶ ἐμοῦ, ἐάν σοι ἔτι ταὐτὰ δοκῇ hear me also [that you may assent], **in case** the same opinion please you, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 358b}}; ἰδὲ δή, ἐάν σοι ὅπερ ἐμοὶ συνδοκῇ look now, **in case** you approve what I do, {{Refs|[prev. work] 434a.}}
**B.VII.2** with apodosis suppressed for rhetorical reasons, εἴ περ γάρ κ᾽ ἐθέλῃσιν Ὀλύμπιος.. στυφελίξαι **if** he wish to thrust him away, [he will do so], {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.580}}; εἰ μὲν δώσουσι γέρας—· εἰ δέ κε μὴ δώωσιν, ἐγὼ δέ κεν αὐτὸς ἕλωμαι **if** they shall give me a prize, [well and good]; but **if** they give not, then I will take one for myself, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1.135, compare 6.150, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 468}}; καὶ ἢν μὲν ξυμβῇ ἡ πεῖρα—· εἰ δὲ μή.. and **if** the attempt succeed, [well]; otherwise.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.3, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 325d.}}
**B.VII.3** with the Verb of the protasis omitted, chiefly in the following expressions:
**B.VII.3.a** εἰ μή **except**, οὐδὲν ἄλλο σιτέονται, εἰ μὴ ἰχθῦς μοῦνον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.200}}; μὰ τὼ θεώ, εἰ μὴ Κρίτυλλά γ᾽ [εἰμί]—nay, **if** I'm not Critylla! i.e. I am, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 898}}; εἰ μὴ ὅσον **except** only, ἐγὼ μέν μιν οὐκ εἶδον, εἰ μὴ ὅσον γραφῇ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.73, compare 1.45, 2.20; εἰ μὴ εἰ 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.17, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 480b, etc.}}; εἰ μή τι οὖν, ἀλλὰ σμικρόν γέ μοι τῆς ἀρχῆς χάλασον **if** nothing else, yet.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Men.” 86e; ironical, εἰ μὴ ἄρα ἡ τῆς ἀρετῆς ἐπιμέλεια διαφθορά ἐστιν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.2.8; εἰ μή πέρ γε τὸν ὑοσκύαμον χρήματα εἶναι φήσομεν [prev. author] “Oec.” 1.13.}}
**B.VII.3.b** εἰ δὲ μή but **if** not, i.e. **otherwise**, προηγόρευε τοῖς Λαμψακηνοῖσι μετιέναι Μιλτιάδεα, εἰ δὲ μή, σφέας πίτυος τρόπον ἀπείλεε ἐκτρίψειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.37, compare 56}}; after μάλιστα μέν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.32, 35, etc.:}} —after a preceding _negative_, μὴ τύπτ᾽· εἰ δὲ μή, σαυτόν ποτ᾽ αἰτιάσει don't beat me; **otherwise**, you will have yourself to blame, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1433; ὦ Κῦρε, μὴ οὕτω λέγε· εἰ δὲ μή, οὐ θαρροῦντά με ἕξεις 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.1.35; οὔτ᾽ ἐν τῷ ὕδατι τὰ ὅπλα ἦν ἔχειν· εἰ δὲ μή [prev. author] “An.” 4.3.6, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.28, 131, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 91c.}}
**B.VII.3.c** εἰ δέ sometimes stands for εἰ δὲ μή, εἰ μὲν βούλεται, ἑψέτω· εἰ δ᾽, ὅτι βούλεται, τοῦτο ποιείτω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 285c, compare “Smp.” 212c; εἰ δ᾽ οὖν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 722; εἰ δ᾽ οὕτως 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1094a24}}; εἰ δὲ τοῦτο and **if** so, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 2.1.29.}}
**B.VII.3.d** εἰ γάρ for **if** so, {{Refs|[prev. author] 7.3.6.}}
**B.VII.3.e** εἴ τις **if** any, i. e. **as much as** or **more than** any, τῶν γε νῦν αἴ τις ἐπιχθονίων, ὀρθῶς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 5.5; ὄτλον ἄλγιστον ἔσχον, εἴ τις Αἰτωλὶς γυνή 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 8, compare “OC” 734}}; εἴ τις ἄλλος, **siquis alius**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 6, etc.; εἴ τινες καὶ ἄλλοι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.2, etc.; εἴπερ τις ἄλλος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 501d}}; also κατ᾽ εἰ δέ τινα τρόπον in **any** way, {{Refs|“IG” 5(2).6.27 (from Tegea).}}
**B.VII.3.f** εἴ ποτε or εἴπερ ποτέ **now if** ever, ἡμῖν δὲ καλῶς, εἴπερ ποτέ, ἔχει.. ἡ ξυναλλαγή {{Refs 7th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.20, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 594; αἴ ποτα κἄλλοτα 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus “Supp.” 7.11, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 6.4.12, etc.}}; but in prayers, εἴ ποτέ τοι ἐπὶ νηὸν ἔρεψα.. τόδε μοι κρήηνον ἐέλδωρ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.39.}}
**B.VII.3.g** εἴ ποθεν (i.e. δυνατόν ἐστι) **if** from any quarter, i.e. from some quarter or other, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1204 (Lyric poetry)}}; so εἴ ποθι somewhere, anywhere, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Aj.” 885 (Lyric poetry); εἴ που 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.193.}}
**B.VII.3.h** εἴ πως{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 388, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.3.11}}: in an elliptical sentence (cf. VII. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1), πρέσβεις πέμψαντες, εἴ πως πείσειαν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.58.}}
**B.VIII** with other PARTICLES:
**B.VIII.1** for the distinction between καὶ εἰ (or καὶ ἐάν, or κἄν) even **if**, and εἰ καί (or ἐὰν καί) even **though**, see at {καί}:—the opposite of καὶ εἰ is οὐδ᾽ εἰ, not even **if**; that of εἰ καί is εἰ μηδέ, **if (although**) not even.
**B.VIII.2** for ὡς εἰ, ὡς εἴ τε, ὥσπερ εἰ, etc., see at {ὡς} and ὥσπερ.
**B.VIII.3** for εἰ ἄρα, see at {ἄρα}; for εἰ δή, εἴπερ, see at {εἰ δή, εἴπερ}; for εἴ γε, see at {γέ}.
**B.IX** in _negative_ oaths, = Hebrew **im**, {{LXX+NT|LXX.Psa.94(95).11, NT.Mark.8.12, +others}}
**C** IN INDIRECT QUESTIONS, **whether**, followed by the _indicative_, _subjunctive_, or _optative_, according to the principles of oratio obliqua:
**C.1** with IN{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator after primary tenses, representing the same tense in the direct question, σάφα δ᾽ οὐκ οἶδ᾽ εἰ θεός ἐστιν}} **whether** he is a god, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.183; εἰ ξυμπονήσεις.. σκόπει 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 41.}}
**C.2** with SUB{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus after primary tenses, representing a dubitative}}_subjunctive_ in the direct question, τὰ ἐκπώματα οὐκ οἶδ᾽ εἰ Χρυσάντᾳ τουτῳῒ δῶ **whether** I should give them, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.4.16: sometimes elliptical, ἐς τὰ χρηστήρια ἔπεμπε, εἰ στρατεύηται ἐπὶ τοὺς Πέρσας 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.75.}}
**C.3** OPT. after past tenses, representing either of the two previous constructions in the direct question, ἤρετο εἴ τις ἐμοῦ εἴη σοφώτερος he asked **whether** any one was wiser than I (direct ἔστι τις σοφώτερο;), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 21a; ἐπεκηρυκεύετο Πεισιστράτῳ, εἰ βούλοιτό οἱ τὴν θυγατέρα ἔχειν γυναῖκα 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.60: rarely}}_aorist_ _optative_ for the _aorist_ _indicative_, ἠρώτων αὐτὸν εἰ ἀναπλεύσειεν I asked him **whether** he had set sail (direct ἀνέπλευσα;), {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 50.55: but}}_aorist_ _optative_ usually represents _aorist_ _subjunctive_, τὸν θεὸν ἐπήροντο εἰ παραδοῖεν Κορινθίοις τὴν πόλιν.. καὶ τιμωρίαν τινὰ πειρῷντ᾽ ἀπ᾽ αὐτῶν ποιεῖσθαι they asked **whether** they should deliver their city to the Corinthians, and should try.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.25:}} —in both constructions the _indicative_ or _subjunctive_ may be retained, ψῆφον ἐβούλοντο ἐπαγαγεῖν εἰ χρὴ πολεμεῖν{{Refs|[prev. work] 119}}; ἐβουλεύοντο εἴτε κατακαύσωσιν.. εἴτε τι ἄλλο χρήσωνται **whether** they should burn them or should dispose of them in some other way, {{Refs|[prev. author] 2.4}}; ἀνακοινοῦσθαι αὐτὸν αὑτῷ εἰ δῷ ἐπιψηφίσαι τοῖς προέδροις [he said that] he consulted him **whether** he should give.., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.68.}}
**C.4** with OPT. and ἄν when this was the form of the direct question, ἠρώτων εἰ δοῖεν ἂν τούτων τὰ πιστά they asked **whether** they would give (direct δοιήτε ἄ;), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.8.7.}}
**C.5** the NEG. used with εἰ in indirect questions is οὐ, when οὐ would be used in the direct question, ἐνετέλλετο.. εἰρωτᾶν εἰ οὔ τι ἐπαισχύνεται **whether** he is not ashamed, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.90, etc.}}; but if μή would be required in the direct form, it is retained in the indirect, οὐ τοῦτο ἐρωτῶ, ἀλλ᾽ εἰ τοῦ μὲν δικαίου μὴ ἀξιοῖ πλέον ἔχειν μηδὲ βούλεται ὁ δίκαιος, τοῦ δὲ ἀδίκου (the direct question would be μὴ ἀξιοῖ μηδὲ βούλετα; he does not see fit nor wish, does he?) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 349b}}:—in double indirect questions, εἴτε.. εἴτε..; εἰ.. εἴτε..; εἴτε.. ἢ.., either οὐ or μή can be used in the second clause, ὅπως ἴδῃς εἴτ᾽ ἔνδον εἴτ᾽ οὐκ ἔνδον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 7; σκοπῶμεν εἰ ἡμῖν πρέπει ἢ οὔ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 451d}}; εἰ ἀληθὲς ἢ μή, πειράσομαι μαθεῖν{{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. work] 339a; πολλὰ ἂν περιεσκέψω, εἴτε ἐπιτρεπτέον εἴτε οὔ·.. οὐδένα λόγον οὐδὲ συμβουλὴν ποιῇ, εἴτε χρὴ ἐπιτρέπειν σαυτὸν αὐτῷ εἴτε μή [prev. author] “Prt.” 313a, 313b; ἀνάγκη τὴν ἐμὴν μητέρα, εἴτε θυγάτηρ ἦν Κίρωνος εἴτε μή, καὶ εἰ παρ᾽ ἐκείνῳ διῃτᾶτο ἢ οὔ, καὶ γάμους εἰ διττοὺς ὑπὲρ ταύτης εἱστίασεν ἢ μὴ.. πάντα ταῦτα εἰδέναι τοὺς οἰκέτας 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 8.9}}; τοὺς νόμους καταμανθάνειν εἰ καλῶς κεῖνται ἢ μή.. τοὺς λόγους εἰ ὀρθῶς ὑμᾶς διδάσκουσιν ἢ οὔ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.14.}}

1537:
** ἐκ**,
before a vowel **ἐξ**, also
**A** ἐξ τῳ ϝοίκῳ {{Refs|“Inscription Cyprian dialect” 135.5 H., in _Attic dialect_ Inscrr. before ς ξ ζ ρ}} and less frequently ; ἐγ- in Inscrr. before β γ δ λ μ ; Cretan dialect and _Boeotian dialect_ **ἐς** {{Refs 6th c.BC+|“Leg.Gort.” 2.49, 6th c.BC: Corinna Lyrica “Supp.” 2.67}}; ἐχ frequently in _Attic dialect_ Inscrr. before χ φ θ (and in early Inscrr. before ς, {{Refs|“IG” 12.304.20); also ἐ Ναυπάκτω [prev. work]9(1).334.8 (_Locrian dialect_); (ἐτ is for ἐπὶ in ib 9(2).517.14 (Thess.))}}:—_preposition_ governing GEN. only (except in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|5th c.AD: Cypria and 4th c.AD(?): Arcadius Grammaticus, with _dative_, “Inscription Cyprian dialect” 135.5 H. (from Idalium), (in form ἐς) “IG” 5(2).6.49 (Tegea, 4th c.BC))}}:—radical sense, **from out of,** frequently also simply, **from.**
**A.I** OF PLACE, the most frequently usage, variously modified:
**A.I.1** of Motion, **out of, forth from,** ἐκ Πύλου ἐλθὼν τηλόθεν ἐξ ἀπίης γαίης {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.269, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 321c, etc.; μάχης ἔκ 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.207; ἂψ ἐκ δυσμενέων ἀνδρῶν 24.288; ἐξ ὀχέων, ἐξ ἕδρης, 3.29, 19.77; φεύγειν ἐκ πολέμοιο 7.119; ἐκ τῶν πολεμίων ἐλθεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 6.2.9; ἐκ χειρῶν γέρας εἵλετο 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.344, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1287 (but ἐκ χειρὸς βάλλειν or παίειν}} to strike with a spear **in** the hand, opposed to ἀντιτοξεύειν or ἀκοντίζειν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.3.15, “Cyr.” 4.3.16; ἐκ χειρὸς τὴν μάχην ποιεῖσθαι [prev. work] 6.2.16, compare 6.3.24, etc.)}}; ἐκ χρυσῶν φιαλῶν πίνειν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 5.3.3; ἐξ ἀγορᾶς ὠνεῖσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Comicus 190.}}
**A.I.2** ἐκ θυμοῦ φίλεον I loved her **from** my heart, **with all** my heart, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.343; ἐκ τῆς ψυχῆς ἀσπάσασθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 10.4; μέγαν ἐκ θυμοῦ κλάζοντες Ἄρη 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 48 (_anapaest meter_); δακρυχέων ἐκ φρενός [prev. author] “Th.” 919 (_anapaest meter_); οὐδὲν ἐκ σαυτῆς λέγεις 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 344}}; ἐξ εὐμενῶν στέρνων δέχεσθαι receive **with** kindly heart, {{Refs|[prev. author] “OC” 486; ἐξ ὀμμάτων ὀρθῶν δὲ κἀξ ὀρθῆς φρενός [prev. author] “OT” 528}}; ὀρθὸς ἐξ ὀρθῶν δίφρων **with** chariot still upright, {{Refs|[prev. author] “El.” 742; ἐξ ἀκινήτου ποδός [prev. author] “Tr.” 875; ἐξ ἑνὸς ποδός [prev. author] “Ph.” 91.}}
**A.I.3** to denote change or succession, frequently with an antithetic repetition of the same word, δέχεται κακὸν ἐκ κακοῦ one evil comes **from** (or **after**) another, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.290; ἐκ φόβου φόβον τρέφω 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 28}}; πόλιν ἐκ πόλεως ἀμείβειν, ἀλλάττειν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 224b, “Plt.” 289e; λόγον ἐκ λόγου λέγειν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.313; πόρους ἐκ πόρων ὑπισχνούμενοι 4th c.AD: Alciphro Epistolographus 1.8; ἀπαλλάττειν τινὰ ἐκ γόων 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 291; ἐκ κακῶν πεφευγέναι [prev. author] “Ant.” 437}}: hence, **instead of,** τυφλὸς ἐκ δεδορκότος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “OT” 454; λευκὴν..ἐκ μελαίνης ἀμφιβάλλομαι τρίχα [prev. author] “Ant.” 1093; ἐλεύθερος ἐκ δούλου καὶ πλούσιος ἐκ πτωχοῦ γεγονώς 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.131, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.7.28, etc.}}
**A.I.4** to express separation or distinction **from** a number, ἐκ πολέων πίσυρες four **out of** many, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.680; μοῦνος ἐξ ἁπάντων σωθῆναι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.87}}; εἶναι ἐκ τῶν δυναμένων to be **one of** the wealthy, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 525e}}; ἐμοὶ ἐκ πασέων Ζεὺς ἄλγε᾽ ἔδωκεν to me **out of** (i.e. **above**) all, {{LXX+8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.431, compare 432; ἐκ πάντων μάλιστα 4.96, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1137 (Lyric poetry), etc.; redundant, εἷς τῶν ἐκ τῶν φίλων σου LXX.Judg.15.2.}}
**A.I.5** of Position, **outside of, beyond,** chiefly in early writers, ἐκ βελέων **out of** shot, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.130, etc.}}; ἐκ καπνοῦ **out of** the smoke, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.7}}; ἐκ πατρίδος **banished from** one's country, {{Refs|15.272}}; ἐκ μεσου κατῆστο sate down **apart from** the company, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.83}}; ἐξ ἠθέων τὸν ἥλιον ἀνατεῖλαι **out of** its accustomed quarters, {{Refs|[prev. author] 2.142}}; ἐξ ὀφθαλμῶν **out of** sight, {{Refs|[prev. author] 5.24}}; ἐξ ὁδοῦ **out of** the road, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 113.}}
**A.I.6** with Verbs of Rest, where previous motion is implied, **on, in,** δαῖέ οἱ ἐκ κόρυθος..πῦρ lighted a fire **from** (i.e. **on**) his helmet, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.4}}; ἐκ ποταμοῦ χρόα νίζετο washed his body **in** the river (**with water from** the river), {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.224: frequently with Verbs signifying hang or fasten, σειρήν..ἐξ οὐρανόθεν κρεμάσαντες}} having hung a chain **from** heaven, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.19}}; ἐκ πασσαλόφι κρέμασεν φόρμιγγα he hung his lyre **from** (i.e. **on**) the peg, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.67}}; ἀνάπτεσθαι ἔκ τινος fasten **from** i.e. **upon**) a thing,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|12.51; μαχαίρας εἶχον ἐξ ἀργυρέων τελαμώνων 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.598}}; πρισθεὶς ἐξ ἀντύγων gripped **to** the chariot-rail, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1030, etc.}}; ἐκ τοῦ βραχίονος ἵππον ἐπέλκουσα leading it [by a rein] **upon** her arm, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.12: with Verbs signifying hold, lead, ἐξ ἐκείνων ἔχειν τὰς ἐλπίδας}} to have their hopes **dependent upon** them, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.84}}; ἐκ χειρὸς ἄγειν lead **by** the hand, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Bion Bucolicus “fragment” 7.2}}; ἐκ ποδὸς ἕπεσθαι{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. work] 6.2; ἐκ τῆς οὐρᾶς λαμβάνεσθαι 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Asin.” 23: with the _Article_ indicating the place of origin, οἱ ἐκ τῶν νήσων κακοῦργοι}} the robbers **of** the islands, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.8, compare 2.5, 13}}; τοὺς ἐκ τῆς ναυμαχίας those **in** the sea-fight, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 32b}}; τοὺς ἐκ τῶν σκηνῶν those **in** the tents, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.169; ἁρπασόμενοι τὰ ἐκ τῶν οἰκιῶν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.2.5; οἱ ἐκ τοῦ πεδίου ἔθεον [prev. author] “An.” 4.6.25: even with Verbs of sitting or standing, εἰσεῖδε στᾶσ᾽ ἐξ Οὐλύμποιο}} **from** Olympus where she stood, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.154}}; καθῆσθαι ἐκ πάγων to sit **on** the heights and look **from** them, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 411; στὰς ἐξ ἐπάλξεων ἄκρων 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 1009}}; ἐκ βυθοῦ **at** the bottom, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 22.40: phrases, ἐκ δεξιᾶς, ἐξ ἀριστερᾶς}} **on** the right, left, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.3.10, etc.; οἱ ἐξ ἐναντίας, οἱ ἐκ πλαγίοὐ [prev. work] 7.1.20}}; ἐκ θαλάσσης, opposed to ἐκ τῆς μεσογείας, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.301.}}
**A.I.7** νικᾶν ἔκ τινος win a victory **over..,** {{NT|NT.Rev. 15.2.}}
**A.II** OF TIME, elliptic with _pronoun_ _relative_ and _demonstrative_, ἐξ οὗ [χρόνου] **since,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.6, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.27, etc.}}; in _apodosis_, ἐκ τοῦ **from** that time, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.296; ἐκ τούτου 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.8.15, etc. (but ἐκ τοῖο}} there**after,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.493, and ἐκ τούτων or ἐκ τῶνδε}} usually **after** this, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.9.4, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 235); ἐξ ἐκείνου 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.15}}; ἐκ πολλοῦ (i.e. χρόνου) **for** a long time, {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.68, etc.; ἐκ πλέονος χρόνου [prev. author] 8.45; ἐκ πλείστου [prev. work] 68}}; ἐξ ὀλίγου **at** short notice, {{Refs|[prev. author] 2.11}} (but also a short time **since,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Caesar” 28); ἐκ παλαιοῦ 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.5.8; ἐκ παλαιτάτου 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.18.}}
**A.II.2** of particular points of time, ἐκ νεότητος..ἐς γῆρας {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.86; ἐκ γενετῆς 24.535}}; ἐκ νέου, ἐκ παιδός, **from** boyhood, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 510d, “R.” 374c, etc.; ἐκ μικροῦ παιδαρίου 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 53.19; ἐξ ἀρχῆς 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 284, etc.}}; καύματος ἔξ **after** hot weather, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.865}}; νέφος ἔρχεται οὐρανὸν εἴσω αἰθέρος ἐκ δίης **after** clear weather,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|16.365; ἐκ δὲ αἰθρίης καὶ νηνεμίης συνδραμεῖν ἐξαπίνης νέφεα 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.87}}; so (like ἀπό II) ἐκ τῆς θυσίης γενέσθαι to have **just finished** sacrifice, {{Refs|[prev. work] 50}}, etc.; ἐκ τοῦ ἀρίστου **after** breakfast, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.6.21}}; ἐξ εἰρήνης πολεμεῖν to go to war **after** peace, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.120; γελάσαι ἐκ τῶν ἔμπροσθεν δακρύων 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.4.28; κάλλιστον ἦμαρ εἰσιδεῖν ἐκ χείματος 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 900; τὴν θάλασσαν ἐκ Διονυσίων πλόϊμον εἶναι 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Characteres” 3.3}}; ἐκ χειμῶνος **at the end of** winter, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Nicias” 20.}}
**A.II.3** **at, in,** ἐκ νυκτῶν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.286; ἐκ νυκτός 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.4.2, etc.; ἐξ ἡμέρας 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 780; ἐκ μέσω ἄματος 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 10.5}}; ἐκ τοῦ λοιποῦ or ἐκ τῶν λοιπῶν **for** the future, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 4.56, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 709e.}}
**A.III** OF ORIGIN,
**A.III.1** of Material, **out of** or **of** which things are made, γίγνεταί τι ἔκ τινος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Parmenides Poeta Philosophus 8.12; ποιέεσθαι ἐκ ξύλων τὰ πλοῖα 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.194; πίνοντας ἐκ κριθῶν μέθυ 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 953; εἶναι ἐξ ἀδάμαντος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 616c; ἐκ λευκῶ ἐλέφαντος αἰετοί 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 15.123; στράτευμα ἀλκιμώτατον ἂν γένοιτο ἐκ παιδικῶν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 8.32}}; συνετάττετο ἐκ τῶν ἔτι προσιόντων formed line of battle **from** the troops as they marched up, {{Refs|[prev. author] “An.” 1.8.14.}}
**A.III.2** of Parentage, ἔκ τινος εἶναι, γενέσθαι, etc., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 20.106, 6.206, etc.}}; ἐκ γὰρ ἐμεῦ γένος ἐσσί (where γένος is _accusative_ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|_absolutely_) 5.896; σῆς ἐξ αἵματός εἰσι γενέθλης 19.111; ὦ παῖ πατρὸς ἐξ Ἀχιλλέως 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 260; πίρωμις ἐκ πιρώμιος 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.143; ἀγαθοὶ καὶ ἐξ ἀγαθῶν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 246a; τὸν ἐξ ἐμῆς μητρός 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 466, etc.}}
**A.III.3** of Place of Origin or Birth, ἐκ Σιδῶνος..εὔχομαι εἶναι {{NT+8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.425, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.25, etc.; ἐκ τῶν ἄνω εἰμί NT.John.8.23}}; ἡ ἐξ Ἀρείου πάγου βουλή the Areopagus, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 4.4, etc.; οἱ ἐκ τῆς διατριβῆς ταύτης 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.54}}; οἱ ἐκ τοῦ Περιπάτου the Peripatetics, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Pisc.” 43}}; ὁ ἐξ Ἀκαδημείας the Academic, {{NT+2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 1.34b; οἱ ἐκ πίστεως NT.Gal.3.7; οἱ ἐξ ἐριθείας NT.Rom.2.8.}}
**A.III.4** of the Author or Occasion of a thing, ὄναρ, τιμὴ ἐκ Διός ἐστιν, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.63, 2.197, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.33, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 707, etc.}}; θάνατος ἐκ μνηστήρων death **by the hand of** the suitors, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 16.447}}; τὰ ἐξ Ἑλλήνων τείχεα walls **built by** them, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.148}}; κίνημα ἐξ αὑτοῦ **spontaneous** motion, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 6.1.21; ὕμνος ἐξ Ἐρινύων 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 331 (Lyric poetry); ἡ ἐξ ἐμοῦ δυσβουλία 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 95; ὁ ἐξ ἐμοῦ πόθος [prev. author] “Tr.” 631.}}
**A.III.5** with the agent after _passive_ Verbs, **by,** poetical and early Prose, ἐφίληθεν ἐκ Διός they were beloved **of** (i.e.**by**) Zeus, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.669}}; κήδε᾽ ἐφῆπται ἐκ Διός{{Refs 8th c.BC+|[prev. work] 70; προδεδόσθαι ἐκ Πρηξάσπεος 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.62; τὰ λεχθέντα ἐξ Ἀλεξάνδρου [prev. author] 7.175, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 124 (Lyric poetry), “Ant.” 93, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.69, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 47b; ἐξ ἁπάντων ἀμφισβητήσεται [prev. author] “Tht.” 171b; ὁμολογουμένους ἐκ πάντων 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.6.1; τὰς ἐκ θεῶν τύχας δοθείσας 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1316, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Lysis” 204c: with _neuter_ Verbs, ἐκ..πατρὸς κακὰ πείσομαι 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.134, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 759; τλῆναί τι ἔκ τινος 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.384; θνήσκειν ἔκ τινος 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 579, “OT” 854, etc.; τὰ γενόμενα ἐξ ἀνθρώπων 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.1.}}
**A.III.6** of Cause, Instrument, or Means **by** which a thing is done, ἐκ πατέρων φιλότητος **in consequence** of our fathers' friendship, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.197; μήνιος ἐξ ὀλοῆς 3.135; ἐξ ἔριδος 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.111; τελευτῆσαι ἐκ τοῦ τρώματος 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.29}}; ἐκ τίνος λόγο; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 548}}; ἐκ το; **wherefore?** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Hel.” 93; λέξον ἐκ τίνος ἐπλήγης 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.8.4}}; ποιεῖτε ὑμῖν φίλους ἐκ τοῦ Μαμωνᾶ τῆς ἀδικίας make yourselves friends **of** (i.e. **by means of**).., {{NT+5th c.BC+|NT.Luke.16.9; ζῆν ἔκ τινος 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 3.2.11 codices; ἐκ τῶν ἰδίων τρέφειν ἐμαυτόν 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 15.152; ἐκ τόξων ἀνύσαι γαστρὶ φορβάν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 710 (Lyric poetry).}}
**A.III.7** **in accordance with,** ἐκ τῶν λογίων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.64; ὁ ἐκ τῶν νόμων χρόνος 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 24.28; ἐκ κελεύματος 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 397, compare 5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 25; ἐκ τῶν ξυγκειμένων 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.25; ἐκ τῶν παρόντων [prev. work] 40, etc.}}; ἐκ τῶν ἔργων κρινόμενοι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.2.21, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 485.}}
**A.III.8** frequently as _periphrastic_ for _adverb_, ἐκ προνοίας {{Refs|“IG” 12.115.11}}; ἐκ βίας **by** force, [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 563; ἐκ δόλου [prev. author] “El.” 279; ἐκ παντὸς τρόπου ζητεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 499a: especially with _neuter_ _adjectives_, ἐξ ἀγχιμόλοιο, ={ἀγχίμολον}, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.352; ἐκ τοῦ ἐμφανέος 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.150}}]; ἐκ τοῦ φανεροῦ, ἐκ τοῦ προφανοῦς, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.106, 6.73; ἐκ προδήλου 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1429}}; ἐξ ἴσου, ἐκ τοῦ ἴσου, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Tr.” 485, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.3; ἐξ ἀέλπτου 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.111, etc.}}: with _feminine_ _adjective_, ἐκ τῆς ἰθέης {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 3.127; ἐκ νέης [prev. author] 5.116; ἐξ ὑστέρης [prev. author] 6.85; ἐκ τῆς ἀντίης [prev. author] 8.6; ἐκ καινῆς 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.92; ἐξ ἑκουσίας 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 727; ἐκ ταχείας [prev. work] 395.}}
**A.III.9** of Number or Measurement, with numerals, ἐκ τρίτων **in** the third **place,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 1178, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 500a, “Smp.” 213b}}; distributively, **apiece,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 15.671b.}}
**A.III.9.b** of Price, ἐξ ὀκτὼ ὀβολῶν {{NT|“SIG” 2587.206; ἐκ τριῶν δραχμῶν [prev. work]283; συμφωνήσας ἐκ δηναρίου NT.Matt.20.2.}}
**A.III.9.c** of Weight, ἐπιπέμματα ἐξ ἡμιχοινικίου {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“Inscription Prien.” 362 (4th c.BC).}}
**A.III.9.d** of Space, θινώδης ὢν ὁ τόπος ἐξ εἴκοσι σταδίων **by the space of** twenty stades, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 8.3.19.}}
**B** ἐκ is frequently separated from its CASE, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.109, etc.}}—It takes an accent in anastrophe, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|14.472, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.518.—_Epic dialect_ use it with _adverbs_ in -θεν, ἐξ οὐρανόθεν, ἐξ ἁλόθεν, ἐξ Αἰσύμηθεν, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.548, 21.335, 8.304; ἐκ Διόθεν 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 765; ἐκ πρῴρηθεν 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 22.11.}} —It is combined with other Preps. to make the sense more definite, as διέκ, παρέκ, ὑπέκ.
**C** IN COMPOSITION (joined with other words), the sense of **removal** prevails; **out, away, off.**
**C.2** to express **completion,** like our **utterly,** ἐκπέρθω, ἐξαλαπάζω, ἐκβαρβαρόω, ἐκδιδάσκω, ἐκδιψάω, ἐκδωριεύομαι, ἐξοπλίζω, ἐξομματόω, ἔκλευκος, ἔκπικρος.
**D** As _adverb_, **therefrom,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.480.}}

1538:
** ἕκαστος**, η, ον,
**each,** opposed to the whole body, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.805, etc.: _sing._ with _plural_ Verb, ἔβαν οἶκόνδε ἕκαστος}} they went home **each** to his own house,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|1.606; δεδμήμεσθα ἕκαστος 5.878, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.158}}; so in _Attic dialect_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 785, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 327e, etc.; ὅτι ἕκαστος ἐπίστασθε ἀγαθόν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 3.3}}: _singular_ in apposition with _plural_ Noun or _pronoun_, which expresses the whole, Τρῶας δὲ τρόμος αἰνὸς ὑπήλυθε γυῖα ἕκαστον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.215; ὔμμι..ἑκάστῳ 15.109; αἱ δὲ γυναῖκες..θαύμαζον..ἑκάστη 18.496, etc.; Περσίδες δ᾽.. ἑκάστα..λείπεται 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 135 (Lyric poetry)}}; αἱ ἄλλαι πᾶσαι [τέχναι] τὸ αὑτῆς ἑκάστη ἔργον ἐργάζεται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 346d, compare “Grg.” 503e}}; ὅστις ἕκαστος **every one** which.. (nisi to be read ὥς τις), {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 459.}}
**2** the _Article_ is sometimes added to the _substantive_ (so regularly in earlier _Attic dialect_ Inscrr., {{Refs|“IG” 12.22.14, +others, except ἑκάστου μηνός [prev. work]6.125) with which ἕκαστος agrees, in which case ἕκαστος is commonly put first, καθ᾽ ἑ. τὴν ἡμέραν}} **every single** day, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.211, etc.; περὶ ἑ. τῆς τέχνης 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 274e}}: also following the _substantive_, κατὰ τὸν οξπλίτην ἕκαστον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.49; κατὰ τὴν ἡμέραν ἑκάστην [prev. author] 6.63, +others}}
**II** in _plural_, **all and each severally,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.550, +others, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 932, etc.}}; οἷστισιν ἑκάστοις to whichsoever **severally,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 799a.}}
**II.2** **each** of two or more groups or parties, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.164, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.169, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 491, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.77, etc.}}
**III** strengthened by the addition of other Prons., εἷς ἕ. (see. εἷς); εἷς τις ἕ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 262}}; ἕκαστός τις **each** one, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 4.92, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.45, etc.; ταῦτα ἕκαστα 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.13, etc.; αὔθ᾽ ἕκαστα}}**all in exact detail,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 950.}}
**III.2** with Preps., especially κατά, καθ᾽ ἕκαστον **singly, by itself,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 188a, +others; καθ᾽ ἕ. καὶ σύμπαντα [prev. author] “Sph.” 259b}}; τὸ καθ᾽ ἕ., τὰ καθ᾽ ἕκαστα, **particulars,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Physica” 189a6, “EN” 1143b4, +others}}; παρ᾽ ἕκαστον, παρ᾽ ἕκαστα, in **every case,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 4.82.5, 3.57.4, etc.; παρ᾽ ἕκαστον καὶ ἔργον καὶ λόγον διδάσκοντες 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 325d}}; παρ᾽ ἕκαστον λέγων **constantly** interjecting, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Ἐπιτρέποντες” 48.}}
**III.3** ὡς ἕκαστοι **each by himself,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.79, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.15, etc.: in _sing._, τῶν δὲ ὡς ἑκάστῳ θύειν θέλει 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.132, compare 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 9.98; οὐχ ὡς ἕ. ἀλλὰπάντες 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1292a12, compare 1283b34.}}
**IV** later, ={ἑκάτερος}, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 3.2 codices (ϝέκαστος “Leg.Gort.” 1.9, +others, “Schwyzer” 409.4 (from Elis), “IG” 9(1).334.9 (from Locris). Apptly. connected with ἑκάς by 5th-6th c.AD: Damascius Philosophus “de Principiis” 423.)}}

1544:
** ἐκβάλλω**,
{{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD(?): Arcadius Grammaticus}} **ἐσδέλλω** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“IG” 5(2).6.49 (Tegea, 4th c.BC)}}_future_ -βᾰλῶ: _aorist_ -έβαλον: _perfect_ -βέβληκα: _passive_, _future_ **-βεβλήσομαι**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 1313}}:— **throw** or **cast out of,** with _genitive_, Ὀδίον μέγαν ἔκβαλε δίφρου {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.39, etc.}}: _absolutely_, **throw out,** ἐκ δ᾽ εὐνὰς ἔβαλον {{Refs|1.436, etc.}}; καὶ τὴν μὲν..ἰχθύσι κύρμα γενέσθαι ἔκβαλον **threw** her **overboard,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.481, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.24: then in various relations, ἐκπίπτω being frequently used as its}}_passive_:
**1** **throw ashore,** τὸν δ᾽ ἄρ᾽..νεὸς ἔκβαλε κῦμ᾽ ἐπὶ χέρσου {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.278; ἄνεμος.. τρηχέως περιέσπε..πολλὰς τῶν νεῶν ἐκβάλλων πρὸς τὸν Ἄθων 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.44; ἐ. ἐς τὴν γῆν [prev. author] 7.170 (but in 2.113 ἄνεμοι..ἐκβάλλουσι ἐς τὸ πέλαγος}} **carry out** to sea; ἐξέβαλεν ἄνεμος ἡμᾶς **drove** us **out of** our **course,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 20)}}:—_middle_, **put ashore,** ἵππους ἐξεβάλλοντο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.101}}; **jettison,** Syngr. cited in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 35.11.}}
**2** **cast out of** a place, Κιμμερίους ἐκβαλόντες ἐκ τῆς Εὐρώπης {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.103}}; ἐ. ἐκ τῆς χώρας, of an enemy, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Lycurgus Orator 99, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 60.8}}; especially of banishment, ἐκ πόλεως ἐ. **drive out of** the country, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 468d, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 430, etc.}}; of a corpse, ἔξω τῆς πόλεως, τῶν ὁρίων, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 873b, 909c}}: with _accusative_ only. **drive out, banish,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus 121, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 646, 770, etc.}}; **turn out,** νεοττούς {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 618b12}}; **cast out of** the synagogue, {{NT+1st c.AD+|NT.John.9.34; ἐκ τοῦ τάγματος 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Bellum Judaicum” 2.8.8}}; **exorcize, cast out** evil spirits, {{NT|NT.Mark.1.34, +others}}; also in weakened sense, **cause to depart,** {{Refs|[prev. work] 43.}}
**3** **expose** on a desertisland, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 257, 1034, 1390}}; **expose** a dead body, ταφῆς ἄτερ {{Refs|[prev. author] “Aj.” 1388}}; ἐ. τέκνα **expose** children, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 964.}}
**4** ἐ. γυναῖκα ἐκ τῆς οἰκίας **divorce** her, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 59.83}}: with simple _accusative_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.125, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 59.63, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 12.18, etc.}}:—_passive_, {{LXX|LXX.Lev.21.7.}}
**5** **cast out of** his seat, **depose** a king, ἐ. ἕδρας Κρόνον {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 203}}; ἐκ τυραννίδος θρόνου τ᾽{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 910; ἐκ τῆς τιμῆς 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.3.9: without ἐκ, ἐ. τινὰ πλούτου 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 649}}:—_passive_, **to be ejected,** of an occupier, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PPetr.” 2p.143 (3rd c.BC), “PMagd.” 12.8 (3rd c.BC)}}, etc.; χάριτος ἐκβεβλημένη {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 808; ἐκ τῆς φιλίας 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.5.6}}; ἐκ τῆς ἀρχῆς ἐξεβλήθησαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.70.}}
**6** **throw decisively** in wrestling. τίν᾽ οὐ παλαίουσ᾽ ἐς τρὶς ἐ.; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 941.13.}}
**7** ἐ. φρέατα **dig** wells, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pompeius” 32.}}
**8** of drugs, **get rid of,** τοξεύματα {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 3.32.}}
**9** **expel** afterbirth, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “γυναικεῖα” 1.78.}}
**10** **publish,** σύνταξιν {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 30.4.11}}; **issue,** δόγμα{{Refs|[prev. work] 19.6; ἀπόκρισιν [prev. author] 29.19.5.}}
**II** **strike out of,** χειρῶν δ᾽ ἔκβαλλε κύπελλα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.396, compare 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 22.210}}; ἐκβάλλεθ᾽..τευχέων πάλους **throw** them **out of** the urns, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 742: _absolutely_, δοῦρα ἐ.}} **fell** trees (properly, **cut** them **out of** the forest), {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.244.}}
**II.2** **strike open, break in,** ἐ. θύρετρα, πύλας, θύρας, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 1474, “Hec.” 1044, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 3.23, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 47.53.}}
**III** **let fall, drop,** χειρὸς δ᾽ ἔκβαλεν ἔγχος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.419; σφῦραν 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 17.28; ξίφος 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 629, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 156; οἰστούς 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.1.6: _metaphorically_, ἦ ῥ᾽ ἅλιον ἔπος ἔκβαλον}} **let fall** an idle word, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.324; εἰ μὴ ὑπερφίαλον ἔπος ἔκβαλε 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.503, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.69, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1662, etc.; ἐ. ῥῆμα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 473e}}: _absolutely_, **utter, speak,** {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 9.7}}; **shed,** δάκρυα δ᾽ ἔκβαλε θερμά {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.362}}; ἐ. ἕρκος ὀδόντων **cast, shed** one's teeth, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Solon Lyricus 27, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 644, etc.}}; **throw up** blood, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1238}}; **spit out,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 4.8.4}}; ἐκβαλεῦσι τὰς κούρας their eyes **will drop out,** proverbial of covetous persons, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 4.64.}}
**IV** **throw away, cast aside, reject,** εὐμένειαν, χάριν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 631, 636, compare 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.14.4; προγόνων παλαιὰ θέσμια 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 360.45; θεούς 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1477}}; **recall, repudiate,** ἐ. λόγους {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Crito” 46b}}; **annul,** τοὔπος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 849}}; **remoue** an official from his post, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.87}}; **drive** an actor from the stage, {{Refs|[prev. author] 19.337}}: _metaphorically_, of a politician, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Axiochus” 368d}}: —_passive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 525; ἐκβάλλεσθαι ἄξια 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 4.3.1.}}
**V** **lose,** properly by one's own fault, φρένας, τἀγαθόν. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 649, “Aj.” 965, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 404, “Ec.” 75.}}
**VI** **produce,** of women, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 4.25 (of premature birth), 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Publicola” 21}}; especially in case of a miscarriage or abortion, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “γυναικεῖα” 1.60, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 9.18.8; βρέφος ἐκ τῆς γαστρός 2nd c.AD(?): Antoninus Liberalis Mythographus 34; with play on 1.2, 3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 2.102, etc.}}; **hatch** chicks, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Scholia 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 251.}}
**VI.b** of plants, ἐ. καρπόν **put forth** fruit, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ φύσιος παιδίου” 22; ἐ. στάχυν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 75)}}:—_passive_, τὰ ἐκβαλλόμενα {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“BGU” 197.12 (1st c.AD).}}
**VII** **put out** a bone or joint, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀγμῶν” 31, “Art.” 67; χεῖρα 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 3.15.4.}}
**VIII** **upset, undo the effect of** a speech, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 11.10.6.}}
**IX** in Mathematics texts, **produce** a line, in _passive_, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Caelo” 71b29, “Mech.” 850a11, 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 2.1.29, etc.}}; ἐ. εἰς ἄπειρον **produce** to infinity, in _metaphorically_ sense, τὰ δεινά {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “D.” 1.12, compare 13.}}
**IX.2** **start counting,** in astronomical calculations, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus “Paraphrasis Ptolemaei Tetrabiblou” 252.}}
**X** _intransitive_, **go out, depart,** ἵν᾽ ἐκβάλω ποδὶ ἄλλην ἐπ᾽ αἶαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 96}}; of the sea, **break out of its bed,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 367b13}}; of a rivcr, **branch off,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 113a: _metaphorically_, ἐπειδὰν ἐς μειράκια ἐκβάλωσιν 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 52.26.}}

1545:
** ἔκβᾰσις**, εως, ἡ,
**way out of,** especially **out of** the sea, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.410; κατὰ τὴν ἔκβασιν τὴν εἰς τὰ..ὄρη 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.3.20, compare 4.1.20}}; περὶ τὰς ἐκβάσεις about the **landing-places,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.14.6.}}
**2** **going out of,** especially **out of** a ship, **di embarkation,** ἔ. στρατοῦ {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 771, compare 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 2.1049, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 4.64.5: _metaphorically_, ἄτης ἔ.}} **escape from.**., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 279, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pyrrhus” 23.}}
**3** ={μετάβασις}, Arist {{Refs|“Cael.” 268b3.}}
**4** **end** of a person's life, {{LXX|LXX.Wis.2.17}}: generally, **termination, completion,** ἐλαιουργίας {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“PFay.” 91.21 (1st c.AD)}}; **accomplishment,** τῶν ἔργων {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Rufus Medicus “Anatomy texts” I.}}
**5** **deviation, declension, departure,** παρὰ [τοῦ ἀγαθοῦ] {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 1.8.7, compare 3.7.6.}}
**II** **issue, event,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 696, 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 2.7.9 (_plural_)}}; **fulfilment** of divination, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Zeno Citieus Stoicus 1.44, Chrysipp.[prev. work]2.342.}}
**III** **emanation, procession,** {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “Sententiae ad intelligibilia ducentes” 35, 5th-6th c.AD: Damascius Philosophus “de Principiis” 283.}}
**IV** **produce,** ἐδαφῶν {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“PRyl.” 122.5 (2nd c.AD).}}
**V** **digression,** {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Servius Grammaticus Latinus ad Virg. “G.” 2.209.}}

1546:
** ἐκβολ-ή**, ἡ,
(ἐκβάλλω) **throwing out,** ψήφων ἐ. **casting** the votes **out of** the urn, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 748.}}
**2jettisoning** of cargo, {{NT+4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Th.” 769 (Lyric poetry), 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1110a9, NT.Act.27.18}} (but simply, **unloading,** {{Refs|Sammelb. 1207): _metaphorically_, ἐ. τῆς δόξης}} **casting out** of it, **getting rid** of it, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 230b, “R.” 412e; ἐ. ἐλέου 4th-5th c.AD: Aphthonius Rhetor “Προγυμνάσματα - Rhet.” 7, compare 2nd c.AD: Diogenes Oenoandensis Epicureus 4.}}
**II** **expulsion, banishment,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 421 (Lyric poetry, _plural_); μετὰ τὴν τῶν τυράννων ἐ. 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1275b36; ἐκβολαὶ ἐκ τῆς πόλεως 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 847b}}; **dislodgement, ejection,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 4.8.4.}}
**II.2** **divorce, repudiation,** γυναικός {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Libanius Sophista “Declamationes” 26.45.}}
**III** **letting fall** or **drop,** δακρύων ἐκβολαί {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 742 (Lyric poetry)}}; ἐ. [ὀδόντων] **casting** or **shedding** of teeth, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Generatione Animalium” 789a15.}}
**IV** **expulsion** of a foetus, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “γυναικεῖα” 1.78.}}
**IV.2** ἐ. σίτου **the time when** the corn **comes into ear,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.1.}}
**IV.3** **shoot,** καυλοῦ {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 3.114.}}
**V** **putting out** of a joint, **dislocation,** ἐκβολαὶ τῶν ἄρθρων {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.164f.}}
**VI** **putting forth, exposing,** μαστῶν {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.56.7.}}
**VII** **debouchure, outlet,** ἐ. Πηνειοῦ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.128}}; **mouth** of a river, in _plural_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.102; in _sing._, [prev. author] 7.35, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 113a}}: **pass leading out** of a chain of mountains, αἱ ἐκβολαὶ τοῦ Κιθαιρῶνος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.38.}}
**VII.2** **by-way,** ἐ. ἐκ τῆς ὁδοῦ τῆς εὐθείας {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 3.10.7: _metaphorically_, ἐ. λόγου}}**digression,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.97, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Heroicus” 19.14 (_plural_), etc.}}
**VII.3** **close** of a verse, {{Refs|12th c.AD: Eustathius Episcopus Thessalonicensis 900.24.}}
**VII.4** **projection,** στόματος a snout, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Philostratus Junior Sophista “Imagines” 12.}}
**VIII** (from _passive_), **that which is cast out,** δικέλλης ἐ. **earth thrown up** by a mattock, **upcast,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 250}}; οὐρεία ἐ. **children cast** or **exposed** on the mountains, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 1079 (_anapaest meter_).}}
**VIII.2** **cargo thrown overboard, jetsam,** πλὴν ἐκβολῆς, ἣν ἂν..ἐκβάλωνται Syngr. cited in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 35.11}}; so ἐκβολαὶ νεώς **wrecked** seamen, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 1424.}}
**IX** in Music, **interval of five** διέσεις, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.1141b, Bacchius Musicus “Εἰσαγωγὴ τέχνης μουσικῆς” 42, 3rd c.AD(?): Aristides Quintilianus Musicus 1.10.}}
**X** ={ἐκβολάς} I, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 14.5.28.}}

1549:
** ἔκγονος**, ον
(η, ον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 1647 codices, “Milet.” 7.71 (from Didyma), “IGRom.” 4.912 (from Cibyra))}}, _Doric dialect_, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.AD(?): Arcadius Grammaticus etc. ἔσγονος, “Schwyzer” 191.32 (from Crete), “SIG” 306.53 (Tegea, 4th c.BC)}}, etc.:— **born of, sprung from,** especially _substantive_, **child,** whether **son** or **daughter,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.813, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.236, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.35, etc.; ὁ Διὸς ἔ. 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 876}} (_anapaest meter_): _plural_, ἔκγονοι **descendants,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.167, 4.179, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 450}}, etc.; ἐκγόνων ἔκγονοι **children's children,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Critias” 112c: _metaphorically_, τῆς χώρας ἔκγονοι [prev. author] “Mx.” 239d; ἀδικία ἔ. ὕβρεως [prev. author] “Lg.” 691c; δειλίας ἔ. ἀργία [prev. work] 901e}}; also of interest as **the child** of the principal, {{Refs|[prev. author] “R.” 555e, compare 507a.}}
**b** **grandchild,** {{Refs|Milet. Ergebnisse [same place], “SIG” 900.5, etc.}}
**2** _neuter_, ἔκγονά τινος one's **offspring,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 137 (Lyric poetry); ἔ. κλυτᾶς χθονός 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 171 (Lyric poetry)}}; [ποιηταὶ] ἔ. ἑαυτῶν καταλείπουσιν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 209d; τὰ [ζωγραφίας] ἔ. [prev. author] “Phdr.” 275d}}; compare ἔγγονος.

1551:
** ἐκδέχομαι**,
_Ionic dialect_ **ἐκδέκ-**, _Epic dialect_ _3rd.pers._ _plural_ ἐκδέχαται {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd-4th c.AD: Tryphiodorus Epicus 197}}: _future_ **ἐκδερμᾰτ-δέξομαι**: —_passive_ (see. below{{Refs|1.6).}}
**I** mostly of persons,
**I.1** **take** or **receive from** another, οἵ οἱ σάκος ἐξεδέχοντο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.710; Ὀρέστην ἐξεδεξάμην πατρί 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 762}}; of a beacon-fire, τρίτον Ἀθῷον αἶπος..ἐξεδέξατο {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ag.” 285}}; ἐ. τὴν αἰτίαν **take** it **on oneself,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.37.}}
**I.2** of a successor, ἐ. τὴν βασιληΐην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.26, etc.: frequently with _accusative_ omitted, ἐξεδέξατο Σαδυάττης (i.e. τὴν βασιληΐην) 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus}} **succeeded,** {{Refs|[prev. work]16, compare 103, +others}}; παῖς παρὰ πατρὸς ἐκδεκόμενος τὴν ἀρχήν, [τὴν τέχνην], {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.7,2.166}}; so ἐκδεξάμενοι (i.e. τὴν μάχην) {{Refs|[prev. author] 7.211.}}
**I.3** **take up the argument,** ὥσπερ σφαῖραν ἐ. τὸν λόγον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 277b}}; ἐκδεξάμενος (i.e. τὸν λόγον) εἰπεῖν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Smp.” 189a; ὁ μὲν πρῶτος εἰπὼν..ὁ δ᾽ ἐκδεξάμενος 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.21.}}
**I.4** **wait for, expect,** κεῖνον ἐνθάδ᾽ ἐ. {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 123; ἐλέφαντας 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.45.6; ἀλλήλους NT.1Cor.11.33}}; ἐ. μεθ᾽ ἡσυχίας ἕως.. {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 6.67}}; πότε.. Tryph. [prev. cited]: _absolutely_, **wait,** ἕως.. {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“POxy.” 1673.8 (2nd c.AD).}}
**I.5** **take** or **understand in a certain sense,** οὕτω δὴ τὴν ἀσωτίαν ἐκδεχόμεθα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1120a3; τοὺς λόγους 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 10.18.12; πρὸς τὸ συμφέρον 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 14.56.}}
**I.6** **entertain,** μεγαλοπρεπέστερον ἐγδεχθῆναι {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PTeb.” 33.7 (2nd c.BC).}}
**I.7** **to be surety for,** τινά {{LXX+3rd c.BC+|“PSI” 4.349 (3rd c.BC), LXX.Gen.43.9.}}
**II** of events, **await,** τοὺς Σκύθας.. ἐξεδέξατο οὐκ ἐλάσσων πόνος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.1}}; ἐ. [αὐτοὺς] περίοδος τῆς λίμνης μακρή {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.185.}}
**II.2** of contiguous countries, **come next,** ἀπὸ ταύτης (i.e. τῆς Περσικῆς) ἐ. Ἀσσυρίη {{Refs|[prev. author] 4.39, compare 99, “Peripl.M. Rubr.” 27.}}
**II.3** in Architecture texts, **support,** καμάραν {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 18.26.}}

1552:
** ἔκδηλος**, ον,
strengthened for δῆλος, **conspicuous,** ἵν᾽ ἔ. μετὰ πᾶσιν Ἀργείοισι γένοιτο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.2}}: hence, **considerable,** σίτου μοῖρα {{Refs|“CPHerm.”}}
**II** **quite plain,** πάντ᾽ ἐποίησεν ἔκδηλα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 2.21, compare “OGI” 665.13 (1st c.AD)}}, etc.: _superlative_, ἐκδηλοτάτη ἐνάργεια {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Herc.” 1251.13.}}
**III** _adverb_ **-λως** **openly, manifestly, plainly,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|[prev. author] “Vit.” p.40J., 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.111, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Otho” 17, etc.}}: _comparative_, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|[prev. author] 2.625d, 4th c.AD: Themistius Sophista “Orationes” 15.192a}}: _superlative_, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Heroicus” 19.12, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 60.3.}}

1553:
** ἐκδημ-έω**,
**to be abroad, to be on one's travels,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.30, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 114, etc.}}; **to be in exile,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 864e; εἰς πόλιν “PPetr.” 3p.76.}}
**II** with _accusative_, **travel through,** δύσιν καὶ ἀνατολήν {{Refs|“IG” 14.905.}}

1554:
** ἐκδίδωμι**,
_3rd.pers._ _singular_ ἐκδιδοῖ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.80, +others}}:—**give up,** especiallysomethingseized and detained unlawfully, Ἑλένην καὶ κτήμαθ᾽ ἅμ᾽ αὐτῇ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.459, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.3}}: generally, **surrender,** especially of **giving up** refugees, {{Refs|[prev. work] 74, 158f}}; τινὰ τοῖς ἐχθροῖς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1386, compare “OT” 1040, etc.; ἐ. τινὰ τοῖς κατηγόροις 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.30, compare 29.38}}; ἐ. δοῦλον **give up** a slave to be examined by torture, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 6.27, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 29.14; αὐτὸν ἐξέδωκεν μαστιγῶσαι Εὐριπίδῃ 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1311b32; αὑτὸν ἐς τιμωρίαν τοῖς δικασταῖς 2nd c.AD: Polyaenus Historicus 6.7.1}}; **surrender** a city, Ἀμφίπολιν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.253, compare 257:}} —_middle_, θυμὸν ἐκδόσθαι πρὸς ἥβαν **give up one's** heart to jollity, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.295.}}
**2** **give out of one's house,**
**2.a** ἐ. θυγατέρα **give** one's daughter **in marriage,** τινί {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.196, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 132 (_anapaest meter_), compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Characteres” 22.4}}; θυγατέρας παρὰ σφῶν αὐτῶν ἐκδόντες **having provided for** their **marriage** at their own expense, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 27.69; Ἄλκηστιν ἐ. πρὸς γάμον 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 4.53}}; frequently also without any _accusative_, **give in marriage,** ἐ. εἰς οὒς ἂν ἐθέλωσι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 613d, compare 362b, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.21, etc.}}: _metaphorically_, of the elements, συνοικίζειν καὶ ἐ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 242d}}:—less frequently in _middle_, ἐκδίδοσθαι θυγατέρα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.47, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Characteres” 30.19; ἐξέδου κόρην ὅτῳ σε θυμὸς ἦγεν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 309}}:—_passive_, [{{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD(?): Arcadius Grammaticus ἐσδοθένσᾳ (={ἐκδοθείσῃ})}}] **given in marriage**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“SIG” 306.7 (Tegea, 4th c.BC).}}
**2.b** **give** one's son **for adoption,** τοὺς μὲν (i.e. υἱοὺς) εἰς ἑτέρας οἰκίας {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 31.28.2, compare “POxy.” 1206.6 (4th c.AD)}}; also ἐ. τὸν παῖδα ἐπὶ τέχνην **put** him **out** as an apprentice, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitandi ratione” 2.2, compare “BGU” 1021.6, etc.}}
**3** **farm out, let for hire,** τὴν αὐλήν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.68, compare “SIG” 1044.29 (Halic., 4th-3rd c.BC), etc.}}; ἐ. ἀνδράποδα **to let out** slaves for work, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Vectigalibus” 4.15; πῶλον [prev. author] “Eq.” 2.2 (also in _middle_, ἐξέδοτο [ἀμπελῶνα] γεωργοῖς NT.Mark.12.1)}}: with _infinitive_, χαλινὸν χαλκεῖ ἐ. σκευάσαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Parmenides” 127a}}; ἐ. [θύλακον] τῷ σκυτοδέψῃ ἐπιρράψαι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Characteres” 16.6; ὅταν ἐκδῷ θοἰμάτιον ἐκπλῦναι [prev. work] 22.8}}; ἐκδόντος μοι Δημοσθένους..στέφανον χρυσοῦν ὥστε κατασκευάσαι Test. cited in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.22}}; ὥσπερ ἀνδριάντ᾽ ἐκδεδωκὼς κατὰ συγγραφήν like **one who has contracted for** the execution of a statue, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.122.}}
**4** **give in charge** to another, πολλοὺς ἐξέδωκα Προδίκῳ (with play on _signification_ 2) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 151b}}; ἐκδιδοὺς νεικέων so as to be out of the way of quarrels, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 293 (assuming variant)}}: with _infinitive_, Δὶ τοῦτ᾽..ἐκδώσομεν πράσσειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 13.106.}}
**5** **bring out,** ἀλλ᾽ ἐκδότω τις..δᾷδας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 1194; ἐκδότω δέ τις..δίφρω δύο [prev. author] “fragment” 348.}}
**6** **lend out money** on security, etc., Legal cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 35.51; ναυτικὰ ἐκδεδομένα 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 32.6.}}
**6.b** simply, **pay out,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Oeconomica” 1349b31, “PSI” 3.204 (2nd c.AD).}}
**7** **put out, publish,** of books, etc., chiefly in _passive_, λόγος ὁ πρότερον ἐκδοθείς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 5.11, compare 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.37.6, 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 1.2.2; τοῖς ἐκδεδομένοις λόγοις 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Poetica” 1454b18}}:—in _active_, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Romulus” 8.}}
**8** of a woman, **bring to the birth,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Bella Civilia” 1.83.}}
**9** of land, etc., **return, yield, produce,** μέταλλα.. μονολίθους ἐκδιδόντα πλάκας {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 5.2.5.}}
**10** **hand over, deliver** a document, ἀποχήν {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“BGU” 260.6 (1st c.AD)}}, etc.:—_middle_, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“PFlor.” 384.113 (V 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator).}}
**11** **betray,** {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}
**II** _intransitive_, of rivers, **empty themselves, disembogue,** ἐς θάλασσαν, ἐς τὴν Σύρτιν, ἐς τὸν Μαίανδρον, etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.80, 2.150, 7.26, etc.}}
**II.2** τῶν ἄλλων [ζῴων] τὰ μὲν εἰς ὀδόντας ἐκδίδωσι..τὰ δὲ εἰς κέρατα.. **run to** teeth, etc., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 898a22}}; **find an outlet,** εἰς κεφαλήν {{Refs|[prev. work] 29.}}
**II.3** **emerge,** τὴν Ἀφροδίτην ἐκδοῦναι τῆς θαλάσσης {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Philostrati majoris imagines” 2.1 (to be read -δῦναι).}}

1556:
** ἐκδῐκ-έω**,
**avenge, punish,** φόνον {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Ctesias Historicus “fragment” 37; παρακοήν NT.2Cor.10.6; τινάς “PGen.” 47.17 (4th c.AD)}}; **exact vengeance for,** τὰ αἵματα τῶν δούλων {{LXX+2nd c.BC+|LXX.2Ki.4.8; τὸ αἷμα τὸ ἀναίτιον “SIG” 1181.12 (Jewish, circ. 2nd-1st c.BC).}}
**2** **decide** a case, δίκην, ἀγῶνα, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.432, “POxy.” 1020.6 (2nd c.AD).}}
**II** **avenge** or **vindicate** a person, by taking up his cause, {{NT+1st c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollodorus Damascenus Mechanicus 2.5.11, “PAmh.” 2.134.10 (2nd c.AD), 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Comp. Ag.Gracch.” 5; ἑαυτούς NT.Rom.12.19, etc.}}; ἐ. τινὰ ἀπό τινος **avenge** one on another, {{NT+5th c.BC+|NT.Luke.18.3: with _dative_, Scholia 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 627}}:—_passive_, {{LXX|LXX.Psa.36(37).28.}}
**II.2** **act as** ἔκδικος {{Refs 1st c.BC+|11.3, “AJA” 18.325 (Sardes, 1st c.BC),compare “CIG” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” 2824 (from Aphrodisias), “BCH” 23.182 (from Pisidia).}}
**III** **claim**, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|“CIG” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” 3488 (from Thyatira), “Inscription Perg.” 245; σιτία καὶ ποτά 5th c.AD: Hierocles Platonicus Philosophus “in Carmen Aureum - FPG” 8p.431M.}}
**IV** ἐ. τισί **make retribution for** them, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-4th c.BC: Aesopus Fabularum Scriptor 279b.}}

1558:
** ἔκδῐκ-ος**, ον,
**lawless, unjust,** ἔκδικα πάσχω {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 1093 (_anapaest meter_)}}; of persons, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 920, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 16.5 (_superlative_)}}. _adverb_ **-κως**{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 976, etc.}}
**II** **maintaining the right, avenging,** ἔχει θεὸς ἔ. ὄμμα {{LXX+1st c.AD+|Batrachomyomachia 97; ἔ. χρόνος “Anthologia Graeca” 12.35 (1st c.AD: Diocles Epigrammaticus), cf. LXX.Wis.12.12.}}
**II.2** _substantive_, **avenger,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 7.4.5; αἱ Ἰβύκου ἔ. 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.509f.}}
**II.3** **public advocate** or **prosecutor,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|Inscriptiones Graecae 9(1).61, 1st c.BC: Cicero, M. Tullius Orator et Philosophus “Epistulae ad Familiares” 13.56.1, “Michel” 459 (from Telmessus), “BMus.Inscription” 481*.315 (Ephesus, 2nd c.AD).}}
**II.4** generally, **legal representative,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|POxy. 261.14 (1st c.AD), 1st c.AD: Plinius Rerum Naturalium Scriptor “Ep.Traj.” 110, etc.}}

1560:
** ἔκ-δοτος**, ον,
**given up, delivered,** especially **betrayed,** ἔκδοτόν μιν ἐποίησε ἐς τοὺς Πέρσας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.1, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.122; τὴν Βοιωτίαν Θηβαίοις 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.142; ἱκέτην ἔ. διδόναι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 23.85, etc.; τοῖς πολεμίοις παραδιδόναι 4th c.BC: Lycurgus Orator 85; οὔτε σοὶ οὔτε ἄλλῃ οὐδεμιᾷ περιστάσει δώσομεν ἑαυτοὺς ἐ. 4th c.AD: Metrodorus Epigrammaticus “fragment” 49; λαβών τινα ἔ. ὑπὸ τοῦ ὕπνον 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 6.13.9; ἔκδοτος ἄγεσθαι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.85}}; γίγνεσθαι {{NT+5th c.BC+|[prev. passage], 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 1251; ἔ. διὰ χειρὸς ἀνόμων NT.Act.2.23: _metaphorically_, παρέχειν ἑαυτὴν ἔ. τινι}} to **give** herself **entirely up** to him, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “DDeor.” 20.13; ἔ. σεαυτὴν τῷ ποταμῷ ἐᾶσαι 3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “ad Marcellam - Porphyrii Opuscula” 5; [χώρα] ἔ. τῷ κακῷ [prev. author] “Chr.” 49; πρὸς ὕβριν ἔ. 4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus “Protrepticus” 2.}}
**II** **given in marriage,** {{Refs 6th c.AD+|PMasp. 5.10 (6th c.AD).}}

1561:
** δόξᾰ**, ἡ,
(δοκέω, δέκομαι) **expectation,** οὐδ᾽ ἀπὸ δόξης not otherwise than **one expects,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.324, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.344; in Prose, παρὰ δόξαν ἢ ὡς κατεδόκεε 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.79, etc.}}; ἐν δόξᾳ θέμενος εὖχος **hoping for**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 10(11).63}}; δόξαν παρέχειν τινὶ μὴ ποιήσεσθαι.. to make one **expect** that.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 7.5.21}}; δόξαν παρέχεσθαί τινι ὡς.., with _participle_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 216d}}; ἀπὸ τῆς δ. πεσέειν, = Latin _spe excidere,_ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.203.}}
**II** after {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}}, **notion, opinion, judgement,** whether well grounded or not, βροτῶν δόξαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Parmenides Poeta Philosophus 1.30, compare 8.51; ψυχῆς εὐτλήμονι δόξῃ 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 28 (_anapaest meter_); ἃ δόξῃ τοπάζω 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 235; δόξῃ γοῦν ἐμῇ [prev. author] “Tr.” 718}}; κατά γε τὴν ἐμήν, with or without δόξαν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 472e, “Phlb.” 41b: opposed to ἐπιστήμη, [prev. author] “Tht.” 187bf, “R.” 506c, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “νόμος” 4, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 1074b36; φάσεις καὶ δ. [prev. author] “EN” 1143b13}}; opposed to νόησις, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 534a}}; ἀληθεῖ δόξῃ δοξασταί capable of being subjects of true **opinion,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Tht.” 202b; δ. ἀληθεῖς ἢ ψευδεῖς [prev. author] “Phlb.” 36c; δόξης ὀρθότης ἀλήθεια 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1142b11; δ. ἐμποιεῖν περί τινος [prev. author] “Pol.” 1314b22}}; κύριαι δ. philosophical **maxims,** title of work by Epicurus, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Ir.” p.86 W., etc.; αἱ κοιναὶ δ.}}**axioms,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 996b28.}}
**II.2** **mere opinion, conjecture,** δόξῃ ἐπίστασθαι, ἡγεῖσθαι, **imagine, suppose** (wrongly), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.132, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.105; δόξης ἁμαρτία [prev. author] 1.32}}; δόξαι joined with φαντασίαι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 161e, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Physica” 254a29 (but distinguished from φαντασίαι, [prev. author] “de An.” 428a20)}}; κατὰ δόξαν, opposed to κατ᾽ οὐσίαν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 534c}}; ὡς δόξῃ χρώμενοι speaking **by guess,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 8.8, compare 13.8.}}
**II.3** **fancy, vision,** δ. ἀκόνας λιγυρᾶς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 6.82; δ. βριζούσης φρενός 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 275; οὐκ εἰσὶ δόξαι τῶνδε πημάτων [prev. author] “Ch.” 1053, compare 1051}}; of a dream, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Rhesus” 780; δ. ἐνυπνίου 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vita Apollonii” 1.23}}: _plural_, **hallucinations,** {{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Alexander Trallianus Medicus 1.17.}}
**III** **the opinion which others have of one, estimation, repute,** first in {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Solon Lyricus 13.4 ἀνθρώπων δόξαν ἔχειν ἀγαθήν, compare 34; δ. ἐπ᾽ ἀμφότερα φέρεσθαι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.11.}}
**III.2** mostly, **good repute, honour, glory,** {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus “Supp.” 25.11, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 373 (Lyric poetry, _plural_), 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 8.64, etc.; δόξαν φύσας 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.91}}; δόξαν σχεῖν τινός **for** a thing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 157; ἐπὶ σοφίᾳ δ. εἰληφώς 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 13.2; ἐπὶ καλοκἀγαθίᾳ καὶ σωφροσύνῃ δ. ὁμολογουμένην πεποιημένος 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 35.4.8; δόξαν ἀντὶ τοῦ ζῆν ᾑρημένος 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 2.15; δόξαν εἶχον ἄμαχοι εἶναι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Menexenus” 241b}}; δ. ἔχειν ὥς εἰσι.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 2.17; δ. καταλιπεῖν [prev. author] 3.24: in _plural_, οἱ ἐν ταῖς μεγίσταις δόξαις ὄντες 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.51.}}
**III.3** rarely of **ill repute,** [δ.] ἀντὶ καλῆς αἰσχρὰν τῇ πόλει περιάπτειν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 20.10; λαμβάνειν δ. φαύλην [prev. author] “Ep.” 3.5; κληρονομήσειν τὴν ἐπ᾽ ἀσεβείᾳ δ. 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 15.22.3.}}
**III.4** **popular repute** or **estimate,** εἰσφέρων οὐκ ἀφ᾽ ὑπαρχούσης οὐσίας.. ἀλλ᾽ ἀπὸ τῆς δόξης ὧν ὁ πατήρ μοι κατέλιπεν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.157.}}
**IV** of external appearance, **glory, splendour,** especially of the **Shechinah,** {{LXX+NT|LXX.Exo.16.10, +others; δ. τοῦ φωτός NT.Act.22.11}}: generally, **magnificence,** πλοῦτον καὶ δ. {{LXX+NT|LXX.Gen.31.16, cf. NT.Matt.4.8, +others}}; especially of **celestial beatitude,** {{NT|NT.2Cor.4.17: _plural_, NT.1Pet.1.11}}; also of **illustrious persons, dignities,** δόξας οὐ τρέμουσιν {{NT|NT.2Pet.2.10; δ. βλασφημεῖν NT.Jude.8.}}

1562:
** ἐκδύω**
(ἐκδύνω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.9, etc.)}}:
**I** causal in _present_ ἐκδύω: _imperfect_ ἐξέδυον· _future_ ἐκδύσω: _aorist 1_ ἐξέδῡσα: late _perfect_ ἐκδέδῠκα {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“Anthologia Graeca” 5.72 (2nd-3rd c.AD: Rufinus Epigrammaticus)}}:— **take off, strip off,** with _double_ _accusative_ _person_ et _of things_, ἐκ μέν με χλαῖναν ἔδυσαν they **stripped** me **of** my cloak, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.341; ἐκδύων ἐμὲ..ἐσθῆτα 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1269; ἐκδύσας αὐτὸν [τὸν χιτῶνα] 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.3.17}}: with _accusative_ only, **strip,** πάντας ἐ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 24.204; ἐξέδυσαν [ἐκεῖνον] [prev. author] 54.8.}}
**I.2** _passive_, ἐκδύομαι, _aorist 1_ ἐξεδύθην [ῠ]: _perfect_ ἐκδέδῠμαι:—**to be stripped of** a thing, τὸν χιτωνίσκον ἐκδεδύσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 10.10; [Μαρσύας] τὸ δέπμα ἐκδύεται 4th c.AD: Palaephatus Paradoxographus 47}}: _absolutely_, **to be stripped,** ἐκδυθῆναι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.2.5, compare 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 15.27.9.}}
**I.3** _middle_, ἐκδύομαι, Cretan dialect ἐσδ- {{Refs|“GDI” 5100}}_future_ -δύσομαι: _aorist 1_ ἐξεδυσάμην:—**strip oneself of** a thing, **put off,** τεύχεά τ᾽ ἐξεδύοντο they **were putting off their** armour, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.114}}; ἐκδύσασθαι (to be read -δύσεσθαι) τὸν κιθῶνα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.106; ἐκδεδύσθαι θοἰμάτιον 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 54.35; θηρία ἐκδύεται τὸ ἄγριον 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pompeius” 28}}: _absolutely_, **put off one's clothes, strip,** θᾶττον ἐκδυώμεθα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 686, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.4.19}}; technically, of ephebi, {{NT+3rd c.BC+|“SIG” 527.99 (Dreros, 3rd c.BC), “GDI” 5100: _metaphorically_ of death, NT.2Cor.5.4.}}
**II** _active_ in _middle_ sense, **put off,** μαλακὸν δ᾽ ἔκδυνε χιτῶνα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.437; ἐκδὺς χλαῖναν 14.460; τῶν ἱματίων κατὰ ἓν ἕκαστον ἐκδύνουσα 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.9: _metaphorically_, τὸ γῆρας ἐκδύς 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 336, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 600b15; τὸ κέλυφος [prev. work] 549b25}}:—_passive_, of the clothes, **to be put off,** ἅμα κιθῶνι ἐκδυομένῳ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.8.}}
**III** _aorist 2_ ἐξέδυν: _perfect_ ἐκδέδῡκα:—**go** or **get out of,** with _genitive_, ἐκδὺς μεγάροιο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.334; ἐκδὺς καὶ ἀνακύψας τῆς θαλάσσης}}**emerging from..,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 109d: _metaphorically_, ἐξέδυ δίκης 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 416; ἐκδῦναι κακῶν [prev. author] “IT” 602.}}
**III.2** _perfect_ and _aorist 2_ with _accusative_, **escape, shun,** νῶϊν δ᾽ ἐκδῦμεν ὄλεθρον [grant] us **to escape..,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.99; ἐκδεδυκέναι τὰς λῃτουργίας 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 20.1; τὸν φθόνον ἐκδύς 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pompeius” 30; τὴν ἀληθινὴν οὐσίαν ἐκδεδυκέναι ταῦτα 3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 6.6.8.}}
**III.3** _absolutely_, **escape,** {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 358}}; **escape one's memory,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus Alcaeus2.147e.}}

1563:
** ἐκεῖ**
(not in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus)}}, _Aeolic dialect_ **κῆ** {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 51}}: _Doric dialect_ **τηνεῖ** (which see):— _adverb_ **there, in that place,** opposed to ἐνθάδε, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.83; οἱ ἐ. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 685, etc.}}; τἀκεῖ **what is** or **happens there, events there,** {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 578.5, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.90; redundant, οὗ ἦν ἐ. LXX.1Ki.9.10.}}
**2** frequently as euphemistic for ἐν Ἅιδου, **in another world,** κἀκεῖ δικάζει τἀμπλακήματα Ζεὺς ἄλλος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 230, compare “Ch.” 359 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 76; εὐδαιμονοίτην, ἀλλ᾽ἐ. 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 1073; εὔκολος μὲν ἐνθάδ᾽, εὔκολος δ᾽ ἐκεῖ 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 82, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 64a, +others; in full, ἐκεῖ δ᾽ ἐν Ἅιδου 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 418}}; οἱ ἐ. euphemistic for **the dead,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 355 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 776, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 427b, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 14.61.}}
**3** Philos., **in the intelligible world,** {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 1.2.7, 2.4.5, etc.}}
**II** with Verbs of motion, for ἐκεῖσε, **thither,** ἐ. πλέομεν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.147}}; ἐ. ἀπικέσθαι variant in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 9.108; ὁδοῦ τῆς ἐ. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1019; οἱ ἐ. καταπεφευγότες 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.71, compare 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 5.101.10; βλέψον δὲ κἀκεῖ 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Ἐπιτρέποντες” 103.}}
**III** rarely, of Time, **then,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 395 (Lyric poetry), 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 22.38.}}

1564:
** ἐκεῖθεν**,
_poetry_ **κεῖθεν** (the only form used by {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, also in Trag. where metre requires)}}: _Aeolic dialect_ **κήνοθεν** {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 86}}: _Doric dialect_ **τηνῶθεν** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 754}}; **τηνῶθε** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 3.10}}:— _adverb_ **from that place, thence,** opposed to ἐκεῖσε, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 490, etc.}}; of a person, τἀκεῖθεν εἰ ποθούμεθα **on his part,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Tr.” 632; ὁ ἐ. ἄγγελος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 619b; τὸ σκῆπτρον ἐ. παραλαβόντες 4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 6.181b.}}
**2** ={ἐκεῖ}, οἱ ἐ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.62: with _genitive_, τοὐκεῖθεν ἄλσους}} **on yon side of** the grove, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 505; ἕζοντο τὸ κεῖθεν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 1411 (Lyric poetry).}}
**3** by attraction for ἐκεῖσε, βῆναι κεῖθεν ὅθενπερ ἥκει {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1227 codices (Lyric poetry).}}
**II** **thence, from that fact,** γνοίη δ᾽ ἄν τις ἐ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.224, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 45.48, etc.}}
**III** of Time, **thenceforward,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.234; ἐ. ἤδη 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 54.25.}}

1565:
** ἐκεῖνος**, ἐκείνη, ἐκεῖνο,
also **κεῖνος** (regular in _Epic dialect_, _Ionic dialect_ (as {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“SIG” 37.3 (Teos, 5th c.BC), though 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus prefers ἐκεῖνος), and Lyric poetry, in Trag. κεῖνος}} only where the metre requires,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 230, 792, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 220 (_anapaest meter_)}}, etc.; but not in _Attic dialect_ Prose, and in Comedy texts only in mock Trag. passages): _Aeolic dialect_ **κῆνος** {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 2.1}}: _Doric dialect_ **τῆνος** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 1.4, etc.}}: in Comedy texts, strengthened **ἐκεινοσί** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 277 (probably), 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 1196, etc.}}; **ἐκεινοσίν** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 59.24}}: (ἐκεῖ):—_demonstrative_ _pronoun_ **the person there, that person** or **thing,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, etc.}}: generally with reference to what has gone immediately before, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 106c, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.6.9, etc.}}; but when οὗτος and ἐκεῖνος refer to two things before mentioned, ἐκεῖνος, properly belongs to **the more remote,** in time, place, or thought, οὗτος to **the nearer,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 271b, etc.: but ἐκεῖνος}} sometimes = **the latter,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.3.13, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 8.72, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1325a7, etc.: ἐκεῖνος is frequently the predicate to οὗτος or ὅδε, οὗτος ἐκεῖνος τὸν σὺ ζητέεις 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.32; τοῦτ᾽ ἔστ᾽ ἐκεῖνο 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 622}}; ἆρ᾽ οὗτός ἐστ᾽ ἐκεῖνος ὅν..; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 240, etc.}}: also joined as if one _pronoun_, τοῦτ᾽ ἐκεῖνο..δέρκομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1115, etc.}}; κατ᾽ ἐκεῖνο καιροῦ at **that point** of time, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Alexander” 32, etc.; ἐς ἐ. τοῦ χρόνου 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 46.49}}; ἀλλ᾽ ἐκεῖνο, **à propos,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Nigr.” 8.}}
**2** to denote wellknown persons, etc., κεῖνος μέγας θεός {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.90; ἐκεῖνος ἡνίκ᾽ ἦν Θουκυδίδης 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 708; καίτοι φασὶν Ἰφικράτην ποτ᾽ ἐκεῖνον.. 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.62; ὦ παῖ 'κείνου τἀνδρός 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 36d.}}
**2.b** ἐκεῖνα **the ideal world,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “Phdr.” 250a.}}
**3** for things, of which one cannot remember or must not mention the name, ={ὁ δεῖνα}, **so-and-so,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 195.}}
**3.b** in formulae, τεθνάτω καὶ οἱ παῖδες οἱ ἐξ ἐκείνου {{Refs|“IG” 12.10.33.}}
**4** with simple _demonstrative_ force, Ἶρος ἐκεῖνος ἧσται Irus sits **there,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.239}}; νῆες ἐκεῖναι ἐπιπλέουσιν **there** are ships sailing up, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.51.}}
**5** in indirect speech where properly the _reflexive_ _pronoun_ αὑτοῦ would stand, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 1.6.14, 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 8.22, etc.}}
**6** after a Relat. in apodosi almost _pleonastic_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.4.19 (assuming variant).}}
**7** in _Aeolic dialect_ and _Attic dialect_ the _substantive_ with ἐκεῖνος properly has the _Article_ (κῆνος ὤνηρ {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus “Supp.” 25.6), and ἐκεῖνος}} may precede or follow the _substantive_, ἐκείνῃ τῇ ἡμέρᾳ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.20, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 57a}}; τὴν στρατείαν ἐ., τὸν ἄνδρ᾽ ἐ., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.10, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 649: in Poets the _Article_ is frequently omitted, ἤματι κείνῳ 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.37, etc.}}; but when this is the case in Prose, ἐκεῖνος follows the _substantive_, ἡμέρας ἐκείνης {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.59, etc.}}
**II** _adverb_ ἐκείνως **in that case,** {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.77, 3.46}}; **in that way,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀγμῶν” 27; ζῆν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 516d, etc.}}: _Ionic dialect_ **κείνως** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.120.}}
**III** _dative_ _feminine_ ἐκείνῃ as _adverb_,
**III.1** of Place, **at that place, in that neighbourhood,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.106, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.77, etc.}}; κείνῃ (i.e. ὁδῷ) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.111.}}
**III.2** of Manner, **in that manner,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 556a, etc.}}
**IV** with Preps., ἐξ ἐκείνου from **that time,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Agesilaus” 1.17; ἀπ᾽ ἐκείνου 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “DMar.” 2.2}}; κατ᾽ ἐκεῖνα in **that region,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 3.5.17, etc.; μετ᾽ ἐκεῖνα}}**afterwards,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.81}}; compare ἐπέκεινα.

1566:
** ἐκεῖσε**,
_poetry_ **κεῖσε** (the only form in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, used by Trag. where the metre requires)}}, _adverb_ **thither, to that place,** opposed to ἐκεῖθεν or ἐνθένδε, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.29, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 717, etc.; ἐκεῖσε κἀκεῖσε}}**hither and thither,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 1131, “Hel.” 533; δεῦρο καὶ αὖθις ἐ. [prev. work] 1141 (Lyric poetry)}}; κἀκεῖσε καὶ τὸ δεῦρο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ph.” 266; τῇδε ἐ. [prev. author] “Tr.” 333 (_anapaest meter_); τὸ κεῖσε δεῦρό τε 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 929; τὸ τῇδε καὶ τὸ κεῖσε καὶ τὸ δεῦρο 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 425.}}
**2** **to the other world,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 363}}; ἐνθένδε ἐ. from this world **to the other,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 117c.}}
**3** with _genitive_, ἄνειμι δ᾽ ἐ. τοῦ λόγου {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.239, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 864c.}}
**II** ={ἐκεῖ}, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ διαίτης” 2.38, 3rd c.BC: Chrysippus Stoicus 2.244, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 5.51.3, LXX.Job.39.29, 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 3.2.1, Scholia 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 9.108; τοὺς ἐ. ὄντας NT.Act.22.5.}}

1567:
** ἐκζητ-έω**,
**seek out,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Aristides Rhetor 1.488 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus, “PMag. Osl.” 1.354; τινάς “POxy.” 1465.11 (1st c.BC)}}; περίτινος {{NT|NT.1Pet.1.10.}}
**II** **demand an account of,** τὸ αἷμα {{LXX+NT|LXX.2Ki.4.11, +others, cf. NT.Luke.11.50 (_passive_).}}

1568:
** ἐκθαμβ-έω**,
**to be amazed,** {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Orphica “Argonautica” 1218 (tmesis).}}
**II** _transitive_, **amaze, astonish,** {{LXX|LXX.Sir.30.9}}:—_passive_, {{NT+2nd c.AD+|NT.Mark.9.15, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 16.493.}}

1569:
** ἔκθαμβ-ος**, ον,
**amazed, astounded,** {{NT+2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 20.10.9, NT.Act.3.11, “Tab.Defix.” 5.20, 5th-6th c.AD: Orphica “Fragmenta” 49 vi88.}}
**II** **terrible,** {{LXX+2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD(?): Theodotion LXX.Dan.7.7.}}

1570:
** ἔκ-θετος**, ον,
**sent out of the house, sent away,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 70}}; **exposed,** of a child, {{NT+4th c.AD+|NT.Act.7.19, 4th c.AD(?): Manetho Astrologus 6.52}}; **cast away,** {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}
**II** **projecting, salient,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.68}}; opposed to κρυπτός, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Heliodorus Medicus (?)cited in 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 49.4.23.}}
**II.b** _neuter_, ἔκθετον, τό, ={ἐκθέτης}, Al.{{LXX|LXX.Eze.42.3.}}

1571:
** ἐκ-κᾰθαίρω**,
_Ionic dialectaorist_ I-εκάθηρα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.86}}, _Attic dialect_ **-εκάθᾱρα**{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Dinarchus Orator 2.5}}:— **cleanse out**:
**1** with _accusative_ of the thing cleansed, **clear out,** οὐρούς τ᾽ ἐξεκάθαιρον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.153}}; τὴν κοιλίην Hdt.[same place]; μήτρας, ὀδόντας, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “γυναικεῖα” 1.88, 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus “Synopsis” 5.25.3}}; χθόνα ἐκκαθαίρει κνωδάλων he **clears** this land of monsters, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 264}}; τὸν βίον (i.e.the world) {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “DDeor.” 13.1}}; ἐ.τινά, ὥσπερ ἀνδριάντα, εἰς τὴν κρίσιν **clear** him **of all roughness, polish** him **up,** _metaphorically_ from the finishing touches of a sculptor, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 361d; ἑαυτὸν ἀπό τινος NT.2Tim.2.21}}; ἐ.λογισμόν **clear off** an account, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.64f}}:—_passive_, **to be cleansed, purified,** ἐκκεκαθαρμένοι τὰς ψυχάς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 1.4, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 527d}}; **to be cleared up, explained,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 2p.36U.}}
**2** with _accusative_ of the thing removed, **clear away,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthyphro” 3a, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 625b34; τὸ τοιοῦτον ἐ. γένος 4th-3rd c.BC: Diphilus Comicus 32.17}}; τὴν δωροδοκίαν ἐκ τῆς πόλεως Din.[same place]; κόπρον {{Refs|“APl.” 4.92.7.}}

1572:
** ἐκκαίω**,
_Attic dialect_ **ἐκκάω**, _future_ -καύσω: _aorist_ I ἐξέκαυσα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.134}}, but _participle_ ἐκκέαντες {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Rhesus” 97}}:—**burn out,** τοὺς ὀφθαλμούς τινος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.18; τὸ φῶς Κύκλωπος 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 633, compare 657 (_anapaest meter_)}}:—_passive_, ἐκκάεσθαι τοὺς ὀφθαλμούς **to have one's eyes burnt out,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 473c.}}
**II** **light up, kindle,** τὰ πυρά {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.134, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Rh.” [prev. cited]; ἐκκέας τῶν ξύλων ἅττ᾽ ἂν ᾖ δανότατα 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 1133 (Lyric poetry): _metaphorically_, ἐ. πόλεμον, ἐλπίδα, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.3.3, 5.108.5; τοὺς θυμούς 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 7.35; τὴν πρὸς αὐτὸν ὀργήν 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Fabius Maximus” 7}}; **provoke** to anger, ἔκ με κάεις {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 4.49}}; **inflame** with curiosity, **excite,** τινά {{Refs 1st c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Alex.” 30; ἴσῃ φιλοτιμίᾳ πρός τε τὸν δῆμον ἑαυτοὺς καὶ τὸν δῆμον πρὸς ἑαυτοὺς ἐκκαύσαντες 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Agis” 2}}:—_passive_, **to be kindled, burn up,** τὸ πῦρ ἐκκάεται {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 340; ἐ. τὸ κακόν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 556a; ὀργὴν ἐκκαῆναι LXX.2Ki.24.1; ὁ δῆμος ἐξεκάετο 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Tiberius Gracchus” 13, compare 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Cal.” 3, etc.; ἐ. εἰς ἔρωτα 4th c.AD: Alciphro Epistolographus 3.67, compare 2nd c.AD(?): Charito Scriptor Eroticus I.I; ὑπὸ μέθης 1st c.BC: Parthenius Mythographus 24.2.}}
**II.2** **stimulate,** τὴν βλάστησιν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Causis Plantarum” 2.1.3.}}
**III** **scorch,** ἐκκαίων ὁ ἥλιος {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 867a20}}; of thirst, **parch,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Dips.” 4.}}

1574:
** ἐκκεντέω**,
**prick out, put out,** ὄμματα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 508b6.}}
**II** **pierce, stab,** {{LXX+2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 5.56.12, LXX.Num.22.29, 2nd c.AD: Polyaenus Historicus 5.3.8.}}
**II.2** **massacre,** {{LXX|LXX.Josh.16.10.}}
**III** _intransitive_, of hair, **stand out, project,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Sat.” 24.}}

1575:
** ἐκκλάω**,
**break off,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 611d (_passive_), 4th c.AD: Alciphro Epistolographus 2.4 (_passive_)}}; δάκτυλον {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 8.40.2.}}
**II** _passive_, **grow weak, to be enfeebled,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.671a (assuming variant)}}; τὸ θράσος ἐκκέκλασται {{Refs 1st c.BC+|[prev. work]762f,compare 1st c.BC(?): Maximus Astrologus Tyr. 35.3.}}

1576:
** ἐκκλείω**,
_Ionic dialect_ **ἐκκληΐω** or **ἐκκλήω**, old _Attic dialect_ **ἐκκλήω**: _Attic dialect_ _future_ - κλῄσω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 1127}}: _Doric dialect_ _aorist 1_ - κλᾳξα {{Refs|“Comica Adespota” 1203.7 (uncertain)}}: _perfect_ ἐκκέκλεικα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Σαμία” 201}}:—**shut out from,** c._genitive_, ἐ. ἄλλον ἄλλοσε στέγης E. [prev. cited]:—_passive_, **to be shut out,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “HF” 330.}}
**2** _metaphorically_, **shut out, exclude from,** πόλιν τῆς μετοχῆς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.144}}; τῆς συμμαχίας, τῶν ὅρκων, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.85, 3.74}}: with _accusative_ et _infinitive_, ἐξέκλειον λόγου τυγχάνειν τοὺς ἄλλους {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.26.}}
**3** **hinder, prevent,** τῷ καιρῷ τὴν κατηγορίαν {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 18.8.2; τὴν θήραν 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 3.16:}} —_passive_, ἐκκληϊόμενοι τῇ ὥρῃ **being prevented** by [want of] time, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.31; ἐκκλεισθεὶς ὑπὸ τῶν καιρῶν 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 18.3}}: with _infinitive_, ἐ. ποιεῖν τι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] 4.32, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Magna Moralia” 1198b16.}}
**4** **shut off, cut off,** ζωῆς ὁδούς {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Oppianus Apamensis Epicus “Cynegetica” 2.342.}}

1577:
** ἐκκλησί-α**, ἡ,
(ἔκκλητος) **assembly duly summoned,** less general than σύλλογος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.22, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 456b, etc.}}; applied to the Homeric Assemblies, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1285a11}}; to the Samian Assembly, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.142}}; to the Spartan, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.87}}; to the meeting of the Amphictyons at Delphi, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.124}}; at Athens, ἐ. κύριαι, opposed to σύγκλητοι, Arist.{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 43.4}}; κυρία ἐ., at Amorgos, {{Refs|“IG” 12(7).237.46}}; ἐ. συναγείρειν, συνάγειν, συλλέγειν, ἀθροίζειν, call **an assembly,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.142, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.60, 8.97, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 1.6.8; ἐ. ποιεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 746, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.139, +others; ἐ. ποιεῖν τινί 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 169; διδόναι τινί 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 4.34.6}}; ἐ. γίγνεται **an assembly** is held, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.8; καταστάσης ἐ. [prev. author] 1.31; ἦν ἐ. τοῖς στρατηγοῖς 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.2}}; ἐ. διαλύειν, ἀναστῆσαι, dissolve **it,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.69 (_passive_), 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.4.42; ἀφιέναι 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Tiberius Gracchus” 16}}; ἐ. ἀνεβλήθη was adjourned, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.45; ἐ. περί τινος 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1030, etc.}}
**2**=Latin _Comitia,_ ἐ. λοχῖτις, φρατρική, = **Comitia Centuriata, Curiata,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 4.20.}}
**3** ={ψήφισμα}, ἀναγιγνωσκομένης ἐ. {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vitae Sophistarum” 2.1.11.}}
**II** in LXX, _the Jewish congregation,._{{LXX|LXX.Deu. 31.30, +others}}
**II.2** in **NT, the Church,** as a body of Christians, {{NT|NT.Matt.16.18, NT.1Cor.11.22; ἡ κατ᾽ οἶκόν τινος ἐ. NT.Rom.16.5}}; as a building, {{Refs|“Codex Justinianus [4th-6th c.AD]” 1.1.5 “Intr.”, etc.}}

1578:
** ἐκ-κλίνω**[ῑ],
**bend out of the regular line, bend outwards** or **away,** opposed to ἐγκλίνω, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 38 (assuming variant)}}; **change the form of** a word, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 404d.}}
**2** **dislocate,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 7 (_passive_).}}
**3** **embezzle,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Dionysius Comicus 3.10.}}
**4** **pervert,** δικαιώματα {{LXX|LXX.1Ki.8.3.}}
**II** _intransitive_, **turn away,** ἀπό τινος {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.73, LXX.Num.22.32(33); ἐκ τῆς ὁδοῦ [prev. work] 23}}; ἐκ νόμου θεοῦ{{LXX|LXX.Job.34.27}}: _absolutely_, **give ground, retire,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.4.23}}; **give way, fall from its place,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “Cyn.” 6.10.}}
**II.2** with _accusative_, **avoid, shun,** ἐ. τι καὶ μὴ πράττειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 746c; ἐπερχόμενον ἐ. νέφος 4th c.BC: Demades Orator 15; τὴν τῶν θηρίων ἔφοδον 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.34.4; στρατείαν [prev. author] 5.42.4,etc.}}:—_passive_, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Epictetus Philosophus “Enchiridion” 2.}}
**II.3** with _preposition_, **turn away** or **aside towards,** κατά τι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.1.30; ἐπὶ τὰς ἔξω οἰκίας “BGU” 1215.9 (3rd c.BC)}}; ἐ. εἰς δῆμον, εἰς ὀλιγαρχίαν, **decline** into a democracy or oligarchy, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1273a5}}; πρός τινα **visit** a person on one's journey, {{LXX|LXX.Gen.19.3.}}

1580:
** ἐκκομ-ίζω**,
**carry** or **bring out,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.34, 3.24, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 294}}; especially to a place of safety, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.160, 3.122, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.6}}; ἐκκομίζειν τινὰ ἐκ τοῦ μέλλοντος γίνεσθαι πρήγματος **to keep** him **out** of trouble, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.43}}:—_middle_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 8.20, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.78}}; ἐσεκομίσαντο καὶ ἐξεκομίσαντο ἃ ἐβούλοντο, of those relieved from a state of siege, {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.117}}: _absolutely_, **remove,** ἐς τοὺς Λοκρούς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.32.}}
**2** especially **carry out** a corpse, **bury,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 35.6.2 (_passive_), 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Cicero” 42 (_passive_), etc.}}
**3** ἐ. σῖτον, of a horse, **throw** the provender **out of** the manger, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitandi ratione” 4.2.}}
**4** **carry home,** ἄνδρας {{Refs|[prev. author] “An.” 6.6.36.}}
**II** **endure to the end,** τὸ πεπρωμένον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 1269.}}
**III** _middle_, **receive** what is due, λόγους, ὀψώνια, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PLille” 3.79 (3rd c.BC), “PSI” 4.436 (3rd c.BC).}}

1581:
** ἐκκόπτ-ω**,
**cut out, knock out,** τοὺς γομφίους {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Phrynichus Comicus 68; τῶν ἑρπετῶν ἐξέκοψε τὸ φθέγμα 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Iambi” 1.163:}} —_passive_, ἢν..τωφθαλμὼ᾽ κκοπῇς **have** your eyes **knocked out,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 342; τὸν ὀφθαλμὸν ἐκκεκομμένος 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.67}}; ἐξεκέκοπτο τὴν φωνήν **had lost** his voice, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “JTr.” 16.}}
**2** **cut** [trees] **out of** a wood, **fell,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.37 (_passive_), 9.97, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.99, etc.; δένδρα ἐκκεκόφασι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 6.5.37}}; παράδεισον **laid waste** the park, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|[prev. author] “An.” 1.4.10; χωρία 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 8.87; νήσους καὶ πόλεις 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pompeius” 24: hence}}
**2.b** _metaphorically_, **cut off, make an end of,** τοὺς ἄνδρας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.110}}; ἐ. φενακισμόν, ἱεροσυλίαν, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Dinarchus Orator 2.4, 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 8.39}}; **eradicate** abuses, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“OGI” 669.64 (Egypt, 1st c.AD)}}; τὴν αἰσθητικὴν ἐνέργειαν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Partibus Animalium” 656b5}}; **extirpate,** [λύπας] {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Diogenes Oenoandensis Epicureus 2}}:—_passive_, ἡ θρασύτης ἐξεκέκοπτο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Charmides” 155c.}}
**2.c** ἐ. πλοῖα **scuttle** ships, {{Refs|“IG” 12(7).386.9 (from Amorgos).}}
**3** as military term, **beat off, repulse,** τὰς ἀκροβολίσεις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 6.2.15; τοὺς ἐπὶ τῷ λόφῳ [prev. author] “HG” 7.4.26.}}
**4** **win,** in throwing the dice, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 44, Menecr.I 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator}}:— _passive_, **to be ruined at play,** {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}
**5** ἐ. θύρας **break open,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 3.6; οἰκίαν ἐ. 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 4.3.10.}}
**6** **cut out** or **erase** an inscription, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“SIG” 38.38 (Teos, 5th c.BC), 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1400a33; οὐδενὶ ἐξέσται..γράμμα ἐκκόψαι “CIG” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” 3028 (from Ephesus), +others}}; ἐ. τὴν χεῖρα {{NT|NT.Matt.5.30}}; **cut out,** as a surgeon does, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Cat.” 24.}}
**7** **coin, stamp** money, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 11.26.}}
**7.b** _metaphorically_, φαντασίαν ἐ. ὡς.. {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ παρρησίας” p.56}} O.; γένη οὐκ ἐκκοπτόμενα ἰδίοις τέλεσι genders not **marked** by different terminations, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 104.23}}; ἐ. ἀναφθέγματα **coin** expressions, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “D.” 3.14.}}
**8** **hinder, bring to a stop,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|PAlex. 4.1 (3rd c.BC), 2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 268.6.}}
**II** _intransitive_, **pause, come to a stop,** {{Refs|[prev. author] 260.24.}}

1582:
** ἐκκρεμ-άννῡμι**,
_future_ -κρεμάσω, **hang from** or **upon** a thing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 22 (uncertain); τι ἔκ τινος 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 1363; λίθον τοῦ ποδός “Anthologia Graeca” 11.100 (1st c.AD: Lucillius Epigrammaticus); τινὰ ἐξ Ὀλύμπου 2nd c.AD: Apollodorus Damascenus Mechanicus 1.3.5.}}
**II** _passive_, like{ἐκκρέμαμαι}, **hang on by, cling to,** with _genitive_, τῶν τε ξυσκήνων ἤδη ἀπιόντων ἐκκρεμαννύμενοι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.75, compare 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Tox.” 6.}}
**II.2** _metaphorically_, **to be devoted to,** Ἄρεος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 950.}}

1584:
** ἐκλάμπ-ω**,
**shine** or **beam forth,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.82, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 1083 (_anapaest meter_); ὅπλα ὥστε κάτοπτρον ἐξέλαμπεν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.1.2, etc.; ὀμμάτων ἐ. πόθος “APl.” 4.182 (1st c.AD: Leonidas Alexandrinus Epigrammaticus); ὥσπερ ἀστραπήν 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 7.88; πῦρ ἐκ λίθων ἐ. 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 516b11: _metaphorically_, δίκας δ᾽ ἐξέλαμψε θεῖον φάος “Tragica Adespota” 500; ὥσπερ ἐκ πυρείων ἐ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 435a; ἐξ. ἡ δόξα 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 31.23.2}}; of persons, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.326, +others}}; **burst forth violently,** of a fever, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀρχαίης ἰητρικῇς” 16}}; of sound, **to be clearly heard,** [ἐκ τῆς κραυγῆς] ἐξέλαμψε τὸ καλεῖν τὸν βασιλέα {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 15.31.1.}}
**2** **to be distinguished,** δι᾽ εὐφυΐαν {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Cicero” 2; τῶν ἄλλων 4th c.AD: Libanius Sophista “Orationes” 62.37.}}
**II** with _accusative_ _cognate_, **flash forth,** πυρωπὸν γλῆνος ἐκλάμψαν φλόγα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 300.4}}; σέλας uncertain reading in {{Refs 6th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 330, compare 3rd c.BC: Lycophron Tragicus 1091; πῦρ 2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Συριακή” 56, compare 6th c.BC: Bias Lyricus “fragment Lyric poetry”: _metaphorically_, νοῦς ἐ. αἰσθήσεις 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.72; ἀπὸ τοῦ ἑνὸς ὁ θεὸς ἑαυτὸν ἐξέλαμψε 4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus “de Mysteriis” 8.2.}}
**III** Astrology texts,= διαυγάζω {{Refs 1st c.AD+|111, “PLond.” 1.132.95 (1st-2nd c.AD).}}

1585:
** ἐκλανθάνω**,
**escape notice utterly**:—_middle_, **forget utterly,** with _genitive_ _of things_, ἐκ χόλω τῶδε λαθοίμεθα {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus “Supp.” 23.9}}; τοῦδ᾽ ἐκλανθάνει thou **forgettest** this **entirely,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1005; ἐγλαθόμενος τῆς εὐθύνης “POxy.” 1203.8 (1st c.AD),compare 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.247, +others; ἐ. ὅτι.. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Axiochus” 369e.}}
**II** causal in _present_ **ἐκληθάνω**, with _aorist 1_ ἐξέλησα, _Aeolic dialect_ ἐξέλᾱσα (see. below): _Epic dialect_ _reduplicate_ _aorist 2_ ἐκλέλᾰθον:
**II.1** _active_, **make** one **quite forgetful of** a thing, c._genitive_ _of things_, ἐκ δέ με πάντων ληθάνει ὅσσ᾽ ἔπαθον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 7.220; ἔκ μ᾽ ἔλᾱσας ἀλγέων 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 95:}} with _accusative_ _of things_, ἐκλέλαθον κιθαριστύν **made** him **quite forget** his harping, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.600: _absolutely_, Ἀΐδας ὁ ἐκλελάθων (reduplicate _present_) 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 1.63.}}
**II.2** _middle_ and _passive_, **forget utterly,** ὀϊζύος ἐκλελαθέσθαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.285; ἀικῆς ἐξελάθοντο 16.602; ὡς ἐκλέλησμαί γ᾽ ἃ πάρος εἴπομεν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 1272}}: with _infinitive_, ἐκλάθετο..καταβῆναι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.557; λελάθοντο.., οὐ μὰν ἐκλελάθοντ᾽ 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 93.}}

1586:
** ἐκλέγω**,
_future_ _passive_ ἐκλεγήσεσθαι {{Refs|“IG” 12.76.16}}: _perfect_ _passive_ ἐξείλεγμαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus Alcaeus1.121e}}, and in _middle_ sense, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 20.131, but ἐκλέλεγμαι 4th-3rd c.BC: Diphilus Comicus 44, 3rd c.BC: Posidippus Comicus 27.9 (probably)}}:—**pick** or **single out,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.59, etc.}}; especially of soldiers, rowers, etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 1.6.19, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 535a; ἐκ πάντων κεφάλαια [prev. author] “Lg.” 811a:}} —_passive_ {{Refs|[prev. author]Alcaeus[same place]; ἐκλελεγμένος}}**select, recondite,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Diogenes Cynicus Philosophus}} Oen.23:—_middle_, **pick out for oneself, choose,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.199, 3.38, D.[same place]; τὰ κάλλιστα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 198d, +others; ἐξ ἁπάντων 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 9.58.}}
**2** Lit. Crit., **select,** λέξεις καλάς {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Compositione Verborum” 3}}; compare ἐκλογή.
**3** _middle_, of God, **elect, choose,** {{LXX+NT|LXX.Deu.4.37, NT.Eph.1.4, etc.}}
**4** ἐκλέγειν τὰς πολιὰς (i.e. τρίχας) **pull out** one's grey hairs, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 908, “fragment” 410.}}
**II** **levy taxes** or **tribute,** χρήματα παρά τινος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.44; τὰς ἐπικαρπίας 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.92, compare “IG” 12.76.8 (_passive_, [prev. work]16)}}; ἔκ τινων {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 49.49}}; **take toll of,** χαλκοῦς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Characteres” 6.4: with _accusative_ _person_, ἐ. τέλη τοὺς καταπλέοντας 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.113: c._accusative_ et _genitive_, τὴν δεκάτην τῶν πλοίων 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 1.1.22.}}
**III** **declare,**{{Refs|Prisc. p.294D., “Corpus Glossariorum Latinorum”}}

1587:
** ἐκλείπ-ω**,
**leave out, pass over,** πολλὰ δ᾽ ἐκλείπω λέγων {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 513; ἐ. ὄχλον λόγων [prev. author] “Pr.” 827, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 25.47}}; ἐ. Ἄνδρον **leave out, pass over** Andros, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.33; ἐ. ὁτιοῦν τῆς παρασκευῆς 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.48; τὴν στρατιάν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 5.2.22; εἴ τι ἐξέλιπον, σὸν ἔργον ἀναπληρῶσαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 188e:}} —_passive_, ὄνειδος οὐκ ἐκλείπεται **fails** not **to appear,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 97.}}
**2** **forsake, desert, abandon,** τὰς πατρίδας, τὴν ξυμμαχίην, etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.169, 6.13, etc.; θήρας μόχθον 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 52; τὸ ξυνώμοτον 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.74; τὸν ὅρκον 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 750}}; **abandon, quit,** τὴν τάξιν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.24, +others; τὴν χώρην [prev. author] 4.105, 118, +others; τὸν πλοῦν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 911, compare 58}}; **give up,** τὴν τυραννίδα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.123; τὰ ὑπάρχοντα 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.144; θρήνους 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 1635; see below 11.2.}}
**3** frequently in elliptic phrases, ἐκλείπειν τὴν πόλιν εἰς τὰ ἄκρα **abandon** the city **and go** to the heights, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.100, compare 8.50, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.4.2; ἐξέλειπον οἴκους πρὸς ἄλλον εὐνάτορα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 1040 (Lyric poetry).}}
**4** εἴ τις ἐξέλιπε τὸν ἀριθμόν (of the Persian immortals) if any one **left** the number **incomplete,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.83.}}
**5** **fail** one, ἐκλελοίπασιν ὑμᾶς αἱ προφάσεις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 8.16, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 657d.}}
**II** _intransitive_, of the Sun or Moon, **suffer eclipse,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.28; in full, ὁ ἥλιος ἐκλιπὼν τὴν ἐκ τοῦ οὐρανοῦ ἕδρην 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.37; ἐ. τὰς ὁδούς 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nu.” 584.}}
**II.2** **die,** οἱ ἐκλιπόντες the **deceased,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 856e; τῶν ἄλλων ἐκλελοιπότων 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 11.10, etc.}}; of trees, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“BGU” 1120.33 (1st c.BC)}}; more frequently in full, ἐ. βίον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1131}}; ὑφ᾽ ὧν ἥκιστα ἐχρῆν τὸν βίον ἐκλιπών (=ἀποθανών) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 1.21; so ἐ. φάος 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 1186, etc.}}
**II.3** **faint,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “προρρητικόν” 1.71.}}
**II.4** generally, **leave off, cease,** τῇ μοι [ὁ λόγος] ἐξέλιπε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.239; ἐ. πυρετός 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἀφορισμοί” 4.56, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.87}}; ἐκλέλοιπεν εὐφρόνη, i.e. it is day, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 19; ὥστε μὴ 'κλιπεῖν κλέος [prev. work] 985, compare 1149}}; [αἱ ἐργασίαι] ἐκλελοίπασιν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 8.20}}: with _participle_, **leave off** doing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Menexenus” 234b, compare 249b: with _genitive_, θεραπείας 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Marcellus” 17.}}
**II.5** **fail, be wanting,** ῥώμη γὰρ ἐκλέλοιπεν ἣν πρὶν εἴχομεν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 230, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 485d; τῶν ἐπιτηδείων ἐκλειπόντων 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 16.75; ἡ φωνὴ ἐξέλιπε 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Nigr.” 35}}; περὶ ὧν ἐ. [ὁ νόμος] {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1286a37}}: Grammars, of words in a sentence, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 11.17}}; of grammatical forms,{{Refs|[prev. work] 168.21.}}
**II.6** **remain, be left,** {{LXX|LXX.4Ki.7.13.}}
**II.7** **depart,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 128 (Lyric poetry), “Th.” 219.}}
**II.8** ἐκλείπων σφυγμός **remittent** pulse, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 9.66.}}

1588:
** ἐκλεκτ-ός**, ή, όν,
**picked out, select,** {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Ibycus Lyricus 22, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.100; τὸ τῶν ἐ. δικαστήριον 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 938b}}; ἐ. δικασταί,=Latin _iudices selecti_, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“OGI” 499.3(2nd c.AD).}} _adverb_ -τῶς interpolated in Suid. see at {ἐπίλεκτος}.
**2** **choice, pure,** σμύρνη {{LXX+3rd c.BC+|LXX.Exo.30.23; βοήθημα 3rd c.BC: Asclepiades Epigrammaticus cited in 6th c.AD: Aëtius Medicus 9.12; ἀνδρών “PRein.” 43.9 (2nd c.AD)}}; ἐκλεκτόν,= **corn,** {{LXX+2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Aquila LXX.Psa.64(65).14.}}
**II** **chosen of God, elect,** {{LXX+NT|LXX.Isa.43.20, NT.Mark.13.20, etc.}}

1589:
** ἐκλογ-ή**, ἡ,
**choice, selection,** τῶν ἀρχόντων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 414a, 536c; ἐ. ποιεῖσθαι [prev. author] “Lg.” 802b}}; ἐ. [τῶν ἀρίστων νόμων] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1181a18; τῶν ἐναντίων [prev. author] “Metaphorical” 1004a2; ὀνομάτων 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 1.162 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus, 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Compositione Verborum” 1, etc.; ὀνόματος 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 71.10; κατ᾽ ἐκλογὴν ἀριστίνδην κεκριμένοι 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 6.10.9; ἐπὶ ἐγλογῇ γεωργεῖν “PTeb.” 5.166 (2nd c.BC).}}
**2** **levying of troops,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 5.63.11.}}
**3** **collection of tribute,** etc., κριθῶν Legal Attica cited in {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 6.235c; χρημάτων 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 42.6}}; σίτου Cratesap.{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 6.235b.}}
**4** Theol., **election**{{NT|NT.Rom.9.11}}, etc.; σκεῦος ἐκλογῆς {{NT|NT.Act.9.15.}}
**5** **balancing** of accounts, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“PRyl.” 157.6 (2nd c.AD).}}
**II** **extract, quotation from a book,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Biographus Cit. 3, 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 14.663c, 3rd c.BC: Antigonus Carystius Paradoxographus “Mirabilia” 15.}}
**II.2** **choice collection** of passages, such as **the Eclogae** or **'Elegant Extracts'** of Stobaeus: ἐκλογαὶ Ἀρχιγένους **select prescriptions** of {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 14.343.}}
**II.3** διὰ τὴν ἐ. τῶν ἀνθρώπων because they were **picked** men, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.47.9,compare 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.362.}}
**II.4** ἐκλογήν· κάλαθον (_Laconian dialect_), {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}

1590:
** ἐκλύω** [ῡ,
see at {λύω}], **set free,** πόνων **from** labours, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 328}}; **release,** ὕδατα {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PTeb.” 49.6 (2nd c.BC)}}:—_passive_, **to be set free,** ἐκλέλυμαι πόθου {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 1339; ἐκ δεσμῶν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 67d}}:—_middle_, **get** one **set free, release,** ἀλλ᾽ ἄγε δή σε κακῶν ἐκλύσομαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.286; τοῦ φόβου σ᾽ ἐξελυσάμην 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1003; θανάτου νιν ἐκλύσασθε 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 818; ἐξελυσάμην βροτοὺς τὸ μὴ μολεῖν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 237: with _accusative_ _person_ only, ἐξελύσαντο τοὺς Ἀργείους 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 7.1.25: _absolutely_, ἐξελυσάμην}} I **delivered** him **from danger,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 531.}}
**II** **unloose,** ἐ. τόξα **unstring** a bow, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.173; ἐ. ἁρμούς 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 825}}; σκαιὸν ἐκλύσων στόμα **likely to let loose** a foolish tongue, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1225.}}
**II.2** **make an end of,** ἐξέλυσας..σκληρᾶς ἀοιδοῦ δασμόν **paid** it **off,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “OT” 35; ἐπίπονον ἁμέραν [prev. author] “Tr.” 654; μόχθον 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 695; ἔριν καὶ φιλονικίαν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 9.14}}; ἐξελύσατε (variant{-λύσασθε}) τὰς παρασκευάς {{Refs|[prev. author] 18.26.}}
**II.3** **relax,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 610a27; τῆς φροντίδος τὸ ἀκριβές 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Dom.” 17}}:— _passive_, **to be faint, fail,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἀφορισμοί” 2.41, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 15.59, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.224, 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Ir.” p.69 W., etc.; πρὸς τὸν πόλεμον 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.150}}; ἐκλυθῆναι τοῖς σώμασι, τῇ ψυχῇ, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Fragmenta” 144, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 29.17.4}} (so _intransitive_ in _active_, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Bellum Judaicum” 1.33.5)}}, etc.; of things, **to be unserviceable,** τὰ τῶν πλοίων ἐκλελυμένα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1320b37}}; ἐκλύεται ὁ ῥοῦς, τὰ ῥεύματα, **cease,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 4.43.9, 4.41.5.}}
**II.4** in Medicine texts, ἐ. κοιλίαν **relax** the bowels, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 4.169.}}
**II.5** **pay in full,** δάνειον {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Caesar” 12 (_passive_).}}
**II.5.b** **purchase,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 6.91.}}
**II.6** **resolve** a doubt, in _passive_, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 176.24; also τὰ ὑπ᾽ ἀμφιβολίαν πίπτοντα ἐκλύεται τοῦ ἀμφιβόλου [prev. work] 311.11.}}
**II.7** **dissolve,** τι ὄξει {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 11.106.}}
**III** _intransitive_, **to break up, depart,** {{LXX|LXX.2Mac.13.16.}}

1591:
** ἐκμάσσω**,
_Attic dialect_ **ἐκμάττω**, _perfect_ ἐκμέμᾰχα (ἐκμέμα-κα codices) conjecture in {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Demosthene” 4:}}_aorist 2_ _passive_ -εμάγην [ᾰ] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 191d}}; also _aorist_ I _participle_ ἐκμαχθείς {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}:—**wipe off, wipe away,** κάρᾳ κηλῖδας ἐξέμαξεν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 446}}; ἔκμασσε [τὸ αἷμα] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 1400; ἀλωπεκίας ὀθονίῳ 2nd c.AD: Archigenes Medicus cited in 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 12.406}}:—_middle_, **wipe away one's tears,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|Anthologia Graeca 5.42 (2nd-3rd c.AD: Rufinus Epigrammaticus).}}
**2** **wipe dry,** ὑπὸ σπόγγου {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ διαίτης ὀξέων” 65 (_passive_),compare 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 6.9}}; [τοὺς ἔμπροσθεν πόδας] ἐ. εἰς τοὺς μέσους, of bees, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 624b1.}}
**II** of an artist, **mould** or **model** in wax or plaster, αὑτὸν ἐκμάττειν τε καὶ ἐνιστάναι εἰς τοὺς τῶν κακιόνων τύπους **to mould** and adapt oneself to.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 396d}}; of pessaries, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Steril.” 230}}:—so in _middle_, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Nat.Mul.” 109}}; ὧν ἔτι θερμὰ κονία.. ἐκμάσσεται ἴχνη of whose yet warm footsteps the dust **receives the impress,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 17.122}}; **express, imitate,** ἵππου γενεήν {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Nicander Epicus “Theriaca” 740; τὸν Λυσιακὸν χαρακτῆρα ἐκμέμακται 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Demosthene” 13 (so in _active_, [prev. work] 4 codices, uncertain)}}; ἐς τὸ ἀκριβέστατον ἐξεμάξατο τὸν διδάσκαλον he **was the image of** his master, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Alciphro Epistolographus 3.64:}} —_passive_, μάλθης ἄναγνα σώματ᾽ ἐκμεμαγμένοι (variant{-μένα}) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ichn.” 140}}; ἐκεῖνος αὐτὸς ἐκμεμαγμένος his very **image,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 255; βασιλέως..εἰκόν᾽ ἐκμεμ. “IGRom.” 1.1190 (from Memnon)}}; ὃ ἂν ἐκμαγῇ whatever **be impressed,** whatever **impression be made** (compare ἐκμαγεῖον), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 191d}}; τὴν ἰδέαν τοῦ παιδὸς ἐκμεμάχθαι **had impressed upon him** the image of the boy, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Cicero” 44; ποιότης ἀπὸ μένοντος ἐκμαγεῖσα θείου λόγου 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.548.}}

1593:
** ἐκνεύω**,
_aorist_ ἐξένευσα, **turn the head out of** its natural position, of a horse, ἐ. ἄνω to **toss** the head, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitandi ratione” 5.4}}; τῇ κεφαλῇ ἐκνεύσας **by a side-movement** with the head, of the wild boar, {{LXX|[prev. author] “Cyn.” 10.12, cf. LXX.2Ki.23.16.}}
**2** with _accusative_, **shun, avoid,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ σημείων καὶ σημειώσεων” 27, 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.146, 5th-6th c.AD: Orphica “Argonautica” 458; ξίφος Hegesias cited in 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Compositione Verborum” 18; πληγήν 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 17.100.}}
**II** **fall headlong,** εἰς θάνατον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 1268; ἐ. πρός τι}}**to turn aside,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.297: with _genitive_, τῶν παρόντων 3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 6.7.34.}}
**III** **motion away,** ἐξένευσ᾽ ἀποστῆναι πρόσω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 1330.}}

1594:
** ἐκνήφω**,
_future_ **-ψω**{{LXX|LXX.Gen.9.24}}:—**sleep off a drunken fit, become sober again,** [same place], Hippoloch. cited in {{NT+1st c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 4.130b, “Anthologia Graeca” 5.134, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Demosthenes” 20: _metaphorically_, NT.1Cor.15.34}}: with _genitive_, **recover from,** χάρμῃ ἐ. τῆς δυσθυμίης {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Aretaeus Medicus “περὶ αἰτιῶν καὶ σημείων χρονίων παθῶν” 1.5.}}
**II** _transitive_, **carry off,** ἀρρώστημα ἐκνήψει ὕπνος {{LXX|LXX.Sir.34(31).2 (uncertain).}}

1595:
** ἑκούσι-ος**, α, ον
{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 727, 1123, etc.}}; also ος, ον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ph.” 1318, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 151, 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.2.3, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.44, etc.}}: (ἑκών):—of actions, **voluntary,** πόνοι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 240; βλάβαι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ph.”}}[same place]; φυγή E.[same place]; ἁμάρτημα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.92, etc.; συμβόλαια 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 556b; πράξεις [prev. work] 603c, +others}}; ἀδικήματα {{Refs|[prev. author] “Lg.” 860e, +others, etc.; γυμνασιαρχία}}**undertaken voluntarily,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|POxy. 473.3 (2nd c.AD)}}; τὰ ἑ. **voluntary acts,** opposed to τὰ ἀκούσια, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“IG” 1.1, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.1.18, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1109b31.}}
**2** rarely of persons, **willing, acting of free will,** ἥμαρτεν οὐχ ἑκουσία {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 1123; ἑ. ἀποθανεῖν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.138.}}
**II** _adverb_ **-ίως**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 1037, etc.; also ἑκουσίῳ τρόπῳ [prev. author] “Med.” 751}}; ἐξ ἑκουσίας (i.e. γνώμης) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 727; καθ᾽ ἑκουσίαν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.27.}}

1596:
Included with: ** ἑκούσι-ος**, α, ον
{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 727, 1123, etc.}}; also ος, ον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ph.” 1318, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 151, 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.2.3, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.44, etc.}}: (ἑκών):—of actions, **voluntary,** πόνοι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 240; βλάβαι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ph.”}}[same place]; φυγή E.[same place]; ἁμάρτημα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.92, etc.; συμβόλαια 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 556b; πράξεις [prev. work] 603c, +others}}; ἀδικήματα {{Refs|[prev. author] “Lg.” 860e, +others, etc.; γυμνασιαρχία}}**undertaken voluntarily,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|POxy. 473.3 (2nd c.AD)}}; τὰ ἑ. **voluntary acts,** opposed to τὰ ἀκούσια, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“IG” 1.1, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.1.18, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1109b31.}}
**2** rarely of persons, **willing, acting of free will,** ἥμαρτεν οὐχ ἑκουσία {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 1123; ἑ. ἀποθανεῖν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.138.}}
**II** _adverb_ **-ίως**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 1037, etc.; also ἑκουσίῳ τρόπῳ [prev. author] “Med.” 751}}; ἐξ ἑκουσίας (i.e. γνώμης) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 727; καθ᾽ ἑκουσίαν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.27.}}

1599:
** ἐκπέμπω**:
**I** of persons, **send out** or **forth from,** with _genitive_ loci, ὅπως Πρίαμον..νηῶν ἐκπέμψειε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.681; ὅς τίς σε..δώματος ἐκπέμψῃσι 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.336, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1128; ἐ. ἐκ τῆς πόλεως 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 6.78}}:—_middle_, δόμου ἐκπέμψασθε θύραζε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 20.361,compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 612 (Lyric poetry), etc.}}
**I.2** **bring out by calling, call** or **fetch out,** τινὰ ἐκτὸς πυλῶν {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ant.” 19}}:—so in _middle_, {{Refs|[prev. author] “OT” 951}}:—_passive_, **go forth, depart,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “OC” 1664.}}
**I.3** **send forth, dispatch,** πρέσβεις, στρατιάς, οἰκήτορας, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.90, 141, 4.49; ἐ. συμπρεσβευτὰς τοὺς ἐχθρούς 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1271a24; ἐ. ἀποικίας οἷον σμήνη μελιττῶν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 293d, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1273b19}}:—_passive_, τῶν -ομένων καὶ εἰσαγομένων ἐπιστολῶν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeneas Tacticus 10.6.}}
**I.4** **send away,** τινὰ ἐς.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.160; ἐ. τινὰ ἄτιμον 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 789; καθάρμαθ᾽ ὥς τις ἐκπέμψας 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 98}}: in Prose, **divorce** a wife, ἐ. γυναῖκα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.59, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 14.28, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 59.55}}:—also in _middle_, γῆς φυγάδας ἐκπέμψασθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 309.}}
**I.5** with _double_ _accusative_, **conduct across,** τινὰ τὸν Ἰορδάνην {{LXX|LXX.2Ki.19.31.}}
**II** of things, **send out, send abroad,** κειμήλια πολλὰ καὶ ἐσθλά..ἵνα περ τάδε τοι σόα μίμνῃ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.381; δῶρά τινι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.136; σῖτόν τινι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.16 (nisi to be read ἐσ-).}}
**II.2** **export,** ἐ. ὧν ἐπλεόναζον {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1257a32:}} —_middle_, τὰ πλεονάζοντα τῶν γιγνομένων ἐκπέμψασθαι **export** their surplus products, {{Refs|[prev. work] 1327a27.}}
**II.3** **send forth, give out,** σέλας {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 281}}; πνεῦμα, [ὑγρόν], {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Partibus Animalium” 664a18, “HA” 589b18; δυσοσμίαν 4th c.AD: Alciphro Epistolographus 3.28.}}
**II.4** **utter, pronounce,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 35.1 (_passive_).}}
